ortion from Km.3.100 to \n5.100) & Construction of Major \nBridge at river Aami on NH -328A on \nEPC mode \n11.00 04-08-22 28.02.23 \n20 \nReconstruction of minor bridge at \nkm.244.20 of NH -76 (New NH-35) in \nthe State of Uttar Pradesh \n1 Job 24-07-22 31.03.23 \n21 \nStrengthening of NH -727B from \nSikandarpur to Ballia (km.102.000 to \nkm.136.900) in the State of Uttar \nPradesh under EPC mode \n34.90 10-11-22 28.02.23 \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |164", "t": "Education in Maithili Language", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 164\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nSHORTAGE OF PILOTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether there is an acute shortage of pilots in the country and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(b) the gap between the demand and supply of pilots along with the steps\nbeing taken by the Government to meet the demand; and\n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up universities/training and flying\nschools to meet the requirements of rapidly growing aviation sector and if so,\nthe details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) and (b) There is no shortage of pilots in India. The total number of CPLs\nissued by DGCA in 2022 is 1165 which is the highest on record.\n \n(c) DGCA approves Flying Training Organisations (FTOs) as per laid down\nRules and Regulations and reviews its regulations as per international\npractices from time to time to further improve the standards of FTOs and to\nfacilitate opening up of more number of FTOs. At present, there are 35 Flying\nTraining Organisations (FTOs) operating at 53 bases as approved by the\nDirectorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA).\n \nIn 2021, after a competitive bidding process, AAI awarded nine FTO slots at\nfive airports at Belagavi (Karnataka), Jalgaon (Maharashtra), Kalaburagi\n(Karnataka), Khajuraho (Madhya Pradesh) and Lilabari (Assam).\nIn June 2022, under second round of bidding, six FTO slots were awarded by\nAAI at five airports namely: two slots at Bhavnagar (Gujarat), and one each\nat Hubballi (Karnataka), Kadappa (Andhra Pradesh), Kishangarh\n(Rajasthan) and Salem (Tamil Nadu).\n \n164.  MS. NUSSRAT JAHAN\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |165", "t": "Renovation and Reconstruction of Naamghars", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n165\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2023\n \n \nPRICE OF RAW MATERIALS FOR MSMEs\n \n \n165.\n \n \nSHRI THIRUMAAVALAVAN THOL\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is aware of the price of raw materials such as metals, \nplastics, etc., for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs); \n \n(b) \n \nif so, whether the raw \nmaterial prices have affected MSMEs productions;\n \n(c) \n \nif so, whether there is decline in the demand for MSME products in the recent years;\n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n(e) \n \nthe measures taken by the Government to support MSMEs?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDMUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) to (d)\n: \n \nThe price of raw materials such as metals, chemicals, plastics, rubbers etc., is \nmonitored through the Wholesale Price Index (Base 2011\n-\n12). The details are at \nAnnex\n-\n1\n.\n \n \n                \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprise (MSME) does not mai\nntain \ndata with respect to effect of raw material prices on production or aggregate demand of \nMSME products.\n \n \n(e):    The Government has taken several steps to support MSMEs which inter alia include, \nproviding financial assistance under \nRaw Material Assist\nance (RMA) Scheme of National \nSmall Industries Corporation Limited (NSIC),\n \nExemption on Basic Custom Duty on steel \nscrap has been extended for up to 31st March, 2023, TMT bars below 8 mm have been \nexempted from the purview of the Quality Control Order & wa\nived off import duty for \nseveral raw materials like coking coal, Ferro\n-\nnickel etc.\n \n \n \n \n****\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nTable2: WPI (Base: 2011\n-\n12) and WPI bases Inflation of Industry\n-\ngroups of \nPaper, \nChemical, Rubber, Plastic and Basic Metal during the last three years\n \n \n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n2021\n-\n22*\n \nWPI\n \n \nPaper and Paper Products\n \n121.1\n \n121.7\n \n137.5\n \nChemical and Chemical Products \n \n117.5\n \n118.2\n \n133.5\n \nRubber and Plastic Products\n \n108.5\n \n111.3\n \n124.8\n \nBasic Metals\n \n106.2\n \n111.4\n \n140.1\n \nWPI\n-\nbased Inflation (Year\n-\non\n-\n \nYear)\n \nPaper and Paper Products\n \n-\n1.77\n \n0.52\n \n12.96\n \nChemical and Chemical Products\n \n-\n1.28\n \n0.60\n \n12.91\n \nRubber and Plastic products\n \n-\n0.99\n \n2.57\n \n12.19\n \nBasic Metals\n \n-\n5.35\n \n4.87\n \n25.75\n \n*Provisional\n \n \n \n \n \n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |166", "t": "Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS)", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI  \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.166 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \n \nIHHLs in Maharashtra \n \n166. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) t he details of the number of Individual  Household Latrines (IHHLs) that have been  \nconstructed in Maharashtra especially in Thane district  under the Swachh Bharat Mission -\nGramin (SBM-G) till date; \n \n(b) the details of the number of Community  Sanitary Complexe s that have been constructed \nunder phase-II of the SBM-G scheme in Maharashtra, especially in Thane district; \n \n(c) the details of the funds sanctioned and  utilized under the SBM -G Mission in Thane district  \nsince its inception to till date, year-wise; and \n \n(d) the details of the number of facilities for  biodegradable waste management and grey water  \nmanagement that have been constructed or are being  constructed under this scheme in \nMaharashtra especially in Thane district? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n \n(a) As per data reported by the State on the Integrated Management Information System \n(IMIS) of Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [ SBM(G)], 68.90 lakh Individual Household \nLatrines (IHHLs) have been constructed in Maharashtra under SBM(G) from 2014-15 to 2022-\n23 (upto 30.01.2023), out of which 90,436 IHHLs have been constructed in Thane district.   \n \n(b)  As per data reported by the State on the IMIS, 6,605 Community Sanitary Complexes \n(CSCs) have been constructed in Maharashtra under phase-II of SBM(G) from 2020-21 to \n2022-23 (upto 30.01.2023), out of which 58 CSCs have been constructed in Thane district. \n \n(c)  Under SBM(G), funds are released to State s/UTs.  Further distribution of the same to \nthe districts is done by the States /UTs.  Details of Centre share funds allocated by the \nGovernment   of India to Maharashtra under  SBM(G)  from  2014-15 to 2022-23 and  the funds  \n \n \n \n \n \n\ndrawn by the State, are as under: \n \n(Rs. in crore) \nYear Funds allocated Funds drawn by the State \n2014-15 236.11 236.11 \n2015-16 1014.30 567.45 \n2016-17 528.94 528.94 \n2017-18 1235.34 1235.34 \n2018-19 2709.52 1352.92 \n2019-20 679.67 396.97 \n2020-21 340.62 276.75 \n2021-22 1372.73 0.00 \n2022-23          \n(upto 30.01.2023) \n1740.11 0.00 \n           Total 4594.48 \n \n(d)  The details of  facilities for  biodegradable waste management and grey water  \nmanagement constructed under SBM(G) and in convergence with other schemes in \nMaharashtra and Thane district, as reported by the State on SBM(G) IMIS, are as under: \n \nBiodegradable waste management facilities  Maharashtra Thane district \nNo. of community compost pits  10,922 265 \nNo. of community bio-gas plants  861 1 \nNo. of vehicles for collection & transportation of waste 4,762 91 \nNo. of segregation bins at public places 6,213 11 \nNo. of waste collection and segregation sheds 3,084 2 \n \nGrey water management facilities  Maharashtra Thane district \nNo. of community soak pits/leach pits/magic pits  14,214 71 \nNo. of community grey water management systems (Waste \nStabilization Ponds, Constructed Wetlands, etc.) \n1,767 11 \nNo. of drainage facility  23,913 110 \nNo. of household compost pits  53,046 112 \nNo. of individual bio-gas plants  6,920 13 \nNo. of individual soak pits/leach pits/magic pits/kitchen \ngardens  \n6,48,675 3,522 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |167", "t": "Budgetary Allocation under PMKVY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO - 167 \nANSWERED ON – 13/03/2023 \n  \nBUDGETARY ALLOCATION UNDER PMKVY \n \n*167. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: \n           SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state:- \n \n(a) the details of the budgetary allocation made by  the Government under the \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana Phase-3 (PMKVY 3.0), State-wise particularly for \nOdisha and Maharashtra; \n \n(b)  the details of the funds actually released and  utilised there under in 2021-22 and \n2022-23 so far, State-wise particularly to Odisha and Maharashtra; \n \n(c) the aggregate cost incurred by the Government u nder the PMKVY Phase 3 \n(PMKVY 3.0) in placing one candidate under the said  scheme in the country particularly \nin Odisha and Maharashtra; \n \n(d) whether the aggregate cost incurred was greater  than the expected cost for \nplacement; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the reasons ther efore? \n \n \nANSWER  \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n  \n(a) to (e)         A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *167 TO BE ANSWERED ON 13.03.2 023 ASKED \nBY SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU AND SHRI GIRISH BHALCHA NDRA BAPAT \nREGARDING BUDGETARY ALLOCATION UNDER PMKVY  \n \n(a) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurs hip (MSDE) launched the \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY 3.0) on 15.01.2021 to deliver skills to \nyouth of the country to make them employable for better livelihood, with an outlay of Rs. \n948.90 crore. PMKVY 3.0 has two components, namely,  Centrally Sponsored Centrally \nManaged (CSCM) known as Central component which was  implemented through \nNational Skill Development Corporation (NSDC) and C entrally Sponsored State \nManaged (CSSM) known as State component which was i mplemented through State \nSkill Development Missions (SSDMs) of the State/UT.  Out of the total outlay of the \nscheme, 75%, i.e., Rs. 712 Cr. (approx.) was for Ce ntral component and 25%, i.e.,Rs. \n237 Cr. (approx) was for State component.Under Cent ral component of PMKVY, Rs. \n644.27 crore has been disbursed to NSDC for the imp lementation of scheme through \ntraining centres across the country covering all St ates. The State-wise allocation of \nfunds, including the states of Odisha and Maharasht ra under CSSMPMKVY 3.0, is \ngiven at Annexure I . \n \n(b) The State-wise fund utilised under CSCM compone nt and fund released and \nutilized under CSSM component of PMKVY 3.0 during t he Financial Year 2021-22 and \n2022-23 as on 28.02.2023 are given at Annexure II . \n \n(c) to (e) The overall training cost paid to Traini ng Providers (TPs) is inclusive of \ncost elements as laid down under Common Norms, whic h includes placement \nexpenses. The placement expenses are not separately identified. \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure I \nANNEXURE REFER TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUE STION NO. *167 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023  \n \nState-wise allocation of funds under Centrally Spon sored State Managed (CSSM) \ncomponent of PMKVY 3.0. \n(Rs. in Crore) \nS.NO State Funds Allocation \n1 A & N Islands 0.56 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 8.21 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 2.77 \n4 Assam 12.50 \n5 Bihar 15.77 \n6 Chandigarh 0.56 \n7 Chhattisgarh 4.86 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 0.83 \n9 Daman & Diu 0.00 \n10  Delhi 5.40 \n11  Goa  0.77 \n12  Gujarat 11.34 \n13  Haryana 6.57 \n14  Himachal Pradesh 2.12 \n15  Jammu & Kashmir 3.06 \n16  Jharkhand 5.95 \n17  Karnataka 11.06 \n18  Kerala 6.75 \n19  Lakshadweep 0.36 \n20  Madhya Pradesh 11.87 \n21  Maharashtra  20.05  \n22  Manipur 2.75 \n23  Meghalaya 2.16 \n24  Mizoram 1.75 \n25  Nagaland 2.28 \n26  Odisha  7.83  \n27  Puducherry 0.77 \n28  Punjab 6.06 \n29  Rajasthan 12.19 \n30  Sikkim 1.21 \n31  Tamil Nadu 13.98 \n32  Telangana 8.58 \n33  Tripura 2.53 \n34  Uttar Pradesh 27.91 \n35  Uttarakhand 2.93 \n36  West Bengal 12.78 \n37  Ladakh 0.13 \n  Total  237.23  \n  \n\nAnnexure II \nANNEXURE REFER TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUE STION NO. *167 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023  \n \nState-wise fund utilised under CSCM component and f und released and utilized \nunder CSSM component of PMKVY 3.0 during the Financ ial Year 2021-22 and \n2022-23 as on 28.02.2023 \n(Rs. in crore) \nS.No \n. \nState  Fund released \nunder CSCM-\nPMKVY 3.0 \nCSSM - PMKVY 3.0  \n2021 -22  2022 -23  \n2021 -22  2022 -\n23 \nFunds \nRelease \nd \nFunds \nUtilised  \nFunds \nRelease \nd \nFunds \nUtilised  \n1 Andaman and \nNicobar Islands \n- 0.07 - - - - \n2 Andhra Pradesh 5.52 2.75 - - - - \n3 Arunachal \nPradesh \n2.21 1.32 - 0.83 - - \n4 Assam 8.99 9.17 - - - - \n5 Bihar 13.53 14.70 - - - - \n6 Chandigarh 0.24 0.20 0.17 - 0.17 - \n7 Chhattisgarh 1.72 2.23 - - - - \n8 Dadra and Nagar \nHaveli \n- 0.01 - - - - \n9 Daman and Diu - - - - - - \n10 Delhi 3.42 2.68 1.62 0.63 - - \n11 Goa 0.03 0.05 - 0.03 - 0.03 \n12 Gujarat 5.96 3.13 - - 1.91 - \n13 Haryana 3.95 4."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |168", "t": "Development of Tourist Places in Border Areas of Rajasthan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*168 \nANSWERED ON 13.03.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST PLACES IN BORDER AREAS OF RAJASTHAN \n \n†*168. SHRI NIHAL CHAND: \n      Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any new tourist places have been identified by the \nGovernment, particularly in the border areas of Rajasthan;  \n(b)  whether the development of the said tourist places is being \nconsidered and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the steps taken by the G overnment for the preservation of \nhistorical monuments in the country including Rajasthan during the \nlast two years; and  \n(d)  whether provision for any separate budget has been made by the \nGovernment for the protection of said historical heritages? \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM   (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n******** \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO †*168 ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST PLACES IN BORDER AREAS OF \nRAJASTHAN \n \n(a) & (b): Identification and development of tourist places is  primarily \nthe responsibility of the concerned State Government/ Union Territory \n(UT) Administration however the Ministry of Tourism under its ‘Swades h \nDarshan’, ‘Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation \nDrive (PRASHAD)’ and  ‘Assistance to Cen tral Agencies’ schemes \nprovides financial assistance to the State  Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations/Central Agen cies for developmen t of tourism  \ninfrastructure. The details of the projects sanctioned un der the above \nmentioned schemes in the State  of Rajasthan including ‘Development of \nborder tourism at Border Security Forc e Check Post, Tanot Complex’ is \nat Annexure-I. In additi on, the Ministry of Tourism has now revamped \nSwadesh Darshan scheme as  Swadesh Darshan  2.0 (SD2.0) with the \nobjective to develop sustainable and responsible tourism destinations \nand has identified ‘Jodhpur’ and  ‘Bundi (Keshoraipatan)’ as destinations \nin Rajasthan for development. \n \n(c):  Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) has 3696 Centrally Protected \nMonuments in various States of the country. ASI is taking up \nconservation work of monuments as and when necessary, as per \navailability of resources.  Upkeep of the monuments is a c ontinuous \nprocess. The detail of expenditure incurred on conservation and \ndevelopment of centrally protected monuments in the country including \nRajasthan is given at Annexure-II. \n \n(d):   The protection of historical heritages is within the ambit of Ancient \nMonuments an d Archaeological Sites and  Remains (AMSAR) Act 1958 \n(Amendment and Validation Act 2010) for which no specific budget head \nis earmarked. However, there is a specific budget head ‘Professional \nServices’ for expenditure on protection (to include physical security) of \nearmarked ASI Centrally Protected Monuments (CPMs).   \n*********** \n\nANNEXURE-I  \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO . †*168 ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST PLACES IN BORDER ARE AS OF \nRAJASTHAN \n                                                                                      (Rs. in Crore) \nA Details of project sanctioned in the State of Rajasthan under \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme of Ministry of Tourism.  \nS. \nno. \nCircuit / \nyear of  \nsanction \nProject name Sanctioned  \ncost  \nAmount  \nreleased \n1 \nDesert  \nCircuit/ \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Sambhar Lake \nTown and Other Destinations 50.01 50.01 \n2 \nKrishna  \nCircuit/ \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Govind Dev ji \ntemple (Jaipur), Khatu Shyam  Ji \n(Sikar) and Nathdwara (Rajsamand) \n75.80 72.01 \n3 \nSpiritual \nCircuit/ \n \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Spiritual Circuit – \n'Development of Churu (Salasar \nBalaji)-Jaipur (Shri Samodke Balaji, \nGhatke Balaji, Bandheke Balaji) - \nViratnagar (Bijak, Jainnasiya, \nAmbika Temple)- Bharatpur (Kaman \nRegion)- Dholpur (Muchkund) - \nMehndipur Balaji - Chittorgarh \n(Sanwaliyaji) \n87.05 72.23 \n4 \nHeritage \n Circuit/ \n \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of Heritage Circuit \nDevelopment of Rajsamand \n(Kumbhalgarh Fort) - Jaipur (Facade \nIllumination in Jaipur and \nNahargarh Fo rt) -Jhalawar (Gagron \nFort) - Chittorgarh (Chittorgarh Fort) \n– Jaisalmer (Jaisalmer Fort) - \nHanumangarh (Gogamedi) - Udaipur \n(Pratap Gaurav Kendra) - Dholpur \n(Bagh-I-Nilofor and Purani Chawani)  \n- Nagaur (Meera Bai Smarak, Merta) \n- Tonk (Sunehri Kothi) \n70.61 60.91 \n\nB Details of CFA sanctioned in Rajasthan under Assistance to Central \nAgencies for Tourism Infrastructure Development. \n \n2022-23 Development of border tourism at \nBorder Security Force Check Post, \nTanot complex.  \n17.67 1.60 \nC Details of project sanctioned in Rajasthan under PRASHAD Scheme.  \n 2015-16 \n \nIntegrated Development of \nPushkar/Ajmer 32.64 26.11 \n******* \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE -II  \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRE"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |169", "t": "Emission due to Household Cooking", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 169 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SMART CITIES \n \nNO. 169.  SHRI HAJI FAZLUR REHMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any actio n plan for \ndevelopment of smart cities and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the funds allocated to smart cities during the  last three years, State-\nwise; \n \n(c)  the funds allocated for the development of sma rt cities in Uttar \nPradesh, especially Saharanpur Smart City during th e last three years; \nand \n \n(d)  the details of developmental works and the war ds for which the funds \nallocated to Saharanpur Smart City have been spent/utilised? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  to (d) : Government of India launched the Smart Cit ies Mission (SCM) \non 25 June 2015.  100 Smart Cities have been select ed through 4 rounds of \ncompetition from January 2016 to June 2018. As per SCM Guidelines, \nCentral Government will provide financial support t o the extent of \n\u0001 48,000 \ncrore over five years to the 100 Smart Cities i.e.,  on an average \u0001 100 crore \nper city per year.  An equal amount on a matching b asis will be contributed \nby the State Government/Urban Local Body (ULB).   \n…2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \n \nAs on 27 January 2023, Government of India has rele ased \u0001 36,447 crore \nunder SCM of which \u0001 32,095 crore (88%) has been utilized. The Details of \noverall funds released including for Smart Cities o f Uttar Pradesh including \nSaharanpur, are given in Annexure-I.  In the 100 Sm art Cities, work orders \nhave been issued in 7,804 projects worth     \u0001 1,81,322 crore of which 5,246 \nprojects worth \u0001 98,796 crore have been completed.  Saharanpur Smar t City \nhas taken up 70 projects worth \u0001 \u0001 839 crore out of which 18 projects worth  \n103 crore have been completed.  These projects, int er alia, relate to setting \nup of Integrated Command & Control Centre (ICCC), d evelopment of Smart \nRoads, construction of Multi-Level Car Parking & Co nvention Hall, Smart \nClassrooms, Retrofitting of Primary Schools, Instal lation of Solar High-mast \nLights and Solar Street-Lighting etc. 22 Square Kil ometer area (comprising \n21 Wards) has been chosen for Area Based Developmen t (ABD) in \nSaharanpur. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha  Unstar