BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2023 \n  \nNATUROPATHY TREATMENT  \n \n458. SHRI GUMAN SINGH DAMOR: \n���������������������AYUSH��������������������� \n��� �����������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��� ������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��� ������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��� �����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��� �������� ���� ����������� ��������� ������� ����������������������� ��������� ���������� ���\n�����������������������������������\n�\n��� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������\n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a): ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������\n(b): ��������� ��� ������ ��������� ������������ �������� ���� ���� ����������� ������� ��������\n�������� �������� ���� ��������� ��� ����� �� ������������ ��������� ���� ������ ���� ���������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������� �������������������������������������������\n\n2 \n \n�������� ���������� ������������� �������� ����������� ����� ������� �������� ������������� �������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ��������� ��� ������������� ���� ���������� ���������� ������� ��� ��������� ������ ��������\n������ �������� ��������� ������������ ���� ���� ������������ ������������ ��� ���������� ������\n�������� ����������� ������������� ��� ���� �������� ���������� ������ ������ ���� ���������� ����������\n����������� ��� ����� ��� ���� ���� ���������� ��������� ��� ������ ������ ������� ������� ������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������\n(c) and (d)�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������ ���� ��������� ��� ������������� ������� ������� ��� �� ������ ��������� ������������\n��������������� ��� ���������������������� ������������������ ����������������������� ����������� �����\n��������������������������������������\n(e) and (f) : ��� ��������� ������ ��� ��� ����� ��������� ������� ����������������������� ���������\n������������������������������������������� \n�\n������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |459", "t": "Data Breaches in Ed-Tech Apps", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.459 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF AYUSH IN RAJASTHAN \n \n459. SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \n   \n Will the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the  budget allocation made under various heads like Ayurveda education, Ayurveda \nmedicines, AYUSH doctors, AYUSH publicity department etc. in Rajasthan; and  \n \n(b) the facilities, resources, medicines provided/ likely to be provided by the Government for the \npromotion of AYUSH in view of affordability of this system in rural areas of Rajasthan? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTEROF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Ayush  is implementing the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the National \nAYUSH Mission (NAM) through State/UT Governments and supporting their efforts for the \ndevelopment and promotion of AYUSH systems of medicine  in the country including rural areas \nof Rajasthan in view of affordability of these systems by providing financial assistance to them \nunder different activities as per the provision of NAM guidelines against their submitted State \nAnnual Action Plans (SAAPs).   The NAM inter-alia envisages better access to AYUSH services \nthrough increase in number of AYUSH Hospitals and Dispensaries, operationalization of \nAYUSH Health and Wellness Centres by upgrading existing AYUSH dispensaries and Sub \nHealth Centres to provide comprehensive primary health care at gra ss root level  to general \npublic with focus on AYUSH system of medicine, mainstreaming of AYUSH through co -\nlocation of AYUSH facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Community Health Centres \n(CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs), ensure availability of AYUS H drugs and trained \nmanpower and AYUSH Public Health programmes. It also aims at improvement in quality of \nAYUSH Education through enhancement in the number of upgraded educational Institutions.  \nUnder NAM, as per the proposals received from State Government of Rajasthan through SAAPs, \nMinistry of Ayush has released Rs. 230.51 Crore from 2014-15 to 2021-22 for different activities \nas per the provision of NAM guidelines.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |46", "t": "Digitalisation of Ancient Museums", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 46 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBURARY 02, 2023 \n \nACCIDENTS DUE TO NEGLECTED DRAINS \n \nNO. 46.  SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that there have been unnat ural deaths/grave injuries of \nindividuals due to fall in open, neglected drains i n States/UTs, including \nthose under the land owning agency Delhi Developmen t Authority (DDA) in \nDelhi; \n(b) if so, the details of such incidents, State/UT wise, especially in Delhi, Tamil \nNadu and Punjab; \n(c) the details of compensation paid to the family members or the individuals \nwho are victims of such incidents; \n(d) the details of criminal cases filed and action taken against officers \naccountable in such bodies in States/UTs and DDA, State/UT-wise; and \n(e) whether the Government is taking any measures t o minimize such accidents \ndue to open, neglected drains and if so, the detail s thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor? \n \n…2/- \n \n \n\n-2- \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n(a)&(b): The instant matter is a State subject. How ever, as per the \ninformation furnished by National Crime Records Bur eau (Statistical \nBranch), during 2019 - 2021, there have been 378 ac cidental deaths due to \nfall into manhole/sewer including Delhi, Tamil Nadu, and Punjab. Details are \nat ANNEXURE–I. Further, Delhi Development Authority  (DDA) has also \ninformed that no such incident has occurred. \n(c)& (d): The instant matter is a State subject. As  such no information/ \ndetails are maintained. \n(e): Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs has publ ished a Manual on \nStorm Water Drainage Systems, 2019 whereby safety &  social safeguard \nrequirements have been mentioned. Many State(s) and Central Government \nagencies have also taken various measures to minimi ze such accidents \ndue to open, neglected drains.  \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)& (b) OF  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 46 REGARDING “ACCIDENTS DUE TO NEGLECTED \nDRAINS”, DUE FOR ANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 02 FEBURARY, 2023. \n \nState/UT-wise Number of Accidental Deaths due to Fall into Manhole/Sewer# during \n2019-2021 \nS.No. \n State/UT 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0 0 1 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 \n3 Assam 0 1 0 \n4 Bihar 6 4 10 \n5 Chhattisgarh 0 0 1 \n6 Goa 0 0 1 \n7 Gujarat 30 23 17 \n8 Haryana 0 2 3 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 0 2 0 \n10 Jharkhand 0 0 5 \n11 Karnataka 0 0 1 \n12 Kerala 2 3 2 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 1 3 9 \n14 Maharashtra 19 16 19 \n15 Manipur 0 0 0 \n16 Meghalaya 0 0 5 \n17 Mizoram 0 0 0 \n18 Nagaland 0 0 0 \n19 Odisha 4 4 8 \n20 Punjab 5 8 7 \n21 Rajasthan 13 49 7 \n22 Sikkim 0 0 2 \n23 Tamil Nadu 1 4 3 \n24 Telangana 1 3 4 \n25 Tripura 0 0 6 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 16 14 12 \n27 Uttarakhand 2 0 0 \n28 West Bengal 0 0 0 \nTOTAL STATE(S) 100 136 123 \n29 A & N Islands 0 0 0 \n30 Chandigarh 0 0 0 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman&Diu @ + 0 0 0 \n32 Delhi UT  2 6 3 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir @ * 0 0 8 \n34 Ladakh @  0 0 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 0 \n\n36 Puducherry 0 0 0 \nTOTAL UT(S) 2 6 11 \nTOTAL (ALL INDIA) 102 142 134 \nSource: Accidental Deaths & suicide in India, National Crime Records Bureau  \n(Statistical Branch) \n‘+’ Combined data of erstwhile Dadra & Nagar Haveli And Daman & Diu (UT) \nduring 2019 \n‘*’ Data of erstwhile JAMMU & KASHMIR State Including LADAKH during \n2019 \n‘@’ Data of newly created Union territory \n# Word \"Sewer\" included in year 2020 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |460", "t": "Development of ST in Kerala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 460 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 3RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nQUALITY EDUCATION IN MEDICINE \n \n460. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:  \nSHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA: \nSHRI N. REDDEPPA:  \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \nSHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH:  \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of new medical colleges sanctioned by the Government in the Andhra Pradesh \nsince 2021;  \n(b) whether the Government is aware about the drain of wealth and knowledge taking place due \nto students pursuing medical degree abroad on account of non -availability of the institutions \nfor providing good quality of higher education in the medical sector;  \n(c) if so, the details of the schemes or programmes introduced by the Government to tackle such \nissue;  \n(d) whether any scheme or programme has been formulated to prov ide good quality and \naffordable higher education in medicine; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY \nWELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Health & Family Welfare administers a Centrally Sponsored Scheme \n(CSS) for ‘Establishment of new Medical Colleges attached with existing district/referral \nhospitals’ with preference to underserved areas and aspirational districts, where the re is no \nexisting Government or private medical college. Under the Scheme, the envisaged 157 new \nmedical colleges have been approved in three phases  including three medical colleges in Andhra \n\nPradesh at Piduguralla, Paderu and Machilipatnam  in the year 202 0. Further, financial support \nhas also been provided to the State of Andhra Pradesh for increase of 150 MBBS seats in 03 \nMedical Colleges and 1040 PG seats in 11 Medical Colleges under CSSs for upgradation  of \nexisting State Government/ Central Government Medical Colleges for increasing MBBS (UG) \nseats and PG seats. \nIn addition, support has also been provided to the State of Andhra Pradesh under the \nPradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) for upgrada tion of 0 3 Government \nMedical Colleges by setting up of Super Specialty blocks.  Further, an All India Institute of \nMedical Sciences has also been established at Mangalagiri.  \nThere is an increase of 6 9% in Medical Colleges from 387 before 2014 to 654 as of now. \nFurther, there is an increase of 94% in MBBS seats from 51,348 before 2014 to 99,763 as of now \nand increase of 107 % in PG seats from 31,185 before 2014 to 64, 559 as of now. Some of the \nmeasures/steps taken by the Government  to augment the medical edu cation facilities and to \nimprove the medical standards in the country include: - \ni. Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS)  for establishment of new medical college by \nupgrading district/ referral hospital under which 157 new medical colleges have been \napproved, out of which 94 are already functional. \nii. CSS for strengthening/ upgradation of existing State Government/  Central Government \nMedical Colleges to increase the number of MBBS (UG) and PG seats. \niii. Under “Upgradation of Government Medical Colleges by construction of Super Specialty \nBlocks” of Pradhan Mantri Swasthya Suraksha Yojana (PMSSY) scheme, a  total of 75 \nprojects have been approved, of which 60 projects are complete.     \niv. Under the Central Sector Scheme for sett ing up of new AIIMS, 22 AIIMS have been \napproved. Undergraduate courses have started in 19 of these. \nv. Relaxation in the norms for setting up of Medical College in terms of requirement for \nfaculty, staff, bed strength and other infrastructure. \nvi. DNB qualification has been recognized for appointment as teaching faculty to take care \nof shortage of faculty. \nvii. Enhancement of age limit for appointment/ extension/ re -employment against posts of \nteachers/dean/principal/ director in medical colleges upto 70 years. \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |461", "t": "Steps to Regulate Artificial Intelligence", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO:- 461 \nANSWERED ON-06/02/2023 \n \nPedagogical Training for Teachers \n \n461.  SHRI SHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has observed that the country’s dysfunctional teacher education system \nleaves our teachers ill -prepared as they are neither adequately equipped with the pedagogical \ncapacities nor they learnt how to effectively handle children; \n(b) if so, t he remedial steps proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is planning to use state of the art AI based softwares to educate \nteachers about pedagogical methods and to benefit the students and the details thereof; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n(a) & (b) The National Education Policy (NEP), 2020, empowers teachers and places them at the \nheart of the learning process through quality teacher education, recruitment, continuous \nprofessional development and positive working environment while ensuring accountability.  As per \nNEP, 2020, National Council for Teacher Education (NCTE) has introduced four year In tegrated \nTeacher Education Programme (ITEP), on pilot basis, to equip teachers with enhanced curriculum \nand pedagogical knowledge. Similarly, National Professional Standard for Teachers (NPST) for \nenhancing capabilities of teachers and National Mentoring M ission (NMM) for individual \nmentoring of school teachers by senior and retired teachers are at pilot study stage.  \n \n(c) & (d) The Department of School Education and Literacy has launched an integrated Teacher \nTraining Programme called NISHTHA – National Initiative for School Heads and Teachers Holistic \nAdvancement, on the DIKSHA platform, to improve learning outcomes at the elementary level. The \nNISHTHA programme has been further expanded to NISHTHA 2.0 for secondary/senior secondary \nlevel teachers and NISH THA 3.0 – Foundational Literacy and Numeracy (FLN) for teachers and \nschool heads from pre-primary to class V. \n \n\n \n \nThe National Education Policy, 2020 has recommended introduction of contemporary subjects \nincluding Artificial Intelligence (AI) at relevant stages as a part of curricular and pedagogical \ninitiatives. T he Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has introduced Artificial \nIntelligence as a subject in Class IX from the session 2019 -2020 and in Class XI from the session \n2020-2021 in their affiliated schools. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |462", "t": "Make in India Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nBEEDI WORKERS \n \n462.  SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY:  \nSHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY:  \nSHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)whether a group of researchers has urged the Government to declare \nbeedi rolling a hazardous process and recommended alternative \nsources of livelihood for them, if so, the details thereof and the action \ntaken thereon; and  \n(b)whether the studies suggest around 87 percent of workers reported \nsymptoms such as pain in the back, shoulder, neck, knee, joints, \nspondylitis, tiredness and body -ache and most of the workers had \nmusculoskeletal, gastr ointestinal, skin, cardiovascular, oral and eye \ndiseases and the women workers had more miscarriages and \nabortions, lower fertility rate, and higher uterine problems and also \nnewborns of mothers involved in the work were at 1.9 times higher risk \nfor low bi rth weight and 1.7 times at higher risk for smaller for \ngestational age, if so, the details thereof and corrective steps being \ntaken to protect such beedi rolling workers? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): Few studies have been conducted by researchers on the \nhealth problems faced by Beedi Workers. T he Ministry of Labour & \nEmployment (MoL&E) in collaboration with Ministry of Skill Development \nand Entrepreneurship has been providing Skill Development tra ining to \nthe Beedi Workers and their dependents to engage them in alternative \njobs. The MoL&E is also providing the Health care facility to Beedi \nworkers and their family members with a network of 276 Dispensaries \nand 10 Hospitals located across the Countr y and also reimbursement of \nexpenditure for specialized treatment under the Government recognized \nHospitals in case of critical diseases i.e. Cancer, Tuberculosis, Heart \nDiseases, Kidney Transplantation, Hernia, Appendectomy, Ulcer \nGynecological diseases and Prostrate diseases to the beneficiaries. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |463", "t": "New Railway Lines in Assam", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.04.2023 \n \nNEW RAILWAY LINES IN ASSAM \n \n*463.  SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH:  \n \n  Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of surveys conducted for laying of new railway lines \nin the State of Assam during the last three years, district-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Railways has set a target to link all the States of \nthe North Eastern Region (NER) with broad gauge railway lines; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the deadline fixed in this \nregard; \n \n(d) the number of States for which approval has been accorded to \nprovide such linkages and the number of States in which works \nhave been started so far; and \n \n(e) the amount of funds likely to be incurred for the development \nof the proposed rail network in the NER along with its present \nstatus? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*****  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 463 BY SHRI KRIPANATH MALLA H TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 05.04.2023 REGARDING NEW \nRAILWAY LINES IN ASSAM \n \n \n(a) to (e): Survey for construction of railway line is taken up Zonal \nRailway-wise and not State -wise or District -wise as Indian Railways’ \nprojects may span across State/District boundaries. Total 11 new line \nsurveys of length 1251 km falling fully/partly in the State  of Assam \nhave been completed during the Financial Years 2019-20, 2020 -21, \n2021-22 and 2022- 23 of which details are appended. \n \nAll North Eastern States except Sikkim i.e. Arunachal Pradesh, \nAssam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland and Tripura are \nconnected with Broad Gauge network of Indian Railways. For \nconnectivity of Sikkim, Railway has taken up Sivok -Rangpo new line \n(44.39 km) project. Cost of the project is ₹ 8043 crore. Work on the \nproject has been taken up. So far, an expenditure of ₹ 4868 crore has \nbeen incurred. Budget outlay of ₹ 2500 crore has been provided for \nthe project for Financial Year 2022-23. \n \nRailway projects a re sanctioned Zonal Railway -wise and not \nRegion-wise/State-wise/District-wise, as Indian Railways’ projects \nmay span across State boundaries. However, as on 01.04.2022, 19 \nRailway infrastructure projects covering total length of 1,909 km at a \ncost of ₹ 77,930 crore, falling fully/ partly in North Eastern Region are \nunder different stages of planning/ approval/ execution, out of which \n409 km length has been commissioned and an expenditure of ₹ 30,312 \ncrore incurred upto March, 2022. These include- \nContd…2 \n \n \n\n-2- \n \n(i) 14 New Line Projects covering total length of 1,181 km at a \ncost of ₹ 61,520 crore, out of which 361 km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of ₹ 27,458 crore incurred \nupto March, 2022. \n \n(ii) 5 Doubling Projects covering total length of 728 km at a cost of \n₹ 16,410 crore, out of which 48 km length has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of ₹ 2,854 crore incurred \nupto March, 2022. \n \nRailway projects falling fully/ partly in North Eastern States are \ncovered in Northeast Frontier Railway Zone. Details o f Railway \nprojects including cost, expenditure and outlay are made available in \npublic domain on Indian Railways website i.e. \nwww.indianrailways.gov.in >Ministry of Railways >Railway Board \n>About Indian Railwa ys >Railway Board Directorates >Finance \n(Budget) >Rail Budget/Pink Book (Year)> Railway wise Works \nMachinery and Rolling Stock Programme. \n \nCompletion of Railway project/s depends on various factors like \nland acquisition by State Government, forest clearanc e by officials of \nforest department, shifting of infringing utilities, statutory clearances \nfrom various authorities, geological and topographical conditions of \narea, law and order situation in the area of project/s site, number of \nworking months in a year  for particular project site due to climatic \nconditions etc. and all these factors affect the completion time of the \nproject/s. With above constraints, every effort is being made to \nexecute the project/s expeditiously. \nContd…3 \n \n\n \n \n-3- \n \nSince 2014, there has been substantial increase in budget \nallotment and commensurate commissioning of infrastructure \nprojects. Average annual Budget allocation for infrastructure projects \nand safety works, falling fully/ partly in the North Eastern Region , \nduring 2014 -19 has been enhanced to  ₹ 5,531 crore per year from  \n₹ 2,122 crore per year during 2009 -14, which is 161% more than \naverage annual Budget allocation during 2009 -14. Annual Budget \nallocations for these projects have  been increased to ₹ 7,542 crore in \nFinancial Year 2021 -22 (255% more than the average annual Budget \nallocation of 2009 -14) and ₹ 9,970 crore in Financial Year 2022 -23, \n(370% more than the average annual Budget allocation of 2009 -14). \nFo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |464", "t": "Connecting Madarshah Mazar with Tirath Railway", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������� �\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�������������������������� �\n������������������� �� ��������������������������������������� �\n�\n��