\u001a\u0019\u001aCF\u001a\u001a\t H\u001bCC\u001a\u0016A@ \t C\u0019\u0019\u0016@\u0017 \t \u001c\u001bH\u001cE\u0016@D \t D\u0017\u0016\u0019\u0017 \t\n\u001d5''$%\"\t\n\u000f$,'\t\t\n.\u001a\u0019\u001a\u001aF\u001a\u001c/\t\nH\u0019H\u0016\u001c\u0019 \t \u0017\u0016E\u0019 \t F\t C\u0016E\u0017 \t\n\b\u0002\b\r\u0013\t CA\u001b\u0017\u0017C\u0016\u0017\u0019 \t AHE\u0016\u0017C\t CD\u001bC\u0017@\u0016DA\t HDD\u0016\u001aD \t\n\u0001\n=====\t\n\t\n\n\n %,%3 ,!\t \n\u000f$,'\t\n\u001d$%\"',!\t\r&& &\",%3$\t \n'$!$,&$-\t\n\u0011\" ! :,\" (%\t\u001d$'\" ) 3,\"$\t \n'$3$ 4$-\t,0, %&\"\t'$!$,&$-\t \n\u001d$%\"',!\t\r&& &\",%3$\t\t\n\n('\t<'(6$3\"& \t\n.\u0001\t %\t3'('$/\t\n\n('\t(\"#$'\t \n3(1+(%$%\"& \t\n.\u0001\t %\t3'('$/\t\n\n('\t<'(6$3\"&\t\n.\u0001\t %\t3'('$/\t\n\n('\t(\"#$'\t \n3(1+(%$%\"& \t\n.\u0001\t %\t3'('$/\t\n\u001a\u0019C@F\u001a\u0019\t \u0017\u001bHAH\u0016\u0019\u0019 \t \u0017\u001aD\u0016C@ \t \u0017\u001bH\u001a\u001c\u0016HH\t D\u001cC\u0016A@ \t\n\u001a\u0019\u001a\u0019F\u001aC\t H\u001bCDH\u0016\u001cC \t \u001a\u001cH\u0016HE \t D\u001bA@E\u0016AA\t CH\u0017\u0016\u0017D \t\n\u001a\u0019\u001aCF\u001a\u001a\t H\u001bCC\u001a\u0016A@ \t C\u0019\u0019\u0016@\u0017 \t \u001c\u001bH\u001cE\u0016@D \t D\u0017\u0016\u0019\u0017 \t\n\u001d5''$%\"\t\n\u000f$,'\t\t\n.\u001a\u0019\u001a\u001aF\u001a\u001c/\t\nH\u0019H\u0016\u001c\u0019 \t \u0017\u0016E\u0019 \t F\t C\u0016E\u0017 \t\n\b\u0002\b\r\u0013\t CA\u001b\u0017\u0017C\u0016\u0017\u0019 \t AHE\u0016\u0017C\t CD\u001bC\u0017@\u0016DA\t HDD\u0016\u001aD \t\n\u0001\n=====\t\n\t"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |50", "t": "Progress of PM POSHAN", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 50\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nPARTNERSHIP OF AAI WITH CIDC\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether the Airports Authority of India (AAI) in collaboration with\nConstruction Industry Development Council (CIDC) has trained youth in the\naviation sector and if so, the details thereof along with the number of people\nwho got trained during the last three years;\n(b) if not, the reasons therefor;\n(c) the number of commercial pilot licences issued by the Directorate General\nof Civil Aviation (DGCA) to the trainees who got trained by various institutes\nin the country during the last three years; and\n(d) the number of pilot who got employment out of the said license-holders?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) and (b) Airports Authority of India (AAI) in collaboration with\nConstruction Industry Development Council (CIDC) provided job oriented\nskill training and employment opportunities through livelihood promotion in\nbackward and remote areas. The details are as under:-\n \nProgram                                                        No. of Trained\n \nEmployment Oriented                                         1999\nTraining & Skill Development\nprogramme of unemployed\nyouth SC/ST/OBC Women and\nEWS of Society for 2000 persons\nby CIDC (PAN India).\nFY 2018-19 (CIDC).\n \n \n50. SHRI SHANKAR LALWANI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nEmployment Oriented Training                          1604\nand Skill Development Programme\nfor SC/ST/OBC/ Women, Unemployed\nYouth & EWS Society for 2000\nPersons North Eastern Region\nFY 2018-19 (CIDC).\n \n(c) The total number of Commercial Pilot Licenses (CPL) issued by the\nDirectorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) during the last three years is as\nunder\n \nYear    CPL issued to     CPL issued to      Total\n           cadets from         cadets from              \n           Indian Institute    Foreign Institute\n                     \n \n2020              335                243                  578\n2021              504                358                  862\n2022              616                549                 1165\nTotal            1455              1150                 2605\n \n(d) DGCA issues licenses as per the provisions and on compliance of Aircraft\nRules 1937. Post issue of licenses, license holders can undetake employment in\nany International Civil Aviation Organisation (ICAO) contracting state.\n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |51", "t": "Tribal University", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 51 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nTribal Universities \n \n†*51. SHRIMATI GEETA KORA: \n SHRI B.B. PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of tribal students who go outside the tribal dominated areas to get education; \n \n(b) the number of Central Tribal Universities in the country along with the tribal students \nstudying therein, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to set  up Tribal Universities in the country including \nBihar, Jharkhand, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh and Telangana, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Union Government has received  proposals from State Governments in this \nregard and if so, the details thereof along with the action taken thereon; and \n \n(e) the standard prescribed to open Central Tribal University? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a ) TO ( e) OF  LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  51 FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING TRIBAL \nUNIVERSITIES ASKED BY SHRIMATI GEETA KORA, HON’BLE MP AND SHRI B.B \nPATIL, HON’BLE MP \n \n(a): As per All India Survey on Higher Education  (AISHE), 2020-21, 24.10 lakh tribal \nstudents have enrolled in the Higher education Institutions in the country. \n \n(b) to (e): There are  two Ce ntral Tribal Universities in the country namely Indira Gandhi \nNational Tribal University (IGNTU) , Amarkantak , Madhya Pradesh and Central Tribal \nUniversity of Andhra Pradesh (CTUAP) , Vizianagram, Andhra Pradesh wherein 523 tribal \nstudents are studying.  Setting up of a Central University is a continuous process and is setup as \nand when need arises. There is no prescribed standard for setting up such university.  The Union \nGovernment has not received any proposal from State Governments in this regard.  However, as \nper Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act, 2014, there is a provision for setting up of a Central \nTribal University in the State of Telangana. \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |52", "t": "Development of Sabarimala", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*52  \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SABARIMALA \n \n*52.  SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is true that Rs.80 crore out of a total of Rs.100 crore \nthat was allotted by the Union Government for the development \nof Sabarimala under Swadesh Darshan Scheme lapsed on \nDecember 31,2022; \n(b)  if so, the reasons leading to this situation along with the details \nof the master plan submitted by the State Government on \nSabarimala and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government to direct the State \nauthorities for the utilisation of the fund before the stipulated \ndeadline; \n(d)  whether the Government is aware that the projects costing \nRs.20 crore as per the master plan of Sabarimala have not been \ncompleted yet; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n\n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*52 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF SABARIMALA. \n \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned a project \n‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ in \n2016-17 for an amount of Rs.99.99 Cr. The completion date of this \nproject was December, 2022. In addition, the Ministry of Tourism also \nsanctioned a project ‘Development of Sree Padmanabha- Aranmula- \nSabrimala in 2016-17 for Rs.92.44 Cr., which also included \ndevelopment work at Sabarimala. The development work of Rs. 6.10 \nCrore (plus contingency/consultancy) sanctioned at Sabarimala site \nwas dropped from the second project and added to the first project i.e. \n‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’, thus \nelevating the sanctioned cost of this project to Rs.106.42 Cr.  \n \n ‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ \nproject was reviewed by the Ministry of Tourism in August, 2022 and \nthe Ministry of Tourism noted no progress in respect of the \ncomponents for Rs.49.10 Crore and thereafter on the request of the \nState Government, the said project was revised in October, 2022 to \nRs.54.88 Crore. Details of the project sanctioned for Rs.106.41 Crore \nand later revised to Rs. 54.88 Crore is tabulated as under:-  \n \nDestination Sanctioned \namount \n(Rs. in Crore) \nRevised sanction \namount  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSabrimala Temple – Erumeli 2.8 2.8 \nPampa 32.97 11.07 \nTrekking Path 26.56 20.23 \nSannidham 32.90 16.01 \nSabrimala (transferred from \nPadmanabha Project) \n6.12 2.16 \nContingency  3.04 1.57 \nConsultancy  2.02 1.04 \nTotal 106.41 54.88 \n \n\n \n \nTimely completion of the sanctioned project and implementation of \nmaster plan is the responsibility of the State Government. However, \nthe Ministry of Tourism regular monitors the progress of the projects \nto ensure its speedy completion. There is a recorded physical \nprogress of 75% in respect of revised ‘Development of Sabarimala – \nErumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ project.  \n \n*******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |53", "t": "Guidelines for Crematoriums", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 53 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGuidelines for Crematoriums \n \n \n*53.  DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government acknowledges that the crematoriums are also a major \ncontributor to Air Pollution; \n(b) if so, the action taken to mitigate it and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to develop any enforceable standards or guidelines \nfor crematoriums, specifically for those in urban areas; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARAS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 53 DUE FOR REPLY ON 06.02.2023 \nREGARDING ‘GUIDELINES FOR CREMATORIUMS’ RAISED BY DR. \nRAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI AND SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA, HON’BLE \nMEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \n \n \n(a) to (d) \n \n \nAs per the available Source Apportionment study reports from 2018 to 2022, no such \ncontribution in air pollution is observed from crematoriums.  \n \nThe development of guidelines on environment management for crematoria has been taken up \nby Central Pollution Control Board. This includes inventory and in-depth study in selective \ncrematorium at different cities of the country.The activities taken under in-depth study \ninclude: identification of source of emissions, monitoring of fugitive as well as source \nemission to assess the level of concentrations, comparison of various technologies in terms of \nfuel consumption, time for cremation, environmental benefits, cost analysis of various \ntechnologies and assessment of the appropriateness of pollution control devices. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |54", "t": "Development of Religious Places under PRASAD Scheme", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*54 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES UNDER PRASAD \nSCHEME \n \n†*54. SHRI NAYAB SINGH: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has covered various tourist places of \nHaryana under the PRASAD scheme; \n(b) if so, the district-wise details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is making any  special provision for \nreligious places like Mata Mansa Devi Mandir, Nada Sahib \nlocated in Panch kula and Adi Badri in Haryana through the \nsaid scheme so that the devotees visiting the said places may \nget the benefit of the said scheme as well as to develop the \nsaid places with ultra modern facilities; \n(d) whether the Government is likely to include n ew components \nin the PRASAD scheme which may also benefit the rural areas; \nand \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e) : A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n**** \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO .†*54 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \nREGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES UNDER \nPRASAD SCHEME. \n \n \n(a) to  (c): Under PRASHAD Scheme of Ministry of Tourism, \ndestinations are identified and projects are approved on the basis of \nproposal requests received from the respective State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations. Based on the \nproposals received from Sta te Government of Haryana , Nada Saheb \nGurudwara and Mata Mansha Devi Temple in Panchkula di strict \nhave been included under PRASHAD scheme. A Project viz. \n“Development of Nada Saheb Gurudwara and Mata Mansha Devi \nTemple in Panchkula District”  at the estimated  cost of Rs. 49.52 Cr. \nhas been approved under the scheme for development of pilgrimage \ntourism amenities at these destinations. \n \n(d) & (e): PRASHAD is a  scheme for development of Pilgrimage and \nHeritage tourism infrastructure at the identified destination s across \nthe country irrespective the location of identified destination , \nwhether it falls under rural, urban, tribal or in any other area and the \nbenefits of the projects are available to the local population. \n  \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |55", "t": "Bonus to Non-Gazetted Officers of Autonomous Institutions/Organisations", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n�\n��������� �\n� ������������������ �����\n����������� �������������\n�\n������������ ���������������������������������\n�������������������������� �\n�\n�\n��������������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� ������� ������ ��� ������������� ��������� ��� �����\n����������� ��������������������������� ����� �������� ��������� ������������ ����� ���� ��������\n��������� ��������� ������������ ��������� ���������� ������������ ��������� ���� ������ ��������\n�����������������������������\n���������������������������������\n���� ������������ ������� ������ ��� ���� ������ ������������ ������� ����� ������������� ��������� ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n���� ������������ ���������� �\n������������������������ ��\n����������� �����������������������������������������������\n�\n������\n� �\n\n���������� ��������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ������ ���� ��� ���� ��� ���� ������\n����� ��� ��������� ���� ��� ���� ����������� ������ ��� ����� ������� ������\n����� ���� ���������������� �������������� ������ ���������������������� ���������������������� ���� ���\n��� ����������������������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� ��������� ����������� ������������� ������������� ������������� ���� �����������\n��� ���������� ��������� ��� ����������������� ������������ ����� ���� �������� ��������� ���������\n������������ ��������� ��������������������� ��������� ���� ������ �������� ������������������ ����\n����������������������������\n�\n������\n�\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |56", "t": "Alleged Harassment by Income Tax Authorities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56 \nANSWER ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nMAINTENANCE AND REPAIR OF NHs \n56. DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the work of maintenance and repair of National Highways  \n(NHs) is carried out annually as per available budgetary outlay and \ntraffic density to keep such NHs in traffic worthy condition; \n(b) if so, the role played by the National Highways Authority of India  \n(NHAI), National Highways and Infrastructure Development Corporation \nLimited (NHIDCL) and Border Roads Organization (BRO) in maintenance \nand repair of NHs in the state of Bihar; \n(c) whether the responsibility of maintenance and repair of NHs in \ndistrict Gopalganj of Bihar rests with the agency to which the NHs are \nentrusted;  \n(d) if so, the measures taken by the said agency for maintena nce and  \nrepair the NHs in the said district; and \n(e) whether the damaged sign boards are replaced from time to time \nalong NHs in the said district and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (c)   The development and maintenance of N ational Highways \n(NHs) is a continuous process. The works on NHs are accordingly taken \nup from time to time through various agencies such as National \nHighways Authority of India (NHAI), National Highways and \nInfrastructure Development Corporation Limited (NHIDCL),  Border \nRoads Organisation (BRO) and State Government’s Public Works \nDepartments as per  available budgetary outlay, inter-se priority  and \ntraffic density, and to keep the NHs in traffic worthy conditions. \nIn state of  Bihar including district Gopalganj, National Highways \nare entrusted to NHAI and Road Construction Department, Government \nof Bihar.  \n The M aintenance & Repair of stretches of NHs, where either \nDevelopment works have co mmenced or Operation, Maintenance and \nTransfer (OMT) Concessions/ Operation and Maintenance (O&M) \nContracts have been awarded, are the responsibil ity of the concerned \nConcessionaires/Contractors till the Concession Period/Defect Liability \nPeriod (DLP). \nM&R of balance stretches of NHs  are carried out regularly as per \navailable budgetary outlay, inter -se priority and traffic density to keep \nsuch NHs in traffic worthy conditions. \n \n(d) & (e) In the Gopalganj district, NHAI has awarded the O&M works of \nBihar/UP Borders to Kotwa section from km. 360.915 to km. 440.00 of \nNH-27 to O&M contractor and the work has commenced. The work is \ntargeted for completion by 16.06.2023. The other three sections of NHs \nin Gopalganj distri ct with total length 41.26 km are under Defect \nLiability Period . As part of development and maintenance work , sign \nboards are replaced/installed along NHs in the said district from time to \ntime as per requirements and standard and specifications.  \n***** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |57", "t": "Dust Mitigation Measures", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 57 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDust Mitigation Measures \n \n \n*57.  MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken cognizance of increasing cases of non-compliance of \ndust mitigating measures as per the mandate of Commission for Air Quality Management \n(CAQM) which consequently cause waste from the activities of construction and \ndemolition works and of Thermal Power Plants to spread into the air; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the number of cases of non-compliance of dust mitigating measures from Delhi and Uttar \nPradesh as per the mandate of CAQM, which came to the knowledge of the Government \nand the action taken in this regard and the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to create an online system for monitoring of \ncompliance and to create awareness about dust mitigating measures and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(e) the State-wise details of the field visits or inspections carried out in various regions \nincluding NTPC in Bhagalpur for compliance of dust mitigating measures under CAQM \nduring the last one year and the current year; and \n(f) the extent to which poor condition of the roads is responsible for air pollution? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARAS (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 57 DUE FOR REPLY ON 06.02.2023 REGRADING ‘DUST \nMITIGATION MEASURES’ RAISED BY MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE AND SHRI \nSUNIL KUMAR PINTU, HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \n \n(a) & (b) \n \nThe Commission for Air Quality Management in NCR and Adjoining Areas (CAQM) has \nissued statutory directions and advisories for dust mitigation in the NCR, besides calling for \ncompliance of various statutes viz. C&D Waste Management Rules and related CPCB \nguidelines. CAQM has an extensive monitoring mechanism and system towards compliance \nof dust mitigation measures. Flying Squads/ Inspection Teams constituted by the CAQM have \ncarried out incognito inspections of the C&D project sites, besides inspections by the \nrespective State Pollution Control Boards / DPCC.  \n \n(c)  \n \nAs on 31.01.2023,   a total of about 11,910 field inspections have been carried out by the \nFlying Squads constituted by the CAQM across sectors like C&D projects, road construction / \nrebuilding projects, industries etc. In such inspections, 48 projectsin the C&D sector in NCT \nof Delhi and 77 projects in the NCR districts of Uttar Pradesh respectively were found to be \ngrossly violating the directions/ rules/ guidelines. These were issued directions for closure of \nwork at the sites. These projects resume only after due compliance of various statutory \nrequirements and preventive and corrective measures. \n \n(d)  \n \nUnder statutory directions of the Commission, to facilitate remote monitoring of bigger C&D \nprojects, all projects in the NCR on plot areas equal to or more than 500 sqm. are required to \nregister on the respective dedicated web portals in the States of Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, \nRajasthan and the NCT of Delhi and certify self-compliance of various stipulated measures.  \nThe project sites are also required to be video fenced to enable remote monitoring of the \ncompliances by the inspection agencies through the respective web portals.  Such portals have \nbeen developed and are operational in the NCR States and NCT of Delhi. \n \n(e) \nDuring 2021-22, a total of about 5,456 field inspections were carried out by the Flying Squads \nconstituted by the CAQM across sectors like C&D projects, road construction / rebuilding \nprojects and industries etc. During 2022-23 a total of about 6,456 field inspections have been \ncarried out upto 31.01.2023. The Commission has been constituted for the purpose of \nprotecting and improving the quality of air in the NCR and adjoining areas. \n \n(f) \nPoor condition of the roads in the NCR is an important factor contributing to air pollution. A \nComprehensive Study on Air Pollution and Green House Gases in Delhi submitted by IIT \nKanpur in 2016 has concluded that in summer, soil and road dust can contribute about 26% to \nPM10 and PM2.5. \n \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|STARRED   |58", "t": "Preservation of Cultural Heritage in Uttar Pradesh", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION  NO. †*58 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \n \nPRESERVATION OF CULTURAL HERITAGE IN UTTAR PRADESH  \n \n \n †*58.  SHRI  GHANSHYAM  SINGH  LODHI: \n \n Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(a) the measures taken by the Government during the last three years to preserve the cultural \nheritage of the country; \n(b) \n \nthe total amount spent by the Government for the preservation and development of the \nsaid cultural heritages during the said years along with the State/UT-wise details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has set up or proposes to set up new cultural centres in Uttar \nPradesh, especially in district Rampur, and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; and \n(d) whether the Government has received complaints regarding poor maintenance of cultural \nheritage sites in district Rampur and Uttar Pradesh and if so, the action taken by the \nGovernment thereon? \n\n \n \n \n---- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n                                                                                                                             \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*58 FOR 06.02.2023. \n(a) \n& \n(b) \n \n   \n \n \n \nThe Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) undertakes conservation works, as and \nwhen required, at 3696 Centrally Protected Monuments/Sites. Conservation of \nmonuments is a continuous process and all monuments/sites are in a good state of \npreservation. The details of the total funds spent on the conservation and \ndevelopment of ASI monuments/sites during the last three years are given in \nAnnexure- I. \n \n     (c) There is no such proposal for ASI to set up new cultural centres in Uttar Pradesh, \nespecially in district Rampur. ASI protects and maintains ancient \nmonuments/sites of national importance all over the country.  \n \n    (d) No complaints have been received by ASI regarding poor maintenance of \nmonuments/sites in district Rampur and Uttar Pradesh. The centrally protected \nmonuments/sites are in a good state of maintenance.    \n \n \n\nAnnexure- I  \n \nANNEXURE REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.†58 FOR \n06.02.2023. \n \nDETAILS OF EXPENDITURE INCURRED FOR CONSERVATION AND  DEVELOPMENT OF \nCENTRALLY PROTECTED MONUMENTS DURING THE LAST THREE YEARS. \n                                                                                                                         (Amount Rs. in Crore) \n***** \n   Sl. \nNo. \nName of State/UT Circle / Branch 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nExpenditure Expenditure Expenditure \n1. Uttar Pradesh Agra 13.55 12.96 19.35 \nJhansi - 1.00 4.15 \nLucknow 5.60 3.88 10.44 \nSarnath 3.20 4.38 5.50 \nMeerut - 0 2.80 \n2. Maharashtra Aurangabad 6.09 3.16 7.99 \nMumbai 9.50 11.00 12.00 \nNagpur 5.40 4.00 4.50 \n3. Karnataka Bangalore 11.10 5.27 9.75 \nHampi 10.00 9.10 8.00 \nDharwad 7.45 6.40 7.32 \n4.   Madhya Pradesh Bho