s; and  \n(d)  whether provision for any separate budget has been made by the \nGovernment for the protection of said historical heritages? \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM   (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n******** \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO †*168 ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST PLACES IN BORDER AREAS OF \nRAJASTHAN \n \n(a) & (b): Identification and development of tourist places is  primarily \nthe responsibility of the concerned State Government/ Union Territory \n(UT) Administration however the Ministry of Tourism under its ‘Swades h \nDarshan’, ‘Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation \nDrive (PRASHAD)’ and  ‘Assistance to Cen tral Agencies’ schemes \nprovides financial assistance to the State  Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations/Central Agen cies for developmen t of tourism  \ninfrastructure. The details of the projects sanctioned un der the above \nmentioned schemes in the State  of Rajasthan including ‘Development of \nborder tourism at Border Security Forc e Check Post, Tanot Complex’ is \nat Annexure-I. In additi on, the Ministry of Tourism has now revamped \nSwadesh Darshan scheme as  Swadesh Darshan  2.0 (SD2.0) with the \nobjective to develop sustainable and responsible tourism destinations \nand has identified ‘Jodhpur’ and  ‘Bundi (Keshoraipatan)’ as destinations \nin Rajasthan for development. \n \n(c):  Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) has 3696 Centrally Protected \nMonuments in various States of the country. ASI is taking up \nconservation work of monuments as and when necessary, as per \navailability of resources.  Upkeep of the monuments is a c ontinuous \nprocess. The detail of expenditure incurred on conservation and \ndevelopment of centrally protected monuments in the country including \nRajasthan is given at Annexure-II. \n \n(d):   The protection of historical heritages is within the ambit of Ancient \nMonuments an d Archaeological Sites and  Remains (AMSAR) Act 1958 \n(Amendment and Validation Act 2010) for which no specific budget head \nis earmarked. However, there is a specific budget head ‘Professional \nServices’ for expenditure on protection (to include physical security) of \nearmarked ASI Centrally Protected Monuments (CPMs).   \n*********** \n\nANNEXURE-I  \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO . †*168 ANSWERED ON 13.03.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF TOURIST PLACES IN BORDER ARE AS OF \nRAJASTHAN \n                                                                                      (Rs. in Crore) \nA Details of project sanctioned in the State of Rajasthan under \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme of Ministry of Tourism.  \nS. \nno. \nCircuit / \nyear of  \nsanction \nProject name Sanctioned  \ncost  \nAmount  \nreleased \n1 \nDesert  \nCircuit/ \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Sambhar Lake \nTown and Other Destinations 50.01 50.01 \n2 \nKrishna  \nCircuit/ \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Govind Dev ji \ntemple (Jaipur), Khatu Shyam  Ji \n(Sikar) and Nathdwara (Rajsamand) \n75.80 72.01 \n3 \nSpiritual \nCircuit/ \n \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Spiritual Circuit – \n'Development of Churu (Salasar \nBalaji)-Jaipur (Shri Samodke Balaji, \nGhatke Balaji, Bandheke Balaji) - \nViratnagar (Bijak, Jainnasiya, \nAmbika Temple)- Bharatpur (Kaman \nRegion)- Dholpur (Muchkund) - \nMehndipur Balaji - Chittorgarh \n(Sanwaliyaji) \n87.05 72.23 \n4 \nHeritage \n Circuit/ \n \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of Heritage Circuit \nDevelopment of Rajsamand \n(Kumbhalgarh Fort) - Jaipur (Facade \nIllumination in Jaipur and \nNahargarh Fo rt) -Jhalawar (Gagron \nFort) - Chittorgarh (Chittorgarh Fort) \n– Jaisalmer (Jaisalmer Fort) - \nHanumangarh (Gogamedi) - Udaipur \n(Pratap Gaurav Kendra) - Dholpur \n(Bagh-I-Nilofor and Purani Chawani)  \n- Nagaur (Meera Bai Smarak, Merta) \n- Tonk (Sunehri Kothi) \n70.61 60.91 \n\nB Details of CFA sanctioned in Rajasthan under Assistance to Central \nAgencies for Tourism Infrastructure Development. \n \n2022-23 Development of border tourism at \nBorder Security Force Check Post, \nTanot complex.  \n17.67 1.60 \nC Details of project sanctioned in Rajasthan under PRASHAD Scheme.  \n 2015-16 \n \nIntegrated Development of \nPushkar/Ajmer 32.64 26.11 \n******* \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE -II  \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRE"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |169", "t": "Development of Smart Cities", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 169 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SMART CITIES \n \nNO. 169.  SHRI HAJI FAZLUR REHMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any actio n plan for \ndevelopment of smart cities and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the funds allocated to smart cities during the  last three years, State-\nwise; \n \n(c)  the funds allocated for the development of sma rt cities in Uttar \nPradesh, especially Saharanpur Smart City during th e last three years; \nand \n \n(d)  the details of developmental works and the war ds for which the funds \nallocated to Saharanpur Smart City have been spent/utilised? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  to (d) : Government of India launched the Smart Cit ies Mission (SCM) \non 25 June 2015.  100 Smart Cities have been select ed through 4 rounds of \ncompetition from January 2016 to June 2018. As per SCM Guidelines, \nCentral Government will provide financial support t o the extent of \n\u0001 48,000 \ncrore over five years to the 100 Smart Cities i.e.,  on an average \u0001 100 crore \nper city per year.  An equal amount on a matching b asis will be contributed \nby the State Government/Urban Local Body (ULB).   \n…2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \n \nAs on 27 January 2023, Government of India has rele ased \u0001 36,447 crore \nunder SCM of which \u0001 32,095 crore (88%) has been utilized. The Details of \noverall funds released including for Smart Cities o f Uttar Pradesh including \nSaharanpur, are given in Annexure-I.  In the 100 Sm art Cities, work orders \nhave been issued in 7,804 projects worth     \u0001 1,81,322 crore of which 5,246 \nprojects worth \u0001 98,796 crore have been completed.  Saharanpur Smar t City \nhas taken up 70 projects worth \u0001 \u0001 839 crore out of which 18 projects worth  \n103 crore have been completed.  These projects, int er alia, relate to setting \nup of Integrated Command & Control Centre (ICCC), d evelopment of Smart \nRoads, construction of Multi-Level Car Parking & Co nvention Hall, Smart \nClassrooms, Retrofitting of Primary Schools, Instal lation of Solar High-mast \nLights and Solar Street-Lighting etc. 22 Square Kil ometer area (comprising \n21 Wards) has been chosen for Area Based Developmen t (ABD) in \nSaharanpur. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha  Unstarred Question No. 169 for answer on 02.02.202 3 \nregarding Development of Smart Cities asked by Shri Haji Fazlur Rehman, Hon’ble MP.  \n \nState-wise/City-wise details of funds released & expenditure incurred under SCM \n   \n(Amount in ₹ crore)  \nState/UT Name of City During \n2015-16  \nDuring \n2016-17  \nDuring \n2017-18 \nDuring \n2018-19 \nDuring \n2019-20  \nDuring \n2020-21  \nDuring \n2021-22  \nDuring \n2022-23 \nTotal Central  \nfunds \nreleased  \nUtilization of \nGOI Funds  \nAndaman & \nNicobar \nIslands \nPort Blair 0.00 194.00 2.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 49. 00 245.00 195.82 \nAndhra \nPradesh Amaravati 0.00 0.00 18.00 372.00 106.00 0.00 0.00 3 2.00 528.00 488.00 \nAndhra \nPradesh Kakinada 190.00 6.00 0.00 98.00 98.00 98.00 0.00 0. 00 490.00 485.00 \nAndhra \nPrade