TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 202 \nANSWERED ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL HIGHWAYS \n202.      SHRI KALYAN BANERJEE: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the total length and number of National  Highways (NHs) in the \ncountry; \n(b) whether the Government has postponed  the target of 2 lakh \nkilometres of NHs from 2022 to 2025; \n(c) if so, the reasons for not achieving the target by 2022; and \n(d) the remedial steps being taken by the Government in this regard?  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a)     Total length of National Highways (NHs) in the country has \nincreased from about 91,287 km in March, 2014 to about 1,44,983 km at \npresent comprising of 663 numbers (nos.) of NHs. \n(b) to (d)    The Ministry keeps on receiving proposals from various State \nGovernments / Union Territories (UTs) for declaration / upgrad ation of \nState roads, including SHs, as new NHs. The Ministry considers \ndeclaration of some State roads, including SHs, as NHs from time to \ntime based on the requirement of connectivity, inter -se priority and \navailability of funds. \n         During 2022-23, about 3,639 km length of State roads have been \nnotified as NHs so far. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |203", "t": "Construction of Dams in Himachal Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †203 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \nCONSTRUCTION OF DAMS IN HIMACHAL PRADESH \n†203.   SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA  \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has decided to construct  dams at Renuka and Kasau in Himachal Pradesh \nand Lakhwar in Uttarakhand to increase hydropower and availability of water in plain areas; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the total estimated cost of these projects; \n(d) whether any policy decision has been taken by t he concerned States in connection with sharing of \nelectricity and water; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) & (b) In 2008, Renukaji dam project, Lakhwar multipurpose  project and Kishau multipurpose \nproject were included by Government of India under the centrally sponsored National Projects scheme, \nfor providing partial financial assistance to the S tate Governments concerned for implementation of th e \nproject. Thereafter, inter-State agreements have be en formalized in 2018-19 between the upper Yamuna \nbasin States in respect of Renukaji and Lakhwar pro ject. Accordingly, Government of India has, in \nDecember, 2021, approved financial assistance for t he two projects under Pradhan Mantri Krishi \nSinchayee Yojana. Implementation of the two project s has been taken up by Himachal Pradesh and \nUttarakhand, respectively. \nHowever, the detailed project report, as well as in ter-State agreement in respect of Kishau \nmultipurpose project has not been firmed up, due to  which implementation of the project has not been \ntaken up.  \n(c)  Estimated cost of Renukaji dam project and  Lakhwa r multipurpose project is Rs. 6,946.99 crore and \nRs 5,747.17 crore, respectively at October, 2018 pr ice level. The cost for Kishau multipurpose project  \nhas been estimated to be Rs. 11,550 crore at March, 2018 price level. However, the updated cost shall be \navailable after updation of the detailed project report. \n(d) & (e)  Sharing of upper Yamuna water is governed by memor andum of understanding among co-\nbasin States dated 12 \nth  May 1994. Further, as per the agreement between th e upper Yamuna States, \npower benefits of Renukaji dam project and Lakhwar multipurpose project are to be utilized solely by \nHimachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand, respectively. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |204", "t": "Gram Ujala Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.204 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \n \nGRAM UJALA SCHEME \n \n†204. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has implemented the Gram Ujala Scheme; \n \n(b)  whether the districts of Rajasthan have been  identified to be covered under \nthe first phase of the said scheme; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  the details of the steps taken by the  Government to create awareness among \nthe beneficiaries of the said scheme; \n \n(e)  the time by which the said scheme is likely  to be started by the Government; \nand \n \n(f)  the details of the number of villages and  Dhanis of district Tonk and district \nSawai Madhopur of Rajasthan included therein? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c) : Gram Ujala Scheme has been implemented by Convergence Energy \nServices Limited (CESL). Under Gram UJALA Scheme,  1 crore LED bulbs have been \ndistributed in rural areas of Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, and \nTelangana as a Pilot Project.  The distribution under the pilot of Gram Ujala has been \ncompleted and currently, no further distribution activity is underway. \n \n(d) :   Various awareness activities are being done by CESL/ authorised \nagencies for the usage of LED bulbs amongs t beneficiaries such as - local/national \nmedia outreach, information dissemination through radio/TV and other modes of \nmedia like banners, posters, leaflets etc.  \n \n (e) & (f) : The scheme has been implemented in rural areas of Bihar, Uttar \nPradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, and Telangana only as a pilot project. Further, \nimplementation would depend upon assessment of pilot project. \n \n*********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |205", "t": "Domestic Flights under UDAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 205\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nDOMESTIC FLIGHTS UNDER UDAN SCHEME\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the details of the airports selected/proposed to be selected for operating\ndomestic flights under the UDAN scheme in West Bengal, Jammu & Kashmir,\nBhagalpur in Bihar, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra and Jharkhand;\n(b) the details of the airports which are ready to be developed under UDAN\nscheme in West Bengal, Jammu & Kashmir especially Bhagalpur in Bihar,\nTamil Nadu, Maharashtra and Jharkhand;\n(c) whether there is demand to connect more airports from West Bengal,\nJammu & Kashmir, Bihar, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra and Jharkhand;\n(d) if so, the details thereof including destinations already connected and those\nwhich are in pipeline;\n(e) whether any survey was conducted with regard to the airstrips under the\nsaid scheme in West Bengal, Jammu & Kashmir, Bihar and Jharkhand; and\n(f) if so, the details thereof including Bhagalpur?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (f): The details of the airports identified for operating domestic flights\nunder the UDAN scheme in West Bengal, Jammu & Kashmir, Bhagalpur in\nBihar, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra and Jharkhand are given below:\n \nWest Bengal : Cooch Behar,  Burnpur, Durgapur, Hashimara, Kalaikunda &\n205. SHRI AJAY KUMAR MANDAL\nDR. D.N.V. SENTHILKUMAR S.\n MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE\nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA\nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU\nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE\nSHRIMATI GEETA KORA\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nMalda. Durgapur airport has been operationalized under the UDAN scheme.\n \nJammu &Kashmir : Nil\n \nBhagalpur in Bihar is available in the list of unserved airports in the UDAN\ndocument. None of the airline bidders has submitted any proposal to operate\nUDAN flights from Bhagalpur airstrip till the completion of four rounds of\nbidding under UDAN.\n \nTamil Nadu: Vellore, Neyveli, Salem, Ramnad & Tanjore. Salem airport has\nbeen operationalized under the UDAN scheme.\n \nMaharashtra : Jalgaon, Nanded, Kolhapur, Solapur, Amravati, Nasik,\nRatnagiri, Gondia & Sindhudurg. Jalgaon, Nanded, Kolhapur, Nasik, Gondia\n& Sindhudurg airports have been operationalized under the UDAN scheme.\n \nJharkhand: Bokaro, Dumka, Daltonganj, Deoghar, Hazaribagh &\nJamshedpur. Deoghar airport has been operationalized under the UDAN\nscheme. \n \nGovernment has been receiving requests for connecting cities by air across the\ncountry from time to time. Ministry of Civil Aviation continues to interact\nwith the airlines to promote air connectivity in the country.\n \nUDAN is a market driven and ongoing scheme where bidding rounds are\nconducted periodically for covering more destinations/stations and routes.\nInterested airlines based on their assessment of demand on particular routes,\nsubmit their proposals at the time of bidding under UDAN. Selection of\nairlines is done through a transparent bidding process. An airport which is\nincluded in the awarded routes of UDAN and requires upgradation /\ndevelopment for commencement of UDAN operations, is developed under\n\"Revival of unserved and underserved airports\" scheme. \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |206", "t": "Wind Energy Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 206 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \n \nWIND ENERGY PLANTS \n \n206. SHRI KHAGEN MURMU \n        SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA \n        SHRI MAHABALI SINGH \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of wind energy plants functioning in the country as on date and the quantum \nof power being generated therefrom, State/UT-wise; \n(b) the amount of assistance provided by the Government to each State for setting up of these \nplants during the last three years and the current year; \n(c) whether the Government has received any proposals from the State Governments for \nsetting up of such plants in their States; \n(d) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; and \n(e) the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) As on 31 st December 2022, wind energy projects of 41,929 MW capacity have been \ncommissioned in the country. The energy generated from wind energy projects is 59.53 billion \nunits during April -December, 2022. The State wise details of commissioned wind energy \nprojects and the energy generated from these projects in last three financial years including \ncurrent year (upto 31st December, 2022) are given at Annexure I. \n(b) to (e) The wind resource is highly site specific and its commercially exploitable potential \nis available majorly in eight states i.e. Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Madhya Pradesh, \nMaharashtra, Rajasthan, Karnataka and Telangana. The wind power projects are set up mostly \nby private developers based on technoeconomic viability of the project. At present, there is no \nspecific scheme/ programme for providing financial support to State Governments for setting \nup of such plants in their States. \n***** \n  \n\n \nAnnexure I referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo. 206 for 02.02.2023 \nWind Power installed capacity & Energy Generation  \nSTATE \nCumulative \nwind power \ncapacity \n(MW) (as on \n31.12.2022) \nEnergy Generation (in MUs) during last three years \nincluding the current financial year (up to \n31.12.2022) \nFY 2019-20 FY 2020-\n21 \nFY 2021-22 April- \nDecember, \n2022 \n(Million \nUnits) \nAndhra Pradesh 4096.65 7626.63 6557.75 7134.58 6283.17 \nGujarat 9866.62 13748.53 13058.52 17854.77 14942.86 \nKarnataka 5269.65 10148.66 9610.91 9491.62 8012.45 \nKerala 62.5 119.76 130.42 136.41 157.68 \nMadhya Pradesh 2844.29 4425.99 3913.45 4346.66 3653.87 \nMaharashtra 5012.83 7984.27 6384.74 7085.98 6286.56 \nRajasthan 4681.82 6172.70 5708.27 6493.19 4995.24 \nTamil Nadu 9963.015 14126.93 14564.99 15821.18 14976.98 \nTelangana 128.1 238.25 220.91 275.69 223.54 \nOthers 4.3 54.66 0 0 0 \nTotal 41929.775 64646.38 60149.95 68640.07 59532.36 \n \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |207", "t": "Disaster-Resilient Infrastructure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.207 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nDISASTER-RESILIENT INFRASTRUCTURE \n \nNO. 207.  SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \n     MS. DIYA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of Housing and Urban Affairs be pleased to state: \n \na)  Whether the Government concurs with the view that w e must build \ndisaster-resilient critical infrastructure such as hospitals, schools, \ntransport and communication systems; \nb)  If so, the initiatives that are proposed to be take n by the Government \nkeeping in mind that the resilience of our networks  and connectivity \ncan help to bring a city back to its pre-disaster levels; \nc)  Whether the Government proposes on introducing solu tions such as \nbuilding flood-resilient housing systems and installing flood barriers; \nd)  Whether the Government has any data on these disast ers for the last \nfive years and if so, the details thereof, State-wi se including \nRajasthan; and \ne)  Whether it is a fact that there has been an increas ing trend of urban \nfloods in the country wherein States such as Rajast han have been \naffected? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \n MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  to (c):  Yes Sir. Land and Colonisation including d isaster \nmanagement are State subjects. However, Government is taking \nvarious steps to update hazard risk information and  make it easily \navailable to all infrastructure sectors so that all  projects are \ndisaster resilient, including the following: \ni.  Ministry of Home Affairs has established Coalition for Disaster \nResilient Infrastructure (CDRI) to promote resilien ce \ninfrastructure systems and initiated collaborations  in key \ninfrastructure sectors like power and telecommunication. \n…2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \nii.  Ministry of Road Transport and Highways constructs bridges \nunder National Highways as per Indian Road Congress  (IRC) \nstandards which incorporate provision of disaster r esilience to \nflood and earthquakes.  \nFurther, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoH UA) has \nundertaken the following initiatives: \ni.  Urban and Regional Development Plans Formulation an d \nImplementation (URDPFI) Guidelines were issued in 2 015 \naddressing ‘Climate Change Mitigation’.  \nii.  Model Building Bye Laws (MBBL), 2016 were circulate d, which \ninclude standards on construction of disaster resil ient \nbuildings for guidance of the State and UT Governments.  \niii.  National Mission of Sustainable Habitat 2021-30 was  launched \naddressing “Climate Change: Hazards and Vulnerabilities\" \niv.  The Climate Smart cities Assessment Framework (CSCA F) 2.0 \nwas launched under the Smart Cities Mission in Sept ember \n2021. Some of the smart cities have implemented pro jects \nrelated to mitigation of floods such as river bank development, \nconstruction of flood protection retaining wall etc.   \nv.  The Climate Centre for Cities (C-cube) at National Institute of \nUrban Affairs (NlUA) has also been set up on 25th J une 2O21, \naiming to create synergy in Indian cities by variou s \nstakeholders, to consolidate institutionalize & mai nstream the \nlearning from experiences in various Indian cities on climate \nfor a better future & healthy environment.  \n \n(d) & (e): There have been instances of flooding in  various States/UTs, \nincluding Rajasthan. Housing being state subject, f ormulation and \nimplementation of building bye-laws is responsibili ty of states/UTs. \nHowever, for guidance of states/UTs, Ministry of Ho using and urban \nAffairs has issued MBBL, 2016, which has a Chapter on Structural \nsafety. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |208", "t": "Non-payment of Scholarships", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS  \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 208 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15TH MARCH, 2023 \n \nBSNL 5G SERVICES \n*208.  DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether spectrum has been allocated to Bharat Sanchar Nigam Ltd. (BSNL) for rolling out \nits 5G services by August 2023, if so, the details thereof along with the details of the States where \n5G services are proposed by BSNL; \n(b)  the details of the States in which BSNL has provided 4G services so far; \n(c)  the time by which 4G services are likely to be provided by the BSNL in the country \nparticularly in Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar Islands and its remote areas; \n(d)  the details of the tele-density of Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar Islands as compared \nto National tele-density; \n(e)  whether the Government has identified uncovered villages in Maha rashtra, if so, the details \nthereof, district-wise; \n(f)  whether the Government has prepared any action plan to cover various such villages; and \n(g)  if so, the details thereof along with the details of funds allocated, released and utilized for \nexpanding telecom facilities in Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar Islands during each of the last \nthree years and the current year? \n \n ANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND ELECTRONICS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (g)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2 \n \nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF LOK SAB HA IN RESPECT OF PARTS \n(a) TO (g) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *208/8TH FOR 15TH MARCH, \n2023 REGARDING BSNL 5G SERVICES. \n(a) The spectrum in 600MHz, 3300 MHz and 26 GHz bands have been kept reserved for BSNL \nfor providing 5G services. The 5G services by BSNL would be provided after roll out of 4G services \nall over India. \n(b) The details of States where BSNL is providing 4G services on limited scale is encl osed as \nAnnexure-I. \n(c) In line with Atmanirbhar initiative of the Government, BSNL was directed to deploy Indian \ncore for 4G on the recommendations of Empowered Technology Group (ETG). Accordingly, BSNL \nfloated tender on 22-10-2022 for 1 lakh 4G sites. The bid was opened on 23.11.2022. The evaluation \nof the bid has been concluded and is under submission for approval of Group of Ministers (GoM). \n4G services will be started all over India including Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar Islands, \nwithin 18-24 months after placing the Purchase Order. \n(d) The details of tele-density of Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar Islands as compared to \nNational tele-density, is as below: \nTele-density of India 84.86% \nTele-density of Maharashtra 101.55% \nTele-density of Andaman & Nicobar Islands 137.87% \n \n(e) Details of District-wise uncovered villages in Maharashtra is attached as Annexure-II. \n(f) & (g) Department of Telecommunications has taken up various projects with support from \nUniversal Service Obligation Fund (USOF) for provisioning of 4G mobile services in uncovered \nvillages across the country including Maharashtra.  Further, Union Cabinet on 27.07.2022, approved \nan USOF funded project for saturation of 4G mobile services in uncovered villages across the country \n(including Maharashtra). The project will provide 4G mobile services in 24,680 uncovered villages in \nremote and difficult areas across country including the uncovered villages of Maharashtra.  \nThe year-wise details of fund disbursed during 2019 -20 to 2022-23 (up to December, 2022) under \nvarious USOF Projects for providing Telecom facilities in Maharashtra and Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands, are as below:   \n                                                                                                       (Rupees in Crore) \n \nState/ Union \nTerritory  \nF.Y. \n2019-20 \nF.Y. 2020-\n21 \nF.Y. 2021-\n22 \nF.Y. 2022-23 \n(up to December, 2022) \nAndaman & \nNicobar Islands \n310.28 541.64 97.02 23.92 \nMaharashtra 290.51 1490.97 442.6 459.13 \n\n3 \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nSl. No. Name of State/Circle where BSNL is providing 4G services on limited scale  \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar  \n2.  Assam \n3.  Bihar \n4.  Kolkata Telecom District \n5.  Jharkhand \n6.  North East (NE1) \n7.  North East (NE2) \n8.  Odisha \n9.  West Bengal (including Sikkim Circle) \n10.  Chhattisgarh \n11.  Gujarat \n12.  Maharashtra \n13.  Madhya Pradesh \n14.  Haryana \n15.  Himachal Pradesh \n16.  Jammu & Kashmir \n17.  Punjab \n18.  Uttaranchal \n19.  UP (East) \n20.  UP (West) \n21.  Andhra Pradesh \n22.  Chennai Telecom District \n23.  Kerala \n24.  Karnataka \n25.  Tamil Nadu \n26.  Telangana \n \n  \n\n4 \n \nAnnexure-II \nDistrict-wise details of the uncovered villages in Maharashtra \nSl. No. District Name Total No. of villages \n \nNo. of Uncovered villages \n1 Ahmadnagar 1586 97 \n2 Akola 972 17 \n3 Amravati 1981 173 \n4 Aurangabad 1361 14 \n5 Bhandara 885 35 \n6 Bid 1371 22 \n7 Buldana 1438 30 \n8 Chandrapur 1775 79 \n9 Dhule 720 61 \n10 Gadchiro"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |209", "t": "Ethanol Production", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 209 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2 nd February, 2023 \nEthanol Production \n209. DR. KALANIDHI VEERASWAMY: \n        SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH: \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the current ethanol production capacity in the country along with \nits sources; \n(b) whether the Government has projected its demand within the country based on \nthe adoption of vehicles and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has proposed to expand its production within the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) whether the Government has set a new target with regard to its production \nwithin the country and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयम और \u0000ाक ृितक गैस मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n)\u0000ी रामे\u0000र तेली(  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS  \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a): The current annual ethanol production capacity in the country is approximately \n1037 crore litres which includes approximately 700 crore litres of molasses based \nand approximately 337 crore litres of grain based production capacity. Oil Central \nPublic Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) are also setting up Second Generation (2G) \nethanol bio-refineries at Panipat (Haryana), at Bathinda (Punjab) and at Bargarh \n(Odisha), each with a production capacity of 100 Kilo Litre Per Day (KLPD) and \nat Numaligarh (Assam) with a production capacity of 185 KLPD. \n(b):  As per the Roadmap prepared by NITI Aayog which is based on the projected \nsale of Motor Spirit (MS), the estimated requirement of ethanol for blending with \npetrol is 542 crore litres for Ethanol Supply Year (ESY) 2022-23, 698 crore litres \nfor ESY 2023-24, 988 crore litres for ESY 2024-25 and 1016 crore litres for ESY \n2025-26. \n \n\n \n(c) & (d):  Government has taken various steps to increase ethanol production in \nthe country. Under the Ethanol Interest Subvention Scheme to encourage sugar \nmills and distilleries, a total of 1141 projects with estimated ethanol production \ncapacity of approximately 4012 crore litres have been approved. Further, Public \nSector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) had floated an Expression of Interest \n(EoI) for signing Long Term Offtake Agreement (LTOA) with upcoming \ndedicated ethanol plants in ethanol deficit States for supply of anhydrous ethanol. \nThrough this EoI, Public Sector OMCs have signed LTOA with 131 Dedicated \nEthanol Plant proponents in various states which will add an estimated production \ncapacity of approx. 745 crore litres per annum of ethanol in next few years. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |21", "t": "National Highway Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 21 \nANSWERED ON 02ND FEBRUARY, 2023  \n  \nNATIONAL HIGHWAY PROJECTS \n  \n21.  ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government has received any proposal to construct \na National Highway (NH) from Kochi to Sivaganga via Idukki and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has accorded in -principle approval for \nthe said NH;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons for not completing \nthe said NH;  \n(d) whether the Government proposes to ensure that the project \nwhich is passing through E rnakulam, Vytilla, Kolanchery, \nMuvattupuzha, Vazhakulam, Thodupuzha, Vagamon, Kattappana, \nKambammettu, Kambam and Theni to Sivaganga is completed as per \nschedule; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (c) The road connecting Kochi in Kerala and Sivaganga in Tamil \nNadu is already an existing National Highway NH-85 which is mostly \n2 lane and partly 4 lane.  Presently, 10 works on NH -85 having a \n\nlength of 139.56 km costing Rs 813 Crore have been taken up and are \nlikely to be completed by August, 2024. \n  \n(d) & (e) Apart from the existing NH -85, construction of a Greenfield \nHighway is being taken up from Kochi to Madurai by NHAI for which \nDPR is in progress.   However, the existing NH -85 from Kochi to \nSivaganga is being maintained in traffic worthy condition.  DPR works \nfor development of the stretch of NH -85 from Kerala/ Tamil Nadu \nborder to Madurai is also in progress.   \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |210", "t": "Grievance Redressal System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA�\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION�\nLok Sabha�\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 210�\n ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  2nd February 2023 ) �\nGRIEVANCE REDRESSAL SYSTEM�\n210.    SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM                   �\nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION�\nbe pleased to state:-�\n(a) whether the Government has any mechanism to ensure \ngrievance redressal of private airlines;�\n(b) if so, the details thereof; �\n(c) whether the Government proposes to take steps to ensure \nproper grievance redressal by third party flight  booking \nagent/applications regarding issues like refund etc.; �\n(d) if so, the steps being taken/proposed to be taken to protect the \nconsumers; �\n(e) whether the Government proposes to set up any tribunal to \ncheck non-redressal of issues regarding passenger complaints; and �\n(f) if so, the details thereof?�\nANSWER�\nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      \n(GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))�\n(a) & (b): Yes Sir. The aggrieved passengers can lodge their \ngrievances through the Centralized Public Grievanc e Redress and \nMonitoring System (CPGRAMS), an online portal administered by the \nDepartment of Administrative Reforms and Public Grievances \n(DARPG) and AirSewa Portal managed by the Ministry of Civil \nAviation. These grievances are taken up with the concerne d airlines \nfor redressal. If the petitioner is not satisfied with the resolution of \ngrievance by airline, he/ she has option to file an appeal. Disposal of \nthe grievances is monitored at various levels. Every airline appoints \n\na nodal officer and an appella te authority and displays their contact \ndetails on their respective websites.�\n(c) & (d): Civil Aviation Requirements (CARs) Section 3 Air Transport \nSeries M, Part II namely Refund of Airline Tickets to Passengers of \nPublic Transport  Undertakings states th at in case of purchase of \nticket through travel agent/portal, onus of refund shall lie with the \nairlines as agents are their appointed representatives. The airlines \nshall ensure that the refund process is completed within 30 working \ndays. Such grievances a re also taken up with the airlines for \nresolution. �\n(e): No such proposal is currently under consideration.�\n(f): Does not arise.�\n******�\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |211", "t": "National Thermal Power Corporation", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 211 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.03.2023 \n \nEXTENSION OF VISTA DOME TOURIST EXPRESS \n \n*211. DR. RAJDEEP ROY:                \n \n        Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Vistadome Tourist Express which has been started from \nGuwahati to Badarpur junction has been successful; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether there is great demand for extending the said route till Silchar; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) whether there is any possibility to extend the route from Badarpur to \nSilchar, if so, the time by which the project is likely to start? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n (a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******* \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN  REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 211 BY DR. RAJDEEP ROY  TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK \nSABHA ON 15.03 .2023 REGARDING EXTENSION OF VISTA DOME TOURIST  \nEXPRESS \n  \n(a) & (b) The overall average utilization of Train number 15887/15888 \nGuwahati - Badarpur Tourist Express for the period 13.11.2021 to \n02.03.2023 is 70.52% and 86.22% respectively. \n(c) to (e) Request for increasing the frequency/extension of vis tadome \nservice has been received from the Hon’ble Member of Parliament. The \nsame has been examined. However, extension of  15888/87 Guwahati - \nBadarpur Express upto Silchar is not feasible, at present, due to \noperational constraints including inadequate lie -over of the train at \nBadarpur. Besides, the increase in frequency of 15888/87 Guwahati - \nBadarpur Express is also operationally not feasible. Nonetheless, \nextension/increase in frequency of train services is an on -going process on \nIndian Railways, which inter alia  depends on operational fe asibility, \ncommercial viability, and resource availability. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |212", "t": "Closed MSMEs", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 212\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2023\n \n \nCLOSED MSMEs\n \n \n212. \n \nDR. PON GAUTHAM SIGAMANI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it is a fact that during the year 2022, a record number of Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterprises (MSMEs) have been shut down due to various factors;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the reaction of the Government thereto;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government propos\nes to provide assistance to such MSME units as these \nare the units which provide jobs to the people; and \n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWE\nR\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) \n& (b): \n \nWith the launch of Udyam Registration Portal on 01.07.2020 to 30.01.2023, \nmore than 1.38 crore enterprises have registered\n \nduring 01.07.2020 to 30.01.2023\n, of which \n6,222 number of enterprises shut down business during 2021\n-\n22, which constitutes 0.0004% \nof\n \nthe total MSMEs registered on Udyam Registration Portal. \n \n \n(c) & (d): \n \nThe Government has taken a number of initiatives to support MSMEs in the \ncountry. Some of these include:\n \ni\n.\n \nRs. 5 lakh crore Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme for business, including\n \nMSMEs.\n \nii\n.\n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through Self Reliant India Fund.\n \niii\n.\n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \niv\n.\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores.\n \nv\n.\n \n“Udyam Registration” for MSMEs, for Ease of Doing Business\n \nvi\n.\n \nLaunching of an o\nnline Portal “Champions” in June, 2020 to cover many aspects of \ne\n-\ngovernance including redressing grievances and handholding of MSMEs.\n \nvii\n.\n \nInclusion of Retail and Wholesale traders as MSMEs  as well as street vendors for \navailing the Priority Sector Lending.\n \nviii\n.\n \nN\non\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of \nMSMEs.\n \nix\n.\n \nLaunch of Udyam Assist Platform (UAP) on 11.01.2023 to bring Informal Micro \nEnterprises (IMEs) under the formal ambit for availing the benefit under Priority \nSector Lendin\ng (PSL).\n \n \n*****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |213", "t": "Har Ghar Jal Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 213 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.03.2023 \n \nMAINTENANCE OF NEW PIT LINES \n*213. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the new pit line at Rajkot is capable of \nhandling primary and secondary maintenances including Rajdhani, \nVande Bharat and Gatiman like trains and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government is likely to consider the pending requests \nfor extending trains upto Rajkot after these terminate at \nAhmedabad and remain idle for more than fifteen hours in the yard; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the time likely to be taken for its \nextension? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT RFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO  (d) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 213 BY SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI KUNDARIYA  TO \nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 15.03.2023 REGARDING  \nMAINTENANCE OF NEW PIT LINES \n(a) : The new pit line at Rajkot is capable of primary and secondary \nmaintenance of Rajdhani and Gatiman types of trains.  \n \nFor Vande Bharat train s all Zonal Railways have been advised to \ndevelop/upgrade existing maintenance infrastructure. \n \nUp-gradation/Development at Rajkot to maintain Vande Bharat Trains \nwill also be planned as per operational requirement. \n(b) to (d):  Extension of train services, is an ongoing process on Indian \nRailways which is dependent on operational feasibility, commercial \njustification, availability of resources etc. The recent completion of \ndoubling work on the Ahmedabad -Rajkot section has eased out the \nsevere line capacity constraints of the said section and this would \nfacilitate extension of train services upto Rajkot. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |214", "t": "Development of Multi-Modal Logistics Parks", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.214 \nANSWERED ON  2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n  \nDEVELOPMENT OF MULTI-MODAL LOGISTICS PARKS \n  \n  214.        DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n           \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n������ ������������ ���� �������������� �������� \n� \nbe  pleased to state: \n \n(a) the present status of feasibility studies and identification of land \nparcels for the proposed 35 Multi -Modal Logistics Parks (MMLP) under \nPhase-I; \n  \n(b) the current status of progress made in the development of \nproposed MMLP at Kochi along with the timeline set for completion of \nthe same; \n  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up more MMLP in Kerala \nunder Phase-II of the project; and  \n  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n\n�\n(a)     Present status of studies and identification of land parcels for 35 \nMMLP under Phase –I is given in Annexure –I. \n  \n(b)     The Pre-feasibility study for development of MMLP at Kochi has \nbeen completed.  Development of MMLP at Kochi is found feasible.   As \nper the assessment, about 120 acres land is  required.  Cochin Port \nAuthority (CPA) has identified 118  acres land at Willingdon Island.   As \nper policy of MoRT&H, MoU need to be signed for formation of SPV \nbetween Cochin Port Authority (CPA) and National Highway Logistics \nManagement Limited (NHLML), a subsidiary of NHAI for development of \nMMLP. \n  \n(c) & (d)    At present, Kochi only in the State of Kerala, based on pre -\nfeasibility study,  has been identified as part of development of 35 \nMMLPs across country.      \n  \n  \n  \n�\n�\n� �\n\n ANNEXURE -I \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART ( A) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 214 FOR 02.02.2023 ASKED BY DR. SHASHI \nTHAROOR REGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF MULTI -MODLE LOGISTICS \nPARKS. \nS. \nNo. Location State Status of Land \nidentification Present Status \n1 \nDelhi-NCR \n(Delhi, Gurgaon, \nGhaziabad, \nFaridabad, \nNoida) \nDelhi NCR \nState govt. yet \nto identify land Pre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n2 Mumbai Maharashtra Land Identified Feasibility is in \nprogress \n3 \nNorth Gujarat \n(Ahmedabad \nand Vadodara) \nGujarat \nState govt. yet \nto identify land Pre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n4 Hyderabad Telangana Land identified DPR is in progress \n5 \nSouth Gujarat \n(Surat and \nBharuch) \nGujarat \nState govt. yet \nto identify land DPR is in progress \n6 \nSouth Punjab \n(Ludhiana, \nSangrur, Patiala \nPunjab \nState govt. yet \nto identify land DPR is in progress \n7 \nNorth Punjab \n(Amritsar, \nJalandhar, \nGurudaspur) \nPunjab \nState govt. yet \nto identify land Pre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n8 Jaipur Rajasthan State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n9 Bangalore Karnataka \nLand identified Feasibility \ncompleted, Under \nbidding \n10 Pune Maharashtra Land identified DPR is in progress \n11 Anantapur Andhra \nPradesh \nLand identified DPR is in progress \n\nS. \nNo. Location State Status of Land \nidentification Present Status \n12 Chennai Tamil Nadu \nLand identified MMLP \ndevelopment work \nAwarded \n13 Nagpur Maharashtra \nLand identified Feasibility \ncompleted, Under \nbidding \n14 Indore Madhya \nPradesh \nLand identified Feasibility \ncompleted, Under \nbidding \n15 Patna Bihar Land identified DPR to be initiated \n16 Kolkata West Bengal State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n17 Ambala Haryana State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n18 Valsad Gujarat State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n19 Coimbatore Tamil Nadu Land identified DPR is in progress \n20 Jagatsinghpur Orissa State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n21 Nasik Maharashtra Land identified DPR to be initiated \n22 Guwahati Assam \nLand identified Under \nimplementation by \nNHIDCL \n23 Kota Rajasthan State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n24 Panaji Goa State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n25 Hisar Haryana State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n26 Visakhapatnam Andhra \nPradesh \nState govt. yet \nto identify land DPR is in progress \n27 Bhopal Madhya \nPradesh \nState govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n28 Sundargarh Orissa State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n\nS. \nNo. Location State Status of Land \nidentification Present Status \n29 Bhatinda Punjab State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n30 Solan Himachal \nPradesh \nState govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n31 Rajkot Gujarat State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n32 Raipur Chhattisgarh State govt. yet \nto identify land \nPre-feasibility is in \nprogress \n33 Jammu Jammu and \nKashmir \nLand identified DPR is in progress \n34 Kandla Gujarat State govt. yet \nto id"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |215", "t": "Routes under UDAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 215\n(To be answered on the 2nd February 2023)\n \nROUTES UNDER UDAN SCHEME\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the details of the new routes operationalised under the Ude Desh ka Aam\nNaagrik (UDAN) scheme, State/UT-wise including the State of Uttar Pradesh,\nRajasthan and Odisha;\n(b) whether any new airports have been developed under this scheme in Uttar\nPradesh, Rajasthan and Odisha;\n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(d) the details of the total number of heliports that have been developed under\nthe said scheme, State/UT-wise;\n(e) whether the said scheme has been linked with PM GatiShakti; and\n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a): As of 25.01.2023, total 459 Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS) - UDAN\n(Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik) routes including 59 of Uttar Pradesh, 40 routes of\nRajasthan & 22 RCS routes of Odisha have been commenced under the\nUDAN scheme.\n \n(b) & (c): Eighteen Airports have been identified under UDAN in the State of\nUttar Pradesh out of which Kanpur, Prayagraj, Kushinagar, Hindon, Agra &\nBareilly have been operationalised. Six Airports have been identified under\nUDAN in the State of Rajasthan out of which Bikaner, Jaisalmer &\nKishangargh have been operationalised. Six Airports have been identified\n215. SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA\nSHRI BRIJBHUSHAN SHARAN SINGH\nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY\nSHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI\nDR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nunder UDAN in the State of Odisha out of which Jeypore, Jharsuguda &\nRourkela have been operationalised. \n \n(d): Nine Heliports have been developed and operationalized in the NSOP\ncategory under the UDAN scheme in Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand.\nMandi and Rampur are in Himachal Pradesh while Almora, Chinyalisaur,\nGaucher, Haldwani, New Tehri, Sahastradhara and Srinagar are in\nUttarakhand.\n \n(e) & (f): As per the DPIIT Guidelines dated 29.12.2021, the projects costing\nmore than Rs. 500 crore are mapped under PM GatiShakti. The\nrevival/development of the airports selected under UDAN have lower project\ncosting/capex limits than applicable for PM GatiShakti. \n \n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |216", "t": "Vizag Metro Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 216 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nVIZAG METRO PROJECT \n \nNO.216. SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA: \n     DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to provide financia l support to the \nambitious Vizag Metro Project (VMP) which was being  developed with \nan estimated cost of Rs 15,933 crore, and was propo sed to cover a \ntotal distance of 75.3 kms with the deadline set fo r 2028, has hit a \nroadblock, after the Korean EXIM Bank had displayed  their inability \ntofund the same; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(d)  the details of other measures being taken by the Go vernment to \nsupport urban mobility in the State of Andhra Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)  to (c) :Urban Transport, which is an integral part of urban \ndevelopment, is a state subject. Therefore, formula tion of proposals in \nregard to Metro Rail projects with appropriate fund ing pattern, along \nwith preparation of Comprehensive Mobility Plan, Al ternative Analysis \nReport, Detailed Project Report etc., are done by t he respective State \nGovernments. Central Government considers financial  assistance to \nMetro Rail Projects as and when posed by concerned State Government. \nHowever, approval of such projects depends upon fea sibility of proposal \nand availability of resources. Government of Andhra  Pradesh in \nSeptember, 2018 submitted a proposal for a Light Me tro Rail Project for \nVisakhapatnam with a route length of 42.55 km with an estimated cost \nof Rs 8,300 crore for external financial assistance  from Korean EXIM \nBank in Public Private Partnership mode. However, the Korean side,  \n...2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \nafter examining the proposal conveyed their inabili ty to finance the \nproject. Central Government has so farnot received any proposal of \nVizag Metro Project from Government of Andhra Prade sh with route \nlength of 75.3 km at an estimated cost of Rs 15,993 crore. \n \n(d)  Central Government has released funds for conductin g different \ntransportation studies including preparation of Det ailed Project Report \nand Comprehensive Mobility Plans for different citi es of Andhra Pradesh \nunder Urban Transport planning and Capacity Buildin g Scheme. The \ndetails are as under: \nS. \nNo. Name of the Study/Report \nFunds \nreleased by \nCentral \nGovernment \n(in Rs.) \nCompletion \nYear \n1 \nPrepartion of Comprehensive \nMobility Plan for \nVishakhapatnam Metropolitan \nArea \n40,00,000/- 2018-19 \n2 \nPrepartion of Comprehensive \nMobility Plans for 9 Cities of AP \ni.e. Eluru, Kakinda, Nellore, \nOngole, Rajahmundry, \nAnanthapur, Chitoor, Kadapa \nand Kurnool \n2,36,00,000/- 2019-2020 \n3 \nPreparation of DPR  for two \ntransportation studies i.e \nIntegrated Public Transport \nPlan (IPT) & Non-Motorised \nTransport Integration Plan \n(NMT)for Vijayawada Metro \nRail System. \n78,44,000/- 2018-19 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |217", "t": "Exploration of Oil and Natural Gas", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 217 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15.03.2023 \n \nBUDGET OUTLAY AND CAPITAL EXPENDITURE \n \n*217.  SHRI COSME FRANCISCO CAITANO SARDINHA: \n  \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the budget outlay and actual expenditure incurred by the \nRailways, including Konkan Railways for capital \nexpenditure in the State of Goa during the last five years;  \n \n(b) the number of construction projects that were sanctioned \nin the State during the last five years and the progress \nmade in this regard; and \n \n(c) the number of projects that were stalled along with the \nreasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n  \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 217 BY SHRI COSME FRANCISCO CAITANO \nSARDINHA TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 15.03.2023 \nREGARDING BUDGET OUTLAY AND CAPITAL EXPENDITURE \n \n(a): Railway projects are sanctioned and taken up Zonal Railway -\nwise and not State -wise as the Indian Railways’ projects may span \nacross various State boundaries. \n As on 01.04.2022 , two doubling projects of total length 3 79 Km \ncosting `4929 Cr, falling fully/partly in the state of Goa are in different \nstage of planning/ execution, out of which 288 Km doubling has been \ncommissioned and an expenditure of `2889 Cr has been incurred so \nfar. These include: \n \n(i) Hospet-Hubli-Londa-Tinaighat-Vasco da Gama  doubling project \nof length 363  kilometers and costing `3037.1 crores and \n(ii) Chandargaon- Cansaulium Rail Flyover project of length 16 \nkilometers was included in Financial Year 2019 -20 costing `1892 \ncrore. \n \n Railway projects in Goa falls  in South Western Railway zone of \nIndian Railways. Zone-wise details of all Railway projects including \ncost, expenditure and outlay are made available in public domain on \nIndian Railways website i.e.  www.indianrailways.gov.in > Ministry of \nRailways >Railway Board >About Indian Railways >Railway Board \nDirectorates > Finance (Budget) >Rail Budget/Pink Book (Year)> \nRailway wise Works Machinery and Rolling Stock Programme. \n…2/- \n\n \n  \n-2- \n \n For Konkan Railways , Budget outlay of Gov ernment of India is \n`52.84 crores and Budget outlay of Konk an Railway is `28.98 crores  \nduring last five years for capital expenditure in the State of Goa. Fund \nborrowed by Konkan Railway Corporation Ltd. for Capital Expenditure \nin the state of Goa is `82.97 crores. Actual Capital Expedniture  \nincurred by Konkan Railway Corporation Ltd. in th e sta te of Goa is \n`164.29 crores.  \n (b): Chandargaon- Cansaulim Rail Flyover project of length 16 \nkilometers, costing `1892 crore,  was included in Budget 2019-20 \nsubject to obtaining requisite Government Clearances . Final Location \nSurvey for the preparation of Detailed Project Report ha s been taken \nup. \n  (c): The completion of any Railway project(s) depends on \nvarious factors like quick land acquisition by State Government, \nforest clearance by officials of forest department, shifting of \ninfringing utilities, statutory clearances from various authorities, \ngeological and topographical conditions of area, law and order \nsituation in the area of project(s) site, number of working months in a \nyear for particular project site due to climatic conditions et c.  All \nthese factors affect the completion time of the project(s). With above \nconstraints, every effort is being made to execute the project(s) \nexpeditiously. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |218", "t": "Agarbatti Project", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 218\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2023\n \n \nAGARBATTI PROJECT\n \n \n218.\n \nSHRI JAYADEV GALLA\n:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details of agarbatti making and mixing machines provided to individuals and Self \nHelp Groups for the Agarbatti Project under the Gramodaya Vikas Yojana (GVY) in the \ncountry, State\n-\nwise including Andhra Pradesh, district\n-\nwise; \n \n(b) \n \nwhether\n \nsupport is being provided in the form of training, raw material, marketing, \nand financial support to the artisans by Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) \nand if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to establish simila\nr projects under the scheme in \nAndhra Pradesh and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) \n \nthe number of Centres of Excellence have been proposed and to be established under \nthe project; \n \n(e) \n \nwhether any study has been conducted to assess the impact of the schem\ne on the \nproductivity, profits and wages of artisans; and \n \n(f) \n \nif so, the productivity, profits and wages of artisans that have been improved under \nthe scheme classified, State\n-\nwise?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a)&(b):\n \n \nThe Agarbatti activity comes under Wellness & Cosmetics Industries, a \nvertical of Gramodyog Vikas Yojana (GVY) under Khadi & Gramodyog Vikas Yojana \n(KGVY), being implemented by Khadi and Village Industries Commiss\nion (KVIC), \nincludes oil & soap industry, aromatic oil & fragrances, cosmetic & beauty products, etc. \nThese industries are highly labour intensive and eco\n-\nfriendly. Under the scheme, 10 days \ntraining is provided to agarbatti manufacturing artisans through \nmaster trainers who are \ntrained by Centre of Excellence (CoE) and provided Agarbatti machines (automatic and \nmanual), toolkit and equipment to individual beneficiaries/artisans.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n\n \n \nPattern of assistance under the scheme is as follows:\n \n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nParticulars\n \nKVIC contribution\n \nSC\n \nST\n \nGen\n \nBPL\n \n1\n \nNon\n-\nNorth Eastern \nRegion\n \n90%\n \n90%\n \n80%\n \n100%\n \n2\n \nNorth Eastern Region\n \n95%\n \n95%\n \n90%\n \n100%\n \n \n \nState and district wise details of artisans trained and pedal operated agarbatti \nmachines distributed in the country is \nannexed.\n \n \n(c):\n \nKVIC has not proposed similar project under the scheme in Andhra Pradesh.\n \n \n(d):\n \nTarget for the establishment of four Centre of Excellence (CoE) has been fixed, out \nof which two CoEs have already been established.\n \n \n(e):\n \nKVIC has not conducted any study on impact of Agarbatti programme on the \nproductivity, profit and wages of artisans. However, survey study on post policy impact on \nimport restrictions on Agarbatti has been conducted through outsourced agency.\n \n \n(f):\n \nDoes no\nt arise.\n \n* * * * *\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a)&(b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 218 \nfor answer on 02.02.2023\n \n \n \nState and district wise details of artisans trained and pedal operated agarbatti\n \nmachines distributed \nin the country\n \n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nState/UT\n \nDistrict\n \nTarget\n \nAchievement\n \n1\n.\n \nJammu\n \nJammu \n \n40\n \n40\n \n2\n.\n \nHaryana\n \nKaithal \n \n40\n \n0\n \n3\n.\n \nRajasthan\n \nJaisalmer\n \n20\n \n20\n \n4\n.\n \nHanumangarh\n \n20\n \n20\n \n5\n.\n \nJaipur\n \n30\n \n30\n \n6\n.\n \nJhalawar\n \n20\n \n20\n \n7\n.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \nKanpur Nagar\n \n50\n \n50\n \n8\n.\n \nGhazipur\n \n25\n \n25\n \n9\n.\n \nSonbhadra\n \n25\n \n25\n \n10\n.\n \nWest Bengal\n \nJhargram\n \n50\n \n50\n \n11\n.\n \nJharkhand\n \nSeraikela\n-\nKharsawan\n \n90\n \n90\n \n12\n.\n \nBihar\n \nDarbhanga\n \n25\n \n25\n \n13\n.\n \nGopalganj\n \n25\n \n25\n \n14\n.\n \nOdisha\n \nPuri\n \n30\n \n30\n \n15\n.\n \nKhurda\n \n10\n \n10\n \n16\n.\n \nNayagarh\n \n10\n \n10\n \n17\n.\n \nMaharashtra\n \nNanded\n \n50\n \n50\n \n18\n.\n \nSindhudurg\n \n20\n \n20\n \n19\n.\n \nRatnagiri\n \n60\n \n60\n \n20\n.\n \nAhmednagar\n \n70\n \n70\n \n21\n.\n \nSolapur\n \n20\n \n20\n \n22\n.\n \nAmravati\n \n40\n \n40\n \n23\n.\n \nKarnataka\n \nBangalore Rural\n \n50\n \n50\n \n24\n.\n \nHosur\n \n40\n \n40\n \n25\n.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \nVillupuram \n \n40\n \n40\n \n26\n.\n \nCuddalore\n \n50\n \n50\n \n27\n.\n \nMadurai\n \n10\n \n10\n \n28\n.\n \nTirunelveli\n \n10\n \n10\n \n29\n.\n \nRamanathapuram\n \n10\n \n10\n \n30\n.\n \nTeni\n \n20\n \n20\n \n31\n.\n \nArunachal \nPradesh\n \nLower Subansiri\n \n \n20\n \n20\n \n32\n.\n \nPapum Pare\n \n20\n \n20\n \n \n \nTotal\n \n1040\n \n1000\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |219", "t": "Cultivable Area under PMKSY", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA \nREJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †219 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \nCULTIVABLE AREA UNDER PMKSY \n†219.    DR. HEENA GAVIT:  \nPROF. RITA BAHUGUNA JOSHI  \nDR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL  \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL  \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE  \nDR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV  \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) the data on the increased cultivable area and the number of new water resources created under \n‘Har Khet ko Paani’ Component of the Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) till date; \n(b) the periodic assessments, if any, being carried  out to ensure the smooth implementation of the thr ee \ncomponents of the PMKSY; \n(c) the progress being made towards achieving the a im to cover 28.5 lakh hectare area under irrigation  \nby 2024; and \n(d) the measures taken/proposed to be taken to redu ce the wide gap between irrigation potential create d \nand irrigation potential utilized in order to increase the crop yield and reduce dependency of \nfarmers on irrigation? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n \n(a)   Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) is an umbrella scheme, consisting, inter alia, of \ntwo major components being implemented by this Mini stry, namely, Accelerated Irrigation Benefit \nProgramme (AIBP), and Har Khet Ko Pani (HKKP). HKKP , in turn, consists of four sub-components: \nCommand Area Development & Water Management (CAD&WM ), Surface Minor Irrigation (SMI), \nRepair, Renovation and Restoration (RRR) of Water B odies, and Ground Water (GW) Development. \nHowever, while approving continuation of PMKSY for the period 2021-26, Government of India has \nprovisionally approved GW Development component only for ongoing works. \nFurther, PMKSY also included two components being i mplemented by other Departments. Per \nDrop More Crop component of PMKSY was being impleme nted by Department of Agriculture & \n\nFarmers Welfare, which is now no longer a part of P MKSY. In addition, Watershed Development \ncomponent of PMKSY is being implemented by Department of Land Resources.   \nUnder 85 number of projects included under CAD&WM c omponent of PMKSY-HKKP in 2016, \nduring 2016-2022 cultivable command area of 16.42 l akh hectare has been covered. Further, irrigation \npotential of 2.58 lakh hectare has been achieved du ring 2016-2022 under SMI component of PMKSY-\nHKKP, with completion of 2,406 number of projects i n this period. Similarly under RRR of Water \nBodies component of PMKSY-HKKP, 0.84 lakh hectare o f irrigation potential has been achieved \nduring 2016-2022, with completion of work in 1,134 number of water bodies, and revival of storage of \n131.15 million cubic meter. Under GW Development co mponent of HKKP, till June, 2022, 27,962 \nnumber of wells have been completed, creating 70.89 thousand hectare irrigation potential. \n \n(b) Development Monitoring and Evaluation Office (DMEO)  under NITI Ayog has carried out \nevaluation of PMKSY for the period 2015-2020. Furth er \"Performance and Impact of Micro Irrigation \nin Improving Water Use Efficiency in India's Agricu lture: Study of the PMKSY-PDMC\" has been \ncarried out by Indian Institute of Management, Ahme dabad in the year 2021. In addition, progress of \nvarious projects under PMKSY are being reviewed fro m time to time by various authorities and \nagencies of the State Government as well as Government of India. \n \n(c)  Apart from the irrigation potential created under different components of PMKSY-HKKP \nmentioned above, an additional irrigation potential  of 24.35 lakh hectare has been created during 2016 -\n17 to 2021-2022 by major and medium irrigation proj ects being provided partial financial assistance \nunder Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme (AIB P) component of PMKSY-AIBP. In addition, \nunder Per Drop More Crop component of PMKSY being i mplemented by Department of Agriculture & \nFarmers Welfare, during 2015-22, 67.46 lakh hectare  has been covered under micro irrigation. \nSimilarly, under Watershed Development component of  PMKSY being implemented by Department of \nLand Resources, during 2015-22 inter-alia an additi onal area of 14.54 lakh hectare has been brought \nunder protective irrigation.  \n \n(d)  In order to reduce the gap between irrigation pote ntial created and utilized, the State Governments \nconcerned are undertaking command area development and water management works, primarily \nconsisting of on-farm development works. Further, s ince 1974-75, Government of India has been \nproviding partial financial assistance to identifie d projects for CAD&WM works under centrally \nsponsored scheme named Command Area Development sch eme, which was later re-named as \nCommand Area Development & Water Management scheme.  The scheme was brought under the \nPMKSY-HKKP umbrella in 2015. Since 2016, central as sistance to CAD&WM works by Government \nof India u"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |22", "t": "Manufacturing of Solar Modules", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO-22\nANSWERED ON- 03/02/2023\nACTIVITIES UNDERTAKEN UNDER G20 SUMMIT\n*22. SHRI T. R. BAALU\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the activities proposed to be undertaken under G20 Meet to be held in\nIndia in the near future;\n(b) the reasons for consulting and co-opting the State Governments and political parties in\nconducting G20 Summit; and\n(c) whether any tangible economic benefits are expected to be achieved from this Summit\napart from improvement in diplomatic relations at multilateral levels and if so, the\ndetails thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n(DR. S. JAISHANKAR)\n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n1\n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) in respect of Lok Sabha\nStarred  Question  No.  22  for  reply  on  03.02.2023  regarding  Activities\nundertaken under G20 Summit asked by Shri T. R. Baalu.\n(a to c)  During its G20 Presidency, India will host around 200 meetings across\nthe country in more than 50 cities.  30 different work-streams comprising of\nSherpa  Track  Working  Groups,  Finance  Track  workstreams,  Ministerial\nmeetings and Engagement Groups will be holding their deliberative sessions.\nThe G20 Leaders’ Summit has been scheduled for 9-10 September 2023 in New\nDelhi.\nThe  Government  believes that  the  G20  Presidency  is  a  unique  occasion  to\ndemonstrate India’s achievements, capabilities and diversity. Therefore, it was\nfelt that the programmes must be approached in a ‘whole of India’ manner and\nevents  are  scheduled  accordingly.  These  naturally  are  being  organized  in\nconsultation with various state governments. Since this prestigious occasion is\nregarded as a national opportunity, various political parties were also engaged in\nthe process of preparation. This overall endeavour reflects the Government’s\ncommitment to cooperative federalism. \nThe substantive deliberations in broad priority areas during our G20 Presidency\ninclude, inter-alia, matters relating to inclusive and resilient growth; progress on\nSDGs,  green  development  and  Lifestyle  for  Environment  (Mission  LiFE);\ntechnological  transformation  and  public  digital  infrastructure;  reforming\nmultilateral institutions; women led development; and international peace and\nharmony.   Outcomes  in  these  areas  would  emerge  out  of  the  deliberations\nduring the course of our G20 Presidency.\nIt is also expected that India would articulate the concerns of the Global South\nand help build consensus on polarizing issues in contemporary international\npolitics.\n*****\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |220", "t": "Inter-linking of Rivers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 220 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \nINTER-LINKING OF RIVERS \n220.   SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL  SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM S HRANGARE \n   SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA  SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA \n   SHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK NIMBALKAR   \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the inter-linking of rivers in the country, State-wise; \n(b) the details of the ongoing schemes and schemes proposed for various States in this regard; \n(c) the funds sanctioned and utilised during the current financial year for the said purpose; and \n(d) the position or view of the Government on the viability and success of inter-linking of rivers? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI  BISHWESWAR TUDU ) \n(a) & (b)   The National Water Development Agency (NWDA) has been entrusted with the work of inter-\nlinking of rivers under National Perspective Plan ( NPP). NPP has two components,  viz., Himalayan \nRivers Development Component and Peninsular Rivers Development Component. 30 link projects have \nbeen identified under NPP. Pre-Feasibility Reports (PFRs) of all the 30 links have been completed and \nFeasibility Reports (FRs) of 24 links and Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) of 8 links have been complet ed. \nKen-Betwa Link Project (KBLP) is the first link pro ject under NPP, for which implementation has been \ninitiated. Latest status and State-wise details of Inter-linking of Rivers (ILR) projects are given at  \nAnnexure . \n(c)    A budget allocation of ₹ 1,100 crore in the curr ent financial year has been made for KBLP by \nGovernment of India. The total amount spent on the project till  31.12.2022  is ₹ 7,665 crore,  includ ing ₹ \n5,038.28 crore from the Central grant and  ₹ 2,626.70 crore  from the State budget.  \n(d)   The cooperation of States is paramount in the imp lementation of inter-linking of rivers projects. In ter-\nBasin Water Transfer (IBWT) from surplus basins to water deficit basins/areas is essential to address the \nimbalance in water availability across the country and water security in the country.  ILR programme i s \nbeing pursued in a consultative manner by building consensus amongst the Party States for the \nimplementation of the ILR projects. Government of I ndia has accorded priority to the ILR programme and  \nhas been making all efforts to expedite the same.  \nKBLP is the first inter-linking project taken for i mplementation as a result of efforts made by \nMinistry of Jal Shakti and cooperation of  States o f Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh. The project is \nbeing implemented by Centre and both the States joi ntly through a Special Purpose Vehicle (Ken-Betwa \nLink Project Authority) with major funding coming from Central Government.  \n***   \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF  UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 220 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.02.2023 R EGARDING “INTER-LINKING \nOF RIVERS”. \n \nLatest Status and State-wise Details of Inter-Linking of River (ILR) projects under the National \nPerspective Plan (NPP) \nPeninsular Component \n \nSl. \nNo \nName of the link Status States \nbenefited \nAnnual \nIrrigation \n(Lakh ha) \nDomestic \n& \nIndustrial \n(Mm3) \nHydro \npower \n(MW) \n1 Mahanadi (Manibhadra) - \nGodavari (Dowlaiswaram) \nlink  \nFR completed  \nAndhra \nPradesh (AP) \n& Odisha \n  \n4.43 \n  \n700 \n  \n450 \nAlternate Mahanadi \n(Barmul)  - Rushikulya – \nGodavari (Dowlaiswaram) \nlink \nFR completed   AP & Odisha \n 6.25  \n(0.91 + 3.52 + \n1.82**) \n700 \n(MGL) \n% \n+125** \n210 \n(MGL) \n% \n+ 240** \n2 Godavari (Polavaram) - \nKrishna (Vijayawada) link  \nFR completed AP 2.1 162 -- \n3 a.)Godavari (Inchampalli) -  \nKrishna (Nagarjunasagar) \nlink  \n  \nFR completed \n  \nTelangana 2.87 237 975+ \n70=  \n1,045 \nb.) Alternate Godavari \n(Inchampalli) - Krishna \n(Nagarjunasagar) link * \n  \nDPR completed  Telangana 3.67 140 60 \n4 Godavari (Inchampalli) - \nKrishna (Pulichintala) link  \nFR completed  Telangana & \nAP \n6.13 \n(1.09 +5.04) \n413 27 \n5 a.) Krishna \n(Nagarjunasagar) - Pennar \n(Somasila ) link \n  \nFR completed \n  \n  \nAP \n  \n5.81 \n  \n124 \n  \n90 \nb.)Alternate Krishna \n(Nagarjunasagar) - Pennar \n(Somasila ) link * \n  \nDPR completed  AP 2.94   218 90 \n6 Krishna (Srisailam) – \nPennar link \nFR completed  -- -- -- 17 \n7 Krishna (Almatti) – Pennar \nlink \nFR completed  AP & \nKarnataka \n2.58  \n(1.9+0.68) \n56 13.5 \n8 a.)Pennar (Somasila) - \nCauvery (Grand Anicut) \nlink \nFR completed \n  \n  \nAP, Tamil \nNadu & \nPuducherry \n4.91  \n(0.49+ 4.36 \n+0.06) \n1105 -- \nb.) Alternate Pennar \n(Somasila) - Cauvery \n(Grand Anicut) link * \n \n  \