 an agent, \nphysically or through electronic mode; and (b) conducts any business \nactivity in India in any other manner.  \nMany foreign companies have Indian subsidiaries which are Indian \ncompanies but are subsidiaries of foreign holding companies.  Data \nshows that foreign com panies and their subsidiaries keep on \nregistering under Section 380 of the Companies Act, 2013 in various \nparts of the country and also close down their business operations \nfrom time to time. The details are as under:-  \nYear Number of \nForeign \nCompanies \nthat have \nregistered \nunder \nSection 380 \nof the \nCompanies \nAct, 2013 \nNumber of \nforeign \ncompanies \nregistered \nunder \nSection 380 \nof the \nCompanies \nAct, 2013  \nthat have \nclosed their \nplace of \nbusiness \nNumber of \ncompanies \nincorporated \nin India \nwhose \nholding \ncompany is \nincorporated \noutside India \nNumber of \ncompanies \nincorporated \nin India \nwhose \nholding \ncompany is \nincorporated \noutside India \nthat have \nclosed their \nplace of \nbusiness in \nIndia \n2012 182 147 903 4 \n2013 188 167 818 2 \n2014 184 112 732 2 \n2015 153 125 1051 5 \n2016 148 123 986 2 \n2017 149 136 1182 15 \n\n2018 102 111 1364 13 \n2019 137 133 1712 1815 \n2020 90 120 1244 163 \n2021 75 115 1692 349 \n2022 64 78 1940 505 \n2023 till \nJan 1 2 163 49 \n  1473 1369 13787 2924 \n \nThe closing of operations is a matter of commercial business decision. \nIt depends on a host of factors such as viability of operations, sectoral \nstrategy, global priorities, market size, capital and resource allocation \nwithin the company as well as the decision of a particular business \nentity whether to operate in different geographical areas or not. \nFurther, company wise details are available on the MCA website and \ncan be accessed as under: \nMCA Website Parliament Questions & Assurances others \n \n(c) Total number of foreign companies and companies incorporated in \nIndia whose holding companies is incorporated outside India, which \nare currently continuing their business operation in the country is as \nfollows:  \n \nNumber of Foreign Companies \nthat have registered under \nSection 380 of the Companies \nAct, 2013 \nNumber of companies \nincorporated in India whose \nholding company is incorporated \noutside India \n3295 14173 \n \nDetails of the above are available  on the MCA website and can be \naccessed as under: \nMCA Website Parliament Questions & Assurances others \n \n(d) No data in regards to Indian nationals working in foreign companies \nand subsidiaries is maintained with the Ministry. \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |263", "t": "Disability Pension to Defence Personnel", "head": "1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *263 \nANSWERED ON 20.03.2023 \n \nNational Eligibility Test \n \n*263.  SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \nDR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the Government has taken note that the technical glitches experienced by students \nacross several centres while taking the University Grants Commission’s (UGC) National \nEligibility Test (NET) in its last cycle that caused delays, if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government has also noticed that the time wasted due to all the delays was not \ncompensated for as it is a computer-based test as per students; \n(c)  the steps taken to ensure that such issues do not come u p in the next testing cycle to be \nconducted in the months of February-March of this year, if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  whether the Government has also directed the UGC to disburse information regarding test \ncentres and exam dates in a timely manner  since last year as candidates were informed of \nthe exam dates only four days prior to their exam and they received their admit cards with \ninformation of their exam centres very late, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \nPart (a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n2 \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *263 ANSWERED ON 20.03.2023 ASKED BY SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL, \nDR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI , HON’BLE MEMBER (S) OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING \n“NATIONAL ELIGIBILITY TEST”. \n \n(a) & (b) The Examination of UGC -NET December 2021 & June 2022 (merged cycles) was \nconducted for 9,01,221 Candidates in CBT Mode in 4 Phases in 33 Shifts across the coun try. \nDuring this examination, there were technical glitches affecting the examination on 9th July 2022 \n(Shift-1) at 07 Exam Centers involving 650 Candidates. The re -exam was held on 29 th \nSeptember 2022 for these candidates smoothly and they had full allotted time of 03 Hours. \n \n(c) The Exam Centres were audited and exam servers were strengthened before the conduct of \nthe exam in 05 Phases and 32 Shifts for 8,34,537 Candidates during February -March 2023. \nThe Examination has been completed on 16th March 2023. \n \n(d) NTA had announced the Schedule of UGC -NET June 2023 to be held between 13 th June \n2023 and 22nd June 2023 on 05 th January 2023. However, in order to avoid any tie -up with any \nExam Centre by/ on behalf of any candidate for any malpractice/ impersonation , the Exam \nCentres are declared to the candidates through Public Notice few days before the Exam. \nHowever, the Exam Cities are intimated to the candidates much in advance, to enable them to \nmake necessary arrangements, such as, travel and accommodation, wherever required. \n \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |264", "t": "Review of Constitution", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n������\n��\n��������� �\n��\n�������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������ �\n��\n�����������������������\n��\n����������������������������� �\n��\n������������������������������������������������ ����������������������\n��\n����������������� ���� ����������� ���� �������� ���� ���������������� ��� ���� ���������\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������\n��\n������ �\n��\n�������������������������� �\n������������������� �\n������\n��\n���� ��� ��������� ��������� ����������� ��� ������� ���� �������� ��� ���� ������������� ��������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ������� ��� ���� ��������������������� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� ��������� ���\n���������������������������������������������������\n������\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |265", "t": "Lok Adalat in Karnal Region", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†265 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 3rdFEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nLok Adalat in Karnal Region \n \n†265.SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to organize Lok Adalat which works as an important \nalternative dispute resolution mechanism available to the common people at block and \ndistrict level in Karnal region; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether such initiatives are likely to be undertaken there in order to resolve and settle \ndisputes/ cases lying pending in the court of law amicably at the pre-litigation state; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to set up e -seva Kendra in the district court in \ncoordination with the State Government with the main objective to facilitate litigants as \nwell as lawyers in their services that are useful for functioning of the  courts of the \ncountry; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW & JUSTICE \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) and (b) Yes sir, LokAdalat is an important alternate dispute redressal system for the \ncommon people of the country including Karnal region of Haryana state. Haryana State \nLegal Services Authority (HSLSA ) has leveraged the mechanism of ‘LokAdalat’ as an \neffective alternative dispute resolution method for ensuring quick and final consensual \ndisposal of cases binding on the parties/common people, without incurring any extra cost \nor fees. \n HSLSA is regularly  organizing following LokAdalats in all the 22 Districts \nCourts and 33 Sub Divisions of Haryana including Karnal region from time to time. \n\n Daily LokAdalat: Every court of the Sessions Division, after court hours, gets converted \ninto Daily LokAdalat  and judicial officers hold sittings for this, depending upon the \nworkload of cases coming for settlement every day in each court, which also includes \nKarnal region.   \n Jail LokAdalats  are conducted on 1 st and 3 rd Wednesday in all the Jail premises of \nHaryana including Karnal region.   \n National LokAdalats  as per schedule circulated by NALSA are being held on regular \nbasis.  \n In addition to the above, on the initiative of HSLSA, the Haryana Government \nsanctioned 15 Permanent LokAdalats pertaining to public ut ility services including \nKarnal region having jurisdiction for 21 Districts of Haryana exc ept District Charkhi  \nDadri. In these Permanent LokAdalats, cases relating to Public Utility Services, in which \ndisputes up to the value of Rs. one crore are taken up at the Pre-litigative stage and settled \nwithout any expenditure at the earliest, on the basis of conciliation. In case, conciliation \nfails, the PLA has the power to decide special category of cases on merits on the basis of \nequity, justice and good conscience, without going into legal technicalities. \n The details regarding total number of category -wise cases taken up and settled in \nDaily LokAdalats, Jail LokAdalats and National LokAdalats as well as Permanent \nLokAdalat for Public Utility Ser vices in Distric t Karnal  up to 31.12 .2022 are at \nAnnexure– A. \n(c) Cases/ disputes are being resolved or compromised on both stages i.e. at pre -litigation or \nat adjudicating stage in harmonious manner in the LokAdalats . The matter under section \n138 of Negotiable Instrument Act, matrimonial and all civil matters are being taken up at \ninitial stage for amicable settlement. Whereas compoundable matters are taken up at \nadjudication stage in LokAda lat for compromise/ settlem ent. If the matter is \n\ncompromised, the same is disposed of and in case no settlement is arrived, then the \nparties can approach regular court for remedy of their grievances. \n(d) and (e) eSewa Kendras have been rolled out to bridge the digital divide by providin g \neFiling services to lawyers and litigants. Covering all High Courts and one District Court \nas pilot project, it is being expanded to cover all court complexes. In Phase -II of e-Courts \nMission Mode Project, Government has released Rs. 12.54 crore for sett ing up of eSewa  \nKendras. The eSewa  Kendras are being set up at the entry point of the court complexes \nwith the intention of facilitating the lawyer or litigant who needs any kind of assistance \nranging from information to facilitation and e-filing. For setting up the eSewa Kendras in \nNorth-East region, all the Court Complexes have been taken for calculation purpose. All \nHigh Court, Benches and one District Court Complex has been taken for the rest of India. \nAs on 31.12.2022, 689 eSewa Kendras have been made functional under 25 High Courts. \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure – A \nStatement as referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred  Question No. †265 for \nanswering on 03.02.2023  raised by Shri Sanjay Bhatia, MP - LokAdalat in Karnal \nRegion \nStatement containing details of category -wise cases taken up and settled in Daily \nLokAdalats, Jail LokAdalats and National LokAdala"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |266", "t": "Representation of Women in Courts", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW & JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n  \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †266 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 03RD FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nREPRESENTATION OF WOMEN IN COURTS \n \n†266. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAY BHATT : \n  \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government is considering to increase the number of women in the \ncourts of the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken in this direction till date; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (c): Appointment of judges to  the Supreme Court and  the High Courts is done \nunder provisions of Articles 124, 217 and 224 of the Constitution of India which do not \nprovide for reservation for any caste or class of persons. As per Memorandums of \nProcedure for Appointment of Judges to the Supreme Court and High Courts, initiation \nof proposal for appointment of  Judges in the Supreme Court vests with the Chief Justice \nof India, while initiation of proposal for appointment of Judges in the High Courts vests \nwith the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court. All the names recommended by High \nCourt Collegium are se nt with the views of the Government to the Supreme Court \nCollegium (SCC) for advice.    \n \n\nHowever, the Government has been requesting the Chief Justices of High Courts \nthat while sending proposals for appointment of Judges, due consideration be given to \nsuitable candidates belonging to Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Other Backward \nClasses, Minorities and Women to ensure social diversity in appointment of Judges in \nHigh Courts.  Government appoints only those persons as Judges of Supreme Court and \nHigh Courts who are recommended by SCC. \n \nIn case of District and S ubordinate judiciary, as per proviso to Article 309 read \nwith Articles 233 and 234 of the C onstitution, the respective State Government s, in \nconsultation with their High Courts, frame the rules an d regulations regarding the issue \nof appointment, promotion and  reservations of Judicial Officers in the State Judicial \nService.  Hence, in so far as recruitment  or reservation of judicial officers in the States is \nconcerned, the Union Government has no role under the Constitution in the selection and \nappointment of judicial officers in District and Subordinate judiciary \n \n However, the Government remains committed to social diversity in the \nappointment of Judges in the Higher Judiciary and has been reque sting the Chief Justices \nof High Courts that while sending proposals for appointment of Judges, due \nconsideration be given to suitable candidates belonging to Scheduled Castes, Scheduled \nTribes, Other Backward Classes, Minorities and Women to ensure social  diversity in \nappointment of Judges in High Courts.   \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |267", "t": "Digitization of Courts", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *267 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.03.2023 \n \nHoollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary \n \n*267. SHRI TOPON KUMAR GOGOI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE BE pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Hoollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary, Jorhat is divided in two parts by the \nrailway track and whether it is a fact that Gibbons never come down on the earth \nfrom the trees since gibbons are living in two parts and they are monogamous and \nunable to mate within family lines and if so, the steps taken by the Government in \nthis regard; \n(b) whether the Government has any plan so that the Gibbons can easily access both \nparts of the sanctuary and to protect the rare ape which are going to be lost in few \nyears and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a), (b)and (c)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a), (b) and (c) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *267 REGARDING HOOLLONGAPAR GIBBON \nSANCTUARYBY SHRI TOPON KUMAR GOGOI DUE FOR REPLY ON 20.03.2023 \n \n(a) and (b)  As per the information received from the State of Assam, a railway line passes \nthrough the Hoollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary in Jorhat. Hoolock Gibbons are \narboreal species and dwell on trees. However, there are records of Gibbons \ncrossing the railway track on foot.  Hoolock Gibbons are monogamous and avoid \nmating within family lines. Linear plantations along both sides of the railway \ntrack were carried out by the Assam Forest Department and it was observed that \nthe Gibbons cross over the railway tracks using trees planted on either side of the \ntrack.  \n \nThe Ministry of Environment Forest and Climate Change has sanctioned funds \nunder the Centrally Sponsored Scheme: ‘Development of Wildlife Habitats’ for \nmanagement of Hoollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary, including for installation of \nCanopy bridge for facilitating the movement of Hoolock Gibbons. \n \n(c) In view of (b) above, question does not arise. \n \n  \n*** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |268", "t": "Construction of Waterways", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.268 \nANSWERED ON 20/03/2023 \n \nMigration of Youth for Higher Education \n \n*268. SHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note that a large majority of the youth in Odisha migrate to the \nState capital or other cities in search of higher education as the Kandhamal region does not have \nquality educational infrastructure; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the steps taken or likely to be taken by the Government in this regard; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering to establish a University that will focus on research for \ntribal studies, language, history and culture considering almost 24 percent of Odisha’s population \nis tribal and Odisha has 13 Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (e):   A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (E) OF LOK SABH A STARRED QUESTION NO. \n268 ANSWERED ON 20.0 3.2023 REGARDING “MI GRATION OF YOUTH FOR  HIGHER EDUCATION” \nASKED BY HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT SHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA \n(a) to (c):        Enrolment in Higher education after schooling, is determined not only by geographical \nproximity, but also guided by other factors such as desire of students for learning from best \nteachers& researchers, exposure to diverse perspectives &  culture, career pathways, employment \nopportunities, etc.  \n \nIn the Kan dhamal District of Odisha, as per AISHE Report  first year students’ enrolment in Higher \nEducation during the year 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21 is 4001, 4163 and 4663 respectively. Also, \n30 Higher Education Institutions are imparting Higher Education, in the district. \n \nNational Education Policy 2020 provides for more HEIs to be established and developed in \nunderserved regions to ensure full access, equity, and inclusion.  Education being subject matter of \nconcurrent list, it is joint responsibility of Centr e as well as State Government to take action for \nimplementation of NEP 2020. \n \nThe Government is committed to provide quality education to all from pre -primary to higher \neducation level. Accordingly, to provide quality education in the State of Odisha, the Government \nhas established Central University of Odisha, IIT at Bhubneshwar, IIM at Sambalpur, NIT at Rourkela \nand IISER at Berhampur. Besides, The Government has taken initiatives  to expand access of quality \neducation to the remotest area of the country t hrough online and ODL programmes in more Higher \nEducation Institutions and also through 34 SWAYAM Prabha DTH TV channels transmitting \neducation contents 24/7. \n \n(d) and (e): The Central University of Odisha has been established in 2009 in Koraput, an area w ith \nsignificant tribal population.  The objective of the University is to develop it as Institution of Higher \nLearning with aim to strike a balance between teaching scholarship and commitment to address \nsociety’s educational, economic and cultural challeng es.  Further, the university provides avenues \nof education viz. especially higher education and research facilities for the tribal population of India \nincluding Odisha. \n \nThe University focuses on research on health and nutrition of tribals, livelihood, dev elopment and \ndisplacement, tribal rights, impact of modernization on tribal culture and society as well as gender \nand environment issues etc. \n \nThere are two Central Tribal Universities in the country namely Indira Gandhi National Tribal \nUniversity (IGNTU), Amarkantak, Madhya Pradesh and Central Tribal University of Andhra Pradesh \n(CTUAP), Vizianagram, Andhra Pradesh, which provide avenues of higher education and research \nfacilities to the tribal students of the country. \n \nSetting up of a Central University is a continuous process and is setup as and when need arises. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |269", "t": "Deaths due to Cardiovascular Diseases", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 269 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.03.2023 \n \nDATABASE OF MIGRANT LABOURERS \n \n†*269. SHRIMATI REKHA VERMA:  \n \n   Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government maintains a separate database of migrant \nlabourers through registration on e -Shram Portal, if so, the details \nthereof regarding Uttar Pradesh; \n(b)whether the Government has implemented any plan to ensure reach \nof social security sche mes to the migrant labourers at their \ndestinations, if so, the details thereof regarding Uttar Pradesh; \n(c)whether the Government has conducted any monitoring evaluation \nto assess the reach of benefits to the registered labourers through \nthe e -Shram Portal , if so, the details thereof regarding Uttar \nPradesh; and \n(d)if not, whether the Government proposes to assess its \neffectiveness? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                  ****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED  TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d ) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 269 TO BE AN SWERED ON \n20.03.2023 REGARDING DATABASE OF MIGRANT LABOURERS BY \nSHRIMATI REKHA VERMA.  \n \n \n(a)  to (d): Ministry of Labour & Employment launched eShram portal \non 26.08.2021 to create a comprehensive National database of \nunorganised/migrant workers. The registration on eShram is on self -\ndeclaration basis for all the unorganised/migrant workers of the \nCountry. As on 14.03.2023, over 28.66 crore unorganised workers have \nregistered on eShram portal out of which about 8.30 crore are from \nUttar Pradesh. \nIn order to facilitate benefit of social security schemes to \nthe eShram registrants, eShram portal is integ rated with Pradhan \nMantri Shram-Yogi Maandhan (PM-SYM) portal. eShram registrants can \nseamlessly take benefits of PMSYM pension scheme through this \nintegration. The eShram portal is also integrated with National Career \nService (NCS) Portal. The eShram regi strants can seamlessly register \non NCS portal and search for suitable job opportunities.  \nThe data sharing of eShram with the State/ UT \nGovernments has been initiated which will facilitate targeted delivery \nand monitoring of the social security/welfare sch emes implemented by \nthe respective State/UT Governments. Further, data sharing with Uttar \nPradesh has also been started.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |27", "t": "Solar Parks Sanctioned in Andhra Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 27 \nANSWERED ON 02.02.2023 \n \nSOLAR PARKS SANCTIONED IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n27.SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI \n     SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that five solar parks of 4,200 MW capacity are sanctioned in Andhra \nPradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the details of the commissioned capacity of each of the said parks; \n(d) whether it is also true that some of the said parks have been fully commissioned; \n(e) if so, the details along with the generation capacity thereof; and \n(f) the time by which the remaining parks are likely to be commissioned? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (c) The Government has approved 5 solar parks of 4100 MW capacity in Andhra Pradesh \nunder the scheme for Development of Solar Parks. The details of solar park capacity approved \nand the projects commissioned therein are given below:  \nSolar Park Approved \nCapacity \n(in MW)  \nCapacity \nCommissio\nned (in \nMW) \nAnanthapuramu-I Solar \nPark 1400  1400 \nKurnool Solar Park 1000 1000 \nKadapa Solar Park 1000 250 \nAnanthapuramu-II Solar \nPark 500 400 \nSolar Wind Hybrid Park 200 0 \nTOTAL 4100 3050 \n \n(d)&(e) Yes, Sir. Two solar parks namely Ananthapuramu-I Solar Park and Kurnool Solar Park \nhave been fully commissioned for generation capacity of 2400 MW solar power.  \n(f)The solar park scheme has been extended up to March, 2024 for completion of the parks. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |270", "t": "Shortage of Female Health Workers in CHCs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 270 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 3rd FEBRURARY, 2023 \n \nSHORTAGE OF FEMALE HEALTH WORKERS IN CHCs \n \n270. DR. PON GAUTHAM SIGAMANI : \n    \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is a huge shortage of female health workers and auxiliary \nnursing midwives in Community Health Centres (CHCs) in the country and if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(b) whether it is also true that nearly 15 percent of these posts are lying vacant and unfilled in \nsuch Centres and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) whether the Government has taken any action to fill up these vacancies and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND \nFAMILY WELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n  \n(a) to (c): All the administrative and personnel matters related to health human resource lies with \nthe respective State/ UT Governments. Under National Health Mission, Minis try of Health & \nFamily Welfare provides financial and technical support to States/ UTs to strengthen  their \nhealthcare systems, including provision of human resources,  based on the requirements posed by \nthem in their Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs) within their overall resource envelope.  \nAs per Rural Health Statistics 2021-22, the details of female health workers and ANMs in \nSub-Centres and P HCs and Nursing Staff at PHCs and CHCs  in the country are available at \nwebsite of Ministry of Health and Family Welfare at the Uniform Resource Locator (URL) as \nunder:  \nhttps://main.mohfw.gov.in/sites/default/files/RHS%202021%2022.pdf . \n\nShortage of health professionals in PHCs/CHCs varies from State to State depending \nupon their policies and context. States/UTs are advised to put in place transparent policies of \nposting and transfer, and ensure rational deployment  of health professionals. As the posts \nrequired for health facilities are filled up by respective State/UT Governments, they are \nimpressed upon to fill up the vacant posts.  \nUnder NHM, following types of incentives and honorarium are provided for encouragi ng \nHealth Worker and ANM in the country: \n Incentives for ANM for ensuring regular ANC checkup and recording, incentives for \nconducting Adolescent Reproductive and Sexual Health activities.  \n States are also allowed to offer negotiable salary to attract speci alist including flexibility \nin strategies such as “You Quote We Pay”.  \n Non-Monetary incentives such as preferential admission in post graduate courses for staff \nserving in difficult areas and improving accommodation arrangement in rural areas have \nalso been introduced under NHM.  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |271", "t": "Internal Complaint Committee", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 271 \nANSWERED ON 20TH MARCH, 2023 \n \nTeachers involved in Non-Academic Activities \n \n*271. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has conducted any  study to assess the amount of time that \nteachers of Government schools have to spend annually on non-academic activities like meals \npreparation, election  duties, surveys, data collection and implementation  of Government \nprogrammes; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the study shows that the time spent on  such activities by the teachers is \nexcessively high; and \n(d) if so, the measures proposed to be  taken by the Government to reduce the burden of such \nnon-academic activities on the teachers? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n****** \n  \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 271 FOR REPLY ON 20 TH MARCH, 2023 ASKED BY \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL  REGARDING \nTEACHERS INVOLVED IN NON-ACADEMIC ACTIVITIES: \n \n \n(a) to (d): The provisions of the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) \nAct 2009, as amended from time to time as well as the National Education Policy 2020 \nmandate that teachers, to the extent possible, should not be deployed for non-academic duties. \n  \n Section 27 of the Right o f Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009 \nstates that “ No teacher shall be deployed for any non -educational purposes other than the \ndecennial population census, disaster relief duties or duties relating to elections to the local \nauthority or the State Legislatures or Parliament, as the case may be .” Also, NEP 2020 in \npara 5.12 emphasizes the need to prevent the large amounts of time spent by teachers in non -\nteaching activities and they are not to be engaged in work that is not directly rel ated to \nteaching, in particular, strenuous administrative tasks and mid -day meal-related work, so that \nteachers fully concentrate on their teaching-learning duties.  \n \n Education is in the Concurrent List of the Constitution and a majority of the schools ar e \nunder the administrative control of the respective States and Union Territories (UTs). The \nrecruitment, service conditions and deployment of teachers come under the purview of the \nrespective State/ Union Territory (UT) Governments.  The Ministry of Education has issued \nguidelines to all States and UTs that emphasize that teachers should not be deployed for non-\neducational duties other than those which are in accordance with the RTE Act, 2009.  \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |272", "t": "Remote Voting Machine", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�������������������������� �\n�\n������������������ ��� ��� ������������������� �������\n�\n��������������������� �\n�\n����� �������������������� �\n������������������������� �\n������������������ �\n������������������ �\n�\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� �������� ���� ��������� ����������� ��� ������ ������ ���� ���������� ��\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ��������� ��� ���������� ��� ���� ���� ��������� ��������� ��� ���� ��������\n���������������������������������\n����� �������� ���� ��������� ����� ��������� ��� ������� ��������� ���������������\n�������������������������������������������������������\n����� �������� ���� ���� ���� ������� ��� ���������� �������� ��� ������������ ����\n�������� ���� ���� ���� ��������� �������� ������� ���� ���������� ������ ���\n����������� ���� �������� ��� ������������� ��������� ���������� ���� �������\n���������\n����� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������\n����� �������� ���� ���������������� ���� ������ ��������� ����� ������������� ����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������\n����� ����������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n����������