|11|UNSTARRED |512", "t": "Identification of Tourist Places in NE States", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.512 \n ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nIDENTIFICATION OF TOURIST PLACES IN NE STATES \n \n 512. SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH: \n \n  Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of place s in North Eastern States including Assam \nidentified as tourist sites and the number of places for which \nproposals have been received to be included in the list of tourist \nsites along with the State-wise details thereof; \n(b) the funds allocated by the Gove rnment for the development of \nthe said sites during the last three years and the current year; \n(c) the project -wise and year -wise details of the funds utilized and \nremaining out of the funds allocated by the Government; and \n(d) the number of projects which  have been completed out of the \nsaid projects along with the locations-wise details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                            (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (d): Identification of tourist sites is primarily responsibility of \nthe respective States/ Union Territories. State wise details of funds \nallocated, project wise and year wise, with utilization and completion \nduring the last three years and current year to various North Eastern \nStates including Assam under various schemes of the Ministry of \nTourism is at Annexure. \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d ) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 512 ANSWERED \nON 06.02.2023 REGARDING IDENTIFICATION OF TOURIST PLACES IN NE STATES. \n \nProject sanctioned under Swadesh Darshan Scheme in North Eastern States \n(Rs. in Crore) \nS.no. States Sanctioned \nProjects \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \nAmount \nUtilized Amount \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 2 146.49 139.16 124.05 \n2 Assam 2 185.66 176.36 175.95 \n3 Manipur 2 117.57 104.36 104.16 \n4 Meghalaya 2 184.10 166.36 145.12 \n5 Mizoram 2 158.63 141.79 139.77 \n6 Nagaland 2 195.50 185.73 185.73 \n7 Sikkim 2 193.37 187.96 177.95 \n8 Tripura 2 127.68 91.67 79.72 \n TOTAL 16 1309.00 1193.39 1132.45 \n \n1. Arunachal Pradesh             (Rs. in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nYear Name of the Project Swadesh Darshan \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmount \nutilised \nStatus \n1       2014-15 Development of Bhalukpong \nBomdila Tawang Circuit under \nNorth East Theme of Swadesh \nDarshan  \n02 49.77 47.28 39.81 Completed  \n2 2015-16 Integrated Development of New 96.72 91.88 84.24 Completed  \n\nAdventure Tourism in Arunachal \nPradesh under North East \nCircuit of Swadesh Darshan. \n  Total 02 146.49 139.16 124.05  \n \n2. Assam              (Rs. in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nYear Name of the Project Swadesh Darshan \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmount \nutilised \nStatus \n1       2015-16 ‘Development of Wildlife \nCircuit: Manas - Pobitora - \nNameri - Kaziranga - Dibru \nSaikhowa in Assam’ under \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme. \n02 94.68 89.94 89.53 Completed  \n2 2016-17 Development of Tezpur – \nMajuli – Sibsagar as Heritage \nCircuit in Assam’ under \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme. \n90.98 86.42 86.42 Completed  \n  Total 02 185.66 176.36 175.95  \n \n3. Manipur             (Rs. in crore) \nS. No. Year Name of the Project Swadesh Darshan \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmount \nutilised \nStatus  \n1       2015-16 Development of Tourist Circuit \nin Manipur: Imphal - Khongjom \nunder North East Circuit of \nSwadesh Darshan scheme’ \n02 72.23 61.32 61.25 Completed  \n\n2 2016-17 Development Of Spiritual \nCircuit in Manipur - Shri Shri \nGovindajee Temple, Shri Shri \nBijoy Govindajee Temple - Shri \nGopinath Temple - Shri \nBunshibodon Temple - Shri \nKaina Temple  \n45.34 43.04 42.91 Completed  \n  Total 02 117.57 104.36 104.16  \n \n4. Meghalaya              (Rs. in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nYear Name of the Project Swadesh Darshan \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmount \nutilised \nStatus \n1       2016-17 Development of Umiam (Lake \nView) Ulum Sohpetbneng -\nMawdiandiang-Orchid Lake \nResort \n02 99.13 94.14 94.14 Completed  \n2 2018-19 Development of North East \nCircuit: West Khasi Hills \n(Nongkhlaw- Krem Tirot – \nKhudoi & Kohmang Falls – Khri \nRiver- Mawthadraishan, \nShillong), Jaintia Hills \n(KrangSuri Falls - Shyrmang- \nIooksi), Garo Hills (Nokrek \nReserve, KattaBeel, Siju \nCaves) \n84.97 72.22 50.98 Ongoing  \n  Total 02 184.10 166.36 145.12  \n \n\n5. Mizoram               (Rs. in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nYear Name of the Project Swadesh Darshan \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmt. \nSanctioned \nAmount \nreleased \nAmount \nutilised \nStatus \n1       2015-\n16 \nIntegrated Development of New Eco \nTourism under Swadesh Darshan – \nNorth East Circuit at Thenzawl & South \nZote, District Serchhip and Reiek \n02  92.26 92.26 90.24 Completed  \n2 2016-\n17 \n“Development of Eco -Adventure Circuit \nAizawl - Rawpuichhip – Khawhphawp – \nLengpui-Chatlang Sakawrhmuituaitlang \n-Muthee – Beratlawng -Tuirial Airfield – \nHmuifang  \n 66.37 49.53 49.53 Ongoing  \n  Total 02 158.63 141.79 139.77  \n \n6. Nagaland               ( Rs. in crore) \n S. \nNo. \nY"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |513", "t": "Skill Training under NSDM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 513 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n  \nSKILL TRAINING UNDER NSDM \n  \n513. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU:                SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE: \n        SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT:  DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \n  \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Union Government has launched National Skill Development Mission \n(NSDM) for creating convergence across various sectors and States in terms of activities \nrelating to skill training; \n \n(b) if so, whether the NSDM has fixed any targets for skill trainings; \n \n(c) if  so, the details thereof and the extent to which those targets have so far been \nachieved, State-wise particularly in Odisha; \n \n(d) whether there is a need to support weaker and disadvantaged sections of society \nthrough focused outreach programmes in the country; \n \n(e) if so, the details in this regard; \n \n(f) the details of the steps taken by the Union Government to ensuring high-quality training \nstandards in various States particularly in Odisha; and \n \n(g) the other measures taken by the Union Government to achieve the goal set under the \nNSDM? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n  \n(a)       The National Skill Development Mission  (NSDM) was launched by the Hon’ble \nPrime Minister on 15th July, 2015, to provide a strong institutional framework to \nimplement and scale up skill development efforts across the country. Promotion of \nconvergence and co-ordination between skill development efforts of all Central Ministries/ \nDepartments/ States/ implementing agencies is one of the objectives of NSDM.  \n  \n(b) to (e)          Under Skill India Mission, the Ministry of Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship (MSDE) delivers skill training through a comprehensive network of skill \n\ndevelopment centres/ institutes across the country under various schemes viz. Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY), Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) Scheme, National \nApprenticeship Promotion Scheme (NAPS) and Craftsman Tra ining Scheme (CTS) \nthrough Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs). All the Skilling schemes of MSDE are purely \ndemand-driven and joining such courses is a voluntary decision on the part of the potential \ntrainee. While it is a fact that no target can be fixed as such for compulsory enrolment of \nskilling aspirants belonging to the disadvantaged sections under any of the skilling \nschemes, it has been the endeavour of the Government to ensure that opportunity for \nskill training is provided to persons belonging t o these sections through appropriate \nprovisions in budget for the purpose in relevant schemes.   Objectives of NSDM also \ninclude support to weaker and disadvantaged sections of society through focused \noutreach programmes and targeted skill development activ ities through the Skill \nDevelopment Schemes of MSDE.    \n  \n        State-wise total number of candidates trained under schemes of MSDE including in \nthe State of Odisha from 2018-19 till December, 2022 is at Annexure-I. \n  \n(f) and (g)        In order to meet s killing requirement for future workforce, improve the \nquality of skilling and align the training programs with the changes in the economy and \ntechnology to meet the needs of the industry, following steps have been taken by Ministry \nof Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE):  \n \ni. Expansion in infrastructure of Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) through new ITIs \nin Public and Private Sectors. \nii. Upgradation and modernization of ITIs in terms of their infrastructure, pedagogy, \ncurriculum and technology interventions. \niii. Introduction of new -age courses in Internet of Things (IoT), Renewable Energy, \nAdditive Manufacturing Technology (3 -D Printing), Mechatronics, Drone \nTechnology etc in ITIs. \niv. Launching of the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY), for im parting \nshort-term skill training to the youth. \nv. Implementation of JSS to impart Vocational Skills to non-literates, neo-literates and \nthe persons having rudimentary level of education upto   8th standard and school \ndropouts upto 12th standard in the age group of 15-45 years. \nvi. Notification of common norms for bringing about uniformity and standardization in \nimplementation of various Skill Development schemes by different Central \nMinistries/ Departments.  \nvii. Setting up of Sector Skill Councils (SSCs) as industry -led bodies to develop \nNational Occupation Standards (NOSs). \nviii. Implementation of Skills Acquisition and Knowledge Awareness for Livelihood \nPromotion (SANKALP), an outcome -oriented World Bank supported project for \nstrengthening institutional mechanisms at both national and state levels.  \nix. Implementation of Skills Strengthening for Industrial Value Enhancement \n(STRIVE) scheme, a World Bank assisted project"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |514", "t": "Constitution of Committee for Scientific Preservation of Ancient Culture", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 514 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nCONSTITUTION OF COMMITTEE FOR SCIENTIFIC PRESERVATION OF \nANCIENT CULTURE \n \n514. SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the steps taken by the Government for preservation of folk art, folk dance, folk music \nand folk literature in the country; \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to constitute a Committee in this regard for scientific \npreservation and popularising country’s ancient culture, literature, folk music, folk \nliterature and folk art for coming generations; and \n(c)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH  \nEASTERN REGION  \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) For preservation of folk art, folk dance and folk music in the country, the Government \nof India has set up seven (7) Zonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs) with headquarters at Patiala, \nNagpur, Udaipur, Prayagraj, Kolkata, Dimapur and Thanjavur. These ZCCs organize various \ncultural activities and programmes in their member States throughout the year on regular \nbasis for which annual grant-in-aid is provided to them. Folk artists are engaged by these \nZCCs to showcase their talents in these cultural programmes/activities for which they are \npaid honorarium, DA/TA, boarding & lodging etc. Besides, number of schemes are being \nimplemented by these ZCCs for preservation of various art forms which may be seen at \nAnnexure. \n  \n For preservation of folk literature, Sahitya Akademi, an autonomous organization \nunder Ministry of Culture has been organizing a programme called “Loka: The Many \nVoices”, consisting of lectures as well as demonstrations etc., seminars, symposia on folk and \ntribal literature across the country. Number of significant publications in this regard have also \nbeen brought out. Besides, the Akademi has constituted a series of programmes titled \nGramalok which celebrates literature. The series of programmes such as Literary Forum are \nalso conducted in rural areas.  \n \n(b) & (c) No such proposal is under the consideration of the Government. \n \n  \n***** \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE \n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 514 \nfor 06.02.2023 \n \n \nSchemes being implemented by Zonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs) \n \n \ni. Award to Young Talented Artistes: The Scheme “Young Talented Artists” is carried \nout to encourage and recognize the young talents especially in the field of rare art forms. \nTalented youngsters of the age group of 18-30 years are selected and given a onetime  cash \naward of Rs. 10,000/-.  \n \nii. Guru Shishya Parampara: This scheme envisages transmitting our valued traditions \nto the coming generations. Disciples are trained under veterans in art forms which are rare and \nvanishing. Rare and vanishing art forms of the region are identified and eminent exponents \nare selected to carry out the training programmes in ‘Gurukula’ tradition. The monthly \nremuneration for Guru – Rs. 7,500/-, Accompanist – Rs. 3,750/- and Pupils - Rs. 1,500/- each \nfor the period of six month to maximum 1 year for one scheme. The names of the Gurus are \nrecommended by the State Cultural Affairs Departments. \n \niii. Theatre Rejuvenation:  To promote theatre activities including stage shows and \nProduction oriented workshops, etc. Honorarium Up to Rs. 30,000/- per show excluding TA \n& DA is paid. The groups finalized on the basis their credentials as well as the merit of \nproject submitted by them.    \n \niv. Research & Documentation:  To preserve promote and propagate vanishing visual \nand performing art forms including folk, tribal and classical in the field of music, dance, \ntheatre, literature, fine arts etc. in print/ audio – visual media. The art form is finalized in \nconsultation with state Cultural Department.  \n \nv. Shilpgram: To promote folk and tribal art and crafts of the zone by organizing \nseminar, workshops, exhibitions, craft fairs, design development and marketing support to the \nartisans living in the rural areas. \n \nvi. Octave:   To promote and propagate the rich cultural heritage of North East region \ncomprising of eight States namely Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Sikkim, \nNagaland, Manipur and Tripura to the rest of India.   \n \nvii. National Cultural Exchange Programme:  It can be termed as the lifeline of the \nZonal Cultural Centers. Under this scheme, various festivals of performing arts, exhibitions, \nyatras etc are organized in member States. Artists from other zones/states are invited to \nparticipate in these programmes. Participation of artists from the Zone in festivals held in \nother parts of the country are also facilitated. Zonal centres also participate in Major festivals \nhappening in member States by arranging performances during these festivals where large \nnumber of audience get chance to enjoy and understand art forms of o"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |515", "t": "Social Security Coverage", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 515 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSOCIAL SECURITY COVERAGE \n \n515.  SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \n  DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \n  SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: \n  SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY: \n  SHRI RAMSHIROMANI VERMA: \n  SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Union Government is contemplating to expand the \nsocial security coverage for unorganised workers during 2023, if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(b)whether the Union Government has requested the State \nGovernments to make rules for labour codes to strengthen the \ncountry’s labour market; \n(c)if so, the details of the response of the State Governments thereon,  \nState-wise, particularly Maharashtra and Odisha; \n(d)the other steps taken or proposed to be taken to address the \nissues/problems of global skill gaps, gig and platform economy, \nsustainable financing of social security schemes and other related \nissues;  \n(e)whether the Union Government has launched schemes to provide \nthe social security to labourers in unorganised sectors during the \nlast five years, if so, the details thereof; and \n(f)the details of the funds released for the implementation of the said \nschemes and the number of unorganised workers benefitted from \nabove said schemes during the last five years, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Government has notified Code on Social Security 2020 \nby amalgamating, simplifying and rationalizing the  relevant provisions  \nContd..2/- \n \n\n:: 2 :: \nof the existing 8 Central labour  Acts relating to social security for \norganized as well as unorganized workers which aims towards \nexpansion of coverage of social security. The Government of India has \nrequested the State Governments to make rules on Code on Social \nSecurity. The State of M aharashtra and Odisha have already published \nthe draft rules under the said code. \n \n(d):  For the first time, the definition of ‘gig worker’ and ‘platform \nworker’ has been provided in the Code on Social security, 2020. The \nCode on Social Security, 2020 prov ides for gig workers and platform \nworkers on matters relating to life and disability cover, accident \ninsurance, health and maternity benefits, old age protection, etc. The \nCode on Social Security, 2020 also provides for setting up a Social \nSecurity Fund an d one of the sources of fund, is contribution from \naggregator between 1 to 2% of annual turnover of an aggregator \nsubject to the limit of 5% of the amount paid or payable by an \naggregator to such workers. However, the Code is yet to come into \nforce. Sector  Skill Councils have been set up to create occupational \nstandards, develop competency framework, train the trainers, conduct \nskill gap studies to address the problem of skill gaps. \n \n(e) & (f): (i) Government has launched Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti \nBima Yojana (PMJJBY) and Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana \n(PMSBY) to provide the life and disability cover to the workers \namongst others. As on 31.12.2022, a total of 14.82 crore beneficiaries \nhave been enrolled under the PMJJBY and a total of 31.88  crore \nbeneficiaries have been enrolled under the PMSBY. \nii)  The Ayushman Bharat -Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana \n(ABPMJAY) has been launched to provide an annual health cover of Rs. \n5 lakhs per eligible family for secondary and tertiary care \nhospitalization corresponding to 1949 treatment procedures across 27 \nspecialties. As on 4 th December, 2022, a total of 20.96 crore \nbeneficiaries have been provided with the Ayushman Cards. \n \n(iii)  In order to provide old age protection, the Government of India \nlaunched Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan -Dhan (PM -SYM) pension \nscheme in  February, 2019. It provides monthly pension of Rs.3000/ - \nafter attaining the age of 60 years. The workers in the age group of 18 -\n40 years  whose  monthly  income   is Rs. 15000/-  or   less  and  not   a \nContd..3/- \n\n:: 3 :: \nmember of EPFO/ESIC/NPS (Govt. funded) can join the PM -SYM \nScheme. Under this scheme 50% monthly contribution is payable by \nthe beneficiary and equal matching contribution is paid by the Central \nGovernment. Under the scheme, t he funds towards Government’s \ncontribution is provided to LIC being the fund manager. As on \n31.12.2022, the State/UT wise registration under the scheme are at \nAnnexure. \nThese schemes are demand driven and accordingly the \nbudget is allocated. \nApart from abo ve, other schemes such as Public \nDistribution System through One Nation One Ration Card scheme \nunder National Food Security Act, Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Act, Deen Dayal Upadhyay Gramin Kaushal \nYojana, Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana, Gareeb Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyan, \nMahatma Gandhi Bunkar Bima Yojana, Deen Dayal Antyodaya Yojana, \nPMSVANidhi, Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana etc. are also "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |516", "t": "Pollution Control Research Institute", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 516 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPollution Control Research Institute \n \n516.       SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Pollution Control Research Institute has carried out/proposed to carry out \nin-depth study in respect of increasing pollution in Yamuna, Ganga, Saraswati and \nother rivers; \n(b) if so, the outcome thereof along with the recommendations made in above study; \n(c) the reaction of the Government thereto; and \n(d) the effective steps being taken by the Government to check the increasing pollution in \nholy rivers? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (d) \nThe Pollution Control Research Institute in Haridwar (Uttarakhand) has informed that they have \nnot carried out any comprehensive study on increasing pollution in major rivers of the country. \n \nCentral Pollution Control Board (CPCB) in association with Pollution Control \nBoards/Committees in different States/Union Territories (UTs), have been monitoring the water \nquality of rivers and other water bodies across the country through a network of monitoring \nstations under the National Water Quality Monitoring Programme (NWQMP). At present, the \nnetwork consists of 4484 locations spread in 28 States and 7 Union Territories including 2108 \nlocations on 719 rivers. Based on water quality monitoring results, pollution assessment of rivers \nis carried out by CPCB from time to time. \n \nCleaning/rejuvenation of rivers is an ongoing activity. It is the responsibility of the \nStates/UTs and local bodies to ensure required treatment of sewage and industrial effluents to the \nprescribed norms before discharging into rivers and other water bodies, coastal waters or land to \nprevent and control of pollution therein.For conservation of rivers, Ministry of Jal Shakti \nsupplements efforts of the States/UTs by providing financial and technical assistance for \n\nabatement of pollution in identified stretches of rivers in the country through the Central Sector \nScheme of Namami Gange for rivers in Ganga basin and the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of \nNational River Conservation Plan (NRCP) for other rivers \n \nAs per the Provisions of Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and Water (Prevention & \nControl of Pollution), Act 1974, industrial units are required to install effluent treatment plants \n(ETPs) and treat their effluents to comply with stipulated environmental standards before \ndischarging into rivers and water bodies. Accordingly, CPCB, State Pollution Control Boards \n(SPCBs) and Pollution Control Committees (PCCs) monitor industries with respect to effluent \ndischarge standards and take action for non-compliance under provisions of these Acts. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |517", "t": "Skill Development in Drone Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  517 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT IN DRONE SECTOR \n \n517:  SHRI VENKATESH NETHABORLAKUNTA:           DR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \n \nSHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU: \n \n Will the Minister of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the re is significant  growth and innovation in drone sector in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, whether the Government is providing skill development in drone sector, \nregarding its installation, operation, maintenance, service, etc., of drones in the \ncountry; \n(c) if so, details thereof;  \n(d) whether it is true that the Government has given permission to some Industrial \nTraining Institutes (ITIs) to start short-term courses on drone;  \n(e) if so, whether it is also true that the Government has not given permission for \nany ITIs in Telangana for short-term courses on drone; and \n(f) if so, the reaso ns therefor and the steps being taken for giving permission for \nITIs in Telangana to start courses? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) Yes Sir. There is significant growth in drone sector in the country. \n(b) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship is running sixshort-term \nskilling courses in drone sector, including \"Drone Service Technician\" and \"Drone \nManufacturing and Assembly\" to meet the requirement of skilled manpower in drone \nsector. \n(c)  Detail of the courses is attached at Annexure-1. \n\n(d) Yes, The Directorate General of Training under the Ministry of Skill \nDevelopment and Entrepreneurship has  given affiliation to 116 ITIs across the \ncountry for conducting short-term courses on \"Drone Service Technician\" and \"Drone \nManufacturing and Assembly\".The details are attached at Annexure 2. \n(e)& (f)  The Directorate General of Training (DGT) has not given affiliation to any ITI \nin Telangana state for running short-term drone courses, as no proposal(s) has been \nreceived from State Skill Development & Entrepreneurship Committee (SSDEC) , \nTelangana. The administration of Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) including start of \nnew courses in ITIs i s State /UT subject matter . Wheneversuch proposals are  \nreceived form State Directorate s, they are examined  and deci sion istaken on \naffiliation proposal as per the affiliation standards and norms and granted affiliation. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 517 to be answered on 06.02.2023. \nThe Short-TermDrone Courses approved under MSDE: \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the Course Duration \n1 Drone Developer (Software) 510 Hrs \n2 Drone Manufacturing and Assembly Technician 390 Hrs \n3 Drone Monitoring and Maintenance Associate  390 Hrs \n4 Drone Operator- Multi Rotor \n \n390 Hrs \n5 Drone Service Technician \n \n400 Hrs \n6 Kisan Drone Operator \n \n390 Hrs \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-2 \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n517 to be answered on 06.02.2023. \nThe Short-Term Drone Course affiliation given from session 2022 as on date: \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSl. No Name of the State/UT No. of ITIs \n1 Assam 02 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 05 \n3 Andhra Pradesh 10 \n4 Bihar 30 \n5 Chandigarh 01 \n6 Gujarat 20 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 09 \n8 Maharashtra 12 \n9 Manipur 01 \n10 Punjab 05 \n11 Uttarakhand  06 \n12 Uttar Pradesh 15 \n Total 116 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |518", "t": "Reduction in Manpower in PSBs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 518 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2023 / MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nReduction in Manpower in PSBs \n \n518.  SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Public Sector Banks (PSBs) in India have reduced the number of its \nemployees; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether this is because of greater push for computerisation of the banking services; \nand  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n(a) to (d): Public Sector Banks (PSBs) are board governed commercial entities. The \nrequirement of manpower in each PSB is determined by the respective PSB keeping \nin view various factors which include, inter-alia, business requirement, spread of \nactivities, superannuation and other unplanned exits. Appointment of officers and staff \nis done accordingly by the PSBs and it varies from year to year based on their \nrequirements. \n \n      \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |519", "t": "Corporate Social Responsibility", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO -† 519 \nANSWERED ON – 06/02/2023 \n  \nCORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY \n \n†519. SHRI ARUN SAO:      SHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n          SHRI MOHAN MANDAVI: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased \nto state:- \n \n(a) the amount of funds spent by the Government undertakings under the control \nof Union Government under Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) for Skill \nDevelopment and Certification programme during the financial years 2019 -20, 2020-\n21, 2021-22; \n \n(b)  the amount of funds spent by these undertakings through Government \norganizations and Non-Governmental Organisations separately; and \n \n(c) the amount -wise provision made for the State of Chhattisgarh by these \nundertakings themselves and by Union Government under the CSR head of these \nGovernment undertakings? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) The broad  framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has \nbeenprovided under section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’) , formulated and \nenacted by Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA) , Government of India . Theeligible \nactivities that can be undertaken by  the companies  including Public Sector \nUndertakings (PSUs) ,as CSR  also includes ‘ livelihood enhancement projects ’ and \n‘vocational skills’. \n \nOn the basis of filling made by the companies in MCA 21registry up to \n30.09.2022, the CSR expenditure made by PSUs on  “Vocational Skills” and \n“Livelihood Enhancement Projects” during financial year 2019 -20 and 2020 -21 are \nas follows: \n \n[Amounts in Cr.] \nDevelopment Sector 2019-20 2020-21 \nVocational Skills 325.55 115.66 \nLivelihood Enhancement Projects 72.01 42.81 \nTotal 397.56 158.47 \n\nSource: National CSR Data Portal. \n \nFurther, the CSR expenditure data for FY 2021 -22 is not available yet as the \ncompanies are required to file CSR details for FY 2021-22 on or before 31.03.2023. \n \n(b) Ministry of Corporate Affairs, Government of India does not maintain such \ntype of data. \n \n(c) On the basis of filling made by the companies in MCA 21registry  up to \n30.09.2022, the CSR expenditure made by PSUs in the State of Chhattisgarh on \n“Vocational Skills” and “Livelihood Enhancement Projects” during financial year \n2019-20 and 2020-21 are as follows \n  \n[Amount in crore] \nDevelopment Sector 2019-20 2020-21 \nVocational Skills 1.48 2.01 \nLivelihood Enhancement \nProjects \n0.15 0.95 \nTotal 1.63 2.96 \nSource: National CSR Data Portal. \n \n Further, the CSR expenditure data for FY 2021 -22 is not available yet as the \ncompanies are required to file CSR details for FY 2021-22 on or before 31.03.2023. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |52", "t": "Violation of Guidelines under AMRUT 2.0", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*52  \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SABARIMALA \n \n*52.  SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is true that Rs.80 crore out of a total of Rs.100 crore \nthat was allotted by the Union Government for the development \nof Sabarimala under Swadesh Darshan Scheme lapsed on \nDecember 31,2022; \n(b)  if so, the reasons leading to this situation along with the details \nof the master plan submitted by the State Government on \nSabarimala and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government to direct the State \nauthorities for the utilisation of the fund before the stipulated \ndeadline; \n(d)  whether the Government is aware that the projects costing \nRs.20 crore as per the master plan of Sabarimala have not been \ncompleted yet; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n\n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*52 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING \nDEVELOPMENT OF SABARIMALA. \n \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned a project \n‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ in \n2016-17 for an amount of Rs.99.99 Cr. The completion date of this \nproject was December, 2022. In addition, the Ministry of Tourism also \nsanctioned a project ‘Development of Sree Padmanabha- Aranmula- \nSabrimala in 2016-17 for Rs.92.44 Cr., which also included \ndevelopment work at Sabarimala. The development work of Rs. 6.10 \nCrore (plus contingency/consultancy) sanctioned at Sabarimala site \nwas dropped from the second project and added to the first project i.e. \n‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’, thus \nelevating the sanctioned cost of this project to Rs.106.42 Cr.  \n \n ‘Development of Sabarimala – Erumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ \nproject was reviewed by the Ministry of Tourism in August, 2022 and \nthe Ministry of Tourism noted no progress in respect of the \ncomponents for Rs.49.10 Crore and thereafter on the request of the \nState Government, the said project was revised in October, 2022 to \nRs.54.88 Crore. Details of the project sanctioned for Rs.106.41 Crore \nand later revised to Rs. 54.88 Crore is tabulated as under:-  \n \nDestination Sanctioned \namount \n(Rs. in Crore) \nRevised sanction \namount  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSabrimala Temple – Erumeli 2.8 2.8 \nPampa 32.97 11.07 \nTrekking Path 26.56 20.23 \nSannidham 32.90 16.01 \nSabrimala (transferred from \nPadmanabha Project) \n6.12 2.16 \nContingency  3.04 1.57 \nConsultancy  2.02 1.04 \nTotal 106.41 54.88 \n \n\n \n \nTimely completion of the sanctioned project and implementation of \nmaster plan is the responsibility of the State Government. However, \nthe Ministry of Tourism regular monitors the progress of the projects \nto ensure its speedy completion. There is a recorded physical \nprogress of 75% in respect of revised ‘Development of Sabarimala – \nErumeli – Pampa – Sannidhanam’ project.  \n \n*******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |520", "t": "Centrally Sponsored Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 520 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nCENTRALLY SPONSORED SCHEMES \n \n520. SHRI SUNIL BABURAO MENDHE : \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of Central assistance to the States under various Centrally \nSponsored Schemes (CSS) for labour during the last three years and the \ncurrent year, year-wise and scheme-wise including Maharashtra; \n(b) the number of labourers who got benefitted  from the above schemes during \nthe said period; and \n(c) the details of help extended by the Union Government in this regard to State \nGovernments to improve the condition of labourers in the country? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to ( c): Ministry of Labour and Employment is implementing two Centrally \nSponsored Scheme Namely National Career Service (NCS) and Coaching and \nGuidance for SC/ST and Other Backward Classes. The central assistance to the \nStates/UTs is provid ed under the NCS scheme only . Funds are released to \nStates/UTs against their proposals for establishment of Model Career Centre under \nthe NCS scheme . Details of the funds released to State/UTs (including \nMaharashtra) under NCS scheme during last three year s and current year are as \nunder: \n                                                                          (in Rs. Lakh) \nFinancial \nyear \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nFunds \nreleased to \nStates/UTs \n(including \nMaharashtra) \n2595.12 896.72 41.30 2338.76 \nFunds \nreleased to \nMaharashtra \n384.74 - - 246.60 \n \n9.27 lakhs candidates were provided Career Counselling/Vocational  \nGuidance/Confidence building /pre recruitment training during the last three years  \nand current year. This has been done in collaboration with the State/UT \nGovernments. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |521", "t": "GIAN Yojana", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 521 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGIAN Yojana \n \n†521.   DR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \nSHRI SHANKAR LALWANI: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has amended the Global Initiative for Academic Network (GIAN) \nYojana to improve the quality of Higher Education; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the amended scheme has the provision enabling Indian teachers to teach in foreign \ninstitutions; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e) whether the Government proposes to introduce any new technique etc. in the field of teaching; \nand \n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes  Sir. Ministry of Education in 2021, in the wake of  COVID led disruption and travel \nrestrictions, had extended the GIAN Scheme with relevant amendments to ensure wider participation \nfrom reputed international faculty and academic continuity . Provisions were made to offer 100 online \ncourses in 2021 and 300 courses for subsequent years, with 50:50 ratio of online and in-person. \n \n(c) & (d) No Sir. There is no provision in GIAN scheme that enable the Indian teachers to teach in \nforeign institutions.  \n \n \n\n \n-2- \n \n(e) & (f) Yes Sir. National Education Policy released in 2020 envisages multiple new techniques in the \nfield of teaching etc.  such as promoting multilingualism and the power of language in teaching and \nlearning, removing language barriers,  focus on regular formative assessment for learning, use of \ntechnology in teaching and learning etc.  \nNEP also emphasizes that Pedagogy must evolve to make education more experiential, holistic, \nintegrated, inquiry -driven, discovery -oriented, learner -centred, discussion-based, flexible, and  \nenjoyable. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |522", "t": "Trade in Domestic Currency", "head": "                                                      GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 522 \n \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, 6th FEBRUARY, 2023/ MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nTRADE IN DOMESTIC CURRENCY \n \n \n522.  SHRI P.C. MOHAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n \na)   whether the Government is proposing for increase in trade in the domestic currency; \nb)   if so, the details thereof; and \nc)   the steps taken by the Government to promote “Rupee trade”? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n(a) to  (c): The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has allowed invoicing and payments for international \ntrade in Indian Rupee vide A.P (DIR Series) Circular No. 10 RBI/2022 -2023/90 dated 11.07.2022 on \n“International Trade Settlement in Indian Rupees (INR)”. The Circular lays down that all exports and \nimports under the arrangement may be denominated and invoiced in Rupee (INR) and the settlement \nof trade transactions under the a rrangement shall take place in INR. RBI has put in place the \narrangement to promote growth of global trade with emphasis on exports from India and to support \nthe increasing interest of global trading community in INR. The framework put in place by RBI is \napplicable for any partner country seeking to undertake trade with India in INR in terms of RBI’s \nCircular dated 11.07.2022. \n \nFurther, the Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) has introduced a provision in the Foreign \nTrade Policy vide Notification N o. 33/2015 -20 dated 16.09.2022, to allow for International Trade \nSettlement in INR i.e., invoicing, payment, and settlement of exports / imports in Indian Rupees in \nsync with RBI’s Circular dated 11.07.2022. Further changes have been introduced in the Fore ign \nTrade Policy vide DGFT’s Notification 43/2015 -20 dated 09.11.2022 and Public Notice 35/2015 -20 \ndated 09.11.2022 for grant of exports benefits and fulfilment of Export Obligation for export \nrealisations in INR as per RBI guidelines. \n \n \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |523", "t": "Showcasing India’s Tourism Potential Abroad", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 523 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSHOWCASING INDIA’S TOURISM POTENTIAL ABROAD \n \n523.  SHRI BHOLANATH (B.P. SAROJ): \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of promotional activities undertaken by the Government \nduring the current year to showcase India’s tourism potential \nabroad;  \n(b) the countries selected for this purpose; \n(c) the new features being highlighted during this exercise; and \n(d) the measures tak en to expand tourism  infrastructure in this \nregard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): Ministry of Tourism promotes India as a holistic destination in \nthe tourism generating markets to promote various Indian tourism \nproducts a nd tourism destinations of the country to increase India’s \nshare of the global tourism market. \n \nThe above objectives  are met through an integrated marketing and \npromotional strategy, and a synergized campaign in association with the \nTravel Trade, State Governments and Indian Missions abroad.  \n \nMinistry of Tourism participated in Dubai Expo from Dec. 31, 2021 to \nJan. 15, 2022. The Tourism Fortnight comprised of various sessions at \nIndia Pavilion which were presided over by representatives from the \nMinistry o f Tourism and various Indian states along with the \nrepresentatives of Travel trade associations. \n \nMinistry of Touris m participated in Arabian Travel Market Dubai, 2022  \nheld from 9th May to 12th May, 2022. \n \n\nThe Ministry of Tourism and various State Tourism department \nparticipated in World Travel Market 2022 (WTM) during 07 th to 09 th  \nNovember, 2022.  \n \nDuring the event th e Ministry of Tourism had a business to business \n(B2B) meetings with Tour Operators, Travel Agents and various other \nstakeholders and organi zed the press Meet to apprise the travel media \nof India’s preparedness to welcome international tourists post \npandemic. Further, India pavilion showcased varied tourism products of \nIndia including culture, heritage and niche tourism products such as \ncuisine, wellness, yoga, wildlife and luxury etc. \n \nMinistry of Tourism had organized international quiz competitions in \nJapan, Bhutan, Vietnam, Thailand, Sri Lanka, Cambodia, South Korea \nand Malaysia focussing on the life of Buddha and various place/sites \nassociated with Buddha and Buddhism in India. The objective of the quiz \ncompetitions was to arouse the curiosity among the  youth and students \nin Buddhist countries and winners of quiz competition were taken on a \nfam trip from 24th to 28th November, 2022. \n \nMinistry of Tourism had conducted Virtual road shows - “Incredible India \nReconnect event ” in  seventeen countries from April -September, 2022  \n(USA, Oman, Germany, Australia, Singapore, Thailand, Spain, UK,  \nFrance, Italy, Portugal, Japan, South Korea, Malaysia, Sri Lanka, \nNetherlands and Canada). \n \nIn order to boost visitor arrival, Ministry of Touri sm, Government of India \nhas declared “Incredible India! Visit India Year 2023”. \n \n(d):  For development of tourism infrastructure in the country, the \nMinistry of Tourism ha s launched two schemes in 2014 -15 i.e. Swadesh \nDarshan - Integrated Development of Theme -Based Tourist Circuits and \nPRASAD- Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual Augmentation Drive. \n \nUnder Swadesh Darshan scheme, a total of 76 projects were sanctioned \nfor an amount of Rs.5315.59 Crore in 31 States/UTs. The Ministry of \nTourism has now revamped the scheme as Swadesh Darshan 2.0 with \nthe objective to develop sustainable and responsible tourism \ndestinations, following a destination and tourism centric approach. \n \nUnder PRASHAD scheme also 45 projects have been identified for \ndevelopment for an amount of Rs.1586.10 Crore. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |524", "t": "Land Subsidence in Joshimath", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.524  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nLand Subsidence in Joshimath \n \n524. SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \n SHRI SUBBARAYAN K.: \n SHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN: \n SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n SHRI DEEPAK BAIJ: \n SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV): \n SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \n SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)  whether it is a fact that Joshimath in Uttrakhand is sinking and numerous homes in \nJoshimath had significant damage and constituted a severe risk to the people living there, \nif so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor along with the remedial steps taken in \nthis regard; \n(b)  whether any research or study has been conducted before starting the development work \nin Joshimath, if so, the details in this regard; \n(c)  the details of the hilly area projects given environmental clearance in the vicinity of \nJoshimath during the last six years and whether the Government had conducted necessary \nsurveys before giving clearance certificate for infrastructure development projects in the \nmountain areas including Chardham Project, if so, the details of reports on the same and \nif not, the reasons for giving permissions to those projects; \n(d)  whether the scientific organisations ever officially objected to such projects and if so, the \ndetails of the reasons/basis on which such development was allowed; \n(e)  whether it is also a fact that several hilly areas are on the verge of facing the Joshimath \nlike landslide conditions, if so, the details of the survey conducted for the hilly cities of \nthe country along with the details of the action plan chalked out by the Government to \nsave the hilly destinations and prevent such kind of incident in future; and \n(f) whether the Government is likely to review the environmental clearance of big projects \nsuch as National Thermal Power Corportion in view of the Joshimath mountain land \nsubsidence and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (f) Land subsidence incidents were reported at various places around Joshimath in Chamoli \nDistrict of Uttarakhand. Due to land-subsidence, many structures have seen reported to have \n\nmoderate to major damages. As reported by the State Government, cracks have been observed in \n863 buildings and after taking into consideration safety of people, 296 families with 995 \nmembers have been shifted to safer places. After the incidence, all construction activities have \nbeen stayed by the State Government in the entire Joshimath area, including Tapovan-Vishnugad \npower project and Helong Marwari By-Pass Road. There are institutional mechanisms at the \nNational, State and District level in the country viz. National Disaster Management Authority \n(NDMA), State Disaster Management Authorities (SDMAs) & District Disaster Management \nAuthorities (DDMAs) respectively to develop appropriate preparedness, coordination and \nprompt response mechanism for effective management of natural disasters. \n \nThe situation is continuously monitored on 24X7 basis at various levels in the State and \nCentral Government. Further, the Central Government and State Government are working in \nclose coordination with all the agencies concerned to mitigate the effect of land subsidence in \nJoshimath area. \n \nThe Geological Survey of India (GSI) has prepared landslide susceptibility maps for the \nhill areas. These maps are to be taken into account by the local administration in development \nplanning. \n \nThe Ministry has delineated a detailed procedure for comprehensive assessment of \nenvironmental and social impacts of the project in the Environment Impact Assessment \nNotification, 2006, as amended; which inter-alia provides for four stages of consideration \nprocess i.e., Screening, Scoping, Public Consultation and Appraisal by the Expert Appraisal \nCommittee (EAC), for assessment of environmental and social impacts taking into account the \nlocation of the Project and also for monitoring of projects. Study of the aforesaid and other \nrelated factors in the context of specific projects forms the basis for preparation of the \nEnvironmental Impact Assessment/Environmental Management Plan. \n \nThe EAC comprising domain area experts, after detailed examination and deliberations \non various environmental and social aspects of the project including appraisal of the \nstudies/information related to seismology, geological profile, study of landslide prone areas, risk \nanalysis studies, recommend the project for grant of Environmental Clearance (EC) by \nsuggesting suitable mitigation measures and Environment Management to minimize the \nenvironmental and social impacts associated with the project. It is only after such detailed study \nand analysis that environmental clearances are issued subject to compl"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |525", "t": "Modernisation of EMRS in Madhya Pradesh", "head": "  \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 525 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nMODERNISATION OF EMRS IN MADHYA PRADESH \n  \n†525.   SHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to modernise the schools being run under Eklavya Model \nResidential Schools (EMRSs) scheme; \n \n(b) the district-wise number of EMRSs being run in Madhya Pradesh; and \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to open EMRSs in various tribal dominated villages of \nBhitarwar and Pohri Legislative Assemblies under Gwalior Parliamentary Constituency?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA):  \n  \n(a) and (b):     Government has decided to set up one Eklavya Model Residential School (EMRS) in \nevery block with 50% or more ST population and at least 20,000 tribal persons as in Census 2011, \nsubject to availability of suitable land to be provided by the State Government, to provide quality \neducation to the tribal students in their local environment. Accordingly, Ministry has set the target of \nestablishing 740 EMRSs in areas with significant tribal population. With the objective of providing \nall modern state-of-the-art infrastructure facilities such as Academic block, Labs, Hostel, Sports \nFacility, Staff Residences, provision of adequate purified drinking water and sanitation facilities etc., \nGovernment has enhanced the construction cost of each schools from Rs. 20 cr for plain area and Rs. \n24 cr. for Hilly/LWE/NE area to Rs. 37.80 cr and Rs. 48 cr. respectively.  Further, Ministry also \nprovides grant of Rs. 1.09 lakh per student per annum for direct expenditure on students like \nnutritional food, study materials, uniforms etc.  Besides, Government has also decided to sanction \nfunds for upgradation of EMRSs sanctioned under the previous provision of Grants under Article \n275(1) subject to a maximum of Rs. 5 crores per school. As on date, a total number of 70 EMRSs \nhave been sanctioned in the State of Madhya Pradesh, out of which 63 are reported to be functional. \nDistrict-wise number of the functional EMRSs being run in Madhya Pradesh is at Annexure. \n \n(c):    As on date, no block has been identified meeting the existing criteria for establishment of \nEMRS in Gwalior District of Madhya Pradesh. \n  \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \nAnnexure as referred in part (a) and (b) of the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n†525 to be answered on 06.02.2023 raised by Shri Vivek Narayan Shejwalkar regarding \n\"Modernisation of EMRS in Madhya Pradesh\". \n \nDistrict-wise number of functional EMRSs in Madhya Pradesh \n \nSl.No. Districts Number of Functional Schools \n1 Alirajpur 4 \n2 Anuppur 1 \n3 Balaghat 2 \n4 Barwani 5 \n5 Betul 3 \n6 Bhopal 1 \n7 Burhanpur 1 \n8 Chhindwara 4 \n9 Dhar 5 \n10 Dindori 4 \n11 Guna 1 \n12 Harda 1 \n13 Hoshangabad 1 \n14 Indore 2 \n15 Jabalpur 3 \n16 Jhabua 4 \n17 Khandwa 1 \n18 Khargone 2 \n19 Mandla 5 \n20 Ratlam 2 \n21 Satna 2 \n22 Sehore 1 \n23 Seoni 1 \n24 Shahdol 3 \n25 Sheopur 1 \n26 Sidhi 1 \n27 Singrauli 1 \n28 Umaria 1 \n  Grand Total 63 \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |526", "t": "White Label ATMs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 526 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/ MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \nWhite Label ATMs \n526. SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJI   KUNDARIYA:  \n        SHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details and the salient features of ‘White Label ATMs (WLAs); \n \n(b) the details of benefits extended to entities for installations of White Label ATMs; \n \n(c)  the number of entities permitted to install White Label ATMs across the country, \ndistrict-wise and State-wise; and \n \n(d) whether any complaints have been received against the WLA, if so, the details \nthereof along with the mechanism followed to resolve the same? \n  \nAnswer \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(DR BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) Reserve Bank of India has apprised that in order to drive ATM penetration in the \ncountry with a greater focus on Tier III to VI centres, non-bank companies have been \npermitted to set up, own and operate White Label ATMs (WLAs) in the country in 2012. \nSuch non-bank entities that set up, own and operate ATMs are called \"White Label \nATM Operators\" (WLAO) and such ATMs are called WLAs. They provide banking \nservices to the customers of banks in India, based on the cards (debit / credit/ prepaid) \nissued by banks. The WLAO role is confined to acquisition of transactions of all banks' \ncustomers by establishing technical connectivity with the existing authorised shared \nATM Network Operators / Card Payment Network Operators. In addition to dispensing \ncash, ATMs / WLAs may offer many other services / facilities to customers which \ninclude: \ni. Account Information \nii. Cash Deposit \niii. Regular Bill Payment \niv. Mini / Short Statement Generation \nv. PIN Change \nvi. Request for Cheque Book  \n\n(b) The measures which have been taken to increase the penetration, viability and effective \nfunctioning of WLAs inter-alia are as under: \ni. RBI, vide its circular dated December 30, 2016,  has allowed WLAOs to source \ncash from retail outlets to address the cash sourcing constraints.  \nii. To further give impetus to WLA deployment and to encourage more non -bank \nplayers to enter ATMs industry, RBI, vide its circular dated March 7, 2019, have \npermitted WLAOs to (i) buy wholesale cash, above a threshold directly from the \nReserve Bank (Issue Offices) and Currency Chests, (ii) source cash from any \nscheduled bank, including Cooperative Banks and Regional R ural Banks, (iii) \noffer bill payment and Interoperable Cash Deposit services, (iv) display \nadvertisements pertaining to non- financial products / services, and in addition, \n(v) banks were permitted to issue co -branded ATM cards in partnership with \nthe authorised WLAOs and extend the benefit of ‘on -us’ transactions to WLAs \nas well.  \niii. RBI, vide press release dated October 15, 2019 has enabled on -tap \nauthorisation for WLAs.  \n(c) RBI has informed that currently there are four authorised  non-bank entities which \nare operating WLAs in the country. \n(d) RBI has apprised that they have not received any complaint in the past one year \nagainst authorised WLA. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |527", "t": "Conversion of ST Community", "head": " \n \n \n \n          GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n             MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \n      UNSTARRED NO- †527 \n        TO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \n       CONVERSION OF ST COMMUNITY \n  \n†527. SHRI GOPAL CHINNAYA SHETTY:  \n \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether there have been cases/instances related to the conversion of people \nbelonging to Scheduled Tribes community; \n(b) if so, the details thereof as on date, State-wise; \n(c) whether any action has been taken/being taken by the Government against the \nculprits in the matter of converting the people of this community; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) the steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government to ban the forceful \nconversion of people of this community?   \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU)  \n  \n(a) to (e): ‘Public Order’ and ‘Police’ are State subjects as per the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. Hence, prevention, detection, registration, \ninvestigation and prosecution of offences related to religious conversions are \nprimarily the concerns of the State Government/Union Territory (UT) \nAdministrations. Action is taken as per existing laws by the law enforcing agencies \nwhenever instances of violation come to notice.  Figures about religious \nconversion are not being maintained by the Ministry of Tribal Affairs. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |528", "t": "Modification of Public Library System", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.528     \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nMODIFICATION OF PUBLIC LIBRARY SYSTEM \n \n528. SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \na) whether the Government is taking steps to modify the public library system, if so, \ndetails thereof; \n \nb) whether the Government is using Information and Communication Technology (ICT) \nto upgrade public libraries; \n \nc) if so, the details thereof; \n \nd) whether the Government is assisting to start community libraries in towns and \nvillages; and \n \ne) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM and  \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a), (b) & (c) Library is a State subject as per the Seventh Schedule under Article 246 of \nConstitution of India and Public Libraries function under the administrative control of the \nrespective State / U.T.  \n \nHowever, Raja Rammohun Roy Library Foundation, Kolkata, an autonomous organization \nunder Ministry of Culture provides financial assistance to public libraries under its various \nMatching and Non-Matching Schemes inter-alia for modernization of library services \nkeeping in mind the latest development in the field of Information and Communication \nTechnology (ICT). \n \nBesides, Ministry of Culture through its scheme called National Mission on Libraries (NML), \nprovides financial assistance to 1 State Central Library and 1 District Library in each \nState/UT for up-gradation of infrastructure & modernization of libraries.  \n \nThe details of these schemes are available on the website of RRRLF. \n \n(d) & (e) As library is a State subject, establishment of new library comes under the purview \nof the respective State government and Union Territory administration. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |529", "t": "Promotion of Indian Monuments on Global Platform", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.529 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF INDIAN MONUMENTS ON GLOBAL PLATFORM \n \n529. SHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV: \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \nSHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the average footfall in monuments in the country during the last five years; \n(b) whether the Government has undertaken any promotional initiatives to increase the footfall \nin   monuments; \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d) whether any concrete steps have been taken to promote Indian monuments on a global \nplatform to attract international visitors; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) The footfall at centrally protected  ticketed monuments in country  is as under: \nYear footfall \n2017-18 5,17,92,441 \n2018-19 5,31,52,423 \n2019-20 4,83,66,190 \n2020-21 1,19,12,580 \n2021-22 2,63,65,564 \n \n(b) Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) undertakes conservation, preservation and maintenance \nof all protected monuments  and sites and also ensures the ambience in presentable condition. \nIn addition, amenities are also provided for tourists visiting the monument.  \n(c)  Details are at Annexure. \n(d) \n& \n(e) \n \nInscribing Indian monument on World Heritage list of UNESCO plays significant role in \nattracting international visitors. \nKakatiya Rudreswara (Ramappa) temple in Telangana and Dholavira : a Harappan city in \nGujarat were inscribed on the World Heritage list in the year 2021. Further, India has already \nsubmitted nomination dossier for ‘Shantiniketan’ in West Bengal for the year 2022 and \n‘Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysala’ in Karnataka for the year 2023. \nIn addition to this Ministry of Tourism (MoT), Government of India  promotes India as a \nholistic destination in the tourism generating markets to  promote various Indian tourism \nproducts and tourism destinations including monuments of the country to increase India’s \nshare of the global tourism market. \nThe above objectives are met through an integrated marketing and promotional strategy, and \na synergized campaign in association with the Travel Trade, State Governments and Indian \nMissions abroad. In order to boost visitor arrival, MoT has declared \"Incredible India! Visit \nIndia Year 2023\".  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY PART (c) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 529 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \nDETAILS OF AMENITIES AND FACILITIES AVAILABLE AT CENTRALLY \nPROTECTED MONUMENTS. \n \n \nSl. No. Type of Facility Available at Monuments \n1.  Approach Pathway 2201 \n2.  Braille Signage 101 \n3.  Culture Notice Boards 1164 \n4.  Protection Notice Boards 2826 \n5.  Divyang Toilet 270 \n6.  Gents Toilet 546 \n7.  Ladies Toilet 514 \n8.  Benches 584 \n9.  Drinking Water 642 \n10.  Parking 260 \n11.  WiFi Facility 61 \n12.  Audio Guide/App Based Guide 9 \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |53", "t": "Routes under Krishi-UDAN Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 53 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGuidelines for Crematoriums \n \n \n*53.  DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government acknowledges that the crematoriums are also a major \ncontributor to Air Pollution; \n(b) if so, the action taken to mitigate it and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to develop any enforceable standards or guidelines \nfor crematoriums, specifically for those in urban areas; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARAS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 53 DUE FOR REPLY ON 06.02.2023 \nREGARDING ‘GUIDELINES FOR CREMATORIUMS’ RAISED BY DR. \nRAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI AND SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA, HON’BLE \nMEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \n \n \n(a) to (d) \n \n \nAs per the available Source Apportionment study reports from 2018 to 2022, no such \ncontribution in air pollution is observed from crematoriums.  \n \nThe development of guidelines on environment management for crematoria has been taken up \nby Central Pollution Control Board. This includes inventory and in-depth study in selective \ncrematorium at different cities of the country.The activities taken under in-depth study \ninclude: identification of source of emissions, monitoring of fugitive as well as source \nemission to assess the level of concentrations, comparison of various technologies in terms of \nfuel consumption, time for cremation, environmental benefits, cost analysis of various \ntechnologies and assessment of the appropriateness of pollution control devices. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |530", "t": "Global Exchange Programme to Promote Indian Cultural Activities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.530 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGlobal Exchange Programme to Promote Indian Cultural Activities \n530.SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \nSHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY: \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has implemented any global exchange programmesto \npromote Indian cultural activities on an international scale; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps to promote such exchange \nprogrammes on a school and university level to ensure greater youth \nparticipation; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) the total funds sanctioned and spent on such activities, if any during \nthe last five years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND  \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION  \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (d)Ministry of Culture promotes Indian cultural activities on an international \nscale through signing of Cultural Agreements & Cultural Exchange Programme and \ntheir implementation by concerned agencies with mutual discussions through \ndiplomatic channels. Ministry of Culture also promotes Indian Cultural activities \nabroad through Global Engagement Scheme under which Festivals of India are \norganized showcasing folk art and other cultural events. Under the scheme, Grant-\nin-aid is also given to Indo-Foreign Friendship Cultural Societies for organizing \nprogrammes & activities including folk art & other cultural activities for their \npromotion abroad.  \nIndian Council for Cultural Relations, an organization under Ministry of External \nAffairs, also promotes Indian Culture worldwide through its Cultural Centres abroad \nand its activities in various fields including inter-alia, visit of International Students of \nHindi subject under Visitors Programme; supporting Chairs of Indian Studies in \nvarious foreign universities. \n \n(e)         The total funds of Rs.85.69 crore (approx.) had been sanctioned and spent \non Festival of India abroad and Grant in Aid to Indo Foreign Friendship Societies in \nlast five years. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |531", "t": "GST Exemption to Foreign Tourists", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 531 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGST EXEMPTION TO FOREIGN TOURISTS \n531. DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR: \nDR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K.: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the G overnment is considering to accord GST Exemption to \nthe foreign tourists for local shopping to boost tourism in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the other exemptions etc. given by the \nGovernment in the field of tourism to attract foreign tourists; \n(c) whether the Government has signed any agreement with any other \ncountry for the purpose of promoting tourism in India; \n(d) if not, whether the Government is preparing any plan to do so; and \n(e) the number of foreign tourists arrived to India afte r lockdown due to \nspread of corona infections? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM             (SHRI G.KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): As informed by the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs, \nDepartment of Revenue, Ministry of Finance,  there is no proposal at \npresent under consideration of the Government to reimburse GST to \nforeign tourists at Airport on arrival to India.  \n \n(b): Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Cust oms has further informed \nthat the Tour Operator service, which is performed partly in India and \npartly outside India, supplied by a tour operator to a foreign tourist, to the \nextent of the value of the tour operator service which is performed outside \nIndia is exempt from GST vide entry at SL. No. 52A of the notification No. -\n12/2017 CTR dated 28.06.2017. \n \n(c) and (d): The signing of Agreements /Memorandum of Understanding \n(MoUs) with various countries is an ongoing process and the Ministry of \nTourism has si gned 46 Agreements /MoUs with other countries and \nmultilateral fora for bilateral and multilateral cooperation for strengthening \ntourism cooperation and promoting tourism products of India. \n \n(e): Post-lockdown due to Covid -19 pandemic, the number of foreign \ntourists arrived India is annexed at annexure. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (e) OF LO K SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.531 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING GST \nEXEMPTION TO FOREIGN TOURISTS. \n \n \nMonth wise Foreign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) during 2020 to 2022 \nMonth Foreign Tourist Arrivals \n2020 2021 2022 @ \nJanuary 11,19,250 94,662 2,01,546 \nFebruary 10,18,440 1,10,312 2,40,896 \nMarch 3,28,304 1,33,768 3,42,308 \nApril 2,820 78,718 3,92,930 \nMay 3,764 19,765 4,23,701 \nJune 8,590 36,070 5,22,737 \nJuly 12,655 72,501 6,40,858 \nAugust 19,761 92,728 4,98,243 \nSeptember 28,167 1,15,661 5,36,340 \nOctober 41,494 1,91,415 6,56,895 \nNovember 70,977 2,63,867 7,68,675 \nDecember 90,544 3,17,647 9,66,270 \nTotal (Jan-Dec) 27,44,766 15,27,114 61,91,399 \n@ Provisional \n \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |532", "t": "CSR Spending by PSUs", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 532 \n ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/ MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA)  \n \n CSR SPENDING BY PSUs \nQUESTION \n532.  SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n         SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \n         DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \n          \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of aggregate spending in the last seven years by the Public Sector Units \n(PSUs) and Non-Public Sector Units on Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR);  \n(b) whether the Government has received any amount from PSUs and Non -PSUs for the  \ndevelopment of villages and rural infrastructure; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government has any check and monitoring system to examine, if the CSR \nfunds were spent genuinely for the said purposes, if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the amount received from Publ ic Limited and Private Limited Companies for the same \nand the details of the expenditure incurred during the said period,  State-wise? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STA TE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS                                                                 \n   [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n(a): The broad framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has been provided under \nSection 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the Act and Companies (CSR \nPolicy) Rules, 2014. All data related to CSR filed by companies in MCA21 registry is available \nin public domain and can be accessed at www.csr.gov.in. On the basis of annual filings made \nby Companies in the MCA21 registry, the aggregate CSR spent by Public Secto r Under -\ntakings (PSUs) and Non-PSUs during the financial year 2014-15 to 2020-21 are given below:    \n                             (Data up to 30.09.2022)   [Source: National CSR Data Portal] \nContd….2/- \n \n \n \n \n \nNature of Company Total Amount Spent (Rs. in crores) \nPublic Sector Under-takings (PSUs) 27991.45 \nNon-PSUs 98950.83 \nGrand Total                               126942.28 \n\n \n-2- \n \n(b) &  (c):  Schedule VII of the Act indicates the eligible list of activities that can be \nundertaken by the companies as CSR. The Ministry vide General Circular no. 21/2014 dated \n18th June, 2014 and 14/2021 dated 25th August, 2021 had clarified that items mentioned \nin Schedule VII are broad based and can be interpreted liberally. Item no. (x) o f Schedule \nVII, covers “rural development” as eligible CSR activities. On the basis of filings made in \nthe MCA21 registry, the aggregate CSR spent by PSUs and Non-PSUs in ‘Rural Development’ \nduring the Financial Years (FY) 2014-15 to 2020-21 are given below:   \nCSR Expenditure in ‘Rural Development’ sector  \n( In Rs. Crore) \nNature of Company Total Amount Spent (Rs. in crores) \nPSUs 2,876.63  \nNon-PSUs 9,424.62  \nGrand Total 12,301.25 \n                             (Data up to 30.09.2022)   [Source: National CSR Data Portal] \n \n(d):  The CSR framework is disclosure based and CSR mandated companies are required \nto file details of CSR activities annually in the MCA21 registry . The corporate \ngovernance framework along with the existing legal provisions such as mandatory \ndisclosures, accountability of the CSR Committee and the Board, provisions for \nstatutory audit of accounts of the company etc. provide safeguards in this regard. \nWhenever any violation of CSR provisions is reported, action against such non-compliant \ncompanies are initiated as per provisions of the Companies Act, 2013 after due \nexamination of records and following due process of law. Further, the amendments in \nthe C ompanies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014 notified on 22 nd January 2021 have \nstrengthened the CSR eco-system by bringing more objectivity, transparency, entrusting \nmore responsibility on the Board and enlarging the disclosures by the companies.  \n(e):  The details of CSR funds spent in ‘Rural Development’ sector by Public Sector \nUndertakings (PSUs) and Private companies, in different States /Union Territories (UTs) \nfor the financial years 2014-15 to 2020-21 is at Annexure. \n \n                                                          ******* \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) OF LOK SABHA UN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 532 FOR  \n06.02.2023 \n \nState wise, CSR Expenditure in Rural Development Sector (Amount in Crore) \nState/UT FY 2014-\n15 \nFY 2015-16 FY 2016-17 FY 2017-18 FY 2018-19 FY 2019-20 FY 2020-21 \nAndaman \nAnd Nicobar \n- - - - 0.53 0.67 1.22 \nAndhra \nPradesh \n30.87 97.45 49.67 58.60 119.94 83.79 144.80 \nArunachal \nPradesh \n0.85 0.30 4.79 0.15 0.46 - 0.07 \nAssam 13.51 2.78 46.83 6.10 10.60 6.29 9.21 \nBihar 2.93 10.56 12.18 11.91 22.06 5.34 1.28 \nChandigarh 0.05 - 0.03 0.07 1.44 - - \nChhattisgarh 41.88 20.80 6.32 13.98 17.00 12.3"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |533", "t": "Negative Effects of COVID Pandemic on Girls Education", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No - 533 \nANSWERED ON - 06/02/2023 \n \n \nNEGATIVE EFFECTS OF COVID PANDEMIC ON GIRLS EDUCATION \n \n \n533. SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI: \n \n Will the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has assessed the negative effects of the pandemic on girls education \nincluding increased dropout rates, lack of digital devices at home, etc. and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has any data regarding increased dropout rates and lack of digital \ninfrastructure for girls in the past two years and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) the specific steps taken by the Government to address this issue especially in light of successive \nlockdowns; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government has issued any specific guidelines to the State Governments in this regard \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) to (d) : As per the data available on Unified District Information System for Education plus \n(UDISE+), annual average drop-out rate of girls for last three years is as under: \n \nLevel 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nElementary 1.9 1.4 2.0 \nSecondary 15.1 13.7 12.2 \n                        (Source: UDISE+) \nThis data shows that the drop-out rate of girls has not been significantly affected due to     COVID-\n19.   \nFurther, education is in the concurrent list of the Constitution and majority of schools come under \nthe purview of the respective State and UT Governments. In order to mitigate the impact of challenges \nthrown by the COVID-19 pandemic for school children including girls and for preventing drop outs, lower \nenrolments and loss of learning, the Ministry of Education has issued instructions to the States & UTs from \ntime to time for ensuring continued education with quality and equity.  \n\nDuring COVID-19 Pandemic, Department of School Education and Literacy ( DoSEL) held \ncontinuous consultations with the States and UTs a t various levels to assess the various aspects  of  the  \nCOVID-19  pandemic. National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) has conducted a \nsurvey in the month of July, 2020 with the help of Kendriya  Vidyalaya  Sangathan (KVS), Navodaya  \nVidyalaya  Samiti (NVS) and Central  Board  of  Secondary  Education  (CBSE)  to  understand  the  \nscenario  of  Online  Learning  of school  students  in  COVID -19  pandemi c  situation.  The  National \nAchievement  Survey  (NAS)  was conducted on 12.11.2021 in 717 districts of the country to assess \nlearning levels of sampled children of grades 3, 5, 8 and 10. \n \nA comprehensive initiative called PM e-VIDYA has been initiated which unifies all efforts related \nto digital/online/on-air education to enable multi-mode access to education. The initiative includes:  \n \n• DIKSHA (one nation, one digital platform) is the nation’s digital infrastructure for providing \nquality e-content for school education in states/ UTs and QR coded Energized Textbooks for all \ngrades are available on it. \n• One earmarked Swayam Prabha TV channel per class from 1 to 12 (one class, one channel). \n• Extensive use of Radio, Community radio and CBSE Podcast- Shiksha Vani. \n• Special e-content for visually and hearing impaired developed on Digitally Accessible Information \nSystem (DAISY) and in sign language on NIOS website/ YouTube. \n \n \nIn addition, States/UTs have prepared educational content in the form of audios and videos and \ntelecasted on local Radio and TV channels or YouTube Channels. Bridge course material, workbooks, text \nbooks etc. along with digital devices have also been provided to help the students. Mobile applications with \npre-loaded content have been developed. Also alternate academic calendar has been made for home based \nlearning. Regular home visits have been made by the teachers/volunteers and Mohalla classes or \nCommunity classes etc. have been conducted to support education.   \n \nAlso, to bridge the learning gaps and to ensure continuity of education, Ministry of Education has \nshared a comprehensive Learning Recovery Plan with all the States and \nUnion Territories on 01.02. 2022 describing the actions/activities to be undertaken by all the  \nstakeholders, indicative annual calendar of activities, existing interventions which can be  \nutilized and additional support with funds, as a onetime measure. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |534", "t": "Allocation of Funds under Article 275(1)", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- †534 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS UNDER ARTICLE 275(1) \n  \n†534. DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government provides grants for developmental work in tribal areas under Article \n275(1), if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(b) the details of the funds provided to the State Governments under Article 275(1) during the last \nfive years, State/UT-wise; \n  \n(c) the details of the funds provided to Madhya Pradesh, district-wise during the last five years; \n  \n(d) whether the Government has allocated fund for Seoni and Balaghat districts under Balaghat \nParliamentary Constituency; \n  \n(e) if so, the funds allocated for the works under Article 275(1) for these districts during the last five \nyears along with the details of works executed thereby; \n  \n(f) the details of the approved work along with the works completed or current status of these works; \nand \n  \n(g) whether the funds have not been released, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a) & (b): Yes Sir. Under proviso to Grants under Article 275 (1) of the Constitution of India, \nfinancial assitance are provided to the State Governments based upon proposals received from them, \nduly recommended by State level Executive Committee and after appraisal and approval thereof by a \nProject Appraisal Committee (PAC) constituted in this Ministry for the purpose. Under proviso to \nGrants under Article 275(1) of the Constitution, funds are provided for activities related to education, \nhealth, sanitation,  \nwater supply, livelihood, skill development, minor infrastructure etc. with the objective of \ndevlopment of scheduled area and also the welfare of Scheduled Tribes (STs). Details of funds \nreleased under the scheme, State/UT-wise,  during last five years are at Annexure-I.  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n(c) to (g): As per the proposals submitted by State Government, activities have been approved for \nimplementation,inter-alia, for Seoni and Balaghat district of Madhya Pradesh under the scheme of \nGrants under Article 275(1) of the Constitution and details thereof are at Annexure-II.  Further, \nfunds are released to the states subject to submission of the requisite documents like Utilization \nCertificates (UCs), PPR etc.  Accordingly, the entire approved funds after taking into account \npending UCs, if any, have been released to the states including the state of Madhya Pradesh.  \nHowever, implementation of the approved projects at the location / district submitted by the State \nGovernment and release of funds to the implementing agencies as per prescribed procedure is the \nresponsibility of the concerned State Government. District-wise details of work execution and current \nstatus thereof is not maintained centrally and it is maintained by respective State Government(s). \nBesidesMinistry has developed ADIGRAMS( Adivasi Grants Management Sustem), wherein States \nare required to update physical and finacial progress for the funds provided to states.  \n \n \n  \n  \n\n  \nAnnexure-I \nAnnex referred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.†534 for \nanswer on 06.02.2023 \n State-wise details of funds released under Article 275(1) of Constitution during the last 5 years. \n(Rs. in lakhs) \n S.N States 2017-18* 2018-19* 2019-20* 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 4591.11 6390.77 8942.43 2055.55 2638.65 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 8378.82 12170.52 13202.25 6014.00 9830.00 \n3 Assam 0.00 3916.32 10802.85 4592.37 2570.00 \n4 Bihar 991.89 0.00 0.00 0.00 642.08 \n5 Chhattisgarh 10964.49 11352.92 22500.77 9976.24 11604.02 \n6 Goa 103.00 345.66 0.00 0.00 600.41 \n7 Gujarat 11384.08 11648.20 16833.88 5940.04 6923.79 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 2074.70 3378.16 5314.70 1161.00 1500.00 \n9 Jammu & Kashmir 3049.06 2051.79 5410.00 0.00 0.00 \n10 Jharkhand 12386.93 7354.30 11166.77 10278.00 12264.19 \n11 Karnataka 5881.74 5220.61 10435.46 3305.68 3210.00 \n12 Kerala 803.17 472.28 633.29 0.00 0.00 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 22399.48 24635.30 44938.92 4279.78 5319.10 \n14 Maharashtra 13862.24 17015.91 23136.70 4573.16 0.00 \n15 Manipur 2308.80 5367.65 6151.91 0.00 0.00 \n16 Meghalaya 3603.40 5129.79 4799.53 492.71 1595.25 \n17 Mizoram 2504.41 3507.71 6598.83 1909.71 2971.54 \n18 Nagaland 4434.11 9194.49 6802.11 1717.38 3202.39 \n19 Odisha 15995.30 21449.15 15110.66 6304.62 11382.05 \n20 Rajasthan 10240.58 13769.23 15586.19 9166.00 10435.21 \n21 Sikkim 405.30 355.34 3175.35 516.00 2045.00 \n22 Tamil Nadu 378.00 773.57 6356.07 0.00 0.00 \n23 Telangana 5015.32 3248.89 9973.28 2517.00 2050.00 \n24 Tripura 2040.99 2006.73 2952.63 201.74 607.53 \n25 Uttar Pradesh 189.00 252.43 2760.71 927.43 832.71 \n26 Uttarakhand 1577.56 1255.27 428.37 0.00 100.65 \n27 West Bengal 5376.51 9235.73 12127.17 4041.14 0.00 \nGrand Total 150939.99 181498.72 266140.83 79969.55 92324.5"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |535", "t": "Financial Support to Folk Artists of Odisha", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 535 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nFINANCIAL SUPPORT TO FOLK ARTISTS OF ODISHA \n \n†535.  SHRI BASANTA KUMAR PANDA: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has made any provision to provide financial support to folk \nartists and tribal artists in Odisha; \n(b)  if so, the details of expenditure incurred by the Government under the said items during \nthe last three years; \n(c)  the number of the beneficiaries in Odisha, district-wise; and \n(d)  the steps being taken by the Government to promote the art of the said artists and the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH  \nEASTERN REGION  \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) To protect, promote and preserve various forms of folk art and tribal culture in the \ncountry, the Government of India has set up seven (7) Zonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs) with \nheadquarters at Patiala, Nagpur, Udaipur, Prayagraj, Kolkata, Dimapur and Thanjavur. \nOdisha is the member State of Eastern Zonal Cultural Centre (EZCC), Kolkata, an \nautonomous organization under Ministry of Culture. EZCC organizes various cultural \nactivities and programmes in Odisha throughout the year on regular basis for which annual \ngrant-in-aid is provided to them. Folk and tribal artists are engaged by EZCC to showcase \ntheir talents in these cultural programmes/activities for which they are paid honorarium, \nDA/TA, boarding & lodging etc. Besides, all the seven ZCCs also implement a number of \nschemes viz. Award to Young Talented Artistes, Guru Shishya Parampara Scheme, Theatre \nRejuvenation Scheme, Research & Documentation Scheme, Shilpgram Scheme, OCTAVE, \nJ&K Festivals and National Cultural Exchange Programme (NCEP) to help folk and tribal \nartists. \n \n(b)  & (c) Details of expenditure incurred and number of beneficiaries in Odisha during \nthe last three years are as under: \n(Rs. in lakh) \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nAmount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries \n327.36 1069 410.06 7538 726.51 2072 \n \n(d) The following steps have been taken to promote the art of the said artists: \n \ni. Under Guru Shishya Parampara Scheme, training is imparted to the younger \ngeneration in Indian Tribal art forms in which a senior Guru is engaged to train \n15-20 Shishyas for a period of 1-2 years. Shishyas are also paid a stipend during \nthe training period.  \n \n\n \n \n \n \nii. Workshops are conducted to promote and keep alive the dying and vanishing art \nforms of tribal communities to enable them to earn their livelihood.  \n \niii. EZCC organizes programmes relating to tribal & folk dances, tribal & folk \npaintings, tribal & folk music, tribal & folk arts & crafts, tribal festivals, tribal & \nfolk theatre etc. Besides, documentation of different tribal art forms is taken up \nregularly. Documented art forms are being utilized for promotion and \ndissemination of tribal art and culture among the younger generation.  \n \niv. Tribal costumes/musical instruments/daily use utensils/ deities of tribal \ncommunities of Odisha and other member States of EZCC have been displayed at \nthe Srijani Shilpgram, Santiniketan, West Bengal. Tourists and visitors who visit \nthe Shilpgram get an idea of the tribal culture of Odisha. \n \nv. Sending troupes from Odisha to all folk and tribal festivals across the country \nalso helps in the promotion of tribal communities. \n \nvi. In all the craft fairs and festivals organized by EZCC, folk and tribal communities \nof Odisha sell products made by them during these programmes. \n \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |536", "t": "Improvement in Quality of Education under NEP", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 536 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nImprovement in Quality of Education under NEP \n \n536.  SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \nSHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of various initiatives taken/being taken by the Government under the new National \nEducation Policy (NEP) to improve quality of education across all levels from Primary to \nUniversity level;  \n \n(b) the amount of budget earmarked for the purpose in the last three years, year-wise;  \n \n(c) the number of States in the country that have implemented the National Education Policy;  \n \n(d) whether there is any State which has not implemented this policy so far and if so, the details \nthereof along with the reaction of such State;  \n \n(e) the further steps taken/being taken by the Government to implement New National Education \nto all States of the country;  \n \n(f) whether there is any proposal of Government to make education vocation/skill-oriented at the \nSecondary level so that the students after completing XII class can be gainfully employed;  \n \n(g) whether the Government has conducted any study to find out if introduction of breakfast in \nschools along with mid-day meals can significantly reduce dropout rates; and  \n \n(h) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a): In pursuance of the goals and objectives of the National Education Policy 2020 (NEP 2020), \nseveral initiatives have been launched in School Education viz. National Initiative for \nProficiency in Reading with Understanding and Numeracy (NIPUN Bharat) for ensuring \nfoundational literacy and numeracy by the end of Grade 3, Vidya-Pravesh – Guidelines for \n\n \n \nThree-month Play-based School Preparation Module; Indian Sign Language as a Subject at \nsecondary level by National Institute of Open Schooling; NISHTHA (National Initiative for \nSchool Heads' and Teachers' Holistic Advancement) 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 Integrated Teacher Training \nProgramme for different stages of school education for Teachers, Head Teachers/Principals and \nother stakeholders in Educational Management; SAFAL (Structured Assessment for Analysing \nLearning Levels) for competency-based assessment in CBSE schools for grades 3, 5 and 8, etc.  \n \nSimilarly, in Higher Education, various initiatives / reforms have been carried out such as \nAcademic Bank of Credit (ABC); Multiple Entry/Exit in Academic Programmes; Transforming \nHigher Education Institutions into Multidisciplinary Institutions; Common Universities Entrance \nTest; Internship/Apprenticeship embedded Degree Programme; Online and ODL Education;  1st \nYear Engineering Programmes in Regional Languages; Academic Collaboration between Indian \nand Foreign Higher Educational Institutions to offer Twinning, Joint Degree and Dual Degree \nProgrammes; Pursuing two Academic Programmes simultaneously; Establishment of Research \nand Development Cells in HEIs; Professor of Practice; Curriculum and Credit Framework for \nUndergraduate Programme, etc. \n \n(b): Details of total Budget Allocation of the Ministry are given in table below: \n         (Rs. in Crores) \nDetails of Budget Allocation  \nFinancial Year Total Budget Allocation \n2020-21 99311.52 \n2021-22 93224.31 \n2022-23 104277.72 \n \n(c) to (e):  After announcement of NEP 2020, Ministry of Education, other relevant \nMinistries/Departments, State/UT Governments and implementing agencies under their purview \nhave started taking initiatives towards implementation of NEP 2020. During implementation, \nfew States had expressed their concerns on certain issues related to the NEP, 2020. To address \ntheir concerns and also to discuss innovative ideas for NEP implementation, a series of \nworkshops / consultation-cum-review meetings have been held with States/UTs.   \n \n(f): The Department of School Education and Literacy is implementing the initiative of \nVocationalisation of School Education under the umbrella of the Centrally Sponsored Scheme \n‘Samagra Shiksha’. The scheme aims at integrating Vocational Education with general academic \neducation in all Secondary/Senior Secondary schools; enhancing the Employability and \nEntrepreneurial abilities of the students, providing exposure to work environment; and \ngenerating awareness amongst students about various career options so as to enable them to \nmake a choice in accordance with their aptitude, competence and aspirations. The scheme covers \nGovernment and Government aided schools. Under the Scheme, National Skill Qualification \nFramework (NSQF) compliant vocational courses are offered to the students from class 9th to \n12th in the schools covered under the scheme.  \n \nIn order to fulfill various objectives of NEP 2020"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |537", "t": "Pending Work in Government Schools in Maharashtra under Samagra Shiksha Abhiyan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.537  \nANSWERED ON 6th February, 2023 \n \nPending Work in Government Schools in Maharashtra under Samagra Shiksha Abhiyan \n \n537.   SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the proposals of the works such as the construction of new class rooms, \nbuilding and other works in different Government schools of Maharashtra under Samagra \nShiksha Abhiyan which are lying pending for financial approval of the Governmen t, District-\nwise;  \n(b) the time by which the Government is likely to accord approval to the said proposals; and  \n(c) the details of the progress made in Maharashtra as per the targets set under Samagra \nShiksha Abhiyan that was launched by Government, district-wise? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Department had launched Samagra Shiksha – an Integrated Scheme for School \nEducation by subsuming the erstwhile Centrally Sponsored Schemes of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan \n(SSA), Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) and Teacher Education (TE) from \n2018-19. The Samagra Shi ksha scheme has now been aligned with the recommendations of \nNEP 2020 and has been continued for a period of five years with effect from 01.04.2021 to \n31.03.2026. Under Centrally Sponsored scheme of Samagra Shiksha financial assistance is \nprovided as per t he fund sharing pattern as decided by the Ministry of Finance. Under the \nscheme, the annual plans are prepared by the States and UTs based on their requirements and \npriority and this is reflected in their respective Annual Work Plan and Budget (AWP&B) \nProposals. These plans are then appraised and approved/estimated by the Project Approval \nBoard (PAB) in the Department of School Education & Literacy in consultation with the States \nand UTs as per the programmatic and financial norms of the scheme, and physica l and financial \nprogress of the State for the interventions approved earlier. The AWP&B, 2022-23 for State of \nMaharashtra under Samagra Shiksha has been approved and issued. The District-wise details of \nthe approvals and progress for carrying out various interventions under Samagra Shiksha \nAbhiyan in Maharashtra are at Annexure-I. \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 537 FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2023 ASKED BY SHRI \nSUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE REGARDING PENDING WORK IN GOVERNMENT \nSCHOOLS IN MAHARASHTRA UNDER SAMAGRA SHIKSHA ABHIYAN \n \nProgress of civil works sanctioned under Samagra Shiksha from 2018-19 to 2022-23 \n \nResidential School (Z.P.+ M.N.C. ) (December-2022) \nSr. \nNo. \nDistrict \nName \n2018-19 Total Upto 2022-23 \nTarget  Complete IN \nProgress \nNot \nStarted \nTarget  Complete IN \nProgress \nNot \nStarted \n1 Ahmednagar 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n2 Akola 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n3 Amravati 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n4 Aurangabad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n5 Beed 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 Bhandara 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 Buldhana 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Parbhani 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Chadrapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Dhule 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Gadchiroli 1 1 0 0 1 1 0 0 \n11 Gondia 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n12 Hingoli 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n13 Jalgaon 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n14 Jalna 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n15 Kolhapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n16 Latur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n17 Nagpur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n18 Nanded 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 Nandurbar 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n20 Nashik 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n21 Osmanabad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n23 Pune 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n24 Raigad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n25 Ratnagiri 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n26 Sangli 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n27 Satara 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n28 Sinduhdurg 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n29 Solapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n30 Thane 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n31 Palghar 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n32 Wardha 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n33 Washim 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n34 Yavatmal 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n  Total 1 1 0 0 1 1 0 0 \n \n \n \n\nAdditional Class Room ( Z.P.+ M.N.C. ) (December-2022) \nSr. \nNo. \nDistrict \nName \n2018-19 2019-20 \nTarget  Complete IN \nProgress \nNot \nStarted \nTarget  Complete IN \nProgress \nNot \nStarted \n1 Ahmednagar 4 4 0 0 10 10 0 0 \n2 Akola 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n3 Amravati 1 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n4 Aurangabad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n5 Beed 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 Bhandara 0 0 0 0 2 2 0 0 \n7 Buldhana 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 \n8 Parbhani 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Chadrapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Dhule 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Gadchiroli 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 Gondia 0 0 0 0 2 2 0 0 \n12 Hingoli 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n13 Jalgaon 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n14 Jalna 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n15 Kolhapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n16 Latur 0 0 0 0 2 2 0 0 \n17 Nagpur 0 0 0 0 5 0 5 0 \n18 Nanded 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 Nandurbar 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n20 Nashik 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n21 Osmanabad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n23 Pune 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n24 Raigad 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n25 Ratnagiri 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n26 Sangli 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n27 Satara 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n28 Sinduhdurg 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n29 Solapur 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n30 Thane 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n31 Palghar 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n32 Wardha 1 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n33 Washim 4 2 2 0 0 0 0 0 \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |538", "t": "Skill Development in Rajasthan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 538 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023  \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT IN RAJASTHAN \n \n538. COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the steps being taken by the Government for creating an entrepreneurial \necosystem in the country, inclusive of the marginalized self-employed labour-force; \n \n(b) the fund allocation for such schemes and initiatives during the past five years, \nespecially for Rajasthan, year-wise;  \n \n(c) the number of youths connected with skill development schemes till date in \nRajasthan in comparison to other States; and  \n \n(d) the data highlighting and the scope provided by the Government through these \nschemes to benefit the youth, especially in Jaipur Rural? \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) to (b):  To create an enabling ecosystem through entrepreneurship education, \ntraining, advocacy and easy access to entrepreneurship network, Ministry of Skill \nDevelopment and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) has been working for promoting \nentrepreneurial talents in cluding marginalized self – employed labour -force in the \ncountry through National Institute for Entrepreneurship  and Small Business \nDevelopment (NIESBUD) and Indian Institute of Entrepreneurship (IIE). The details of \nthe various initiatives taken by MSDE a re placed at Annexure-I. Further, the details of \nthe various initiatives taken by other Ministries/Departments for promoting \nentrepreneurial talents in the country including Rajasthan are placed at Annexure-II. \n \n(c) to (d): The details of the number of beneficiaries connected with skill development \nunder various initiatives taken by MSDE are as under: \n \n1. Apprenticeship training: Apprenticeship training under Apprentices Act, 1961  \nand the Rules thereof  is one of the key Scheme for skill development of youth in \nthe country including Rajasthan and contributes to ‘Skill India’. State -wise number \nof youths connected with skill development scheme s under Apprenticeship \nTraining including Rajasthan from FY 2016 -17 t ill 30.01.2023 is placed \natAnnexure-III.  \n \n\n41,836 apprentices in Rajasthan including 9,138apprentices in Jaipur District,have \nbeen enrolled for apprenticeship training since its inception i.e., FY 2016 -17 to \n30.01.2023. \n \n2. Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY): Ministry of Skill  \nDevelopment and  Entrepreneurship (MSDE) is  implementing Pradhan Mantri \nKaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) with an objective to deliver skills to youth of the \ncountry through Short Term Training (STT) and Recognition of Prior Learning \n(RPL).  \n \n10,94,168 beneficiaries in Rajasthanincluding 1,99,033beneficiaries in \nJaipurDistrict, have beentrained/oriented under the Scheme of PMKVY till \n31.12.2022. Details of candidates trained/oriented Pan India are placed at \nAnnexure-IV. \n \n3. Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS): The Ministry of Skill Development & \nEntrepreneurship is implementing the Scheme of Jan Shikshan  Sansthan through \nNGOs to impart skill training in non-formal mode  to non -literate, neo -literates, \npersons with rudimentary level of education up to eighth and school dropouts up to \n12th standard in the age group of 15 -45 years in rural areas and urban slums. The \npriority groups are women, SC, ST, Minorities and other backward sections of the \nsociety.The objective of the JSS scheme is to increase household income by \npromoting self/wage employment. \n \n42,566 beneficiariesin Rajasthanincluding  6,835 beneficiaries in Jaipur  District \nbeneficiaries have been trained under the Scheme of Jan Shikshan  Sansthan \nduring the period 2018 -19 till 2022-23. Details of the candidates  trained Pan India \nare placed at Annexure-V. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nThe details of the various initiatives taken by MSDE for Entrepreneurship \nDevelopment are as given below: \n \n(i) Pilot project on Entrepreneurship Development in Holy Cities is a project \nof the MSDE implemented by NIESBUD and IIE which aims to catalyze local \nentrepreneurial activities through involvement of potential and existing entrepreneurs, \nunemployed youth, college dropouts, youth from backward communit ies. The project \nis being implemented in Bodh Gaya, Kollur, Haridwar, Puri, Pandharpur and \nVaranasi. Total beneficiaries trained under the project are 8,600.  \n \n(ii) Organizing Workshops in Artisan Melas and Haats : MSDE is implementing \na project through NIESBUD to undertake the initiative for organising workshops for \ncapacity building and providing entrepreneurial knowledge to the artisans during the \nMelas and Haats with a view to value addition in the existing Products of participants, \ncreating financial and market  linkages and linking their products with e -commerce \nplatforms and making their business"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |539", "t": "Inspection of Industrial Sites by CAQM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 539 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nInspection of Industrial Sites by CAQM \n \n539.  SHRI BRIJBHUSHAN SHARAN SINGH: \nDR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \nSHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the State/UT-wise details of the number of industrial sites inspected by Commission for \nAir Quality Management (CAQM) under its jurisdiction; \n(b) the details of the number of inspections conducted to curb Construction and Demolition \nwaste, State/UT-wise; \n(c) the details of the number of instances of compliance observed during monitoring in lieu \nof CAQM directives at the sites responsible for generating construction and demolition \nwaste, State/UT-wise; \n(d) the number of sites ordered to cease their operations in an attempt to curb Construction \nand Demolition waste, State/UT-wise; \n(e) whether the CAQM has estimation of the monetary value of real estate sites ordered to \nhold back operations, State/UT-wise; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor, State/UT-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (f)  \nAs on 31.01.2023, a total of about 11,910 field inspections have been carried out by the flying \nsquads constituted by the Commission for Air Quality Management (CAQM) across sectors like \nC&D projects, road construction / rebuilding projects and industries etc. in the NCR districts of \nHaryana, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh and NCT of Delhi. \n \nThe number of Closure Directions issued against the C&D Project entities grossly violating the \ndirections/ rules/ guidelines towards mitigation of Dust/ Air pollution in this context, is given at \nAnnexure-I. Out of 218 sites, 105 sites have, since, been permitted to resume activities pursuant \nto fulfilment of statutory requirements/ documentation and necessary corrective and preventive \nmeasures for dust control/ air pollution at such sites. \n \n*****  \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nDetails of Closure Directions issued by CAQM against the C&D Project sites grossly \nviolating the directions/ rules/ guidelines towards mitigation of Dust/ Air pollution \n \nTotal sites \ninspected \nClosure Directions issued against the C&D Project \nentities \nTotal Closure \nDirections \nDelhi Haryana Uttar Pradesh Rajasthan  \n11910 48 59 77 34 218 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |54", "t": "Houses under PMAY-U", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*54 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES UNDER PRASAD \nSCHEME \n \n†*54. SHRI NAYAB SINGH: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has covered various tourist places of \nHaryana under the PRASAD scheme; \n(b) if so, the district-wise details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is making any  special provision for \nreligious places like Mata Mansa Devi Mandir, Nada Sahib \nlocated in Panch kula and Adi Badri in Haryana through the \nsaid scheme so that the devotees visiting the said places may \nget the benefit of the said scheme as well as to develop the \nsaid places with ultra modern facilities; \n(d) whether the Government is likely to include n ew components \nin the PRASAD scheme which may also benefit the rural areas; \nand \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e) : A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n**** \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO .†*54 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \nREGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF RELIGIOUS PLACES UNDER \nPRASAD SCHEME. \n \n \n(a) to  (c): Under PRASHAD Scheme of Ministry of Tourism, \ndestinations are identified and projects are approved on the basis of \nproposal requests received from the respective State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations. Based on the \nproposals received from Sta te Government of Haryana , Nada Saheb \nGurudwara and Mata Mansha Devi Temple in Panchkula di strict \nhave been included under PRASHAD scheme. A Project viz. \n“Development of Nada Saheb Gurudwara and Mata Mansha Devi \nTemple in Panchkula District”  at the estimated  cost of Rs. 49.52 Cr. \nhas been approved under the scheme for development of pilgrimage \ntourism amenities at these destinations. \n \n(d) & (e): PRASHAD is a  scheme for development of Pilgrimage and \nHeritage tourism infrastructure at the identified destination s across \nthe country irrespective the location of identified destination , \nwhether it falls under rural, urban, tribal or in any other area and the \nbenefits of the projects are available to the local population. \n  \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |540", "t": "Tourist Circuits Selected under SDS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †540 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nTOURIST CIRCUITS SELECTED UNDER SDS \n \n†540. SHRI PRADEEP KUMAR SINGH: \nSHRIMATI GEETA KORA:  \nSHRIMATI RITI PATHAK: \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA: \nSHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the tourism circuits across the country coming under \nthe Swadesh Darshan Scheme; \n(b) whether the tourist places in the cities selected across the country \nunder the said scheme are likely to be renovated; and  \n(c) whether any tourist places in Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh, Jammu \n& Kashmir and Bihar and Gujarat have been selected under the \nsaid scheme? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM             (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned a total of 76 projects \nacross the country including in the States/Union Territory of Jharkhand, \nMadhya Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Bihar and Gujarat under  identified \nthematic circuits of Swadesh Darshan Scheme  (SDS 1.0).  The Scheme \nenvisages to improve the tourist facilities and infrastructure at the \nidentified locations.  \n \nThe details of projects sanctioned by Ministry of Tourism in the \ncountry under identified themes of SDS 1.0 is annexed.   \n******** \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. †540 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING TOURIST \nCIRCUITS SELECTED UNDER SDS \n \nThe details of projects sanctioned by Ministry of Tourism in the \ncountry under identified themes of SDS 1.0 \n \nBUDDHIST CIRCUIT     \n  (Amt. in Rs. Crore)                                                                                                                                                                                                \nS.No. Name of \nState \nYear Project Name Amt. \nSanctioned \n1. Madhya \nPradesh \n(2016-17) Development of Sanchi -\nSatna-Rewa-Mandsaur-Dhar. \n74.02 \n2. Uttar \nPradesh \n(2016-17) Development of Srawasti, \nKushinagar, & Kapilvastu. \n87.89 \n \n3. Bihar (2016-17) Construction of Convention \nCentre at Bodhgaya \n95.18 \n4. Gujarat (2017-18) \n \nDevelopment of Junagadh - \nGir Somnath - Bharuch-\nKutch- Bhavnagar- Rajkot- \nMehsana \n26.68 \n5. Andhra \nPradesh \n(2017-18) \n \nDevelopment of Buddhist \nCircuit: Shalihundam -\nThotlakonda- Bavikonda- \nBojjanakonda- Amravati- \nAnupu in Andhra Pradesh  \n24.14 \nTotal 307.91 \n \nCOASTAL CIRCUIT  \n   (Amt. in Rs. Crore)                                                                                                                                                                           \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nState/UT \nYear Project Name Amt. \nSanctioned \n1 Andhra \nPradesh \n(2014-15) Development of Circuit at \nKakinada - Hope Island - \nCoringa Wildlife \nSanctuary - Passarlapudi \n- Aduru - S Yanam - \n67.84 \n\nKotipally \n2 Andhra \nPradesh \n(2015-16) Development of \nNellore,Pulikat Lake, \nUbblamadugu water falls, \nNelapattu Bird Sanctuary, \nMypadu Beach, \nRamatheertham \n49.55 \n3 Puducherry (2015-16) Development of \nDubrayapet, Arikamedu \nChina Veerampattinam, \nChunnabmar, Nallavadu, \nManapet, Kalapet, French \nQuarter, Tamil  Quarter \nand Yanam \n58.44 \n4 West Bengal (2015-16) \n \nDevelopment of Beach \nCircuit: Udaipur - Digha- \nShankarpur- Tajpur- \nMandarmani- Fraserganj-\nBakkhlai-Henry Island \n67.99 \n5 Maharashtra (2015-16) Development of \nSindhudurg Coastal \nCircuit (Shiroda Beach, \nSagareshwar, Tarkarli, \nVijaydurg (Beach & \nCreek), Devgad  (Fort & \nBeach), Mitbhav, \nTondavali, Mocehmad \nand Nivati Fort). \n19.06 \n6 Goa (2016-17) Development of \nSinquerim-Baga, Anjuna -\nVagator, Morjim -Keri, \nAguada Fort and Aguada \nJail.   \n97.65 \n7 Odisha \n \n(2016-17) Development of Gopalpur, \nBarkul, Satapada and \nTampara. \n70.82 \n \n8 Andaman & \nNicobar \nIslands \n(201617) Development of Long \nIsland-Ross Smith Island - \nNeil Island - Havelock \nIsland- Baratang Island -\nPort Blair. \n27.57 \n\n9 Tamil Nadu (2016-17) Development of Chennai - \nMamamallapuram – \nRameshwaram – \nKulasekaranp