 of \nRs. 5,000 crores required for two nodes to join Pharma City of Hyderabad and \nNIMZ at Zahirabad in the Budget and if so, the details thereof and the action \ntaken by the Government thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (FINANCE) \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) & (b ):  Hyderabad Pharma City NIMZ was established  in Rangareddy District, \nTelangana in December, 2019 with the objective to provide impetus and industry-friendly \nintegrated environment to the Pharma industry as well as to attract new investments in \nmanufacturing and allied segments of Pharma industry.  \n \n \n \n \n\n. \n (c) & (d):  The State Government of Telangana had requested in December, 2020  to \nconsider budget provision of Rs. 870 Crores in  the financial year 2021 -22 to support \ndevelopment of Hyderabad Pharma City. They were informed that Department for \nPromotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT) had already conveyed to them that they \nhave already fulfilled their  financial commitment of extending assistance for funding the \ncost of master planning to the said NIMZ and that for development of infrastructure in the \nNIMZ, its role is limited to coordination only. They were also informed that the project \nproposals are required to be  prepared by the State Government as per norms and \nprocedures applicable to Central Sector/ Centrally Sponsore d/State Sector \nschemes/projects. DPIIT forwards any such proposals to the concerned Ministries and \ncoordinates between the State Government and the line Ministries. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |542", "t": "Funds Sanctioned for Pilgrimage Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.542 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nFUNDS SANCTIONED FOR PILGRIMAGE CENTRES \n \n542. PROF. SOUGATA RAY: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of Central funds sanctioned since the last three years to \nvarious pilgrim centres of the country, State-wise; \n(b) whether the Government has any mechanism to monitor proper \nimplementation of its objectives; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): Ministry of Tourism does not release funds to any Pilgrim centre  \nunder any of its schemes.  However, the Ministry under its PRASHAD \nScheme provides Central Financial Assistance to the State \nGovernments/Union Ter ritory Administrations to create pilgrimage \ntourism infrastructure /amenities at the pre -identified destin ations on \nreceipt of project proposals from them in line with the scheme’ \nguidelines.  Details of the project approved under the scheme is Annexed. \n \n(b) & (c): Ministry of Tourism regularly follow -up all the respective State \nGovernments/Union Territory Adm inistrations for timely completion of \napproved projects and monitors execution thereof through Monthly \nProgress Reports, regular site visits and period ic reviews at various \nlevels. \n \n********* \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) OF LOK S ABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.542  ANSWERED ON 06.0 2.2O23 REGARDING FUNDS \nSANCTIONED FOR PILGRIMAGE CENTRES. \nState/Union Territory wise details of the projects sanctio ned under \nPRASHAD Scheme of the Ministry of Tourism  \n \n (Crore ₹) \nState/UTs Project \nNos \nName of the Project Year of \nSanction \nApproved \ncost \nANDHRA \nPRADESH \n1.  Development of Amaravati \nTown, Guntur District as a \nTourist Destination \n2015-16 27.77 \n2.  Development of Srisailam \nTemple 2017-18 43.08 \n3.  Development of Pilgrimage \nAmenities at Sri Varaha \nLakshmi Narsimha Swami \nVari Devasthanam at \nSimhachalam in \nVishakhapatnam \n2022-23 54.04 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH \n4.  Development of Parasuram \nKund, Lohit Distt. 2020-21 37.88 \nASSAM 5.  Development of Kamakhya  \nTemple and Pilgrimage \nDestination in and around \nGuwahati \n2015-16 29.80 \nBIHAR 6.  Development of basic \nfacilities at Vishnupad \ntemple, Gaya, Bihar \n2014-15 4.27 \n7.  Development at Patna \nSahib 2015-16 41.54 \nCHHATTISGARH 8.  Development of Maa \nBamleshwari Devi Temple, \nRajnandgaon, Dongargarh, \nChhattisgarh \n2020-21 43.33 \nGUJARAT \n \n9.  Development of Dwarka 2016-17 13.08 \n10.  Pilgrimage Amenities at \nSomnath 2016-17 45.36 \n\n11.  Development of \nPromenade at Somnath \nunder PRASHAD Scheme \n2018-19 47.12 \n12.  Development of Pilgrim \nPlaza with Queue \nManagement Complex at \nSomnath Gujarat \n2021-22 49.97 \n13.  Development of Pilgrimage \nTourism Infrastructure at \nAmbaji Temple of \nBanaskantha, Gujarat  \n2022-23 50.00 \nHARYANA 14.  Development of Nada \nSaheb Gurudwara  and \nMata Mansha Devi Temple \nin Panchkula District \n2019-20 49.52 \nJAMMU & \nKASHMIR \n15.  Development at Hazratbal,  2016-17 40.46 \nJHARKHAND 16.  Development of \nBaidyanathji Dham, \nDeoghar \n2018-19 39.13 \nKERALA 17.  Development at Guruvayur \nTemple 2016-17 45.19 \nMADHYA \nPRADESH \n18.  Development of \nOmkareshwar 2017-18 43.93 \n19.  Development of \nAmarkantak 2020-21 49.99 \nMAHARASHTRA 20.  Development of \nTrimbakeshwar 2017-18 52.92 \nMEGHALAYA 21.  Development of Pilgrimage \nFacilitation in Meghalaya  2020-21 29.32 \nMizoram 22.  Development of \ninfrastructure for \nPilgrimage and Heritage \nTourism in the State of \nMizoram under PRASHAD \nScheme \n2022-23 44.88 \nNAGALAND 23.  Development of Pilgrimage \nInfrastructure in Nagaland 2018-19 25.26 \n \n24.  Development of Pilgrimage \nand Tourism infrastructure \nat Zunheboto, Nagaland \nunder PRASHAD Scheme \n2022-23 18.18 \nODISHA 25.  Infrastructure 2014-15 50.00 \n\nDevelopment at Puri, \nShree Jagannath Dham – \nRamachandi - Prachi River \nfront at Deuli under Mega \nCircuit \nPUNJAB 26.  Development of \nKarunaSagar Valmiki Sthal \nat Amritsar  \n2015-16 6.40 \n27.  Development of Chamkaur \nSahib at Rupar, Punjab \nunder PRASHAD Scheme \n2021-22 31.57  \nRAJASTHAN 28.  Integrated Development of \nPushkar/Ajmer 2015-16 32.64 \nSIKKIM 29.  Development of Pilgrimage \nFacilitation at Four Patron \nSaints, Yuksom \n2020-21 33.32 \nTAMILNADU 30.  Development of \nKanchipuram 2016-17 13.99 \n31.  Development of Vellankani 2016-17 4.86 \nTELANGANA 32.  Development of Jogulamba \nDevi Temple, Alampur 2020-21 36.73 \n33.  Development of pilgrimage \ninfrastructure at \nBhadrachalam, Bhadadri \nKottagudam Distt of \nTelangana  \n2022-23 41.38 \n34.  Development of Pilgrimage \nand Heritage Tourism \ninfrastructure at \nRudreshwar (Ramappa) \nTemple, Mulugu \n2022-23 62.00 \nTRIPURA 35.  Development of Tripura \nSundari Temple, Udaipur 2020-21 37.80 \nUTTARAKHAND 36.  Integrated Development of \nKedarnath 2015-16 34.77 \n37.  Development of \nInfrastructure for \nPilgrimage Facilitation in \nBadrinath Ji Dham \n(Uttara"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |543", "t": "Funds allocated to Non-attainment City under NCAP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 543 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nFunds allocated to Non-Attainment City under NCAP \n \n543:  SHRI HASNAIN MASOODI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)  the total amount of funds allocated to the non-attainment city of Srinagar under the \nNational Clean Air Programme (NCAP) for the year 2021-22;  \n(b)  the details on the total amount of funds spent under NCAP to mitigate the issue of \nrising air pollution during the financial year 2020-21 in the said city;  \n(c)  whether any action has been taken by the Government to phase out the 15-year old \ndiesel vehicles to mitigate the rising levels of pollution under the micro-action plan of \nNCAP; and  \n(d)  if so, the details of the action plan Chalked out in this regard, if not, the reasons \ntherefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a), (b), (c) & (d):  \nThe amount of Rs.7.95crore has been released to Srinagar city for taking measures for \nimprovement of air quality under National Clean air Programme (NCAP) in the year 2021-\n22.Srinagar City has utilized Rs.3.20 crore in the year 2020-21 for implementing city clean \nair action plan under NCAP. \n \nSrinagar city as part of city action plan has taken following measures in transport sector for \nimprovement of air quality: \n \ni. Ban of Commercial/Passenger Vehicles from plying on road in the state of J&K \non the attainment of 25 years of age w.e.f 01-04-2007. \nii. Compulsory fitness test for every six months for the vehicles between 15 to 25 \nyears of age. \niii. J&K transport department issued notification on 13.02.2020 to install Retrofitting \nEmission Control Devices/Equipments having min. capacity of 70% PM capturing \nefficiency for all Diesel driven commercial vehicles rating 1500 CC. \niv. 20 electric buses were procured and the same are on the road since April, 2019. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |544", "t": "Impact of Global Recession on Indian Economy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION No. 544 \nTO BE ANSWER ON 06 FEBRUARY 2023 \n \nImpact of Global Recession on Indian Economy \n \n544. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \nSHRI SUBBARAYAN K.: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of various reports published in media \nand the warning given by IMF Managing Director regarding global recession which is \nexpected to occur in 2023; \n(b) if so, the reaction of the Government thereto; \n(c) whether the Government has made any assessment on impact of global \nrecession in India; \n(d) if so, the details and the findings thereof; and \n(e) the corrective measures taken/to be taken by the Government to counter the \nsame? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE  \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)-(b): The government has taken note of the January 2023 update of the World \nEconomic Outlook (WEO), which has projected global growth to decline from an \nestimated 3.4 percent in 2022 to 2.9 percent in 2023. The WEO also projects global \ninflation to fall from 8.8 percent in 2022 to 6.6 percent in 2023. The government is \nfurther aware of the downside risks to these projections from uncertain outcomes \nassociated with the reopening of the Chinese economy and persisting war in Europe. \nThe government is therefore of the view that countries around the world will continue \nto face policy challenges to navigate their respective economies.  \n \n(c)-(d): Following the war in Europe, gl obal supply chains were disrupted resulting in \nthe increase of global commodity prices. To restrain the consequent inflation, major \ncentral banks around the world undertook monetary tightening. High global inflation \nand monetary tightening have together sl owed global growth. The government \nrecognises the impact of slowing global growth on the Indian economy. The impact \nwas seen in the first half of 2022 -23, in the widening of the current account deficit  \n(CAD), uptick in retail inflation, the outflow of portfolio investments , and the \n\nappreciation of the US$ against the  ₹. In the second half of 2022 -23, retail inflation \nhas fallen below the tolerance ceiling, portfolio investments have started to return, \nthe ₹ has stabilized against the US$, but export growth has declined with the slowing \nof global growth.   The current account deficit thus needs to be closely monitored. \nIndia has a solid cushion of forex reserves to finance the CAD and intervene in the \nforex market to reduce the excess volatility in the Indian rupee. The Indian economy, \nhowever, as per the January 2023 update of WEO, was the fastest-growing major \neconomy in 2022 and will be so in 2023 as well. This reflects India’s underlying \neconomic resilience and strong macroeconomic fundamentals.  \n \n(e): Government of India has implemented several measures to limit the impact of \nexternal factors on India’s inflation and growth.  The price situation of major essential \ncommodities is monitored by the Central Government on a regular basis and \ncorrective action is taken from time to time. Several supply-side measures have been \ntaken by the Government to address inflation. These include inter alia, reduction in \nexcise duty by Rs. 8 per litre on petrol and Rs. 6 per litre on diesel on May 21, 2022, \nprohibition of export of wheat products under HS Code 1101, imposition of export \nduty on rice, reduction in import duties and cess on pulses, maintenance of buffer \nstock for onion and pulses, rationalisation of tariffs on edible oils, oil seeds, and \ninputs used in manufactured products. \n \nTo boost growth, a series of measures have been announced in the Union Budget \n2023-24, including an increase in capital investment outlay for the third year in a row \nby 33 percent to ₹10 lakh crore (3.3 percent of GDP) ; the enhanced outlay for PM \nAwas Yojana,  launch of Aspirational Blocks Programme covering 500 blocks for \nsaturation of essential government services ; increase in agriculture credit target to \n₹20 lakh crore with a focus on animal husbandry, dairy and fisheries ; and setting up \nof Agriculture Accelerator Fund to encourage agri startups by young entrepreneurs in \nrural areas , among others.  Direct capital investment by the Centre is  also \ncomplemented by the  provision made for creation of capital assets through Grants -\nin-Aid to States. The ‘Effective Capital Expenditure’ of the Centre is budgeted at  13.7 \nlakh crore (4.5 per cent of GDP) for 2023 -24. The newly established Infrastructure \nFinance Secretariat will oversee the increase in private investment in infrastructure . \nFurther, to improve logistics performance, o ne hundred critical transport \ninfrastructure projects, for last and  first mile connectivity for ports, coal, steel, \nfertilizer, and food grains sectors, have been identified and will be prioritised. \n \n*****  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |545", "t": "MUDRA Loans", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial services \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 545 \nANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 06, 2023/MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \nMUDRA Loans \n 545.  SHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA : \n      Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the salient features of the Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana (PMMY); \n(b) whether the said scheme, whose purpose is to provide collateral free loans upto  Rs. 10 lakh to \nthe micro/small business enterprises and individuals to establish and expand their business \nactivities is achieving its unique objective of facilitating self-employment; \n(c) if so, the details thereof alongwith ratio of male and female beneficiaries under the said scheme \nin Rajasthan during the last three years; and \n(d) whether any important steps are being taken to address the anomalies in the implementation of \nthe said scheme and if so, the details thereof? \n ANSWER \nMinister of State in the Ministry of Finance \n(Dr. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n(a) Under the Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY), collateral-free institutional credit up to Rs. \n10 lakh is provided by Member Lending Institutions (MLIs) i.e. Scheduled Commercial Banks \n(SCBs), Regional Rural B anks (RRBs), Non -Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs) and Micro \nFinance Institutions (MFIs) .  Any individual, who is otherwise eligible to take a loan and has a \nbusiness plan for small business enterprise can avail loan under the Scheme. He/  She can avail \nloans for income generating activities in the manufacturing, trading, services sector and also for \nactivities allied to agriculture  across three loan products, viz. Shishu (loans up to  Rs. 50,000), \nKishore (loans above Rs. 50,000 and up to Rs. 5 lakh) and Tarun (loans above Rs. 5 lakh and up to \nRs. 10 lakh).   \n(b) and (c)  Ministry of Labour and Employment (MoLE) had conducted a large sample survey at \nthe national level to assess employment generation under PMMY. As per the survey results, \nPMMY helped in generatin g 1.12 crore net additional employment during a period of \napproximately 3 years (i.e. from 2015 to 2018). \n\n Out of the total 81 lakh loans extended in the State of Rajasth an, more than 52 lakh loans have \nbeen extended to Women entrepreneurs in the last three financial years. This amounts to 64% of \nloans to Women. \n \n(d)   Any matter relating to implementation of PMMY including, delay in turn-around-time (TAT), \nturning down of loan applications and lenders insistence on collateral/guarantor on certain \noccasions are redressed in coordination with the respective banks.  \nThe Scheme is reviewed from time to time wherein the scope and coverage of the Scheme has been \nextended as furnished below : \n• In FY 2016-17, activities allied to agriculture, viz; pisciculture, beekeeping, poultry, livestock \nrearing, grading, sorting, aggregation agro industries, diary, fishery, agri -clinics and \nagribusiness centres, food and agro -processing, etc (excluding crop loans, land improvement \nsuch as canals, irrigation, wells) and services supporting these, which promote livelihood or \nare income generating were brought under the ambit of PMMY. \n• Since FY 2017-18, loans sanctioned for purchase for Tractors and Power Tillers  are included \nas eligible loans under PMMY, subject to the upper ceiling of Rs. 10 lakh. \n•  Further, from 2018-19 onwards, loans sanctioned for purchase of two-wheelers by individuals \nfor commercial purpose have also been included under PMMY. \n \n                                                                                 ******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |546", "t": "Grievances of Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 546 \nANSWERED ON 06/02/2023 \n \nGRIEVANCES OF STUDENTS \n \n†546.   DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \nSHRI BALAK NATH: \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up a grievance redressal cell to address the \ngrievances of the students who apply online for various scholarships in the country including \nRajasthan and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to start a grievance redessal cell in the Financial \nManagement System for redressal of grievances and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) whether the Government has any system in place to rectify the mistake committed while \napplying in the Public Financial Management System and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a):The scholarship schemes are implemented through the National Scholarship Portal (NSP) \nnamely https://scholarships.gov.in. The NSP has phone -based helpdesk, available from 8.00 \n\nA.M. to 8.00 P.M. on all days except holidays. In addition, a dedicated e -mail is also made \navailable on the portal. Further, applicants can also lodge grievances online on Centralised  \nPublic Grievance Redress and Monitoring System (CPGRAMS). Verification of the applications \nare conducted by the authorities in the college/ universities where the stude nts are studying. NSP \nhas a provision where a verification officer can mark an application ‘defective’ if she/ he finds \nsome inconsistency in the application and seeks additional information/ documents for decision. \nThe verification officers are advised to  apply due diligence while verifying an application. In \naddition, the AICTE has developed an online Centralised Support System (CSS) to facilitate \nresolving of grievances of all stakeholders in the AICTE approved institutions. The weblink for \nthe portal is  https://css.aicte-india.org/login. The University Grants Commission (UGC) also \nhave an online facility for handling grievances of students available at  \nhttps://samadhaan.ugc.ac.in/. \n \n(b) & ( c): The applications for scholarships are processed and verified online as per respective \nscheme guidelines. Payment of the students eligible to receive scholarships is done through \nDirect Benefit Transfer (DBT) mode to the Bank Account of the student. The Public  Financial \nManagement System (PFMS) processes the payments for scholarships. To facilitate rectification \nof mistakes committed, if any, PFMS has provided an online facility to check the payment status \nunder ‘Track NSP Payments’ on the portal https://pfms.nic.in/static/TrackNSPpayments.aspx. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |547", "t": "Upgradation of Major Tourist Places", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.547 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nUPGRADATION OF MAJOR TOURIST PLACES \n \n547.  SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:  \n     \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government propos es to upgrade all major tourist places \nwith the new technology; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the details of the steps taken by the Government to make tourist places \nmore attractive;  \n(d) the number of domestic touris t visits in the country especially for the \nState of Uttar Pradesh; \n(e) whether the Government is developing spiritual tourism circuit in the \ncountry; \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(g)  the de tails of the completion o f the Ramayana, Mahabharat and \nBuddhist Circuits in the State of Uttar Pradesh? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM             (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): Development of tourism is primarily the responsibility of the State \nGovernments/Union Territory  Administrations.  However, t he Ministry of \nTourism under its schemes of ‘Swadesh Darshan’ and ‘National Mission on \nPilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive \n(PRASHAD)’ provides central financial assista nce to State Governments/UT \nAdministrations etc. for development of tourism infrastructure in the country \nand to provide complete tourism experience to the visitors. The Ministry of \nTourism has now revamped its Swadesh Darshan scheme as Swadesh \nDarshan 2.0  (SD2.0) for development of sustainable and responsible tourist \ndestinations. The guidelines of SD2.0 includes illustrative list of admissible \ncomponents covering use of clean energy sources, renewable sources of \nenergy for tourist infrastructure, access t o clean technology, asset ma pping, \ndigital technologies and platforms, procurement of equipment for eco -friendly \nmodes of tourist transport etc.  \n \n(d):  The details of the domestic tourist visits in the country including Uttar \nPradesh is at annexure-I.   \n \n(e) to (g): The Ministry of Tourism under the theme of Spiritual Circuit has \nsanctioned 13 projects, the detail of which is at annexure-II. \n********* \n\n \nAnnexure-I  \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART ( d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO.547 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING UPGRADATION OF MA JOR \nTOURIST PLACES. \n \nDETAILS OF DOMESTIC TOURIST VISITS IN VARIOUS STATES OF THE \nCOUNTRY INCLUDING UTTAR PRADESH.  \nS. No. States/UTs DTV 2021 \n1 Tamil Nadu 115336719 \n2 Uttar Pradesh 109708435 \n3 Andhra Pradesh 93277569 \n4 Karnataka 81333659 \n5 Maharashtra* 43569238 \n6 Telangana 32000620 \n7 Punjab 26640429 \n8 Madhya Pradesh 25554067 \n9 Gujarat 24525210 \n10 West Bengal 24325984 \n11 Rajasthan 21988734 \n12 Uttarakhand 19434475 \n13 Jammu & Kashmir 11314920 \n14 Delhi* 10642477 \n15 Kerala 7537617 \n16 Himachal Pradesh 5632270 \n17 Chhattisgarh 4747417 \n18 Odisha 3742221 \n19 Jharkhand 3383642 \n20 Goa 3308089 \n21 Bihar 2501193 \n22 Haryana 2025450 \n23 Assam 1409161 \n24 Puducherry 1253213 \n25 Sikkim 511669 \n26 Daman & Diu 463420 \n27 Leh Ladakh 303023 \n28 Chandigarh 228809 \n29 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 197802 \n30 Tripura 177816 \n31 Meghalaya 154409 \n32 A&N Island 126238 \n\n33 Arunachal Pradesh 102915 \n34 Mizoram 87232 \n35 Manipur 49371 \n36 Nagaland 23968 \n37 Lakshdweep 13500 \n \n*: Data for 2021 is estimated by applying all I ndia growth rate for 2021/19 on \n2019 data \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-II \n  \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART ( e) TO ( g) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.547 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING UPGRADATION OF \nMAJOR TOURIST PLACES. \n \nDETAILS OF PROJECTS SANCTIONED UNDER SP IRITUAL CIRCUIT THEME OF \nSWADESH DARSHAN SCHEME  \n (Amt. in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nState \nYear Project Name Amt. \nSanctione\nd \nAmt. \nReleased \n1. Kerala (2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of \nSabarimala - Erumeli-\nPampa-Sannidhanam as a \nSpiritual Circuit in      \nDistrict Pathan amthitta, \nKerala. \n54.88 27.44 \n2. Manipur (2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Shri \nGovindajee Temple, Shri \nBijoy Govindajee  Temple – \nShri Gopinath Temple – \nShri Bungshibodon Temple \n– Shri Kaina Temple. \n45.34 43.04 \n3. Kerala \n \n(2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Sree \nPadmanabha Temple, \nAranmula- Sabrimala \n78.08 \n \n73.77 \n \n4. Bihar \n \n(2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Kanwaria \nRoute: Sultanganj- Mojma- \nBanka. \n44.76 \n \n42.52 \n \n5. Uttar \nPradesh \n(2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Spiritual \nCircuit-I(Shahjahanpur-\nAllahabad-Basti- Ahar-\nAligharh-Kasgunj-Sarosi-\nPratapgarh-Unnao-\nKaushambi- Mirzapur-\nGorakpur- Kairana- \nDoamriyagunj- Bagpat-\nBarabanki- Azamgarh) \n71.91 68.32 \n6. Uttar \nPradesh \n(2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Bijnor - \nMeerut- Kanpur- Kanpur \nDehat- Banda- Ghazipur- \nSalempur- Ghosi- Balia- \nAmbedkar Nagar - Aligarh- \nFatehpur- Deoria- Mahoba- \n67.51 64.14 \n\nSonbhadra- Chandauli- \nMishrikh- Bhadohi \n7. Rajasthan (2016-\n17) \nDevelopment of Churu \n(SalasarBalaji)– Jaipur \n(Shri SamodeBalaji, \nGhatkeBalaji, \nBandhekeBalaji)- Alwar \n(PandupoleHanuma"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |548", "t": "Decline in Foreign Exchange Reserve", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \n DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 548 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDecline in Foreign Exchange Reserve \n \n548.  SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n         SHRI V.K. SREEKANDAN: \n        \n Will the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the country’s foreign exchange reserves have in recent months witnessed a \nsteep decline, if so the details thereof:  \n(b) whether the Government is taking/intends to take measures in order to stabilise  the \nGold and Forex reserves available with the country, if so, the details thereof and if not, \nthe reasons thereof; \n(c) whether the forex reserves of the country has declined by 691 million US dollars to \napproximately 562 billion US dollars as of December 23,  2022 making it the second \nconsecutive week of decline in the forex kitty and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) whether the forex reserve had been declining to defend the rupee amid pressure caused \nby global development and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to  (c): The Foreign Exchange Reserves of India stood at US$ 607.3 billion as end -\nMarch 2022 followed by a declining trend largely, till October 2022 , when it stood at US$ \n524.52 billion as on 21st October, 2022. Thereafter, it has been on an increasing trend. The \nforex reserves were at comfortable level of US$ 562.72 billion as of end -December 2022, \ncovering 9.3 months of imports.  Further, as on 27th January 2023, the Reserve s stand at \nUS$ 576.76 billion.  \n \n \n \n\n \nAccretion to or depletion of forex  reserves depends on the amount of net capital inflows \nthrough the capital account and the absorption of such inflows by the economy as \nmeasured by the balance on the current account of the balance of payments. If the capital \ninflows are more than the abso rptive capacity represented by the current account deficit \n(CAD), the excess capital inflows would add to the forex reserves.  \nThe Government has taken  various measures to encourage foreign direct investment and \nforeign portfolio investments and also enhan ce exports. The Government has taken, inter -\nalia, the following measures to boost exports and reduce trade deficit: \n Foreign Trade Policy (2015-20) extended upto 31-03-2023. \n Interest Equalization Scheme on pre and post shipment rupee export credit has \nalso been extended upto 31-03-2024. \n Assistance provided through several schemes to promote exports, namely, Trade \nInfrastructure for Export Scheme (TIES) and Market Access Initia tives (MAI) \nScheme.  \n Remission of Duties and Taxes on Exported Products (RoDTEP) scheme. \n Districts as Export Hubs has been launched by identifying products with export \npotential in each district, addressing bottlenecks for exporting these products and \nsupporting local exporters/manufacturers to generate employment in the district. \nTo promote Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), the Government has put in place an \ninvestor-friendly policy, wherein except for a few sectors, most sectors are open for 100% \nFDI under the Automatic route. Further, the policy on FDI is reviewed on an ongoing \nbasis, and its provisions have been progressively liberalized and simplified across various \nsectors in the recent past to make India an attractive investment destination.  \n   \n(d) The value of the Indian Rupee (INR) is market -determined. The RBI intervenes only to \nmaintain orderly market conditions by containing excessive volatility in the exchange \nrate, without reference to any pre-determined target level or band.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |549", "t": "Borrowing Limits of States", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF EXPENDITURE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  549 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, 6th FEBRUARY, 2023 \n 17 MAGHA, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nBorrowing Limits of States \n \n549. SHRI V.K. SREEKANDAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government’s decision to bring down the borrowing limit of States had \nsystematically stymied the raising of funds for infrastructure development and undermined \nwelfare programmes, including pensions of the State Government’s employees in Kerala and \nif so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto; \n(b) whether the Government had set Kerala’s borrowing limit for the 2022-23 fiscal at 3.5 per \ncent of the Gross State Domestic Product which is estimated at Rs. 32,439 crore and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is aware that a more realistic standard is required to fix the net \nborrowing limit for States and if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government \nthereto; \n(d) whether the Government has received any representation from the Government of Kerala to \nallow for more borrowing; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY)  \n \n(a) to (c): Based on the recommendation of the 15th Finance Commission (XV-FC), the normal \nNet Borrowing Ceiling (NBC) of the States including Kerala have been fixed at 3.5 percent of the \nGross State Domestic Product (GSDP) for the financial year 2022-23. GSDP for this purpose has \nbeen calculated based on the methodology prescribed by the XV-FC and data published by \n\nNational Statistical Office (NSO). Accordingly, the normal NBC of the State of Kerala has been \nfixed at Rs. 32,439 crore for the financial year 2022-23. In addition, extra borrowing ceiling of Rs. \n1,700 crore has been allowed to State of Kerala equivalent to estimated combined share of State \nand its employees’ contribution pertaining to the financial year 2022-23 to be actually deposited \nwith the designated authority as per the guidelines of National Pension System (NPS). In addition, \nStates are also eligible to avail additional borrowing of up to 0.50% of GSDP based on certain \nperformance criteria in power sector. Further, in order to boost capital expenditure by the States, \nfifty year interest free loan of Rs. 1,072.44 crore have already been released to the State of Kerala \nduring FY 2022-23 so far (upto 31.01.2023) under Scheme for Special Assistance to States for \nCapital Investment for 2022-23”. This amount is over and above, the normal net borrowing \nceiling. \n Instances of borrowings by certain State Public Sector companies, Special Purpose \nvehicles (SPVs) and other equivalent instruments, where principal and/or interest are to be \nserviced out of the State Budgets, had come to the notice of the Ministry of Finance. Considering \nthe effect of bypassing the Net Borrowing Ceiling (NBC) through such borrowings by some \nStates, as pointed out by the Comptroller & Auditor General, it was decided and communicated to \nthe States in March 2022 that borrowings by State Public Sector companies/corporations, Special \npurpose vehicles (SPVs) and other equivalent instruments, where principal and/or interest are to be \nserviced out of the State Budgets and/or by assignment of taxes/cess or any other State’s revenue, \nshall be considered as Borrowings made by the State itself for the purpose of issuing the consent \nunder Article 293(3) of the Constitution of India. \n(d) and (e): State of Kerala, inter-alia, requested to exclude the borrowings of State Government \nentities and public account liabilities of States in determining the Net Borrowing Ceiling of the \nStates. The State of Kerala also requested to consider off-budget borrowing on a net borrowing \nbasis rather than gross borrowing basis and accordingly, allow replacement borrowing for \nrepayment of such borrowings.  \n In view of magnitude of off-budget borrowings and difficulties expressed by some \nStates, the adjustment for such off-budget borrowing done by the States in 2021-22 was spread \nacross upto four remaining financial years of 15th Finance Commission award period (2022-23 to \n2025-26). Further, replacement borrowing for repayment of off-budget borrowing which has been \nraised on or after 01.04.2021 has also been allowed to the State of Kerala.   \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |55", "t": "Pace of Road Construction", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n�\n��������� �\n� ������������������ �����\n����������� �������������\n�\n������������ ���������������������������������\n�������������������������� �\n�\n�\n��������������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� ������� ������ ��� ������������� ��������� ��� �����\n����������� ��������������������������� ����� �������� ��������� ������������ ����� ���� ��������\n��������� ��������� ������������ ��������� ���������� ������������ ��������� ���� ������ ��������\n�����������������������������\n���������������������������������\n���� ������������ ������� ������ ��� ���� ������ ������������ ������� ����� ������������� ��������� ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n���� ������������ ���������� �\n������������������������ ��\n����������� �����������������������������������������������\n�\n������\n� �\n\n���������� ��������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ������ ���� ��� ���� ��� ���� ������\n����� ��� ��������� ���� ��� ���� ����������� ������ ��� ����� ������� ������\n����� ���� ���������������� �������������� ������ ���������������������� ���������������������� ���� ���\n��� ����������������������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� ��������� ����������� ������������� ������������� ������������� ���� �����������\n��� ���������� ��������� ��� ����������������� ������������ ����� ���� �������� ��������� ���������\n������������ ��������� ��������������������� ��������� ���� ������ �������� ������������������ ����\n����������������������������\n�\n������\n�\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |550", "t": "Inclusion of More Monuments of Assam in Protected Monuments List", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.550 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nINCLUSION OF MORE MONUMENTS OF ASSAM IN PROTECTED MONUMENT’S \nLIST \n \n550.  SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details and location of protected monuments in Assam as on date, monument-wise; \n(b) the amount spent on maintenance, conservation and construction work on these \nmonuments during the last three years, monument-wise; \n(c) whether the State Government of Assam has requested to include more monuments in the \nlist of protected monuments during the said period; and \n(d) if so, the action taken by the Government in this regard so far? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a)  \n& \n(b) \nThere are 55 Centrally Protected Monuments and Sites in Assam. Monument-wise \ndetails of amount spent on maintenance and conservation work during the last three \nyears is given at Annexure. \n (c) \n \nAt present no such request to include more monuments has been received from the \nState government.  \n(d) Question does not arise. \n--- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)&(b) OF LOK SABHA   \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.550 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \n    DETAILS OF CENTRALLY PROTECTED MONUMENTS AND SITES IN THE STATE \nOF ASSAM WITH EXPENDITURE AMOUNT SPENT ON CONSERVATION, \nPRESERVATION AND MAINTENANCE DURING LAST THREE YEARS. \n(amount in Rupees)  \n \nSl.No. Name of the site 2019-20  2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Ancient Cave, Jogighopa, Bongaigaon, \nGuwahati \n480716 422197 721544 \n2 Idgah at Rangamati Hill, panbari, \nDhubri \n200345 327448 286874 \n3 Rangamati Mosque, Panbari, Dhubri 251898 302076 1002258 \n4 Tomb of Lieutenant Cresswell, \nGoalpara \n104922 18108 0 \n5 Mr. B.J.Stow monuments, Goalpara 433022 0 96000 \n6 Sri Surya Pahar Ruins, Goalpara 3414107 781892 2870860 \n7 Urbasi island Carving Inscription & \npillar), Ghy \n114751 193166 180927 \n8 Poa Meeca Mosque (Stone Inscription), \nGuwahati \n0 0 0 \n9 Hayagriva Madhava temple, Hajo, \nKamrup, Ghy \n701248 459506 1049064 \n10 Kedar Temple, Hajo, Kamrup, \nGuwahati \n725015 2280120 143004 \n11 Ganesh temple, Hajo, Kamrup, \nGuwahati \n127525 162525 143435 \n12 Kameswar Temple, Hajo, Kamrup, \nGuwahati \n251019 367322 286016 \n13 Vishnu Janardhan, Kamrup, Guwahati 269154 378910 444009 \n14 Durargarila Rock Inscription,Kamakhya \nfoot hill, Guwahati \n240278 382599 362242 \n15 Rock-cut figures & One stone-\nGateway,Kamakhya Hill \n0 273491 539519 \n16 Bishnudol, Sivasagar 82974 149786 15081 \n17 Devidol, Sivasagar 48930 130242 366591 \n18 Sivadol, Sivasagar 989795 1852616 395216 \n19 Bishnudol, Gourisagar, Sivasagar 1390650 23050 1540664 \n20 Devidol, Gourisagar, sivasagar 556750 178047 131038 \n21 Sivadol, Gourisagar, Sivasagar 106446 164244 1726481 \n22 Gourisagar Tank, Gourisagar, Sivasagar 0 0 220800 \n23 Bishnudo, Joysagar, Sivasagar 747993 1337930 565640 \n24 Devidol, Joysagar, Sivasagar 1438628 223739 141693 \n25 Sivdol, Joysagar, sivasagar 0 0 1604181 \n26 Ghanasyam House, Joysagar, sivasagar 127451 169884 140414 \n27 Rangahar Ruins, Joysagar, Sivasagar 1748451 3149048 935588 \n28 Ranganath dole, Joysagar, Sivasagar 0 0 1094036 \n\n29 Karrenghar of the Ahom King \n(Talatalghar), Joysagar,  \n3066615 1300819 1397828 \n30 Golaghar (Magazine House) Joysagar, \nSivasagar \n257084 184745 141709 \n31 Eighth Cannons near sibasagar tank, \nJoysagar \n0 0 0 \n32 Ahom Raja’s Palace, Gargaon 1834431 587394 1743112 \n33 Sivdol, Negherating, Golaghat 976558 1030621 283420 \n34 Manoliths of Kasomari Pathar, Golaghat 29150 14430 710011 \n35 Group of Four Maidams, Charaido 1667110 2225722 2543372 \n36 Ruins at Singri Hill, Sonitpur, Tezpur 765053 186595 143008 \n37 Bamuni Hills (Masonary remains), \nTezpur \n1062297 1737577 968045 \n38 Chymmery Compund, Tezpur 0 0 0 \n39 Stone temple (Mounds & Ruins) at \nDaparbatia, Tezpur \n1352358 999072 143445 \n40 The rock on the bank of the \nBrahmaputra, Tezpur \n129051 156014 143008 \n41 Sukreswar Rock, Biswanath 702852 152030 143004 \n42 Biswanatha Sivalinga, Biswanath 0 0 0 \n43 Bordole temple, Biswanath 937469 368474 142561 \n44 Grave of Lieutenant Lewis, Biswanath 118914 120596 142567 \n45 Dhandi temple, Gohapur, Biswanath 283291 154854 143008 \n46 Grave of lt. Thomas Kennedy, \nBiswanath,  \n66930 152030 142142 \n47 Gun of Emperor Sher Shah, Sadiya, \nTinsukia \n0 0 0 \n48 Two swivel guns belonging to the \nMughal Nawara, Saidiya, Tinsukia \n0 0 0 \n49 Cacheri Ruins, Khaspur, Cachar,  2290617 2468897 320229 \n50 Derabara Group of monoliths, \nDubungling, Dima  \n0 120603 142140 \n51 Khartong group of monolithis, \nKartongsiip, dima-Haso \n0 0 0 \n52 Kobak Group of monolithis, Kobak, \nDima-Haso \n0 0 0 \n53 Rock cut temple, Maibong, Dima-Haso 126871 153311 140859 \n54 Two inscribed stone, Maibong, dima- \nHasao \n126752 152843 142146 \n55 Bolosan Grop of Manoliths,, \nNuchubunglo, Dima \n0 0 0 \n TOTAL 30345471 25994573 26678789 \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |551", "t": "Quality Education for Tribal Children in their Native Environment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- †551 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n \nQUALITY EDUCATION FOR TRIBAL CHILDREN IN THEIR  \nNATIVE ENVIRONMENT \n  \n†551.  SHRI RAMSHIROMANI VERMA: \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking any concrete steps to provide excellent quality education \nto the tribal children in their native environment, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes any scheme to open Eklavya Model Residential School in \nthe tribal areas of the country, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the total number of EMRS sanctioned and functioning in the country till date including \nShravasti and Balrampur districts of Uttar Pradesh; and \n \n(d) whether there is any population related norms for the said scheme, and if so, the details \nthereof? \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a) to (d): Government has decided to set up one Eklavya Model Residential School (EMRS) in \nevery block with 50% or more ST population and at least 20,000 tribal persons (as per 2011 \nCensus), subject to availability of suitable land to be provided by the State Government, to \nprovide quality education to the tribal students in their native environment. Accordingly, \nMinistry has set the target of establishing 740 EMRSs in the tribal areas across the country. \nEarlier EMRS was a component under ‘Grants under Article 275(1) of the Constitution of \nIndia’. In April 2019, a separate central sector scheme for EMRS was carved out and National \nEducation Society for Tribal Students (NESTS), an autonomous organization under the Ministry \nof Tribal Affairs, was created to manage and implement the scheme of EMRS. \nAs on 31st January, 2023, a total number of 690 EMRSs have been sanctioned, out of which 401 \nare reported to be functional. As per the existing norms for establishment of EMRS, no blocks \nhave been identified in Shravasti and Balrampur districts of Uttar Pradesh. \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |552", "t": "Displacement and Rehabilitation of Villages", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 552 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDisplacement and Rehabilitation of Villages \n \n552.  SHRI GUMAN SINGH DAMOR:  \nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the measures taken to conserve forests in Madhya Pradesh and the details of works done \nin this regard during the last three years alongwith the area of forests increased \nthereunder;  \n(b) the number of forest villages displaced due to conservation of National Parks in Madhya \nPradesh and the number of tribes dominated village out of these villages; \n(c) the details of scheme to rehabilitate the displaced forest villages in Madhya Pradesh and \nwhether all displaced villages have been rehabilitated and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether any families belonging to Tribal community were displaced due to Cheetah \nReintroduction Project in Kuno National Park, if so, the details of number of families who \nwere displaced;  \n(e) whether the displaced families have been promised of a fair rehabilitation and \ncompensation by the Government, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons \ntherefor; and  \n(f) the current status of the disbursement of the compensation and of the rehabilitation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a)  For restoration and conservation of forests, the Government of India is \nimplementing various centrally sponsored umbrella schemes in States/UTs, \nincluding Madhya Pradesh. These include National Mission for a Green India, \nIntegrated Development of Wildlife Habitats, and Forest Fire Prevention and \nManagement (FFPM) schemes. The funds collected under Compensatory \nAfforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority (CAMPA) as \ncompensatory levies from states are also used for plantation activities by \nStates/UTs. The schemes primarily support ecological restoration through \nafforestation in and outside forest areas, forest landscape restoration, habitat \nimprovement, soil and water conservation measures and protection etc.  \n \nAs per the information provided by the Government of Madhya Pradesh, \nplantation to the tune of 1,74,049 hectares has been done in the State from State \nGovernment budget and CAMPA funds. \n\n \nAs per the India State of Forest Report (ISFR) 2021 the State of Madhya \nPradesh has recorded an increase of 11 sq.km. in forest cover as compared to \nISFR 2019. \n \n(b) As per information received from the State of Madhya Pradesh, no forest village \nhas been displaced from the National Parks in Madhya Pradesh during the last \nthree years. However, 6 villages have been relocated on voluntary basis from \nWildlife Sanctuaries.  \n \n(c) As per the information received from the Government of Madhya Pradesh,all \nthe relocated villages have been rehabilitated. The rehabilitation of forest \nvillages is done as per the guidelinesdated 30.10.2008 of the State Government \nwhich are based on the guidelines issued by the National Tiger Conservation \nAuthority. \n \n(d) to (f) The Government of Madhya Pradesh has informed that  no families belonging \nto Tribal community have been displaced due to Cheetah Reintroduction Project \nin the Kuno National Park. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |553", "t": "Campuses of Foreign Educational Institutions in India", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 553 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \nCampuses of Foreign Educational Institutions in India \n553. SHRI SUBBARAYAN K.:  \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the public notice for comments, suggestions and feedback was issued by \nUniversity Grants Commission on January 5, 2023 on the draft regulations for setting up \nand operation of campuses of foreign higher educational institutions in India; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is a fact that a very short time was given for accepting the comments and \nsuggestions from the public; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION  \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n(a) to (d): University Grants Commission has drafted enabling Regulations to facilitate \nestablishment of campuses of Foreign Higher Educational Institutions in India. The draft \nUniversity Grants Commission (Setting up a nd Operation of Campuses of Foreign \nHigher Educational Institutions In India ) Regulations , 2023 was placed in the public \ndomain, vide public notice dated 5 th January, 2023 , seeking feedback , suggestions, \ncomments etc. from all the stakeholders by 18th January, 2023. However, in view of the \nrequests received from the stakeholders, the last date for r eceiving comments on the \ndraft Regulations has been extended till 20th February, 2023. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |554", "t": "Ramayana and Mahabharata on Internet Projects of NSU", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No- 554 \nANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n \nRAMAYANA AND MAHABHARATA ON INTERNET PROJECTS OF NSU \n \n554. DR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \nSHRI SHA.BRA.DR.JAI SIDDESHWAR SHIVACHARYA MAHASWAMIJI: \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \nSHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the status of the Ramayana and Mahabharata on Internet projects of the National Sanskrit \nUniversity; \n(b) the funds sanctioned and utilised for the completion of the projects; \n(c) whether the texts of the two epics were available on the websites of the Rashtriya Sanskrit \nUniversity and are now unavailable on the National Sanskrit University’s website and if s o, the \ndetails thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(d) whether the National Sanskrit University intends to bring back the texts of the two epics on the \nwebsite; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION  \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n(a): The Ramayana and Mahabharata on Internet projects of the National Sanskrit University were \ncompleted in 2008. \n \n(b): The expenditure incurred on Valmiki Ramayana and Mahabharata Projects is Rs.28.13 lakh and \nRs.8.88 lakh respectively. \n \n(c) to (e ): The texts of the two epics  are available at https://nsktu.ac.in/index.php/ \nramayana-on-internet/  and https://nsktu.ac.in/index.php/mahabharata-on-internet/ in the website of \nNational Sanskrit University. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |555", "t": "Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyan in Bihar", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 555 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGARIB KALYAN ROJGAR ABHIYAN IN BIHAR \n \n†555. SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has data bank regarding the people \nworking in unorganised sector in Bihar; and  \n(b)whether the people registered on e -shram portal have received any \nbenefit from the Government and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n \n(a): Yes, Sir.  \n \n(b): The registered workers on e -SHRAM are eligible to enrol \nthemselves into Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maandhan (PM -SYM) \nscheme of the Government. It is a voluntary and co -contributory \nscheme for providing monthly pension of Rs. 3000/ - on attaining the \nage of 60 years. The workers in the age group of 18 -40 years whose \nmonthly income is Rs. 15000/ - or less and not a member of \nEPFO/ESIC/NPS (Govt. funded) can join the scheme. Under the scheme, \n50% monthly contribution is payable by the beneficiary and equal \nmatching contribution is paid by the Central Government. \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |556", "t": "Tribal Research Institute", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-556 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nTRIBAL RESEARCH INSTITUTE \n \n \n \n  \n556. SHRI INDRA HANG SUBBA: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n  \n(a) the details of the fund allocated during the last five years to Sikkim under the Tribal Research \nInstitute (TRI); \n \n(b) the details of training undertaken by TRI in Sikkim during the last five years; and \n \n(c) the initiatives taken by the Government to promote the indigenous craft and textile industry in \nthe North Eastern States, particularly in Sikkim? \n  \n  \n  \n ANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a): Ministry of Tribal Affairs through Centrally Sponsored scheme “Support of Tribal Research \nInstitutes (TRIs)” is providing financial assistance to States / UTs for various activities to be \nundertaken by State Tribal Research Institutes including execution research programmes. The \nbasic objective of the scheme is to strengthen the Tribal Research Institutes (TRIs) and their \ninfrastructural needs, research & documentation activities and training and capacity building \nprogrammes etc. Ministry of Tribal Affairs is funding 27 Tribal Research Institutes in 25 States \nand 2 UTs under the said  scheme. Details of funds released to State Government of Sikkim \nduring last five years under Support to TRIs are at Annexure-I.  \n   \n \n \n(b) & (c): (i) Initiatives of MoTA and TRIFED:State TRIs take up training in their states. Details \nof training undertaken by TRI in Sikkim are at Annexure-II. The Ministry of Tribal Affairs \nprovides funds through the Pradhan Mantri Janjatiya Vikas Mission to Tribal Co-operative \nMarketing Development Federation of India (TRIFED) for providing marketing support to tribal \ngatherers and artisans through procurement of tribal products and produce and facilitating sale \n\nthrough retail outlets as well as different e-commerce platforms; by organising exhibitions, \nmelas, mahotsavs, supplier meets, etc. TRIFED has empanelled tribal products suppliers \ncomprising of individual tribal artisans, tribal Self Help Groups (SHGs), Organisations / \nAgencies / NGOs working with tribals etc. to accelerate growth of the tribal craft and products. \nTribesindia.com is the e-commerce portal through which TRIFED promotes and sells products \nmade by the tribals of India, through its 14 regional offices. In addition to this, TRIFED \nRegional Offices have accounts with the leading 3rd party marketplaces like Amazon, Flipkart, \nSnapdeal etc.  These activities are undertaken all over the country, including in Sikkim. The \nscheme of Marketing and Logistics Development for Promoting Tribal Products from North-\nEastern Region has been approved during 2021-22 which aims to strengthen livelihood \nopportunities for tribal artisans through increased efficiency in procurement, logistics and \nmarketing of tribal products. The scheme has been specifically designed for the North Eastern \nRegion including Sikkim.  \n(ii) The initiatives taken by M/o Development of North Eastern Region are at Annexure –III.  \n(iii) The Directorate of Handicrafts and Handlooms, Government of Sikkim in order to promote \nindigenous crafts has established Branch Training Centres in respect of various Arts and Crafts \nfor local youth in all the six districts of Sikkim. There are Sales Emporium at Gangtok and New \nDelhi and smaller outlets in various locations within the state for display and sale of our \nHandicrafts and Handloom products. \n  \n\n  Annexure-I \nAnnex referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.556 for \nanswer on 06.02.2023. \n \nDetails of funds released under the scheme ‘Support to TRIs’ \n                                                                                                               (Rs. in Lakh) \nSl. \nNo \nState Fund Released \n  2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Sikkim 136.00 194.50 82.50 144.00 273.3 \n  \n  \n \n \n \nAnnexure-II \nAnnex referred to in reply to part (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.556 for \nanswer on 06.02.2023. \n  \nFinancial \nYear Details of Training \n2018-2019 1. Training of TRl officials on implementation of FRA, 2006. \n2. Training of trainers in r/o district officials on FRA, 2006. \n3. Development of training modules and literature on FRA, 2006 in local \nlanguage. \n4. Awareness on FRA to all the Panchayats of the State. \n5. Week long capacity building programme on Lepcha tribal folk theatre-drama. \n6. One day seminar on value addition and market linkage or products of tribal \nartisans \n7. Capacity building-training of Lepcha Hat weaving (dying Art) including \nawareness on patent registration or geographical indication or bar-coding or \npackaging. \n2019-2020 Seven day National Workshop for Bhutia, Lepcha, Limboo, Tamang and Sherpa \nlanguage teachers on teaching and learning tribal languages in Sikkim. \n  \n2020-2021 1. Promotion of  Lepcha art of making ca"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |557", "t": "Development of Backward Areas through CSR Fund", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 557 \n ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/ MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA)  \n \n \n DEVELOPMENT OF BACKWARD AREAS THROUGH CSR FUND \nQUESTION \n557.  SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n          DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY:           \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to develop the socially, economically and  \neducationally backward areas of the country through the Corporate Social \nResponsibility (CSR) fund; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the criteria for selecting the said areas; and  \n(c)  the quantum of CSR funds allocated for the development of the said areas during \nthe last two years? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS \nAND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT \nCHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                                                 \n   [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n \n(a) & (b): The broad framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has been \nprovided under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the \nAct and Companies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014. As per the extant framework, the CSR \nprovisions are applicable across areas or regions including socially, economically \nand educationally backward areas of the country. Though the first proviso to Section \n135(5) of the Act provides that the comp any shall give preference to the local area \nand areas around where it operates, however, the emphasis on local area is only \ndirectory and not mandatory in nature as companies need to balance the local area \npreference with the national priorities. The same has also been clarified through \nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQs) on CSR issued vide General Circular no. 14/2021 \ndated 25.08.2021.  \n \nSimilarly, Schedule VII of the Act enlist the eligible CSR activities. As per Schedule \nVII, activities such as eradicating hunger, poverty and malnutrition, promoting health \ncare, provisioning of sanitation and safe drinking water, livelihood enhancement \nprojects, measures for reducing inequalities faced by socially and economically \nbackward groups, training to promote rural sports, contribution to any other fund set \nup by the central government for socio economic development and relief and welfare  \nContd…..2/- \n\n-2- \nof the schedule caste, tribes, other backward classes, minorities and women etc. are \nconsidered as eligible CSR activities. Further, the Ministry vide General Circular no. \n14/2021 dated 25.08.2021 had clarified that entries in Schedule VII are broad- based \nand must be interpreted  liberally so as to capture the essence of the subjects \nenumerated therein. As such socia lly, economically and educationally backward \nareas of the country are also intended to be important beneficiaries of CSR spending.   \n \nMoreover, as per the input provided by Department of Public Enterprises (DPE),  it \nhas been issuing guidelines from the fin ancial year (FY) 2018 -19 to all the \nadministrative Ministries and Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs) for \nadopting a theme-based focussed approach every year regarding CSR expenditure \nby the CPSEs. These guidelines, inter-alia, provide that CSR expenditure for such \nthematic programmes should be around 60% of annual CSR expenditure of the \nCPSEs and the aspirational districts as identified by NITI Aayog may be given \npreference while undertaking CSR activities.  \n \n(c):  Under the Act, CSR is a Board drive n process and the Board of the company \nis empowered to plan, decide, execute and monitor CSR activities based on the \nrecommendations of its CSR committee. The Government does not issue any \ndirections to the companies to spend in particular geographical are a or activity. \nOn the basis of filings made by the companies in the MCA 21 registry, companies \nhave spent an amount of Rs. 596.78 crores in 93 aspirational districts as identified \nby NITI Aayog in FY 2020 -21 (data up to 30.09.2022) as against Rs.334.78 crores \nin 85 aspirational districts in FY 2019 -20.  All data related to CSR filed by \ncompanies in the MCA21 registry, including district-wise data, are available in the \npublic domain and can be accessed at www.csr.gov.in. Further, the companies are \nrequired to file CSR details for FY 2021-22 on or before 31.03.2023. \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |558", "t": "Heal in India Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 558 \n ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nHEAL IN INDIA PROGRAMME \n \n558. SHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether, in a move to give a major boos t to medical tourism in \nthe country, the Government proposes to rollout a “Heal In \nIndia” programme to build India’s global image in the medical \ntourism; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to  develop a digital \nplatform to p rovide all information about hospitals, treatment, \nprocedures, accommodation, prices of treatment etc.; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the extent to which this step of the Government is likely to \nattract foreigners for medical tourism? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM           (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e): Yes, Sir.  Government of India is working on creating an \nenabling framework for providing health services to world at large \nby institutionalizing medical tourism through a Heal in India \ninitiative. The aim of this initiative is to harness this untapped \npotential and showcase India as a global hub for Medical and \nWellness tourism. \n \nAs conveyed by Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, the \n“Heal in India” initiative aims to provide impetus to  the growth of \nmedical value travel in India and promote India’s com petitive \nstrengths & advantages in terms of world -class care and treatment, \nwide network of traditional and modern medicine infrastructure \nacross the country, largest pool of health profes sionals across the \n\nspectrum of modern and traditional medicine, and unique offerings \nof Ayush.   \n \nThe initiative seeks to invite the world for “integrated and \nholistic treatment” in India by promoting India as a “Destination of \nChoice” for quality healthca re services.  “Heal in India” will perform \nthe two -fold objective of providing a much -needed boost to Indian \nTourism and Hospitality Industry and generating additional revenues \nfor Indian Health sector.  \n \nAlso in this initiative, Ministry of Health and Fam ily Welfare \n(MoHFW) is working on creating a one-stop digital portal for medical \nvalue travel, integrating all the relevant stakeholders in the \necosystem. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |559", "t": "North-East Special Infrastructure Development Scheme", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.559 \n \nTo be answered on \nMonday, February 6, 2023/Magha 17, 1944 (Saka) \n \nQUESTION  \nNorth - East Special Infrastructure Development Scheme \n \n559.  SHRI RAJU BISTA:  \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state:  \n(a)  the details of proposals received, approved and completed under the North East \nSpecial Infrastructure Development Scheme (NESIDS) since inception; \n(b)  the details of funds sanctioned under NESIDS till date, State/year-wise; and  \n(c) the details of projects sanctioned and completed under NESIDS in States of \nManipur and Sikkim, State/year-wise? \n \nANSWER \nThe Minister of the Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region \n[Shri G. Kishan Reddy]  \n \n(a)to (c) In all , 145 projects have been approved/sanctioned for a total cost of \nRs.3392.98 crore under North East  Special Infrastructure Development Scheme \n(NESIDS) since inception. The Year/State -wise details of the projects sanctioned with \ntheir sanctioned cost and projects completed for the North Eastern States (including \nManipur and Sikkim), under NESIDS since inception are annexed. \n \n\nAnnexure \n(Rs. in crores) \nThe Year/State-wise details projects sanctioned with their sanctioned cost and projects completed for the North Eastern States (including Manipur \nand Sikkim), under NESIDS since inception \nName of \nState \n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 Total \n  \nNo. of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nCost of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nNo. of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nCost of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nNo of \nproject \ncompleted \nNo. of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nCost of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nNo of \nproject \ncompleted \nNo. of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nCost of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nNo of \nproject \ncompleted \nNo. of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nCost of \nprojects \nsanctioned \nNo of \nproject \ncompleted \nArunachal \nPradesh 16 312.72 4 105.18 - 3 77.72 - 6 128.25 - 29 623.87 \n- \nAssam  5 209.57 12 300.36 - 1 97.49 - 15 273.40 - 33 880.82 \n- \nManipur 5 137.18 4 57.66 - 1 53.4 1 8 93.08 - 18 341.32 1 \nMeghalaya 6 174.81 1 1.60 1 2 57.56 - 3 106.52 - 12 340.49 1 \nMizoram 3 181.96 1 5.60 - 3 55.75 - 10 102.07 - 17 345.38 - \nNagaland 0 - 10 179.22 - 3 54.46 - 4 99.94 1 17 333.62 1 \nSikkim 2 103.31 1 10 - 1 36.45 - 4 64.75 - 8 214.51 \n- \nTripura - - 4 169.17 - 3 58.88 - 4 84.92 - 11 312.97 \n- \nTotal 37 1119.55 37 828.79 1 17 491.71 1 54 952.93 1 145 3392.98 3 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |56", "t": "Maintenance and Repair of NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 56 \nANSWER ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nMAINTENANCE AND REPAIR OF NHs \n56. DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the work of maintenance and repair of National Highways  \n(NHs) is carried out annually as per available budgetary outlay and \ntraffic density to keep such NHs in traffic worthy condition; \n(b) if so, the role played by the National Highways Authority of India  \n(NHAI), National Highways and Infrastructure Development Corporation \nLimited (NHIDCL) and Border Roads Organization (BRO) in maintenance \nand repair of NHs in the state of Bihar; \n(c) whether the responsibility of maintenance and repair of NHs in \ndistrict Gopalganj of Bihar rests with the agency to which the NHs are \nentrusted;  \n(d) if so, the measures taken by the said agency for maintena nce and  \nrepair the NHs in the said district; and \n(e) whether the damaged sign boards are replaced from time to time \nalong NHs in the said district and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (c)   The development and maintenance of N ational Highways \n(NHs) is a continuous process. The works on NHs are accordingly taken \nup from time to time through various agencies such as National \nHighways Authority of India (NHAI), National Highways and \nInfrastructure Development Corporation Limited (NHIDCL),  Border \nRoads Organisation (BRO) and State Government’s Public Works \nDepartments as per  available budgetary outlay, inter-se priority  and \ntraffic density, and to keep the NHs in traffic worthy conditions. \nIn state of  Bihar including district Gopalganj, National Highways \nare entrusted to NHAI and Road Construction Department, Government \nof Bihar.  \n The M aintenance & Repair of stretches of NHs, where either \nDevelopment works have co mmenced or Operation, Maintenance and \nTransfer (OMT) Concessions/ Operation and Maintenance (O&M) \nContracts have been awarded, are the responsibil ity of the concerned \nConcessionaires/Contractors till the Concession Period/Defect Liability \nPeriod (DLP). \nM&R of balance stretches of NHs  are carried out regularly as per \navailable budgetary outlay, inter -se priority and traffic density to keep \nsuch NHs in traffic worthy conditions. \n \n(d) & (e) In the Gopalganj district, NHAI has awarded the O&M works of \nBihar/UP Borders to Kotwa section from km. 360.915 to km. 440.00 of \nNH-27 to O&M contractor and the work has commenced. The work is \ntargeted for completion by 16.06.2023. The other three sections of NHs \nin Gopalganj distri ct with total length 41.26 km are under Defect \nLiability Period . As part of development and maintenance work , sign \nboards are replaced/installed along NHs in the said district from time to \ntime as per requirements and standard and specifications.  \n***** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |560", "t": "Promotion to Private Sector in Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†560 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPROMOTION TO PRIVATE SECTOR IN TOURISM \n \n†560. DR. RAMESH POKHRIYAL “NISHANK”:        \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   the details of the foreign tourists arriving in the country post \nCOVID;  \n(b)  the steps taken by the Government to make India an attractive \ndestination for tourists; and  \n(c)    the special efforts being made by the Government to promote \nprivate sector in tourism? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                        (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a):  The details of month -wise Foreign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) in \nIndia during 2020 to 2022 is placed at Annexure. \n \n(b): The Ministry of Tourism undertakes several promotional \nactivities in potential international & domestic markets to promote \nvarious tourism destinations and products of the country in holistic \nmanner. This includes release of media campaigns under ‘Incredible \nIndia’ brand-line, social media promotions, webinars, participation & \nsupport to promotional events, dissemination of information and \nengagement through website etc. In addition, the India tourism \noverseas offices of the Ministry of Tourism and Indian Missions \noverseas also undertake various promotional acti vities to attract \nmore global travelers to various tourist destinations of the country. \n \nFor making India attractive tourist destination, the Ministry of \nTourism has been working very closely with Ministry of Home \nAffairs and Ministry of External Affairs for easing of the Visa Regime \n\nin the country. e -Visa scheme is now available to the nationals \nof 165 countries and it is available in following five categories: \n \ni.    e-Tourist Visa, \nii.   e-Business Visa, \niii.  e-Medical Visa, \niv.  e-Conference Visa and \nv.   e-Medical Attendant Visa \n \ne-Visa is valid for entry through  29 designated Airports  and 5 \ndesignated seaports. \n  \nAlso, effective and adequate connectivity is one of the \nimportant aspect for developing a tourist destination. For this \npurpose, Ministry of Civil Aviation, Govt. of India has launched RCS -\nUDAN scheme, whose primary objective is to facilitate/stimulate \nregional air connectivity by making it affordable. Ministry of Tourism \nhas collaborated with the Ministry of Civil Aviation to extend \nfinancial support in t he form of Viability Gap Funding (VGF) under \nthe Champion Service Sector Scheme (CSSS), with the purpose of \nfurther improving connectivity to important tourist places including \nIconic sites. At present, 59 Tourism RCS (T -RCS) routes have been  \napproved by t he Ministry of Tourism, out of which 51 are \noperational. \n \n(c): Tourism is essentially a multi- sectoral activity requiring \nlinkages and coordination with various Ministries, Departments, \nState Governments/UT Administrations and Private entities. \n \nIn order  to develop tourism amenities at heritage/ natural/ \ntourist sites spread across India for making them tourist friendly, in \na planned and phased manner, Ministry of Tourism, Government of \nIndia has launched the “Adopt a Heritage: Apni Dharohar, Apni \nPehchaan” with an aim to encourage companies from public sector, \nprivate sector, trusts, NGOs, individuals and other stakeholders to \nbecome ‘Monument Mitras’ and take up the responsibility of \ndeveloping and upgrading the basic and advanced tourist ame nities \nat th ese sites as per their interest and viability in terms of a \nsustainable investment model under CSR.  They would also look \nafter the Operation & Maintenance of the same. \n********* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UN STARRED \nQUESTION NO. †560 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING \nPROMOTION TO PRIVATE SECTOR IN TOURISM. \n \nForeign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) in India during 2020, 2021 and 2022 \nMonth Foreign Tourist Arrivals \n2020 2021 2022 @ \nJanuary 11,19,250 94,662 2,01,546 \nFebruary 10,18,440 1,10,312 2,40,896 \nMarch 3,28,304 1,33,768 3,42,308 \nApril 2,820 78,718 3,92,930 \nMay 3,764 19,765 4,23,701 \nJune 8,590 36,070 5,22,737 \nJuly 12,655 72,501 6,40,858 \nAugust 19,761 92,728 4,98,243 \nSeptember 28,167 1,15,661 5,36,340 \nOctober 41,494 1,91,415 6,56,895 \nNovember 70,977 2,63,867 7,68,675 \nDecember 90,544 3,17,647 9,66,270 \nTotal (Jan-Dec) 27,44,766 15,27,114 61,91,399 \n@ Provisional \nSource: Bureau of Immigration \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |561", "t": "Allocation of Funds to States as per GER", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-561 \nANSWERED ON-06/02/2023 \n \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS TO STATES AS PER GER \n \n \n561.SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is true, that India's Gross Enrolment Ratio is only 27% while Tamil Nadu's \nGER is 51.4%. But the funds allocated to States are not based on GER performance but on \nState's total population; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and comparison re garding the GER of Large States in the \ncountry; \n \n(c) the funds allocated under various schemes of the Government in the last 5 years, year -\nwise and State-wise; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government has any plans to allocate funds from education cess  or from \nConsolidated Fund of India to various States for sustainable quantitative and qualitative \ngrowth of education to achieve targets set in the New Education Policy and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to ( b) The Gross Enrolment Ratio (GER) of Higher Education, based on the population \nprojections of 2011 for the year 2020 -21, as per All India Survey on Higher Education \n(AISHE) 2020 -21 is 27.3. The State -wise GER is ava ilable \nathttps://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques. \nIn Higher Education, funds are provided to States /UTs under the Centrally Sponsored \nScheme of R ashtriya Uchchatar Siksha Abhiyan (RUSA) . Under the scheme, State/UT \nGovernments submit their proposals as per the identified needs of higher education sector in \ntheir respective States/UTs based on norms set under the scheme. Approval of proposals is \ndone by the Project Approval Board of RUSA in which States/UTs participate as members of \nthe Board.  \n(c) The funds released to States /Union Territories during the last five years i.e. 2017 -18 to \n2021-22 under RUSA is available at https://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques. \n\n(d) The National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 unequivocally endorses and envisions a \nsubstantial increase in public investment in education both by the Central Government and all \nState Governments. The Centre and States work together to increase the public  investment in \neducation Sector to reach 6 % of Gross Domestic Product (GDP).  \nAs per the publication “Analysis of Budgeted Expenditure on Education 2018 -19 to \n2020-21”, Public Expenditure (Centre and States) on Education in India is 4.64% of GDP in \n2020-21, which has grown from 3.84% in 2013-14 (an overall growth of about 21% over the \nperiod). \n \nIn this regard, the Ministry of Education hasbeen allocated a record Budget of mo re \nthan One Lakh Crore rupees in the financial years 2022-23 and 2023-24. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |562", "t": "Promotion of Sustainable Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 562 \n ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF SUSTAINABLE TOURISM \n \n562. SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government organises regional workshops in the \ncountry, with a view to develop sustainable and responsible tourist \ndestinations and promote sustainable tourism; \n(b) if so, the State -wise details thereof and the total expenditure \nincurred during the Financial Years 20 20-21 and 2021-22 in \norganising such workshops; \n(c) whether the Government plans to organise such regional workshops \nin the State of Uttar Pradesh; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                     (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (b): Yes, Sir. With a view to develop sustainable and responsible \ntourist destinations and promote sustainable tourism in the country, \nMinistry of Tourism has organized regional workshops. \n \nThe first regional workshop for States/ UTs of Western and Central \nRegion (Madhya Pradesh, Chattisgarh, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Daman & Diu \n& Goa) was organized at Khajuraho, Madhya Pradesh on 29.11.2022. \n \nThe second workshop for the States/ UTs of Eastern Region (West \nBengal, Jharkhand, Bihar, Odisha and Andaman & N icobar) was organized \nat Kolkata, West Bengal on 11.01.2023.  \n \nFor organizing the aforesaid workshops, an expenditure of Rs. 20.18 \nlakh has been estimated. \n \n(c) to (d): Ministry of Tourism is considering to organize a Regional \nWorkshop in the Northern Reg ion. The location for the same has not been \ndecided. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |563", "t": "Beneficiaries under (PM-SYM) Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 563 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nBENEFICIARIES UNDER (PM-SYM) YOJANA \n \n563.  DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \n  SHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has been implementing Pradhan Mantri \nShram Yogi Maandhan (PM-SYM) scheme for unorganised workers, if \nso, the details thereof; \n(b)the criteria for registration under PM-SYM alongwith number of \nunorganized workers enrolled so far, State-wise; \n(c)the details of the number of beneficiaries under the PM -SYM yojana \nso far, State-wise; \n(d)the amount allocated for this scheme during the last three years, \nState-wise; and  \n(e)other steps taken/being taken by the Government for effective \nimplementation of the scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (e): In order to provide old age protection to unorganised  sector \nworkers, the Government of India launched a pension scheme in 2019 \nnamely Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Ma an-dhan Yojana (PM -SYM) to \nprovide a monthly pension of Rs. 3000/ - after attaining the age of 60 \nyears. This is a voluntary and co -contributory pe nsion scheme. The \nworkers in the age group of 18 -40 years whose monthly income is    \nRs. 15000/- or less and not a member of EPFO/ESIC/NPS (Govt. Funded) \ncan join the scheme. Under the scheme, 50% of monthly contribution \nis payable by the beneficiary and e qual matching contribution is paid \nby the Central Government. As on 31.01.2023, over 49 lakhs \nbeneficiaries have enrolled themselves across the country. Under the \nscheme, the funds are not allocated to any State/UT. The year wise \ndetails of amount allocated for the scheme so far, is as under; \nContd..2/- \n \n\n:: 2 :: \n \nYear Fund allocated \n(Rs. in crores) \nExpenditure (Rs. in \ncrores) \n2019-20 500.00 359.95 \n2020-21 500.00 319.71 \n2021-22 400.00 324.23 \n \nThe number of beneficiaries enrolled under PM -SYM \nscheme, Statewise is at Annexure. \n \nAll the States/UT Governments have been requested to \npopularize the scheme and mobilize the target groups/eligible workers \nfor  enrolment under the PM -SYM scheme. The Central Government \nhas also taken several initiatives  to increase the enrolment under PM -\nSYM Scheme such as periodic review of the progress of the scheme \nwith States/UTs, publicity of the scheme through IEC activities, \nconstitution of State Level Monitoring Committees (SLMC) and District \nLevel Implementation Committees (DLIC) to ensure  implementation of \nthe PM -SYM Scheme,   mobilization of beneficiaries through State \nGovernments. \n \n*                                                 ****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 563 FOR 06.02.2023 REGARDING “BENEFICIARIES UNDER (PM -SYM) \nYOJANA” RAISED BY DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY AND SHRIMATI SANGEETA \nKUMARI SINGH DEO. \n \nSl. No State Name Registration  \n1 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 74231 \n2 HIMACHAL PRADESH 47556 \n3 PUNJAB 58479 \n4 CHANDIGARH 5174 \n5 UTTARAKHAND 38782 \n6 HARYANA 917130 \n7 DELHI 10247 \n8 RAJASTHAN 305714 \n9 UTTAR PRADESH 887760 \n10 BIHAR 216524 \n11 SIKKIM 308 \n12 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 2870 \n13 NAGALAND 4946 \n14 MANIPUR 5695 \n15 MIZORAM 1148 \n16 TRIPURA 31664 \n17 MEGHALAYA 5326 \n18 ASSAM 37649 \n19 WEST BENGAL 105755 \n20 JHARKHAND 135782 \n21 ODISHA 183930 \n22 CHHATTISGARH 229418 \n23 MADHYA PRADESH 177459 \n24 GUJARAT 387287 \n25 MAHARASHTRA 614141 \n26 ANDHRA PRADESH 170262 \n27 KARNATAKA 130033 \n28 GOA 2027 \n29 LAKSHADWEEP 21 \n30 KERALA 15467 \n31 TAMIL NADU 65799 \n32 PUDUCHERRY 2381 \n33 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS 2339 \n34 TELANGANA 48751 \n35 LADAKH 1473 \n36 \nTHE DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI \nAND DAMAN AND DIU 1627 \nTotal 4925155 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |564", "t": "ST List of Assam", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 564 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nST LIST OF ASSAM \n \n564. SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the number of communities included in Scheduled Tribe list over the last three \nyears across the country; \n \n(b) the details of the status of the commitment made by the Government to include Koch \nRajbanshi and five other communities from Assam in the Scheduled Tribes; \n \n(c) whether the Government is aware of the demand for inclusion in Scheduled Tribe list by \nDesi, Goria, Moria and Kalita and Pangals of Manipur; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Government in this regard?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n  \n(a):      The details of the number of communities included in Scheduled Tribe list over the last \nthree years across the country is at ANNEXURE. \n  \n(b):       The Government of India on 15.6.1999 (further amended on 25.6.2002 and on \n14.09.2022) has laid down the modalities for deciding the claims for inclusion in, exclusion from \nand other modifications in Orders specifying Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes lists. As \nper the modalities, only those proposals which have been recommended and justified by the \nconcerned State Government / UT Administration and concurred with by Registrar General of \nIndia (RGI) and National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) are to be considered and \nlegislation amended. All action on the proposal is taken as per these approved modalities. \n  \n(c) to (d):  As per these approved modalities indicated above, the recommendation of the \nconcerned State Government is pre-requisite to process the case further. \n  \n  \n \n \n\n ANNEXURE \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (A) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNo. 564 FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2023 REGARDING ST LIST OF ASSAM. \n  \nList of Communities included in Scheduled Tribes list over the last three years across the country. \n  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of \norder/Act \nDate of \nNotification \nName of \nStates / \nUTs for \nwhich \napplicable \n(as \namended) \nCommunities included \n1.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Amendment\n) Act, 2020 \n(No. 4 of \n2020) \n19.3.2020 Karnataka following changes were made in ST list of Karnataka: \n  \n(a) in entry 38, for the words \"Naikda, Nayaka\", the words \nand brackets \"Naikda, Nayaka (including Parivara and \nTalawara)\" shall be substituted; \n(b) in entry 50, for the brackets and words \"(in Uttar \nKannada district)\", the brackets and words \"(in Belagavi, \nDharwad and Uttar Kannada districts)\" shall be \nsubstituted. \n2.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Amendment\n) Act, 2021 \n(No. 32 of \n2021) \n13.8.2021 Arunachal \nPradesh \nFollowing amendments in the list of STs of Arunachal \nPradesh were made. \n. omission of “Abor” in entry 1, as it is the same as “Adi” \nin entry 16”; \n. substitution of “Khampti” at entry 6 by “Tai Khamti” \n. substitution of “Mishmi, Idu, Taroan” at entry 8 by \n“Mishmi-Kaman (Miju Mishmi), Idu (Mishmi) and \nTaraon (Digaru Mishmi); \n. substitution of “Momba” at entry 9 by “Monpa, Memba, \nSartang, Sajolang (Miji); \n. substitution of “Any Naga tribes” at entry 10 by “Nocte, \nTangsa, Tutsa, Wancho”. \n  \n3.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nCastes and \nScheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Amendment\n) Act, 2022 \n(No. 8 of \n2022) \n8.4.2022 Jharkhand . Omitting Bhogta listed at Sl. No. 3 in the list of \nScheduled Castes. \n. Inclusion of Puran as new entry in the list of Scheduled \nTribes; and \n. Inclusion of Bhogta, Deshwari, Ganjhu, Dautalbandi \n(Dwalbandi), Patbandi, Raut, Maajhia, Khairi (Kheri) as \nsynonyms to Kharwar, entry at Sl. No. 16 in the list of \nSTs. \n. Inclusion of Tamaria/Tamadia as synonym to ‘Munda’ at \nSl. No. 24 in the list of Scheduled Tribes. \n\n4.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Amendment\n) Act, 2022 \n(No. 9 of \n2022) \n18.4.2022 Tripura Darlong’ as sub-tribe of ‘Kuki’ in entry 9 in the list of \nScheduled Tribes of the State of Tripura \n5.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nCastes and \nScheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Second \nAmendment) \nAct, 2022 \n(No. 20 of \n2022) \n24.12.2022 Uttar \nPradesh \nGond, Dhuria, Nayak, Ojha, Pathari, Rajgond in the \ndistricts of Sant Kabirnagar Kushinagar, Chandauli and \nBhadohi districts of Uttar Pradesh \n6.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Second \nAmendment) \nAct, 2022 \n(No. 1 of \n2023) \n3.1.2023 Tamil \nNadu \nNarikorivan alongwith Kurivikkaran \n7.   The \nConstitution \n(Scheduled \nTribes) \nOrder \n(Fourth \nAmendment) \nAct, 2022 \n(No. 2 of \n2023) \n3.1.2023 Karnataka Betta-Kuruba’ community as synonym of ‘Kadu Kuruba’ \nat Sl. No. 16 in the list of STs of Karnataka \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |565", "t": "Awareness on Harmful effect of Tabacco at School Levels", "head": "         GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n     MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n        DEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 565 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nAwareness on Harmful effect of Tabacco at School Levels \n565 MS. RAMYA HARIDAS: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the recent Global Youth Survey Report says that nearly one-fifth of the students aged \n13-15 years use some form of tobacco products (smoking, smokeless and any other form) and \nwhether school and teachers are crucial in creating awareness among children and their parents \nabout the harmful effect of tobacco use; \n(b) whether Government has any plan to dev elop any curriculum to spread awareness among \nschool going children about the harmful effects of tobacco products; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) at what level of education system it may be incorporated? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a):    Ministry of Health and Family Welfare has informed that a s per the National Fact Sheet of \nthe Global Youth Tobacco Survey (GYTS -4), 2019, nearly one -fifth (18.1%) of the students \n(19.3% of boys and 16.9% of girls) aged 13-15 years reported ever use of any tobacco product. \nHowever, the current tobacco use was 8.5%  which has co me down from 14.6% in 2009 . \nAwareness is created among students through school and teachers about harmful effect of \ntobacco use through various  programmes and awareness drives time to time.  School heads are \nalso made aware of The Cigarettes and other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of Advertisement \nand Regulation of Trade and Commerce, Production, S upply and Distribution) Act, (CoTPA), \n2003. \n\n  \n(b) to (d): The National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT) has already \nincorporated age -appropriate content related to tobacco, drugs and intoxicants in the syllabus, \ntextbooks and other teaching learning materials.  The contents on Drug Abuse are discussed in \nthe NCERT’s textbooks of Science for Class VIII, Biology for Class XII, Psychology for Classes \nXI and XII.  \n \nUnder the National Population Education Project (NPEP), the Adolescence Education \nProgramme (AEP) and the Ayushman Bharat School Health and Wellness Programme (SHP),  \nspecial classes of 23 hours in an academic session, 5-6 day training courses for master trainers & \nNodal teachers, special lectures in schools, experiential learning activities like Role Play, Folk \nDance, Poster making, Creative writing, Debate, Discussion and Skill Building activities are \norganised with school students for awareness generation on issues related to tobacco, drug / \nsubstance misuse.  \n \nThe Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has issu ed ‘Guidelines on Protection from \nExposure to Tobacco Smoke’ to their affiliated schools , conveying the World Health \nOrganisation (WHO)’s guidelines on protection from exposure to tobacco/smoke. CBSE has also \nbeen issuing periodic circulars to schools affi liated to it for sensitizing all students about ill -\neffects of tobacco. Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti has already implemented the “National Tobacco \nControl Programme” in all Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas.  Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan \nconducts various awareness programmes to sensitize their students about harmful effects of \ntobacco use through talks in Morning Assembly, competitions through Co -Curricular activities \nand Adolescence Education Programme. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |566", "t": "Non-Permanent Structures at Tourist Destinations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUASTARRED QUESTION NO. 566 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nNon-Permanent Structures at Tourist Destinations \n \n566.  SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWAR: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the construction of non-permanent structures for eco-tourism \nhas been removed from the ambit of non-forest activities;  \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether the construction of even non-permanent structures may harm forest areas and \ncreate disturbances in local ecology; and \n(d) the steps that Government has planned to ensure that these areas remain protected? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) In order to remove the ambiguity in the provisions related to ecotourism in the \nComprehensive Guidelines issued under Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980, the \nMinistry has issued revised guideline dated 25.10.2021, wherein it has been \nclarified that “Development/construction of facilities which are not of \npermanent nature, in forest areas for the purpose of ecotourism by Government \nauthorities shall not be considered as non-forestry activity for the purpose of \nForest (Conservation) Act, 1980”. \n \n(c) & (d) Eco-tourism is a form of responsible tourism in natural areas that conserves the \nenvironment and improves the well-being of people. In order to promote low \nimpact nature tourism which ensures ecological integrity of the eco-tourism \nsites and its environment, the Ministry vide letter dated  29.10.2021 has issued \nthe “Guidelines on Sustainable Eco-Tourism in Forest and Wildlife Areas”. \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |567", "t": "Beautification of Pilgrimage Sites in Uttar Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†567 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nBEAUTIFICATION OF PILGRIMAGE SITES IN UTTAR PRADESH \n \n†567.SHRIMATI KESHARI DEVI PATEL: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether t he Government proposes to provide basic facilities like \ndrinking water, toilets, parking, road connectivity and tourist guides \netc. for the tourists visiting monuments, historical temples and \nworld heritage sites to promote tourism; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, monument-wise; \n(c) whether the Government has taken/proposes to take a ny steps for \nthe beautification of the pilg rimage sites located in Phulpur \nparliamentary constituency of Prayagraj district of Uttar Pradesh \nand to provide the said facilities there; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Tourism under its schemes viz. “PRASHAD” and \n“Swadesh Darshan” provides Central Financial Assistance to the \nrespective State Governments/ Union Territory Administrations for \ncreation of tourism infrastructure at the identified destinations inclu ding \nmonuments, historical temples and world heritage sites on receipt of \nsuitable project proposals from them in li ne with th e guidelines of these \nschemes. The infrastructure under these scheme, inter -alia, include \ndrinking water, toilets, parking, last m ile connectivity etc.  List of the \nprojects approved under these schemes is at Annexure.  Ministry of \nTourism (MoT) is working in coordination with  Ministry of Road Transport \n& Highways (MoRTH) also to improve the road connectivity to the \nimportant tourist destinations.  Two lists of total 164 destinations have \nbeen prepared in consultation with State Gov ernments and UT  \nAdministrations, and shared with MoRTH.   Ministry has launched Pan -\nIndia Incredible India Tourist Facilitator (IITF) Certification Program,  a \ndigital initiative, that aims at creating an online learning platform with \n\nthe objective of creating a pool of we ll trained and professional Tourist \nFacilitators/Guides across the country to provide necessary assistance to \nthe tourists.  \n \n(c) & (d): Prayagraj of Uttar Pradesh has been identified under Swadesh \nDarshan 2.0.  However, n o  proposal  for  beautification of the pilgrimage \nsites located in Phulpur Parliamentary Constituency  is under \nconsideration in the Ministry of Tourism. \n \n \n********* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a)  & (b) OF L OK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.567 ANSWERED ON 06.O2.2O23 REGARDING \nBEAUTIFICATION OF PILGRIAMGE SITES IN UTTAR PRADESH. \n \nState/Union Territory wise details of the projects sanctioned under \nPRASHAD and Swadesh Darshan Schemes of the Ministry of Tourism the \ncountry including Uttar Pradesh \n \nPRASHAD Scheme: \n (Crore ₹) \nState/UTs Project \nNos \nName of the Project Year of \nSanctio\nn \nApproved \ncost \nAmount \nReleased \nANDHRA \nPRADESH \n1.  Development of \nAmaravati Town, \nGuntur District as a \nTourist Destination \n2015-\n16 \n27.77 27.77 \n2.  Development of \nSrisailam Temple \n2017-\n18 \n43.08 43.08 \n3.  Development of \nPilgrimage Amenities \nat Sri Varaha Lakshmi \nNarsimha Swami Vari \nDevasthanam at \nSimhachalam in \nVishakhapatnam \n2022-\n23 \n54.04 Adm. \nSanc. \nOnly \n14.12.202\n2 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH \n4.  Development of \nParasuram Kund, Lohit \nDistt. \n2020-\n21 \n37.88 7.34 \nASSAM 5.  Development of \nKamakhya Temple and \nPilgrimage Destination \nin and around \nGuwahati \n2015-\n16 \n29.80 29.80 \nBIHAR 6.  Development of basic \nfacilities at Vishnupad \ntemple, Gaya, Bihar \n2014-\n15 \n4.27 2.91 \n7.  Development at Patna \nSahib \n2015-\n16 \n41.54 33.23 \nCHHATTISGARH 8.  Development of Maa 2020- 43.33 24.33 \n\nBamleshwari Devi \nTemple, Rajnandgaon, \nDongargarh, \nChhattisgarh \n21 \nGUJARAT \n \n9.  Development of \nDwarka \n2016-\n17 \n13.08 10.46 \n10.  Pilgrimage Amenities \nat Somnath \n2016-\n17 \n45.36 45.36 \n11.  Development of \nPromenade at \nSomnath under \nPRASHAD Scheme \n2018-\n19 \n47.12 47.12 \n12.  Development of \nPilgrim Plaza with \nQueue Management \nComplex at Somnath \nGujarat \n2021-\n22 \n49.97 Adm. \nAppl only \n10.03.22 \n13.  Development of \nPilgrimage Tourism \nInfrastructure at \nAmbaji Temple of \nBanaskantha, Gujarat \n2022-\n23 \n50.00 10.54 \nHARYANA 14.  Development of Nada \nSaheb Gurudwara and \nMata Mansha Devi \nTemple in Panchkula \nDistrict \n2019-\n20 \n49.52 28.77 \nJAMMU & \nKASHMIR \n15.  Development at \nHazratbal, \n2016-\n17 \n40.46 32.37 \nJHARKHAND 16.  Development of \nBaidyanathji Dham, \nDeoghar \n2018-19 39.13 31.23 \nKERALA 17.  Development at \nGuruvayur Temple \n2016-\n17 \n45.19 45.19 \nMADHYA \nPRADESH \n18.  Development of \nOmkareshwar \n2017-\n18 \n43.93 43.93 \n19.  Development of \nAmarkantak \n2020-\n21 \n49.99 4.86 \nMAHARASHTRA 20.  Development of \nTrimbakeshwar \n2017-\n18 \n52.92 27.67 \nMEGHALAYA 21.  Development of \nPilgrimage Facilitation \nin Meghalaya \n2020-\n21 \n29.32 17.59 \n\nMizoram 22.  Development of \ninfrastruct"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |568", "t": "Wild Elephant Attack", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 568  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nWild Elephant Attack  \n \n568. SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of public anger towards increasing number of deaths due \nto wild elephant attack on villagers in Sakakeshpura, Alur, Belur talukas of Hassan Lok \nSabha Constituency;  \n(b) if so, the details of deaths occurred due to wild elephant attack along with the details of \ncompensation paid to the affected families;  \n(c) whether the Government is able to find a permanent solution to the elephant menace, if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(d) the details of action taken by the forest department to install rope barricades, solar fences, \ndigging trenches along with the funds spent for purpose during the last three years; and  \n(e) whether the Government has provided adequate permanent front line staff like forest \nguards, watchers and rapid response teams, anti depredation camps to the area affected \nwith elephant menace and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a)&(b) As per the information received from Stateof Karnataka, the details of human deaths \noccurred due to elephant attack along with the details of compensation paid, in \nSakleshpura, Alur, Belurtalukas of Hassan Lok Sabha constituency are as follows: \n \nDistrict, State Year No. of \nhuman \ndeaths  \nCompensation paid \n(Rs. In Lakhs) \nSakleshpura, Alur, \nBelurtaluks of Hassan Lok \nSabha Constituency, \nKarnataka \n2021 15 90.00 \n2022 07 30.50 \n \n\n(c)to(e) The management of wildlife including mitigation and management of human-elephant \nconflict (HEC) is primarily the responsibility of State Governments/UTs \nAdministration.  The state forest departmentsare working with the local people to \naddress the HEC issues through periodic awareness campaigns to sensitize, guide and \nadvise the general public on man-animal conflict, including dissemination of \ninformation through various forms of media.  Further, Forest Departments are engaging \nwith local communities as animal trackers to monitor the movement of elephants and \nalso to caution local people to avoid human-animal conflict, prevent damage or loss of \nhuman life and elephants.  As per the information received from the State of Karnataka, \nin addition to the permanent frontline staff, non-permanent watchers are deployed as \nAnti-Depredation Camps (ADC) and Rapid Response Teams (RRT) whose details are \ngiven below: \n \nRRT  5 Teams 20 Watchers \nADC  21 Teams 84 Watchers \nTotal 26 Teams 104 Watchers \n \nA total of 74 wild elephants have been captured and rehabilitated since 2000 till date. \nMoreover, in addition to ongoing installation of barricade and solar fence, the State of \nKarnataka has constructed 9.50 km railway barricade and 14.0 km tentacle solar fence \nso far to mitigate human-elephant conflict.  \n \n \nIn addition, the following measures taken by Ministry also help in mitigation of \nhuman elephant conflict:- \n \n(i) The Ministry provides financial and technical assistance to States/UTs under the \nCentrally Sponsored Schemes ‘Project Elephant’ for protection and conservation \nof elephants and their habitats in the country. \n(ii) To reduce man-elephant conflict and to avoid retaliatory killing of elephants.  \nCompensation is provided to local communities for loss of their property and life \ncaused by wild elephants. The Ministry has notified enhancement of ex-gratia \nrates related to wildlife depredation vide letter No. 14-2/2011 WL-I (part) dated \n9th February, 2018. \n(iii) Various other Centrally Sponsored schemes being implemented by this Ministry \ncontribute to the improvement in the natural habitat of elephants by augmenting \nwater sources, planting of fodder trees, regeneration of bamboo etc.  Such \nschemes include Development of Wildlife Habitat and Project Tiger.  The \nCompensatory Afforestation Fund Act 2016 and the Rules made there under also \nprovide for use of the Fund for development of wildlife habitats, including for \nelephants, establishment of animal rescue centres, etc. which also contribute to \nreduction in HEC.  \n(iv) A guideline for Management of Human Elephant Conflict has been issued by the \nMinistry on 6.10.2017 and elephant range states have been requested for \nimplementation of the same. \n\n(v) Critical elephant habitats are notified as ‘Elephant Reserve’ for focus and synergy \nin elephant conservation and to reduce conflict. The notification is carried out \nwith the approval of Steering Committee formed in the Ministry.  So far 33 \nElephant Reserves have been established in 14 major elephant States. \n(vi) A field manual for frontline staff to manage Human Elephant Conflict was \nreleased during the 16th meeting of the Steering Committee on 29th April, 2022. \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |569", "t": "Unorganised and Gig Workers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 569 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nUNORGANISED AND GIG WORKERS \n \n569.  SHRI KARTI P. CHIDAMBARAM:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government held consultations with labour \nunions/associations representing unorganised and gig workers \nbefore launching the e-SHRAM portal;  \n(b)if so, the names of these associations as well details of meetings \nheld thereof; \n(c)whether the Gove rnment has included labour unions/associations \nrepresenting unorganised and gig workers in the e -SHRAM portal \nregistration process to help workers get registered and if so, the \nnames of these associations thereof; \n(d)the number of labourers/workers who are  eligible to register on e -\nSHRAM portal, State-wise;  \n(e)the number of labourers/workers registered till now on e -SHRAM \nportal since its launch in August 2021, State-wise; \n(f)whether the Government has set up any physical centres to assist \nworkers in the r egistration process and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n(g)the data privacy measures taken by the Government to safeguard \nthe registered workers’ data? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes Sir. Prior to the launch of e -SHRAM portal, interaction \nwas held with Trade Union Leaders. During the interaction, features \nand objectives behind the development and launch of e -SHRAM portal \nwere explained. The Labour Unions/ Associations who attended the    \ne-SHRAM discussion session  held on  24th August  2021 are   Bhartiya  \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n \nMazdoor Sangh (BMS), Hind Mazdoor Sabha (HMS), Centre of Indian \nTrade Union (CITU), All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC), Trade \nUnion Coordination Centre (T UCC), Self Employed Women's \nAssociation (SEWA), United Trades Union Congress (UTUC), National \nFront of India Trade Unions (NFITU) \n \n(c):  The ministry has been conducting regular interactions with \nthe Trade Union Associations of unorganised and gig workers with \nregard to registrations in the e-SHRAM portal.  \n(d) & (e): Any unorganised worker having age lying between 16 -59 \nyears, who is not a member of EPFO/ESIC, and not a member of \nGovernment services/PSUs and not an income -tax payee is eligible to \nregister on e -SHRAM portal. The State -wise number of workers \nregistered on e-SHRAM portal as on 31/01/2023 is as per Annexure. \n(f):  Workers can self -enrol themselves on e -SHRAM portal. e -\nSHRAM has provided multi -channel registration facility to unorganised \nworkers across country. Common Service Centre -Special Purpose \nVehicle with its more than 4 lakh Village Level Entrepreneurs have \nbeen onboarded to provide registration facilities to workers on e -\nSHRAM. State Sewa Kendras (SSKs) have also been onboarded to \nfacilitate unorganised workers in registration process. e -SHRAM has \nalso been integrated with UMANG mobile application to provide \nregistration facility to the unorganised workers through a mobile app. \n(g):  The Unique Identification Authority of India  (UIDAI) \nguidelines related to Security and privacy of data, maintenance of data \nvault to store such information, security audit of portal and other best \npractices are being implemented to safeguard the data of registered \nworkers. \n \n*                                                  ****** \n  \n\nAnnexure  \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Part  (d) & (e) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 569 for 06.02.2023 \nregarding “Unorganised and Gig Workers” raised by Shri Karti P. Chidambaram. \nSl.No. State Total Registration \n[as on 31.01.2023] \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands  28,477  \n2 Andhra Pradesh  79,07,981  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  1,40,405  \n4 Assam  69,22,940  \n5 Bihar  2,85,53,787  \n6 Chandigarh  1,74,078  \n7 Chhattisgarh  82,53,697  \n8 Delhi  32,51,071  \n9 Goa  55,773  \n10 Gujarat  91,36,520  \n11 Haryana  52,52,989  \n12 Himachal Pradesh  19,23,013  \n13 Jammu & Kashmir  33,67,355  \n14 Jharkhand  91,36,018  \n15 Karnataka  73,84,000  \n16 Kerala  59,03,354  \n17 Ladakh  28,668  \n18 Lakshadweep  2,425  \n19 Madhya Pradesh  1,68,69,468  \n20 Maharashtra  1,34,44,317  \n21 Manipur  4,04,682  \n22 Meghalaya  2,86,788  \n23 Mizoram  58,198  \n24 Nagaland  2,18,571  \n25 Odisha  1,33,25,936  \n26 Puducherry  1,76,361  \n27 Punjab  54,95,385  \n28 Rajasthan  1,27,82,019  \n29 Sikkim  24,082  \n30 Tamil Nadu  83,52,759  \n31 Telangana  40,69,680  \n32 The Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu  72,846  \n33 Tripura  8,44,264  \n34 Uttar Pradesh  8,30,04,110  \n35 Uttarakhand  29,71,660  \n36 West Bengal  2,57,82,641  \nTotal  28,56,06,318  \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |57", "t": "Compensation to Road Accident Victims", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 57 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDust Mitigation Measures \n \n \n*57.  MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken cognizance of increasing cases of non-compliance of \ndust mitigating measures as per the mandate of Commission for Air Quality Management \n(CAQM) which consequently cause waste from the activities of construction and \ndemolition works and of Thermal Power Plants to spread into the air; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the number of cases of non-compliance of dust mitigating measures from Delhi and Uttar \nPradesh as per the mandate of CAQM, which came to the knowledge of the Government \nand the action taken in this regard and the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to create an online system for monitoring of \ncompliance and to create awareness about dust mitigating measures and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(e) the State-wise details of the field visits or inspections carried out in various regions \nincluding NTPC in Bhagalpur for compliance of dust mitigating measures under CAQM \nduring the last one year and the current year; and \n(f) the extent to which poor condition of the roads is responsible for air pollution? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARAS (a) TO (f) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 57 DUE FOR REPLY ON 06.02.2023 REGRADING ‘DUST \nMITIGATION MEASURES’ RAISED BY MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE AND SHRI \nSUNIL KUMAR PINTU, HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT \n \n(a) & (b) \n \nThe Commission for Air Quality Management in NCR and Adjoining Areas (CAQM) has \nissued statutory directions and advisories for dust mitigation in the NCR, besides calling for \ncompliance of various statutes viz. C&D Waste Management Rules and related CPCB \nguidelines. CAQM has an extensive monitoring mechanism and system towards compliance \nof dust mitigation measures. Flying Squads/ Inspection Teams constituted by the CAQM have \ncarried out incognito inspections of the C&D project sites, besides inspections by the \nrespective State Pollution Control Boards / DPCC.  \n \n(c)  \n \nAs on 31.01.2023,   a total of about 11,910 field inspections have been carried out by the \nFlying Squads constituted by the CAQM across sectors like C&D projects, road construction / \nrebuilding projects, industries etc. In such inspections, 48 projectsin the C&D sector in NCT \nof Delhi and 77 projects in the NCR districts of Uttar Pradesh respectively were found to be \ngrossly violating the directions/ rules/ guidelines. These were issued directions for closure of \nwork at the sites. These projects resume only after due compliance of various statutory \nrequirements and preventive and corrective measures. \n \n(d)  \n \nUnder statutory directions of the Commission, to facilitate remote monitoring of bigger C&D \nprojects, all projects in the NCR on plot areas equal to or more than 500 sqm. are required to \nregister on the respective dedicated web portals in the States of Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, \nRajasthan and the NCT of Delhi and certify self-compliance of various stipulated measures.  \nThe project sites are also required to be video fenced to enable remote monitoring of the \ncompliances by the inspection agencies through the respective web portals.  Such portals have \nbeen developed and are operational in the NCR States and NCT of Delhi. \n \n(e) \nDuring 2021-22, a total of about 5,456 field inspections were carried out by the Flying Squads \nconstituted by the CAQM across sectors like C&D projects, road construction / rebuilding \nprojects and industries etc. During 2022-23 a total of about 6,456 field inspections have been \ncarried out upto 31.01.2023. The Commission has been constituted for the purpose of \nprotecting and improving the quality of air in the NCR and adjoining areas. \n \n(f) \nPoor condition of the roads in the NCR is an important factor contributing to air pollution. A \nComprehensive Study on Air Pollution and Green House Gases in Delhi submitted by IIT \nKanpur in 2016 has concluded that in summer, soil and road dust can contribute about 26% to \nPM10 and PM2.5. \n \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |570", "t": "Conservation of Ancient Heritages", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.570 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nCONSERVATION OF ANCIENT HERITAGES \n \n†570.   SHRI AJAY KUMAR MANDAL: \nMS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA: \nSHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR PINTU: \nSHRI MAHABALI SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of ancient heritages in the country particularly in Hooghly district in West \nBengal, Jammu district in Jammu Kashmir, Sonipat district in Haryana,and Sitamarhi, \nBhagalpur and Rohtas districts in Bihar; \n(b) the steps being taken by the Government for conservation and promotion of Indian culture \nin the country including Hooghly, Jammu, Sonipat, Sitamarhi, Bhagalpur and Rohtas \ndistricts during the last five years; \n(c) the details of the programmes launched by the Government during the said period to \npromote Indian culture and to spread awareness in this regard; \n(d) the State/UT-wise amount of funds released by the Government for the said purpose to \nvarious Non-Governmental Organisatioins including Hooghly district in West Bengal \nJammu district in Jammu Kashmir, Sonipat district in Haryana, and Sitamarhi, Bhagalpur \nand Rohtas districts in Bihar; and \n(e) the steps taken by the Government for maintenance and conservation of the said heritage \nsites including the amount of funds allocated and spent during the last five years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)  The details  of centrally protected monuments and sites in Hooghly district in West \nBengal, Jammu district in Jammu & Kashmir (UT), Sonipat district in Haryana, and \nBhagalpur and Rohtas districts in Bihar are given at Annexure-I. There is no protected \nmonument in Sitamarhi district.  \n(b) Conservation preservation and maintenance of protected monuments and sites and  \nproviding basic amenities and facilities like drinking water, toilet blocks, facilities for \nphysically challenged, pathways, cultural notice boards, signage, parking, cloak \nrooms, etc. to the tourists visiting these monument are regular activities which the \nArchaeological Survey of India (ASI) undertakes, as per need, priority and resources.  \nImprovement and upgradation of these public amenities is a continuous process. \n (c) ASI organizes cultural awareness programmes on various occasions like World \nHeritage Day, World Heritage Week, International Museums Day, Republic Day, \nIndependence Day, Gandhi Jayanti, etc. through exhibitions, workshops and other \nprogrammes involving school & college students and general public.  \n(d) No such fund was released by ASI for the said purpose to any Non-Governmental \nOrganisatioin. \n(e) ASI regularly conserves, and maintains these monuments as per need and priority \nwhich is a regular phenomenon. Details of funds spent on monuments of these  \ndistricts are given at Annexure-II.  \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n \n      ANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED    QUESTION NO. 570 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \nDISTRICT-WISE DETAILS OF MONUMENTS AND SITES; \n \n WEST BENGAL   \n \nSl.No. Name of Monument / Site District \n1.  Hansesvari and Vasudeva Temples Hooghly \n2.  Dupleix Palace (Institute de Chandan Nagar) Hooghly \n3.  Dutch Cemetery together with all tombs & \nmonuments contained therein \nHooghly \n4.  Dutch Memorial monument of Susenne Anne Maria Hooghly \n5.  Group of temples known as Brindaban Chandra's \nMath \nHooghly \n6.  Mounds Hooghly \n7.  Minar Hooghly \n8.  Mosque Hooghly \n9.  Mosque & Tombs Hooghly \n10.  i) Danish Cemetery  \nii) All ancient structures, all tombs stone monument \nremains and inscriptions within the area enclosed by \nthe said walls. \nHooghly \n11.  Dargah of Zafar Khan Ghazi Hooghly \n JAMMU & KASHMIR UT  \n1.  Ancient Fort Jammu \n2.  Remains of Ancient sites (Pambaran) Jammu \n HARYANA  \n1.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n2.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n3.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n4.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n5.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n6.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n7.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n8.  Old Mughal Kos Minar Sonipat \n9.  Tomb of Khawaja Khizr Sonipat \n BIHAR  \n1.  Ancient site of Vikramasila Monastery Bhagalpur \n2.  Rock Temple Bhagalpur \n3.  Patalpuri cave and land adjoining Bateshwar cave on \nthe Patharghata hill \nBhagalpur \n4. Rock sculptures Bhagalpur \n5. Ashokan inscription on the Chandan Shahid hill Rohtas \n6. Rohtasgarh fort Rohtas \n7. Tomb of Hasan Shah Suri Rohtas \n8. Tomb of Sher Shah Suri Rohtas \n9. Three rock inscriptions (Adjacent to Tara Chandi \ntemple) \nRohtas \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-II \n \n      ANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED    QUESTION NO. 570 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \n     DISTRICT-WISE EXPENDITURE DETAILS OF ON MONUMENTS AND SITES  \n(Amount in Rupees) \nDISTRICT    2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nHooghly  30,23,926 36,95,212 35,23,521 53,45,706 45,39,060 \nJammu 4,65,623 16,42,8"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |571", "t": "Nation’s First National Assessment Regulator NCERT", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.571 \nANSWERED ON 6th February, 2023 \n \nNation’s First National Assessment Regulator ‘NCERT’ \n \n571.   SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Council for Educational Research and Training (NCERT) has recently \nnotified the nation’s first national assessment regulator;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the  details of the roles and responsibilities envisaged for this new national assessment \nregulator in the country;  \n(d) whether the introduction of this new regulator would enable the removal of disparities in \nscore of students enrolled with different State boards;  \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) the details of the additional measures being taken/to be taken by the Government to ensure \nfairness and equality in overall comparative assessment of performance of students enrolled \nwith various different State Boards in the country? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n \n(a) to (f). Para 4.41 of the National Education Policy, 2020, envisages “to set up a National \nAssessment Centre, PARAKH (Performance Assessment, Review, and Analysis of Knowledge \nfor Holistic Development), as a standard -setting body under the Ministry of Education (MoE)  \nthat fulfills the basic objectives of setting norms, standards, and guidelines  for student \nassessment and evaluation for all recog nized school boards of India, guiding the State \nAchievement Survey (SAS) and  undertaking the National Achievement Survey (NAS), \nmonitoring achievement of learning outcomes in the country, and encouraging  and helping \nschool boards to shift their assessment patterns towards meeting the skill requirements of the \n21st century in consonance with the stated objectives of this Policy. This Centre will also advise \n\nschool boards regarding new assessment patterns and latest researches, promoting collaborations \nbetween school boards. It will also become an instrument for the sharing of best  practices \namong school boards, and for ensuring equivalence of academic standards among learners \nacross all school boards.” \n \n National Council for Education al Research and Training (NCERT) has notified the \nNational assessment Centre, named as Performance Assessment, Review and Analysis of \nKnowledge for Holistic Development (PARAKH).  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |572", "t": "Corporate Borrowers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 572 \n To be answered on the February 6, 2023/Magha 17, 1944 (Saka)   \nCorporate Borrowers   \n572. SHRI MANISH TEWARI:    \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the top 10 group/corporate borrowers having the highest aggregate exposure \nfrom banks and financial institutions along with the bank/financial institutions borrowed from, \nlist/group-wise data;  \n(b) whether any non-individual/group borrowers have furnished Legal Entity Identifier (LEI) \ncodes;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government is planning to put a cap on those groups/entities that debt fund \ntheir projects in order to protect banks from risk, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons \ntherefor; and   \n(e) whether the Government has a plan/policy/ strategy in place to protect financial \ninstitutions in the country in case of default or payment delay by large borrowers and if so, th e \ndetails thereof?   \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a): Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has informed that under the provisions of section 45E of the \nReserve Bank of India Act, 1934, RBI is prohibited from disclosing borrower -wise credit \ninformation. However, total exposure to top 10 borrowers summarised in bank-group-wise as on \nSeptember-2022, as reported in Central Repository of Information on Large Credits (CRILC) \ndatabase, is at Annex. \n \n(b) & (c): PSBs have informed that 17,234 non-individual / group borrowers have furnished their \nLegal Entity Identifier (LEI) code.    \n\n \n(d): As per inputs received from RBI, in order to protect banks from risk, it has implemented \nlarge exposure framework which limits the exposures that a bank can take to a single counterparty \nand a group of connected counterparties to 20 per cent (extendable to 25 per cent by the Board \nof the bank under exceptional circumstances) and 25 per cent, respectively, of the eligible capital \nbase of the bank. Also, as per RBI guidelines, banks are required to have a clear policy regarding \nthe Debt -Equity R atio for project financing to ensure that promoters bring in equity funds \nproportionate to bank finance. To address the concentration risk emanating from large exposure \nto banking system, RBI has stipulated banks to maintain additional provisions of 3 perc entage \npoints and additional risk-weights of 75 percentage points over the applicable provision and risk-\nweights respectively, on the incremental exposures towards specified borrowers having overall \naggregate sanction credit limit of ₹10,000 crore. \n \n(e): To protect financial institutions in case of default or payment delay by large borrowers, RBI \nhas taken multiple steps which include, inter alia, the following— \n(i) A comprehensive framework has been put in place for early recognition and resolution of \nstress in the borrower accounts manifesting in payment default providing a principle-based \nframework for time -bound resolution of large borrowers. Delayed resolution is \ndisincentivised in the form of additional provisioning on the financial institutions; \n(ii) Concrete steps have been taken in resolution of specific, large -value stressed accounts \nunder the provisions of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code; \n(iii) Minimum provisioning requirements have been prescribed for both standard advances and \nnon-performing advances w hich are expected to mitigate the impact of default by large \nborrowers, on the financial health of the financial institutions;  \n(iv) Institution of comprehensive, automated Early Warning Systems (EWS) in banks,  with \n~80 EWS triggers, use of third-party data and workflow for time-bound remedial actions, \nto proactively detect stress, reducing slippage into NPAs; \n(v) Regulatory framework for Asset Reconstruction Companies (ARCs) has been amended to \nstrengthen transparency in the ARC sector and to improve the corporate governance \nstandards in ARCs; and \n(vi) Market based mechanisms have been strengthened to enable the financial institutions to \nbetter manage the credit risk on their balance sheets through a comprehensive framework \nfor transfer of stressed assets to eligible transferees.   \n***** \n  \n\n \nAnnex \nLok Sabha Unstarred Question no. 572 for answer on 6.2.2023 regarding “Corporate Borrowers” \nTotal exposure to top 10 borrowers summarised in bank-group-wise as on September-2022 \nas reported in Central Repository of Information on Large Credits (CRILC) database \namount in crore ₹ \nBank Group Total Exposure \nPublic Sector Banks             8,10,941 \nPrivate Sector Banks             3,70,973 \nForeign Bank Group                88,998 \nSmall Finance Bank Group-Scheduled                     692 \nScheduled Commercial Banks           12,71,604 \nSource: RBI \n***** \n \n  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |573", "t": "DNA Database of Domesticated Elephants", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 573 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDNA Database of Domesticated Elephants  \n \n \n573.  SHRIMATI RANJANBEN DHANANJAY BHATT: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government is considering to prepare a DNA database of domesticated \nelephants;  \n(b) if so, whether the Government has taken any steps in this regard, so far; and \n(c) if so, the details of the steps taken and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a)to(c) The Ministry in collaboration with Wildlife Institute of India, has developed an App \nnamely ‘Gajah Suchana’ for collection of biological samples from captive elephants \nacross India for DNA profiling. The sample collection of captive elephants from the \nStates of Chhattisgarh, Delhi, Haryana, Punjab, Rajasthan, Tripura, Uttarkhand has \nbeen completed. \n \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |574", "t": "CSR Work in Jharkhand", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 574 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA)  \n \nCSR WORK IN JHARKHAND    \nQUESTION \n574.  SHRI SANJAY SETH:  \n \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any change has been made in the Rules related to Corporate Social \nResponsibility (CSR); \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether any guideline exists related to work under CSR; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; \n(e) the details of the work done under CSR in Jharkhand including Ranchi during \nthe last one year, company and district-wise; \n(f) whether there is any provision to take action against such companies that do \nnot comply with the CSR rules; and \n(g)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                                                 \n   [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n \n(a) & (b):  Yes sir. The amendments in the Companies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014 \nhas been notified on 22 nd January 2021  and 20 th September, 2022 . These \namendments aim at strengthening the Corporate Soc ial Responsibility ( CSR) \necosystem by improving and strengthening the disclosures, simplifying the \ncompliances, bringing in more objectivity, transparency and entrusting more \nresponsibility on the Board of the company.  \n(c) & (d): The Government provides the broad framework for CSR through Section \n135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the Act and Companies \n(CSR Policy) Rules, 2014.  Section 135 of the Act contains provisions cornering  \nContd….2/- \n \n\n-2- \nCSR.  Schedule VII of the Act indicates the eligible list of activities that can be \nundertaken by the companies as CSR  and Companies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014 \nprovides the manner in which the companies shall comply with CSR provisions \nof the Act . In addition,  M inistry vide General Circular no. 14/ 2021 dated 25th \nAugust, 2021 has issued Frequently  Asked Questions on CSR.  \n (e):  All data related to CSR filed by companies in MCA21 registry is available in \npublic domain and can be accessed at www.csr.gov.in. On the basis of the filings \nmade by companies in the MCA 21 registry, the CSR spent by all the companies in \nthe State of Jharkhand during the last financial year 2020 -21 is Rs. 210.27 crore. \nFurther, the companies are required to file the CSR data for the financial year 2021-\n22 on or before 31.03.2023. \n \nThe district-wise and development sector-wise CSR spent by all the companies in \nthe state of Jharkhand during last FY is at Annexure- I & II respectively.  \n \n(f) & (g):  The CSR framework is disclosure based and CSR mandated companies \nare required to file details of CSR activities annually in the MCA21 registry. The \ncorporate governance framework along with the existing legal provisions such \nas mandatory disclosures, accountability of the CSR Committee and the Board, \nprovisions for statutory audit of accounts of the company etc. provide safeguards \nin this regard. Whenever any violation of CSR provisions is reported, action \nagainst such non -compliant companies are initiated as per provisions of the \nCompanies Act, 2013 after due examination of records and following due process \nof law.  CSR related defaults were compoundable offences. Now, the non -\ncompliance of CSR provisions has been converted as a civil wrong w. e. f. 22 nd \nJanuary, 2021.  \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n Annexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 574 FOR \n06.02.2023  \n \nDistrict-Wise CSR Expenditure in Jharkhand \nDistrict FY 2020-21 (Amount in crore) \n Bokaro  1.02 \n Chatra  0.21 \n Deoghar  0.11 \n Dhanbad  6.64 \n Dumka  1.67 \n East Singhbhum  12.30 \n Garhwa  0.11 \n Giridih  1.53 \n Godda  2.70 \n Gumla  0.26 \n Hazaribagh  1.52 \n Jamtara  0.05 \n Khunti  1.42 \n Koderma  0.74 \n Pakur  0.50 \n Palamu  0.01 \n Ramgarh  0.38 \n Ranchi  11.79 \n Sahebganj  0.18 \n Seraikela Kharsawan  2.44 \n Simdega  0.96 \n West Singhbhum  2.95 \n NEC/ Not mentioned * 160.79 \n Grand Total (in Cr.)  210.27 \n \n       (Data up to 30.09.2022)   [Source: National CSR Data Portal] \n*Companies did not specify the names of District where projects were undertaken.   \n*Companies indicated more than one district where projects were undertaken.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 574 FOR \n06.02.2023  \nDevelopment Sector-Wise CSR Expenditure in Jharkhand \nDevelopment Sectors                  FY - 2020-21 \n Agro Forestry  - \n Animal Welfare  3.50 \n Armed Forces, Veterans, War Widows/ Dependants  0.19 \n Art And Culture  3.30 \n Conservation of natural resources  0.16 \n Education  35.98 \n Environmental Sustainability  3.42 \n Gender Equality  0.12 \n Health Care  48.38 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |575", "t": "Budget Allocation", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 575  \n \nTo be answered on \nMonday, February  6, 2023/ Magha 17,1944 (Saka) \n \nQUESTION \nBudget Allocation \n \n \n575. DR. VISHNU PRASAD M.K. :  \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a)  the budget allocation to the Ministry from 2019 to 2021, year-wise; \n(b)    the disbursal and utilization of funds under the Schemes of North Eastern \nCouncil (NEC), State-wise; and \n(c)    the list of the Non -Governmental Organisations and the total financial assistance \nprovided to them by the NEC in the region during the last three years and current year? \n  \nANSWER \nThe Minister of the Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region \n[Shri G. Kishan Reddy] \n \n(a)   The year -wise Budget Estimates (BE), Revised Estimate (RE) and Actual \nExpenditure (AE) of the Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region (MDoNER) \nfrom 2019-20 to 2021-22 is given below : \n \n \n \n \n\nRs. in crore \nYear Budget \nEstimates (BE) \nRevised \nEstimates (RE) \nActual \nExpenditure \n(AE) \nAE as % of \nRE \n2019-20 3000.00 2670.00 2669.72 100 \n2020-21 3048.73 1860.00 1859.99 100 \n2021-22 2658.00 2658.00 2657.47 100 \n \n(b) The state-wise disbursement and utilization of funds under Schemes of NEC \nexcluding the amount disbursed to agencies other than State Government agencies \nduring the last 3 years (2019-20 to 2021-22) is given in Annexure-I. \n(c) The list of  Non-Governmental Organizations (NGOs) and the total financial \nassistance provided to them by NEC in the region during the last 3 years and current \nyear (2019-20 to 2022-23) is given in Annexure-II. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \nDisbursal and utilization of funds under Schemes of NEC from 2019 -20 to 2021 -\n22, State-wise \nRs. in crore. \nState Amount Released Amount Utilized * \nArunachal Pradesh  144.61 197.63 \nAssam 92.42 101.66 \nManipur 118.49 217.11 \nMeghalaya 136.20 115.83 \nMizoram 158.90 324.32 \nNagaland 139.54 142.87 \nSikkim 102.37 74.88 \nTripura 182.19 68.03 \n \n \n*  Including amount utilized from unspent balance of the previously released \namounts, as compiled from utilization certificates received during the year. \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \n \nList of NGOs and the Financial Assistance provided to them during last 3 years \nand current year (2019-20 to 2022-23 till date) \nRs. in Lakh. \nSl. \nN\no \nName of the NGO State Financial \nAssistance  \nProvided  \nRemarks/ \nProjects \n1 Guwahati Tea Auction \nCentre \nAssam 25.00 \n \nSetting up of Tea Lounge at Guwahati \nTea Auction Centre \n2 Indian Chamber of \nCommerce, Guwahati \nAssam 9.50 Workshop on Financial Inclusion in \nRural Areas of NE (Phase III) in \nMizoram \n3 Eastern Beats Music \nSociety \n \nAssam 250.00 Eastern Beats Music Society  \nfor \"Rhythms and Aromas of North East \nIndia\" \n4 M/s LT Eco Bag \n \nMizoram 103.00 Production of Eco - Friendly Bags by \nM/s LT Eco Bags \n5 Balipara Tract and Frontier \nFoundation \n \nAssam 10.00 7th Eastern Himalayan Naturenomics \nForum 2019 at Guwahati on 5th -6th \nNovember 2019 \n6 Ayang Music Society Assam 10.00 Kherkotiya Festival at Majuli \n7 Ranglong Youth Association \n \nManipur 8.00 The Ranglong Khohui Cultural Festival \n8 Ankon, Assam Assam 129.25 Theatre Training-cum-Festival 2019 \n9 Manipur Writers Forum \n \nManipur 4.80 Three Days Festival of Literature, Music \nand Dance \n10 North East Christian \nUniversity \nNagaland 6.40 Naga Cultural Symposium-II \n11 My Home India Delhi 8.00 NEST. Fest 2019 \n12 Dorbar Shnong, Nongrim \nHills, Shillong \nMeghalaya 1.50 71st Independence  Day Celebration \n13 Sri Sankaradeva \nNethralaya, Guwahati \n \nAssam 10.00 Conference on Ocuplastics  Association \nof India(OPAI) held at Vivanta on 6th -\n8th September 2019 \n14 Tangkhul Naga Society, \nDelhi \n \nDelhi 60.00 Financial Assistance for the 13th NEC \nTamchon Trophy 2019 \n15 National Farmer Association \n \nManipur 7.50 Sustainable development Water \npollution environment changes, Eco -\nsystem and integrated approach of \nLoktak Lake Manipur \n \n16 All India Women's \nEducation \nFund Association (AIWEFA) \nDelhi 4.82 North East Expo - 2019 \n \n17 Jeevan Initiative Assam 10.00 8th Jeevan Kite River Festival \n\n18 Makunda Christian Leprosy \nand General Hospital, \nBazaricherra, Karimganj, \nAssam \nAssam 460.52 Upgradation of Services of Makunda \nChristian Leprosy and General \nHospital, Bazaricherra, Karimganj, \nAssam \n19 Sri Sankaradeva \nNethralaya, Guwahati \n \nAssam 130.37 Modernization of Technology of Sri \nSankaradeva Nethralaya for Precision \nEye Care and Opthalmic Education by \nSSN, Guwahati \n20 Bamboo Development \nAgency, Mizoram, Aizawl \n \nMizoram 136.00 North East Handloom & Handicrafts \nExhibition held from 05.10.2019 to 08 -\n10-2019 at Aizawl, Mizoram \n21 Ukhrul Hills Development \nFoundation (UHDF), Ukhrul, \nManipur \nManipur 7.00 Two days seminar on Animal \nHusbandry with Special reference to \nrearing/ farming cows, buffaloes, \nchickens and pigs in Ukhrul District, \nManipur \n22 Se"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |576", "t": "Contribution of Tourism Industry in GDP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.576 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nCONTRIBUTION OF TOURISM INDUSTRY IN GDP \n \n576. SHRI D.K. SURESH:  \n  MS. MIMI CHAKRABORTY:                                                       \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the quantum of contribution of Tourism industry to the GDP of the \ncountry during the last three years;  \n(b)  whether the Government has achieved its target set for the tourism \nindustry and if so, the response of the Government in this regard;  \n(c)  whether the Tourism Sector has supported 40 million jobs in 2019 \nwhich has now dipped to 29 million; and  \n(d) if so, the reasons therefor and the proposal of the Government to \nrecover the country’s travel and tourism sector? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                              (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): As per estimation in accordance with 3 rd Tourism Satellite Account \n(TSA), the contribution of tourism in GDP  for 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-\n21 is as follows: \n \n 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \nShare in tourism in GDP (in %) 5.02 5.19 1.06 \n \nThe above estimates are updated using National Account \nStatistics 2022. \n \n(b): There were 10.93 million Foreign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) in India \nduring pre-pandemic year 2019. Tourism Industry has shown good signs \nof revival after Covid -19 pandemic. According to latest information \nreceived from Bureau of Immigration, India received 6.19 million \nForeign Tourist Arrivals (FTAs) during 2022 as compare to 1.52 million \nduring the same period  of 2 021. Further, Domestic tourism is also \nwitnessing growth in the country. India received 677.63 million \n\nDomestic Tourist Visits (DTVs) in 2021 as compare to 610.22 million in \n2020. \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism has taken several steps/initiatives to boost \ntourism in the country to attract foreign & domestic tourists as per \ndetails given below: \n \ni.  24x7 toll free Multi-Lingual Tourist Helpline. \nii.  Providing facility of e -Visa for 5 sub -categories i.e. e -Tourist visa, \ne-Business visa, e -Medical visa, e -Medical A ttendant visa and e -\nConference visa for nationals of 165 countries. \niii.  E-Visa has been further liberalized and the visa fee has been \nsubstantially reduced. \niv.  Adopt a Heritage Project for development and maintenance of \ntourist amenities at herita ge si tes/monuments and other tourist \nsites. \nv.  Development and promotion of ‘Niche Tourism’ products to attract \ntourist with specific interest and to ensure repeat visits for the \nunique products in which India has a comparative advantage. \nvi.  Promoting I ndia as a ho listic tourism destination including its \nvarious tourism sites and products through Ministry’s social media \nhandles and website. \nvii.  Conducting Programmes under the ‘Capacity Building for Service \nProviders’ (CBSP) Scheme to train and up -grade manpower to \nprovide better service standards. \nviii. New mountain peaks have been opened for \nMountaineering/Trekking to give boost to adventure tourism in the \ncountry. \nix.  Lowering of GST on hotels rooms with tariffs of ₹1,001 to \n₹7,500/night to 12%, those above ₹7,501 to 18% to increase India’s \ncompetitiveness as a tourism destination. \nx.  On the recommendation of the Ministry of Tourism, 59 tourism \nroutes have been awarded to the identified airlines by the Ministry \nof Civil Aviation under the RCS UDAN Scheme, for which Ministry of \nTourism extends financial support in form of VGF (Viability Gap \nFunding). 51 of these routes have been operationalized till date. \n \n(c) & (d): As per estimation in accordance with 3 rd Tourism Sa tellite \nAccount (TSA) and Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS), there were \n69.44 million jobs in tourism sector during 2019 -20. The year 2020 -21 \nwas affected by Covid -19 pandemic. During 2020 -21, the number of jobs \nin tourism sector were 68.07 million.  \n \n\nTo recover from the hardship arose due to the covid -19 pandemic, \nMinistry of Tourism is running the \"Loan Guarantee Scheme for Covid \nAffected Tourism Service Sector (LGSCATSS)\", under which, loan upto  \nRs.10.00 lakh is extended to each Tour Operators/ Travel Agents/Tourist \nTransport Operators approved/  recognized by the Ministry of Tourism, \nupto Rs.1.00 lakh is extended to each Regional Tourist Guide/ Incredible \nIndia Tourist Guide approved/recognized by the Ministry of Tourism and \nTourist Guides approved/ recognized by the State Govt./ UT \nAdministration. \n \n********* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |577", "t": "RGI and National Commission for ST", "head": "  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 577 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nRGI AND NATIONAL COMMISSION FOR ST \n  \n577. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the list of communities recommended by various State Governments for inclusion in the list \nof Scheduled Tribes pending with the Union Government; . \n(b) whether the Registrar General of India (RGI) and the National Commission for Scheduled \nTribes has concurred with recommendation of various State Governments for inclusion of above \nrecommended communities in the Scheduled Tribes list;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the current status of application especially from Odisha; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons for non-inclusion and the process by which these communities will be \nincluded in the list of Scheduled Tribes?  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n  \n(a) to (d): The Government of India on 15.6.1999 (further amended on 25.6.2002 and 14.9.2022) \nhas laid down the modalities for deciding the claims for inclusion in, exclusion from and other \nmodifications in Orders specifying Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes lists. As per the \nmodalities, only those proposals which have been recommended and justified by the concerned \nState Government / UT Administration and concurred with by Registrar General of India (RGI) \nand National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) are to be considered and legislation \namended. All action on the proposals is taken as per these approved modalities. \n  \n          The proposals for inclusion in the list of Scheduled Tribes of a State or Union Territory \nfollow certain processes as per the modalities.  This is a continuous process.  Proposals received \nfrom the State Government should be accompanied by an ethnographic report.   The proposals \nare examined by the Office of the RGI and then by the NCST.  In case the proposal is not \nrecommended  by the RGI, the State Governments are communicated the points raised by the \nRGI, so that additional information, if any, maybe furnished by the State Government.  Many \nsuch proposals may therefore remain under examination at different levels.   \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |578", "t": "Atmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY)", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 578 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nATMANIRBHAR BHARAT ROJGAR YOJANA (ABRY) \n \n578.  SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \nSHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \nSHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \nSHRI LAVU SRI KRISHNA DEVARAYALU: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the targets and timelines under the Atmanirbhar \nBharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY); \n(b) the progress made thus far in respect of the said targets and \ntimelines; \n(c) whether it is true that many employers and employees are deprived \nof the benefits under ABRY due to lack of awareness as well as \ncomplexities involved; \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to address these issues; \n(e) whether the Government proposes to revise the eligibility conditions \nunder ABRY to make it more streamlined; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n (a) to (f): The Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY) was launched \nwith effect from 1st October, 2020 to incentivize employers for creation of \nnew employment and restoration of loss of employment during Covid -19 \npandemic. The scheme was intended to benefit a total of 71.80 lakh \nmembers. The terminal date for registration of beneficiaries was \n31.03.2022 and the total registration under the scheme till the last date of \nregistration is 75.11 lakh. \n \n\nEvery establishments/ New -employees registered under ABRY who \nwere fulfilling the eligibility criteria were benefited under the scheme. As \non 24.01.2023, benefits have been provided to 60.26 lakh beneficiaries \nthrough 1.51 lakh establishments.   \nFurther, to increase awareness among employers and employees, \nfollowing measures have been undertaken from time to time:  \ni. Employers not registered under ABRY were informed through \nmessages on EPFO’s Employer portal.  \nii. Webinars conducted through Zonal & Regional offices of EPFO with \nemployers / employer associations.  \niii. Awareness via social media outreach.  \niv. Targeted attempts made to educate/encourage the establishments \nwhich have fulfilled the required criteria in past, but have not \nregistered under ABRY.  \nv.  SMS  to employers to intimate the last date of registration under \nABRY and to motivate them for registration. \nvi. Publicity through creatives on website.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |579", "t": "Centre for Excellence", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 579 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nCENTRE FOR EXCELLENCE \n \n579. DR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has designated Centre for Excellence for developing India \nspecific knowledge in urban planning and design in existing institutions; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the Government has designated any institutions in Andhra Pradesh in this \nregard; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR)  \n \n \n(a) to (d):   In the Union  Budget 2022-23 speech, it has been announced that “For developing \nIndia specific knowledge in urban planning and design, and to deliver certified training in \nthese areas, up to five existing academic institutions in different regions will be designated as  \ncentres of excellence”. In this regard, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA) is \nhandling the exercise to identify five academic institutions in different regions imparting \nknowledge in urban planning and design for their designation as Centres of  Excellence \n(CoEs).  \nIn order to steer the process of identifying and designating institutions as CoEs, a \ncommittee comprising of officials from Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA), \nDepartment of Higher Education (Ministry of Education), NITI Aay og and leading domain \nexperts was constituted by Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs. \nRequest for Proposal has been issued, inviting proposals from academic institutes of \nurban planning for identification and subsequent designation of CoEs from amongst the se \ninstitutions. Proposals have been re ceived which are under scrutiny in Ministry of Housing & \nUrban Affairs.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |58", "t": "Smart City Infrastructure", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION  NO. †*58 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \n \nPRESERVATION OF CULTURAL HERITAGE IN UTTAR PRADESH  \n \n \n †*58.  SHRI  GHANSHYAM  SINGH  LODHI: \n \n Will the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(a) the measures taken by the Government during the last three years to preserve the cultural \nheritage of the country; \n(b) \n \nthe total amount spent by the Government for the preservation and development of the \nsaid cultural heritages during the said years along with the State/UT-wise details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has set up or proposes to set up new cultural centres in Uttar \nPradesh, especially in district Rampur, and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; and \n(d) whether the Government has received complaints regarding poor maintenance of cultural \nheritage sites in district Rampur and Uttar Pradesh and if so, the action taken by the \nGovernment thereon? \n\n \n \n \n---- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n                                                                                                                             \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*58 FOR 06.02.2023. \n(a) \n& \n(b) \n \n   \n \n \n \nThe Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) undertakes conservation works, as and \nwhen required, at 3696 Centrally Protected Monuments/Sites. Conservation of \nmonuments is a continuous process and all monuments/sites are in a good state of \npreservation. The details of the total funds spent on the conservation and \ndevelopment of ASI monuments/sites during the last three years are given in \nAnnexure- I. \n \n     (c) There is no such proposal for ASI to set up new cultural centres in Uttar Pradesh, \nespecially in district Rampur. ASI protects and maintains ancient \nmonuments/sites of national importance all over the country.  \n \n    (d) No complaints have been received by ASI regarding poor maintenance of \nmonuments/sites in district Rampur and Uttar Pradesh. The centrally protected \nmonuments/sites are in a good state of maintenance.    \n \n \n\nAnnexure- I  \n \nANNEXURE REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.†58 FOR \n06.0