Reserve - - 0 \n  Total   435.38 260.84 269.58 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |580", "t": "Consolidation of Schools", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-580 \nANSWERED ON 06/02/2023 \n \nConsolidation of Schools  \n \n580. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of one-teacher schools as on date, State-wise;  \n(b) the number of primary, secondary and senior secondary schools as on date, State-wise;  \n(c) the list of States which have carried out the exercise of consolidation of schools, along with the \nnumber of schools closed down in the last three years, State-wise;  \n(d) whether the ministry has issued any guidelines for rationalisation/consolidation of schools; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the weblink of the webpage where these g uidelines are \navailable? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a): The Department of School Education and Literacy (DoSEL ), Ministry of Education has \ndeveloped the Unified District Information System for Education Plus (UDISE+) system to record \ndata on indicators of school education provided by all the States and Union Territories. As per data \nrecorded in UDISE+, one-teacher schools for the year 2021-22 are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b): As per data recorded in UDISE+, Primary, Secondary and Senior Secondary schools for the \nyear 2021-22 are at Annexure-II.  \n \n(c) to (e): Education is in the Concurrent list of the Constitution and the Consolidation of schools are \nwithin the purview of respective State Government and UT Administration. The Right of Children \nto Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009 provides children’s access to elementary \nschools within the defined area or limits of neighbourhood. In pursuance to Section 6 of the RTE \nAct, all States have notified area or limits of their neighbourhood norms. Further, Section 8 of the \nRTE Act, 2009 also mandates that the appropriate government shall provide free and compulsory \nelementary education to every child and will ensure availability of neighbourhood schools.  As per \ndata recorded in UDISE+, total schools for the year 2019-20 to 2021-22 are as under: \n \nSchool Management/Year 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nTotal schools 1507708 1509136 1489115 \n \n****** \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n580 ASKED BY SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH, HON’BLE MP, LOK SABHA TO BE ANSWERED ON \n06.02.2023, REGARDING ‘CONSOLIDATION OF SCHOOLS’ \n \nTotal number of one-teacher Schools for the year 2021-22 - State-wise \nSr. no. Name of State/UT one-teacher \nSchools \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 4 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 12386 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 769 \n4 Assam 3361 \n5 Bihar 5227 \n6 Chandigarh 0 \n7 Chhattisgarh 5480 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 1 \n9 Delhi 19 \n10 Goa 243 \n11 Gujarat 1657 \n12 Haryana 841 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 3039 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 2448 \n15 Jharkhand 7322 \n16 Karnataka 7848 \n17 Kerala 310 \n18 Ladakh 68 \n19 Lakshadweep 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 16630 \n21 Maharashtra 5906 \n22 Manipur 148 \n23 Meghalaya 1320 \n24 Mizoram 61 \n25 Nagaland 29 \n26 Odisha 3169 \n27 Puducherry 0 \n28 Punjab 2523 \n29 Rajasthan 10878 \n30 Sikkim 43 \n31 Tamilnadu 2702 \n32 Telangana 6392 \n33 Tripura 204 \n34 Uttarakhand 3455 \n35 Uttar Pradesh 8040 \n36 West Bengal 4762 \nIndia 117285 \nSource: UDISE+  \n\nAnnexure-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b)  OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n580 ASKED BY SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH, HON’BLE MP, LOK SABHA TO BE ANSWERED ON \n06.02.2023, REGARDING ‘CONSOLIDATION OF SCHOOLS’ \n \nPrimary, Secondary and Senior Secondary schools for the year 2021-22 - State-wise \nSr. \nno. Name of State/UT Primary Secondary Senior Secondary \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 218 53 69 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 38430 11877 3164 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1847 319 164 \n4 Assam 40482 7290 2415 \n5 Bihar 42505 3855 8620 \n6 Chandigarh 21 71 102 \n7 Chhattisgarh 32723 2737 4610 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 184 45 54 \n9 Delhi 2594 357 1842 \n10 Goa 892 420 123 \n11 Gujarat 13827 4638 8126 \n12 Haryana 9716 3009 5578 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 11085 1728 2538 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 14824 3150 1266 \n15 Jharkhand 23706 3191 1757 \n16 Karnataka 24470 15385 5671 \n17 Kerala 6812 1941 2972 \n18 Ladakh 379 112 50 \n19 Lakshadweep 16 0 13 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 62413 8306 9757 \n21 Maharashtra 51152 17741 10871 \n22 Manipur 2422 958 271 \n23 Meghalaya 9223 1462 386 \n24 Mizoram 1481 706 205 \n25 Nagaland 1157 576 198 \n26 Odisha 29326 9497 2419 \n27 Puducherry 275 190 197 \n28 Punjab 13536 4186 5337 \n29 Rajasthan 38983 10775 20631 \n30 Sikkim 685 150 117 \n31 Tamilnadu 35062 5499 8997 \n32 Telangana 20711 12045 2639 \n33 Tripura 2522 702 479 \n34 Uttarakhand 13404 1407 2578 \n35 Uttar Pradesh 138078 12783 20763 \n36 West Bengal 75299 3291 7419 \nIndia 760460 150452 142398 \nSource: UDISE+  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |581", "t": "Role of Cultural Enterprises", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.581 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \n \nROLE OF CULTURAL ENTERPRISES  \n \n \n581.  SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is cognizant that our cultural enterprises can play a powerful role \nin evoking an emotional connection with their consumers who would take pride in taking \na piece of Indian heritage back home; and \n(b) if so, the initiatives that are proposed to be taken by the Government keeping in view the \nfact that these enterprises also have greater brand recall value? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)  \n& \n(b) \nYes Sir, Archaeological Survey of India works according to the provisions of the \nAncient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act,1958 which mandates \nit to survey, excavate, protect, conserve and preserve archaeological sites and \nmonuments in the country. However, along with other amenities for visitors, \npublication counter with well researched source material, wherever feasible are also set \nup for better understanding and connection to monument / site.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |582", "t": "Young Writers under Yuva-2.0 Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 582 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nYoung Writers under Yuva- 2.0 Scheme \n \n†582 SHRI JASWANT SINGH BHABHOR: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  recently launched a Mentoring Programme for writers \nunder the Prime Minister’s Scheme ‘Yuva 2.0 ’ (Young, U pcoming and Versatile \nWriters); \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with its objectives and salient features thereof; and  \n(c) whether National Education Policy (NEP -2020) lays emphasis on empowerment of \nyoung talent creating an ecosystem for learning which  can prepare young \nreaders/learners for leadership roles in the future world? \n \n  ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n(a) & (b): Yes Sir. This scheme has been conceptualized on the premise that the twenty first \ncentury India needs to groom a generation of young authors to create ambassadors of Indian \nliterature and world view. The objectives and salient features of the scheme is as under: \n \n(i) The Theme of PM -YUVA 2.0 is Democracy (Institutions, Events, People, \nand Constitutional Values). \n(ii) The maximum age of the contestant should be exactly 30 years or below as \non 2nd October 2022. \n(iii)   The entries/book proposals can be submitted in 22 Indian languages and \nEnglish in a maximum of 10,000 words. \n(iv) This All-India contest is being conducted through MyGov. \n(v)     At the end of training and mentorship a consolidated  scholarship of \n₹ 50,000 per month for a period of six months. \n(vi)   The book proposals would be developed as books under this mentorship \nscheme by National Book Trust, India. \n(vii) A royalty of 10% will be payable to the authors on successful publication of \ntheir books at the end of the mentorship program \n \nThe schedule of YUVA 2.0 is as under: \nS. No. Item Timelines \n1. Announcement of the Scheme 2nd October 2022 \n\n2. Duration of All India Contest 2nd October to 15th January 2023 \n3. Evaluation of Proposals 16th January 2023 to 31st March 2023 \n4. Meeting of  National Jury April 2023 \n5. Announcement of Results May 2023 \n6. Mentorship Duration 1st  June 2023 to 30th  November 2023 \n7. Publication of the first set of books 1st  February 2024 \n \n (c): This Scheme takes inspiration from the broad vi sion of NEP 2020 as reflected its  \nIntroduction part as cited below:- \n“These elements must be incorporated taking into account the local and global needs \nof the country, and with a respect for and deference to its rich diversity and culture. \nInstilling knowledge of India and its varied social, cultural, and technological needs, \nits inimitable artistic, language, and knowledge  traditions, and its strong ethics in \nIndia’s young people is considered cr itical for purposes of national  pride, self -\nconfidence, self-knowledge, cooperation, and integration.” (page 4 of NEP 2020) \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |583", "t": "Cesses and Surcharges", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Revenue \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 583 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023 \nMAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nCESSES AND SURCHARGES \n583. SHRI A. RAJA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has beefed up its revenue in the last few years by levying \ncesses and surcharges which are not shareable with the States thereby considerably \nreducing their share in gross tax revenues and if so,  the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether various State Governments have demanded for cess on crude oil to be included \nin the divisible pool; and  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n \n(a)  to (c) : The details of collection of major Cess & surcharges are as under:- \n \n(Rs. In crore) \nFinancial Year Collection of major Cess and surcharges \n2017-18 218553 \n2018-19 298229 \n2019-20 303646 \n2020-21 401605 \n2021-22 481217 \n2022-23 (RE) 510549 \n \n       Cess and Surcharges are levied by levied by the Central Government for the \npurposes of the Union under Article 271 of the constitution of India. The proceeds of \nsuch surcharge and cess go towards meeting certain specific needs such as financing of \nCentrally sponsored Schemes.  The benefit of such expenditure also percolates to the \nStates. \n     Article 270 of the Indian Constitution excludes the surcharge on taxes and duties \nreferred to in Article 271 and any cess levied for spec ific purposes under any law made \nby parliament from the distribution between the Union and the States. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |584", "t": "Female Labour Force Participation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 584 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nFEMALE LABOUR FORCE PARTICIPATION \n \n584.  SHRI RAHUL GANDHI: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of initiatives are being taken to  address the declining female \nlabour force participation in India, particularly in urban areas; \n(b) whether the Government has conducted studies to evaluate the impact of \nthe Covid pandemic  on women’s participation in the workforce,  \nparticularly in the unorganised sector; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to offer  incentives to employers to \nincrease recruitment of women, if so, the details thereof; \n(d) the measures are being taken to safeguard  the rights of w omen workers \nand strengthen grievance  redressal mechanisms in the unorganised \nsector; \n(e) the initiatives are being taken to strengthen  the implementation of the \nSexual Harassment of  Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and  \nRedressal) Act, 2013 (POSH Ac t), particularly for  women in the \nunorganised sector; and \n(f) the State-wise details of the local committee  constituted under the POSH \nAct, complaints received and disposed of during the last four years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (f): The official data on Employment and Unemployment is collected through \nPeriodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) conducted by the Ministry of Statistics &  \nProgramme Implementation (MoSPI) since 2017 -18. The survey period is July to \nJune of the next year. As per the latest available Annual PLFS reports, the \nestimated female Labour Force Participation Rate (LFPR) and female Worker \nPopulation Ratio (WPR) on usual status for age 15 years and above during 2017-18 \nto 2020-21 are as follows: \n \nYears LFPR (in %) WPR (in %) \n2017-18 23.3 22.0 \n2018-19 24.5 23.3 \n2019-20 30.0 28.7 \n2020-21 32.5 31.4 \n           Source: PLFS, MoSPI \n\nThe above data indicates that the female LFPR and WPR in the country \nhas increased during 2020-21 as compared to 2017-18.  \n \n    The Government has taken various steps to improve women’s \nparticipation in the labour force and quality of their employment. A number of \nprotective provisions have been incorporated in the labour laws for equal \nopportunity and congenial work environment for women workers. These  \ninclude the strengthening of constitutional and  legal frameworks such as \nprogressive rules for maternity leave which has been increased from 12 \nweeks to 26 weeks, Prohibition of Sexual Harassment of Women at \nWorkplace, equal pay for equal work and other initiatives to ensure that \nwomen can work in the best possible circumstances. The laws for the safety \nof women in thework place have been made stricter. \n \n The Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY) was launched with \neffect from 1st October, 2020 to incentivize employers for creation of new \nemployment and restoration of loss of employment during Covid -19 pandemic. \nThe terminal date for registration of beneficiaries was 31.03.2022.  \n \n The Code on Social Security, 2020 has the provisions for enhancement \nin paid maternity leave from 12 weeks to 26 weeks, provision for mandatory \ncrèche facility in the establishments having 50 or more employees, permitting \nwomen workers in the night shifts with adequate safety measures, etc. The \nUnion Public Service Commission (UPSC) and Staff Selection Commission \n(SSC) have exempted the women candidates from payment of fees for \ncompetitive examinations. \n \n The Code on Occupational  Safety, Health And Working Conditions \n(OSH), 2020 has the provisions for the employment of women in the \naboveground mines including opencast workings has been allowed between 7 \npm and 6 am, and in below ground working between 6 am and 7 pm in \ntechnical, supervisory and managerial work where continuous presence may \nnot be required.  \n \n The Code on Wages 2019 has provisions that there shall be no \ndiscrimination in an establishment or any unit thereof among employees on \nthe ground of gender in matters relating  to wages by the same employer, in \nrespect of the same work or work of similar nature done by any employee. \nFurther, no employer shall make any discrimination on the ground of sex while \nrecruiting any employee for the same work or work of similar nature in  the \nconditions of employment, except where the employment of women in such \nwork is prohibited or restricted by or under any law for the time being in force.  \n \n\n To enhance the employability of female workers, the Government is \nproviding training to them th rough a network of Women Industrial Training \ninstitutes, National Vocational Training Institutes and Regional Vocational \nTraining Institutes. \nSafety and security of women in the country is of utmost priority for the \nGovernment. Keeping this in view, the Go vernment has enacted “The Sexual \nHarassment of Women at Workplace (Preventi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |585", "t": "Plantation Labour", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 585 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPLANTATION LABOUR \n \n585.  ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE: \n  SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n  SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has the details of the plantations that \nhave closed down or is under financial stress in Kerala including \nIdukki, if so, the details thereof during the last ten years; \n(b)whether the Government reopen the closed down plantations \nincluding tea plantations for helping the plantation labour in Kerala, \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)whether the Government allocate a Package to save the tea  \nplantation sector and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; \n(d)the details of the provisions made to ensure the welfare of \nplantation labourers/workers under the Central Wage Board; \n(e)whether Central Wage Board has revised its poli cies/decisions from \ntime to time and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f)whether wages are not revised to meet the requirement of the \nchanging needs of the labourers and if so, the reasons therefor and \nthe reaction of the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): As per Tea Board, Ministry of Commerce and Industries, the \nfollowing Tea Estates(T.E.) are presently closed in the State of Kerala \nincluding Idukki: \n \n(i) Peermade & Lonetree T.E., Idukki, Kerala  \n(ii) Kottamala & Bonami T.E., Idukki, Kerala  \n(iii) Bonnacord T.E., Trivandrum, Kerala  \nContd..2/- \n\n \n:: 2 :: \n \nThe status of closed down tea gardens in Kerala along their  \ncurrent status is at Annexure . There is no proposal to identify \nplantations which have been closed down in respect of Rubber & \nCoffee plantations. \n \n(c):  With an outlay of Rs. 967.78 crore, Tea Development & \nPromotion Scheme is implemented through Tea Board for the 15 th \nFinance Commission (2021 -22 to 2025 -26) for holistic development of \nthe tea industry.  \n \n(d) & (e): Welfare measures for plantation workers are implemented \nby concerned State Government in line with the provisions of The \nPlantation Labour Act, 1951. At pre sent, there is no wage board in \nexistence for plantation sector. \n \n(f):  The wage revision takes place by tripartite agreement \nthrough the process of collective bargaining. \n \n*                                                       ****** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a)  & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question for \n06.02.2023 \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the  \nTea Estates \nState District Date of Closure Current status \n \n1 \nKottamala \n& Bonami  \nKerala Idukki October, 2002 Reopened on  \n11.06.2007 \nAgain closed on \n23.12.2013/ \n11.10.2014. \n2 Vagamon Kerala Idukki 09.06.2002 Functional \n3 Mungyamullai Kerala Idukki 09.01.2005 Functional \n4 Thengakal Kerala Idukki 10.01.2005 Functional \n5 Pambanar Kerala Idukki 09.01.2005 Functional \n6 Koliekanam Kerala Idukki January, 2000 Functional \n7 Pasumullai Kerala Idukki January, 2002 Functional \n8 Granby Kerala Idukki 31.01.2002 Functional \n9 Mount Kerala Idukki 13.10.2000 Functional \n10 Tungmullay Kerala Idukki January, 2002 Functional \n11 Nellikai Kerala Idukki 11.01.2005 Functional \n \n12 \nBonaccord Kerala Trivandum 11.04.2007 Reopened on  \n03.12.2007 \nAgain closed on \n05.03.2015 .  \n13 Methanathu Kerala Idukki 12.11.2001 Functional \n14 Vengakotta Kerala Wayanad 06.10.2004 Functional \n \n15 \nPeermade & \nLonetr ee Kerala Idukki 01.04.2016 Closed \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |586", "t": "Steps to prevent Erosion in Coastal Areas", "head": " \n \n \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 586 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSteps to prevent Erosion in Coastal Areas \n \n586.  SHRI RAMALINGAM S.: \n          SHRI DULAL CHANDRA GOSWAMI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government has taken any steps to prevent erosion on low lying \nhabitations on Mayiladuthurai and Nagapattinam districts and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Union Government has taken any steps to make National Coastal Zone \nManagement Authority (NCZMA) as a permanent body and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to develop a special forest area in the coastal areas of \nthe country to prevent erosion due to floods; \n(d)  if so, whether there is any plan for developing forest areas to prevent erosion in Katihar, \nPurnia and Kishanganj in Bihar; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a): The subject of coastal protection falls within the purview of States. Accordingly, the \nmeasures of erosion control in coastal areas are formulated and implemented by concerned \nmaritime States and UTs as per their priority. The Union Government supplements the efforts of \nStates/UTs through technical assistance, regulatory measures as well as through financial support \nfrom schemes such as National Coastal Mission for mangrove plantation. Key measures taken by \nthe Government are as follows: \n \n(i) Under the Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM) project of the Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change, the Erosion line projected to the next 100 years \nbased on the annual rate of erosion computed from high resolution aerial photographs and \nsatellite images has been delineated over the entire coast line covering the mainland \ncoastal states/UTs, by the National Centre for Sustainable Coastal Management \n(NCSCM), Chennai in co-ordination with the Survey of India (SoI), as part of Hazard line \nmapping carried out by the SoI.  \n \n(ii) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC) has notified \nCoastal Regulation Zone Notification, 2019 with a view to conserve and protect coastal \nstretches, marine areas and to ensure livelihood security to the fisher and other local \n\n \n \n \ncommunities. The coastal regulations however permit setting up of erosion control \nmeasures in the coast. The notification also provides for No Development Zones (NDZ) \nalong various categories of coastal areas to protect India’s coastline from encroachment, \nerosion and accretion. \n \n(iii) A national strategy for coastal protection along with guidelines has been framed for all \nCoastal States and Union Territories by MoEFCC. \n \n(iv) The Flood Management scheme of Ministry of Jal Shakti, including anti-sea erosion \nschemes, are planned and executed by the State Governments with their own resources as \nper priorities of States. Union Government renders assistance to states which is technical, \nadvisory, catalytic and promotional in nature. \n \n(v) Considering the importance of collection of data on coastal processes towards coastal \nprotection measures, a new component ‘Coastal Management Information System \n(CMIS)’ was initiated under the Central Sector Plan Scheme ‘Development of Water \nResources Information System’. CMIS is a data collection activity carried out to collect \nnear shore coastal data which can be used in planning, design, construction and \nmaintenance of site specific coastal protection structures at vulnerable Coastal stretches. \nEstablishment of three sites each in the State of Kerala, Tamil Nadu and Puducherry has \nbeen completed. \n \n(b): MoEFCC constituted the National Coastal Zone Management Authority (NCZMA) on 26th \nNovember, 1998 under Section 3 of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. There are 25 \nmembers in NCZMA, out of which 24 are ex-officio members and one non-official expert \nmember which makes NCZMA as de-facto permanent and continuing authority.  \n \n(c) to (e): Union Government is implementing several schemes for increasing forest and tree \ncover in the country, including mangroves in coastal areas, to prevent erosion. The scheme for \n‘Conservation and Management of Mangroves and Coral Reefs’ is being implemented on a 60:40 \nfund sharing basis between the Center and the States. There are other schemes/programmes such \nas Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority (CAMPA), National \nAfforestation Programme etc., that support afforestation activities across the country, including \ndistricts in Bihar. Besides, States/UTs have their own conservation and afforestation \nprogrammes, including for mangroves.  \n \n \n \n \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |588", "t": "National Lake Conservation Plan", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 588 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nNational Lake Conservation Plan \n \n588. SHRI MAHABALI SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of the lakes selected in the State of Bihar under the National Lake \nConservation Plan (NLCP), district-wise; and  \n(b) the details of the amount released and spent in Bihar under the National Lake \nConservation Plan along with its progress report? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) Motijheel at Motihari in East Champaran District of Bihar was selected under \nNational Lake Conservation Plan (NLCP) during 2019-20. Central Government \napproved and sanctioned for ‘Renovation, Rehabilitation and Conservation of \nMotijheel’ under NLCP programme at a total cost of Rs 21.99 Crores on cost sharing \nbasis between central and state government of Bihar.Till now, an amount of Rs 9.89 \nCr has been released for various activities such as ‘Deweeding, Desilting, Sewage \nTreatment Plant, Peripheral Drain, improving bunds, Pathways, Bathing Ghats, \nDevelopment of Parks, Water Supply, Sanitary and Plantation works.    \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |589", "t": "Poor Pupil-Teacher Ratio in Government Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 589 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6th FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \n \nPoor Pupil-Teacher Ratio in Government Schools \n \n \n589. SHRI CHANDESHWAR PRASAD: \n \n Will the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that many schools in the country are having poor \npupil-teacher ratio particularly in Government schools;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government has issued any direction to the States to improve this ratio;  \n(d) if so, the action taken by the State Governments thereon; and  \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n \n(a) to ( e): As per Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, \n2009, the Pupil-Teacher Ratio (PTR)  norms for primary and upper primary level are \n30:1 and 35:1 respectively. The PTR at national level for Government primary schools \nis 2 8:1 and for upper primary schools is 2 4:1 as per UDISE 20 21-22 (provisional), \nwhich is better than the prescribed norms, the state-wise detail of Pupil-Teacher ratio is \nplaced at Annexure. \n Education is in the concurrent list of the Constitution. The recruitment, service \nconditions and deployment of teachers come under the purview of the respective State \nand Union Territory (UT) Government. The recruitment of teachers in schools is a \ncontinuous process and the vacancies keep arising due to retirement, resignation and \nadditional requirements on account of enhanced students’ strength/new schools. \nFurther, under Samagra Shiksha, Central Government provides assistance to the State \nGovernments and UTs for additional teachers to maintain appropriate Pupil Teacher \nRatio as per the  prescribed norms for various levels of schooling. The Central \nGovernment has been consistently pursuing the matter of expeditious recruitment and \nredeployment of teachers wi th the States and UTs. Advisories to maintain appropriate \nPupil-Teacher Ratio (PTR) have also been issued to States and UTs from time to time. \n***** \n\nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( B) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 589 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 ASKED BY HON’BLE MP SHRI \nCHANDESHWAR PRASAD REGARDING POOR PUPIL-TEACHER RATIO IN GOVERNMENT \nSCHOOLS \n \nState-wise Pupil Teacher Ratio (PTR) - 2021-22 \n \nStates/UTs Government \nPrimary Upper Primary \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands 10 11 \nAndhra Pradesh 24 17 \nArunachal Pradesh 11 10 \nAssam 23 18 \nBihar 60 31 \nChandigarh 36 19 \nChhattisgarh 23 23 \nDaman & Diu & Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli 33 34 \nDelhi 40 39 \nGoa 15 12 \nGujarat 30 26 \nHaryana 32 25 \nHimachal Pradesh 15 11 \nJammu & Kashmir 13 9 \nJharkhand 33 38 \nKarnataka 20 16 \nKerala 26 29 \nLadakh 5 3 \nLakshadweep 17 16 \nMadhya Pradesh 25 34 \nMaharashtra 22 25 \nManipur 10 12 \nMeghalaya 20 13 \nMizoram 14 7 \nNagaland 7 5 \nOdisha 19 19 \nPuducherry 19 15 \nPunjab 25 24 \nRajasthan 26 16 \nSikkim 5 9 \nTamil Nadu 25 18 \nTelangana  25 16 \nTripura 19 22 \nUttar Pradesh 29 33 \nUttarakhand 18 18 \nWest Bengal 29 35 \nIndia 28 24 \n \n(Source: UDISE+ 2021-22)   \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |59", "t": "Fellowship Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.59 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \n \nILLUMINATION OF MONUMENTS \n \n \n*59.  DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Archaeological Survey of India illuminated 100 monuments with G20 logo \nacross the country from 1st December to 7th December; \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the expenditure incurred thereon; \n(c) the list of Central Government protected monuments in the country where highest number \nof foreign tourists have visited during 2021-22; \n(d) the list of Central Government protected monuments in the country where highest number \nof domestic tourists have visited during 2021-22; and \n(e) the other new initiatives and steps being taken by the ASI for the preservation and \nconservation of monuments in the country? \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n  \n(a) To (e)  A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.59 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \n \n \n(a) Yes Sir. \n(b) The details  are given at Annexure-I \n(c) \n& \n(d) \nThe list of centrally protected monuments in the country where highest number of \nforeign and domestic tourists have visited is given as Annexure-II.  \n(e) New national conservation policy has been framed in 2014 for better preservation and \nconservation of centrally protected monuments. Besides, Archaeological Survey of \nIndia collaborate with IIT and other expert bodies, whenever it is required.   \n--- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 59 FOR 6.2.2023 \n \nDETAILS OF 100 MONUMENTS ILLUMINATED BY ASI WITH G20 LOGO ACROSS \nINDIA FROM 1ST DECEMBER TO 7TH DECEMBER, 2022 ALONG WITH \nEXPENDITURE INCURRED \n(Amount in Rupees)   \nSl. \nNo. \nState City/District Name of Centrally Protected \nMonument   \nExpenditure \non \nillumination \nAmaravati Circle \n1 Andhra \nPradesh Anantapuramu Veerabhadra Temple, Lepakshi 15,66,000 \nGuwahati Circle \n2 Assam Sivasagar Ranghar Ruins 2,45,000 \nPatna Circle \n3 \nBihar Gaya \nTemple Complex, Ancient Stupa and \nother remains locally known as \nSujatagarh \n7,84,700  \n4 Bihar Gaya Ancient ruins and a colossal statute of \nBuddha, Kurkihar \n7,84,700 \n5 Bihar Nalanda Ancient Remains at Nalanda  7,84,700  \n6 Bihar Nalanda Ancient Remains with structures, walls \nand monuments at Rajgriha \n7,84,700 \n7 Bihar Muzaffarpur Asokan Column, Kolhua 7,84,700 \n8 Bihar East Champaran Buddhist Stupa, Tajpur Deur Kesariya 7,84,700  \n9 Bihar Sasaram Tomb of Sher Shah Suri & Tank, \nSasaram  \n7,84,700 \nRaipur Circle \n10 Chhattisgarh Mahasamund Monuments & Temple, Sirpur 10,31,809 \nGoa Circle \n11 Goa Candolim Aguada Fortress (Upper)  11,79,680 \n12 Goa Candolim Basilica of Bom Jesus 8,78,680 \n13 Goa Candolim Se’ Cathedral 6,09,180 \n14 Goa Candolim Chapel of St. Cajetan 6,05,680 \n15 Goa Old Goa Church of Lady of Rosary 8,78,680 \n16 Goa Old Goa Church of St. Augustine 3,85,180 \n17 Goa Old Goa Chapel of St. Francis Xavier and \nconnected buildings \n3,85,180 \n18 Goa North Goa Church and Convent of St. Francis \nAssisi \n8,78,680 \nVadodara Circle \n\n19 Gujarat Ahmedabad Teen Darwaja or Tripolia gate 2,05,400 \n20 Gujarat Ahmedabad Sidi Saiyad's Mosque 2,28,500 \n21 Gujarat Patan Anavadapati Rani-ki-Vav Stepwell 2,49,500 \n22 Gujarat Ahmedabad Bhadra Gate beside Bhadrakali temple 2,46,700 \n23 Gujarat Ahmedabad Adalaj Rudabai stepwell with \nInscription \n2,47,500 \n24 \nGujarat Panchmahals \nChampaner-Pavagarh Archaeological \nPark (Shahar-ki-Masjid & Jami \nMasjid) \n4,96,800 \n \n25 Gujarat Mehsana Sun Temple, Modhera Already \nilluminated \nby State \nAuthorities  \n26 Diu (UT) Diu (UT) Fort together with the inside building \nFort Walls \n2,48,500 \nRajkot Circle \n27 Gujarat Rann of Kutch Ancient Site, Dholavira (Kotada) 6,47,200 \n28 Gujarat Porbandar House where Mahatma Gandhi was \nborn \n3,81,500 \n29 Gujarat Dwarka Rukmini Temple 4,13,000 \nChandigarh Circle \n30 Haryana Kurukshetra Sheikh Chilli Tomb 3,48,600 \nShimla Circle \n31 Himachal \nPradesh Kangra Ruined Fort Kangra 17,48,000 \nSrinagar Circle \n32 Jammu & \nKashmir Srinagar Shankaracharya Temple 3,23,700 \nHampi Circle \n33 Karnataka Bellary/Hampi Group of Jain Temples on the \nHemakuta hill \n11,59,428 \n34 Karnataka Bellary/Hampi Virupaksha Temple and Bazar 15,40,000 \n35 Karnataka Bellary/Hampi Monolithic Bull Hampi Bellary 6,50,300 \n36 Karnataka Bellary/Hampi Sasvikallu Ganesa Temple 7,75,390 \nBangalore Circle \n37 Karnataka Bangalore \nUrban Tipu Sultan’s Palace 20,73,175 \n38 Karnataka Chikkaballapur Nandi Bhoganandisvara Temple 31,86,612 \nDharwad Circle \n39 Karnataka Vijayapura The Gol Gumbaz 23,34,857 \n40 Karnataka Bagalkot Group of Monuments at Pattadakal 23,19,185 \nThrissur Circle \n\n41 Kerala Kochi St. Francis C"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |590", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Innovating Learning Programme-DHRUV", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.590 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPradhan Mantri Innovating Learning Programme-DHRUV \n \n†590. SHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has launched Pradhan Mantri Innovative Learning Programme-DHRUV; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the aims and objectives of the programme and any guidelines issued by the Government in this \nregard; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRIMATI ANNPURNA DEVI) \n  \n(a) to ( d)  The Pradhan  Mantri Innovative Learning Programme - ‘DHRUV’ was launched by the \nGovernment on 10 th October, 2019 at Bengal uru with active participation & consultation of State \nGovernments to mentor and nurture talented children to enrich their skills and knowledge so that they \ncan realize their full potential and contribute to society. Under this Programme, 30 students of Science \ngroup and 30 students of Performing Arts group were selected from class 9 to 12. \nSelection of 30 students of Science group and 30 students of Performing Arts group has been \nmade by an Advisory Committee headed by the Principal Scientific Advisor in co nsultation with \nthe Department of Science and Technology, Atal Innovation Mission, NITI Aayog and National \nCouncil for Education Research and Training (NCERT), Centre for Cultural Resources and \nTraining (CCRT) and Sangeet Natak Akademi, Ministry of Culture.  \nDuring the 14 days programme, structured activities were carried out for students of Performing Arts \ngroup at National BalBhavan, New Delhi and for students of Science group at IIT -Delhi and during \nevenings, combined activities for both groups were orga nized. The students were mentored and \nnurtured by renowned mentors from IIT- Delhi, Atal Innovation Mission, NCERT, CCRT etc.  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |591", "t": "Malviya Mission for Teacher Training", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 591 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nMalviya Mission for Teacher Training \n \n591. DR. PON GAUTHAM SIGAMANI: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the University Grants Commission (UGC) has issued draft guidelines on Indian \nKnowledge System training for faculty under the Malviya Mission for Teacher Training which \nis linked to the Career Advancement Scheme for University and college teachers;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the said training includes learning Chemistry through Ayurveda, Mathematics in the \nVedas, and the history of the Indian economy in the Mahabharata and the Arthashastra, if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n \n(d) whether the UGC has invited comments from stakeholders in this regard; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof?? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (e): Yes Sir. In pursuance to the National Education Policy (NEP-2020), which lays \nspecial emphasis on the promotion of Indian Languages, Arts and Culture, UGC has circulated \nthe draft ‘Guidelines for Training of Faculty on Indian Knowledge Systems’ to facilitate a \nseamless integration of Indian traditional knowledge with modern subjects. The draft IKS \nguidelines has been considered as part of the training modules under “Malviya Mission” which \nwill also be linked with Career Advancement Scheme as per the UGC regulations. \n \nThe draft guidelines inter -alia includes learning Chemistry through Ayurveda, Mathematics in \nthe Vedas, and the history of the Indian economy in the Mahabharata and the Arthashastra. \n \nThe draft guidelines are in public domain seeking comments from stakeholders. \n  \n ***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |592", "t": "Development of Libraries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.592 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF LIBRARIES \n \n592. SHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY: \n SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to undertake a Quantitative & Qualitative Survey \nof Libraries to prepare a baseline data of libraries in India; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefore; and \n \n(d) the details of other proposed measures to be taken by the Government to promote \ndevelopment of libraries in the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM and  \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)&(b) Quantitative & Qualitative Survey of Public Libraries was undertaken under the \nNational Mission on Libraries (NML) in all major States / Union Territories of India to \ncollect statistics about the public Libraries, for assessing the existing infrastructure of the \nlibraries, to study the pattern of library usage and also to take feedback from the citizens \nregarding services required etc. \n \nData were collected from 5140 Libraries and 7120 citizens. The Survey report has been \nuploaded on the website of RRRLF (www.rrrlf.nic.in) - a nodal agency for the National \nMission on Libraries. \n \n(c) Not applicable \n \n(d) Ministry of Culture through its scheme called National Mission on Libraries (NML), \nprovides financial assistance to 1 State Central Library and 1 District Library in each \nState / UT for up-gradation of infrastructure & modernization of libraries. \n \nAlso, Raja Rammohun Roy Library Foundation, an autonomous organisation under the \nMinistry of Culture provides financial assistance under Matching & Non-Matching \nschemes for development of public library services in collaboration with State / UT library \nauthorities. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |593", "t": "Sites identified for Eco-Tourism Development in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 593 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSITES IDENTIFIED FOR ECO-TOURISM DEVELOPMENT  \nIN TAMIL NADU \n \n593.  SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI:  \n       \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of sites identified for eco -tourism development in \nTamil Nadu;  \n(b)  the details of financial assistance given by the Government for \nthe development of these sites;  \n(c)  the details of approved projects and the funds  sanctioned for \nEco-Tourism in Tamil Nadu;  \n(d)  whether the Government is providing any incentives to the \nprivate sector to promote ecotourism in Tamil Nadu; and  \n(e)    if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (e): Development of Tourism is primarily th e responsibility of \nthe State Governments/UT Administrations. However, the Ministry of \nTourism under its scheme of ‘Swadesh Darshan’ provides central \nfinancial assistance to State Governments /UT Administrations etc.  \nfor development of tourism infrastructure in the country and the \nprojects under the scheme are sanctioned in consultation with the \nState Governments/UTs subject to availability of funds, submission \nof suitable detailed project reports, adherence to scheme guidelines \nand utilization of funds released earlier etc.  \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned ₹73.13 crore for the project \nDevelopment of (Chennai - Mamamallapuram – Rameshwaram – \nManpadu – Kanyakumari) in the year 2016 -17 under Coastal Circuit \n\ntheme of its Swadesh Darshan scheme. ₹69.48 crore has been \nreleased and the project is complete.  \n \nThe Min istry of Tourism under its scheme of ‘National Mission on \nPilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive \n(PRASHAD)’ has sanctioned two projects in the State of Tamil Nadu \nas per detail below: \n                                                          (Amt. in ₹crore) \nName of the Project  Year of Sanction \n/sanction amount.   \nrelease \namount/Status  \nDevelopment of \nKanchipuram \n2016-17/13.99 13.99 \nDevelopment of Vellankani 2016-17/4.86 4.86 \n \nPresently, there is no scheme in the Ministry of Tourism to provide \nincentive to the private sector to promote ecotourism in Tamil \nNadu. However, the Ministry of Tourism as part of its Domestic \nPromotion and Publicity including Hospitality (DPPH) scheme \npromotes various tourism destinations and products including those \nrelated to Eco-Tourism in the country. \n \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |594", "t": "Budget Allocation and Expenditure in Mid-day Meal Scheme", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-594 \nANSWERED ON- 06.02.2023 \n \n \nBUDGET ALLOCATION AND EXPENDITURE IN MID-DAY-MEAL SCHEME \n \n \n594.   SHRI SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI:   \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that funds to Mid -Day- Meal Scheme (MDMS) has been reduced in the \nlast three years; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  if not, the details of the budget allocation and actual expenditure during the last three years, \nState-wise; and \n \n(d)  the details of the share of the States and Centre in this regard during the last three years  \nthereof, year-wise and State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \n     MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n     (SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n \n \n \n(a)  to (c): The budget allocated under the PM POSHAN Scheme ( earlier known as Mid Day \nMeal Scheme) for the year 2019 -20 was Rs. 9 ,912.21 Crore which increased to Rs. \n12,900.00 crore for the year 2020-21.  Budget allocation for the year 2020-21 had additional \nprovision for an amount of Rs. 1900 crore for disbursing Food Security Allowance to all the \neligible children over and above the approved allocation, due to Covid -19 Pandemic.  The \nBudget for 2021 -22 was Rs. 10233.75 crore .  An amount of  Rs. 12800 crore has been  \nallocated for implementation of the scheme for the year 2022 -23.  The State-wise and year-\nwise details of funds allocated/released and utilized under the scheme for the last three years \nare given at Annexure-I.  \n(d): The State-wise and year -wise details of funds allocated/released by Central Government \nand State Governments under the Scheme for the last three years are given at Annexure –II. \n \n******* \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 594 \nfor 06.02.2023  raised by  Shri Sisir Kumar Adhikari Hon’ble MP regarding Budget \nAllocation and Expenditure in Mid-Day-Meal Scheme \n \nState-wise and year-wise details of funds allocated/released and utilized under the Scheme                               \n                                                                                                                                    (Rs. in lakh)                                                     \nS.No. States/UTs \nCentral \nAssistance  \nReleased \nUtilization \nCentral \nAssistance \nReleased \nUtilization \nCentral \nAssistance \nReleased \nUtilization \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 28563.77 26861.33 37510.17 37320.99 35731.48 35460.14 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 2367.9 2367.9 2917.87 2917.87 2968.58 647.16 \n3 Assam 55325.82 56024 75703.77 76469.2 61570.1 67949.19 \n4 Bihar 109313.34 113259.9 139248.01 138557.69 103016.32 142606.72 \n5 Chhattisgarh 25489.23 26886.06 38080.87 42439.58 21315.32 34067.95 \n6 Goa 1276.05 1161.03 1709.34 981.35 668.18 1416.47 \n7 Gujarat 39287.11 37962.06 52871.83 59593 50706.46 49297.72 \n8 Haryana 10889.91 13315.45 15572.73 15764.16 10163.12 8456.23 \n9 Himachal Pradesh  7557.54 7789.72 10500.11 9477.67 6290.49 9731.03 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 2666.45 7949.01 15960.26 14306.84 12221.88 14788.59 \n11 Jharkhand 32310.9 30621.39 35203.74 33824.83 25189.45 56534.44 \n12 Karnataka 52056.79 49733.8 51553.53 56520.02 48834.32 60863.39 \n13 Kerala 19962.41 20305.5 27688.82 28776.38 18482.19 32123.1 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 50407.62 51791.74 82754.74 87607.86 47220.22 47793.37 \n15 Maharashtra 99468.82 84615.36 100250.03 86677.29 49075.07 121547.2 \n16 Manipur 2192.3 2262.23 3900.45 4099.46 1678.74 2862.5 \n17 Meghalaya 7835.83 7466.38 8733.94 8777.11 8564.77 8624.13 \n18 Mizoram 2047.93 1858.51 2313.45 1340.07 892.98 558.94 \n19 Nagaland 2279.38 2557.08 2744.38 2675.07 1472.51 2411.82 \n20 Orissa 40358.68 39699.88 58301.22 64346.08 48208.87 50065.72 \n21 Punjab 13855.77 14797.65 21769.15 20113.24 19146.03 19277.64 \n22 Rajasthan 47252.76 44287.52 71100.05 71641.38 53106.11 78354.32 \n23 Sikkim 817.45 762.31 840.58 856.96 500.65 848.59 \n24 Tamil Nadu 43121.49 41150.06 49221.67 34969.3 23264.94 42238.65 \n25 Telangana 18821.14 16223.73 4524.22 0 4334.63 9641.08 \n26 Tripura 5598.51 6976.09 6220.56 8385.98 7718.82 7240.19 \n27 Uttarakhand 10273.31 9604.07 13024.3 12473.54 12477.64 12380.16 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 118201.96 111492.96 207166.14 210748.19 199805.66 193883.87 \n29 West Bengal 107102.66 107482.58 134330.72 131530.83 139412.35 138035.74 \n30 A&N Islands 754.85 354 648.71 456.33 540.19 416.03 \n31 Chandigarh 884.26 722.45 1338.56 908.99 1721.62 1616.13 \n32 D&N Haveli 572.89 775.96 1307.94 1200.32 1221.23 1274.38 Daman & Diu 258.31 \n33 Delhi 10319.99 7022.89 11297.29 9955.46 4403.79 6972.51 \n34 Lakshadweep    98.93 93.17 32.62 144.74 0 0 \n35 Ladakh 122.35 10.01 555.79 320.36 376.96 380.82 \n36 Puducherry 290.01 397.36 503.45 532.06 373.33 577.47 \nTOTAL (In Lakh) 970004.42 946641.1 1287401.01 1276710 1022675 1260943 \nTOTAL (In Crore) 9700.04 9466.41 12874.01 12767.1 10226.75 12609.43 \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II \n \nAnnexure referr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |595", "t": "Inclusion of Kondapalli Fort under Adopt A Heritage Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.595 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nINCLUSION OF KONDAPALLI FORT  \nUNDER ADOPT A HERITAGE SCHEME \n \n595.  SHRI KESINENI SRINIVAS:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the list of the monuments currently under adopt a Heritage \nScheme specifically in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b)  whether the Government is planning to include more \nmonuments in the list from Andhra Pradesh and if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)  whether the Government plans to include the Kondapalli Fort \nin the list of Adopt A Heritage Monument Scheme; and \n(d)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): Under t he Adopt a Heritag e proj ect, 2 9 Memorandum of \nUnderstandings (MoUs) have been a warded to 15 Monument Mitras \nfor twenty-seven (27) sites and two (2) Technological interventions \nacross India. \n \nIn Andhra Pradesh, an MoU has been signed with Dalmia Bharat Ltd.  \nto develop, upgrade and maintain amenities at Gandikota Fort under \nAdopt a Heritage project. \n \nCurrently, all the centrally  protected monuments adopted under \nAdopt a Heritage project including Gandikota Fort have been \ntransferred to Ministry of Culture/Ar chaeological Survey of I ndia for \nfurther processing. \n \n \n\n(b) to (d): The State pro tected monuments in Andhra Pradesh are \nincluded in the list of potential monuments to be promoted for \nadoption under Adopt a Heritage project. \n \nUnder Adopt a Heritage project, potential Monument Mitras may put \nforth their interest wherein they propose adoption of any heritage \nsite/monument for impl ementing basic amenities, advanced \namenities and technological interventions etc. \n \nTill date, no proposal has been received to develop, upgrade and \nmaintain amenities at Kondapalli Fort under the project. \n \nHowever, Kondapalli Fort is included in the list o f potential \nmonuments to be promoted for adoption along with ot her State \nprotected monuments of Andhra Pradesh. \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |596", "t": "Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS)", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-596 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nSpecial Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) \n  \n596. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA:       \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)   the details of the scheme under Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to \nTSS), the disbursal of funds for the tribal regions in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana during the \nlast five financial years; \n(b) the list of Non- Governmental Organisations operating in the backward districts of \nBhupalapally, Asifabad and Khammam districts of Telangana and Vizianagaram, \nVisakhapatnam and Kadapa districts of Andhra Pradesh along with the grants provided to each \nof them during the last five years, year-wise; and  \n(c)  the details regarding the steps taken to increase the funding in the backward districts of \nAndhra Pradesh and Telangana under the afore-mentioned scheme? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a): Ministry of Tribal Affairs had provided financial assistance to Government of Andhra \nPradesh and Telangana under the erstwhile scheme of Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-\nScheme (SCA to TSS) till 2020-21 against the activities related to education, health, sanitation, \nwater supply, livelihood, skill development etc.  \n  \nDuring 2021-22, Government has revamped the earlier ‘SCA to TSS’ with nomenclature \n‘Pradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojna (PMAAGY)’, for implementation during 2021-22 to \n2025-26, which aims at integrated developementof  36,428 villages with significant tribal \npopulation having at least 50% tribal population and 500 STs across States / UTs with notified \nSTs. This includes 517 tribal villages of Andhra Pradesh and 533 tribal villages of Telangana. \nThe details of the funds released to the Government of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana during \nlast five years under the scheme of ‘SCA to TSS’ / ‘PMAAGY’ is as under: \n \n \n\n \nRs. in lakh) \nName of State Name of Scheme Year Fund Released \nAndhra Pradesh \n  \nScheme of Special Central Assistance to \nTribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) \n2018-19 5617.39 \n2019-20 12470.50 \n2020-21 4954.96 \n*Pradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojana \n(PMAAGY) \n2021-22 0.00 \n2022-23 0.00 \nTelangana Scheme of Special Central Assistance to \nTribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) \n2018-19 2850.32 \n2019-20 5361.29 \n2020-21 4191.00 \nPradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojana \n(PMAAGY) \n2021-22 2262.18 \n2022-23 593.06 \n  \n* Under PMAAGY funds could not be released to Andhra Pradesh for FY 2021-22 and 2022-23 \nbecause of pending Utilization Certificates and unspent balance available with states. \n  \n(b): During the last five years, none of the organization in district of Bhupalapally and Asifabad \nin Telangana and Vizianagaram, Visakhapatnam and Kadapa districts of Andhra Pradesh have \nreceived grants from Ministry of Tribal Affairs under the scheme of 'Grant-in-Aid to Voluntary \nOrganizations working for the welfare of Scheduled Tribes'. The details of Non- Governmental \nOrganizations (NGOs) in backward districts of Khammam in Telangana received grants during \nthe last five years as shown below: \n  \nNGO Name District Project Amount \nreleased  \n(In Rs.) \nFinancial \nYear \nRemarks \nSarojini Devi \nHarijan \nMahilaMandali \nKhammam Residential \nSchool \n13,57,190.00 2018-19 Ministry has not \nreceived \nrecommendation \nfrom State \nGovernment for \nother years. \n   \n(c):      Under the revamped scheme of ‘Pradhan Mantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojna (PMAAGY)’, a \nsum of ₹20.38 lakh per village as ‘Gap-filling’ has been provisioned for approved activities \nincluding administrative expenses . Besides States / UTs are encouraged for convergence of \nresources as Central / State Scheduled Tribe Component (STC) funds and other financial \nresources available with them for saturation of gaps in the identified villages. The District-wise \ndetails of the villages selected under the scheme of PMAAGY in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana \nare as under: \n  \n\n \n \nAndhra Pradesh: \n \nS.No Name of District No. of Villages selected under PMAAGY  \n  Anantpur District  04 \n  Chittoor District  01 \n  East Godavari District  69 \n  Guntur District  06 \n  Krishna District  07 \n  Kurnool District  02 \n  Prakasam District  06 \n  Srikakulam District  66 \n  Visakhapatnam District  225 \n  Vizianagaram District  104 \n  West Godavari District  26 \n  Y.S.R. District  01 \nTotal 517 \n  \nTelangana: \n  \nS.No Name of District No. of Villages selected under PMAAGY \n  Adilabad  204 \n  Karimnagar  01 \n  Khammam  163 \n  Mahbubnagar  31 \n  Medak  22 \n  Nalgonda  16 \n  Nizamabad  17 \n  Rangareddy  11 \n  Warangal  68 \nTotal 533 \n  \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |597", "t": "Declaration of Shree Sammed Shikharji as Tourist Place", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.597 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nDECLARATION OF SHREE SAMMED SHIKHARJI AS TOURIST PLACE  \n \n597.  SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE:  \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to declare Shree Sammed \nShikharji a holy place which is located on the Parasnath hills and is \nthe highest mountain in Jharkhand State as a tourist place; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): No, Sir. \n(b): Does not arise. \n(c): Declaration of any destination a s “tourist place” does not come  \nunder work allocation of the Ministry of Tourism, Government of India. \n \n \n********* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |598", "t": "Employment Generation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 598 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nEMPLOYMENT GENERATION \n \n598.   SHRIMATI RAJASHREE MALLICK: \nSHRI VIJAYAKUMAR ALIAS VIJAY VASANTH: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has proposed to generate employment for around 10 \nlakh youth in the country, if so, the details thereof and the time by which it is \nlikely to be implemented; \n(b)  whether the Government has issued any guidelines/directions in this regard, if \nso, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government is aware that still  unemployment crisis continues to \nincrease, if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  the steps taken by the Gover nment to solve  this issue, if not, the reasons \ntherefor; and \n(e)  the funds allocated in this regard during the last three years, sector-wise? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a) to (e):  Employment generation coupled with improving employability is the priority \nof the Government. Accordingly, the Government of India has taken various steps for \ngenerating employment in the country.  \n  \nCreation and filling up of posts in the Central Ministries/Departments is the \nresponsibility of the concerned Ministry/ Department and all Ministries/ Departments \nhave been directed to take action in mission mode for filling up the vacant posts in \ntime bound manner. \n \nAs per the latest available Annual Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) reports,  \nthe estimated Unemployment Rate (UR) on usual status for persons of age 15 years \nand above was 6.0%, 5.8%, 4.8% and 4.2% during 2017 -18, 2018 -19, 2019 -20 and \n2020-21, respectively, which shows that unemployment in the country has declining \ntrend. \nEmployment generation coupled with improving employability is the priority of \nthe Government. Accordingly, the Government of India has taken various steps for \ngenerating employment in the country. The Government of India  has announced \nAatmanirbhar Bharat package to provide stimulus to business and to mitigate the \nadverse impact of Covid -19. Under this package, the Government is providing fiscal \nstimulus of more than Rupees Twenty Seven lakh crore. This package comprises o f \nvarious long term schemes/programmes/policies for making the country self -reliant \nand to create employment opportunities. \n \n\n   \n  The Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rojgar Yojana (ABRY) was launched with effect from \n1st October, 2020 to incentivize employers for creation of new employment and \nrestoration of loss of employment during Covid -19 pandemic. The terminal date for \nregistration of beneficiaries was 31.03.2022. Since inception of the scheme, till \n24.01.2023, benefits of Rs. 8474.55 Crore have been provided to 60.26 lakhs \nbeneficiaries under the scheme. \n \n  Government is implementing Prime Minister Street Vendor’s AtmaNirbharNidhi \n(PM SVANidhi Scheme) since June 01, 2020 to facilitate collateral free working \ncapital loan to street vendors to restart their busi nesses, which were adversely \nimpacted during the Covid -19 pandemic. As on 17.01.2023, 37.84 lakh loans \namounting to Rs. 4,379 Crore have been disbursed under the scheme.  \n \n  Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) was launched by the Government for \nfacilitating self-employment. Under PMMY, collateral free loans upto Rs. 10 lakh, are \nextended to micro/small business enterprises and to individuals to enable them to \nsetup or expand their business activities. During 2022-23 (till 20.01.2023), an amount \nof Rs 2.66 lakh crore was disbursed in 3.60 crore loan accounts sanctioned under the \nscheme.  \n \n  The Production Linked Incentive (PLI) schemes is being implemented by the \nGovernment with an outlay of Rs. 1.97 lakh crore, for a period of 5 years starting from \n2021-22 which have potential for creating 60 lakh new jobs.  \n \n   PM GatiShakti is a transformative approach for economic growth and \nsustainable development. The approach is driven by seven engines, namely, Roads, \nRailways, Airports, Ports, Mass Transport, Waterways and Logistics Infrastructure. \nThis approach is powered by Clean Energy and Sabka Prayas leading to huge job and \nentrepreneurial opportunities for all. \n \n  The Government of India is encouraging various projects involving substantial \ninvestment and public expenditure on schemes like Prime Minister’s Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP), Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment \nGuarantee Scheme (MGNREGS), P t. Deen Dayal Upadhyaya  Grameen Kaushalya \nYojana (DDU -GKY) and Deen Dayal Antodaya Yojana -National Urban Livelihoods \nMission (DAY-NULM) etc. for employment generation. \n   \n  Besides these initiatives, various flagship programmes of the Government such \nas Make in India, Start-up India, Stand-up India, Digital India, Housing for All etc. are \nalso oriented towards generating employment opportunities. \n \nAll these initiatives are expected to collectively ge"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |599", "t": "Sovereign Green Bond", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 599 \nAnswered on Monday, Feb 06, 2023 / Magha 17, 1944 (Saka)  \nSovereign Green Bond \n \n599.  SHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:  \n DR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE \n be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether  Government has proposed to issue Sovereign Green Bonds (SGrB), for \nmobilising resources for green infrastructure; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has released the framework for Sovereign Green Bonds; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the other steps being taken by the Government regarding management of proceeds from \nauction of Sovereign Green Bonds?  \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \na) to (b):   Sovereign Green Bonds amounting Rs.16000 Cr are proposed to be issued in the \ncurrent financial year for mobilising resources for green infrastructure projects. Rs. 8000 \ncrores has already been raised in the first tranche of the SGB's. \n(c) to (d): These bon ds will be issued according to Framework for Sovereign Green Bonds. \nThe framework has been released on 9th Nov, 2022 by the Government of India.  \n(e): The resources mobilised by issuing Sovereign Green Bonds is part of the Government's \noverall market borro wings in the year 2022 -23. The proceeds will be deployed in public \nsector projects which help reduce the economy's carbon intensity. \n \n***************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |6", "t": "Upgradation of State Highways", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 6 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 02, 2023 \n \nAMRUT SCHEME IN KERALA \n \nNO.6.   ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate: \n(a)  whether the Union Government has approved the proposal for \nsecond phase of Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and U rban \nTransformation (AMRUT) in the State of Kerala; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the tota l number of projects \nsubmitted by the State Government under the Mission  and \napproved by the Union Government;  \n(c)  the current status of first phase of AMRUT pro jects in the State \nalong with the details of proposals approved and th e funds \nreleased, utilized and works completed as on date; and \n(d)  whether the Government would consider any exte nsion of time for \nthe completion of pending projects under first phas e of the \nMission and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the  House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PART (a) TO (d) O F THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 6 DUE FOR ANSWER IN THE LOK \nSABHA ON 02 FEBRUARY, 2023 REGARDING “AMRUT SCHEME IN \nKERALA”: \n \n(a) and (b):   Under Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban \nTransformation 2.0 (AMRUT 2.0), tranches 1 & 2 of S tate Water Action \nPlan (SWAP) have been approved for the State of Ker ala so far. For \ntranche-1, 222 projects costing \u00011,938.52 crore including committed \nCentral Assistance of \u0001743.69 crore have been approved in August, \n2022 for 81 Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) of Kerala. Ou t of these 222 \nprojects, 199 projects costing \u00011828.66 crore are in Water Supply \nsector and 23 projects costing \u0001109.86 crore are in Sewerage & \nSeptage Management sector. \n For tranche-2, 268 projects costing \n\u0001683.07 crore including \ncommitted Central Assistance of \u0001234.52 crore have been approved in \nJanuary, 2023 for 70 ULBs of the State. Out of thes e 268 projects, 10 \nprojects costing \u0001184.51 crore are in Water Supply sector, 3 projects  \ncosting \u0001416.22 crore are in Sewerage & Septage Management s ector \nand 255 projects costing \u000182.35 crore are in the Water Bodies \nRejuvenation sector. \n(c): For the State of Kerala, State Annual Action P lans (SAAPs) \namounting to \n\u00012,359.38 crore, including committed Central Assista nce \nof \u00011,161.20 crore have been approved under AMRUT for t he entire \nMission period. So far, \u0001945.85 crore has been released as Central \nfunds for implementation of AMRUT projects against which, Utilisation \nCertificates (UCs) of \u0001715.26 crore have been received from the State.  \n\n \n \nSo far, 1,001 projects worth \u00012,195.38 crore have been grounded, \nof which, 831 projects worth \u00011,094.75 crore have been completed. \nOverall, works worth \u00011,734.36 crore have been physically completed \nin ongoing & completed projects and amount of \u00011,440.83 crore has \nbeen expended. \n(d): AMRUT Mission has been subsumed under AMRUT 2. 0 which was \nlaunched on 01 October, 2021 and central funding fo r ongoing projects \nof AMRUT shall be provided till 31 March, 2023. No proposal for \nextension of funding period for AMRUT projects is u nder consideration \nin the Ministry at present. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |60", "t": "Tap Connections under Har Ghar Nal Se Jal Yojana", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*60 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nIDENTIFICATION OF TOURIST DESTINATIONS UNDER PRASAD \nSCHEME \n \n*60. SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH: \n SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number and details of the tourist destinations identified \nunder the PRASAD Scheme in the State of Karnataka; \n(b) the funds allocated and spent under the scheme till date; \n(c) whether all the identi fied destinations were fully dev eloped \nwith all facilities to attract tourists; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether the Government has taken steps to expand the \nPRASAD scheme in identifying more places to provide better \nconnectivity to tourist destinations; and \n(f) if so, the response of the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (f) : A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*60 ANS WERED ON 06.02.2023 \nREGARDING IDENTIFICATION OF TOURIST DESTINATIONS UNDER  \nPRASAD SCHEME. \n \n(a) & (b ): Under PRASHAD Scheme of Ministry of Tourism, \ndestinations are identified and projects are approved on the basis of \nproposal requests from the respective S tate Governments/Union \nTerritory administrations. Based on the requests received from the \nState Government of Karnataka , Sri Chamundeshwari Devi Temple \nof Mysuru, Sri Madhwa Vana of Udupi, Papnash Temple of Bidar \nDistrict and Sri Renuka Yallamma Temple, Sa udatti, Belagavi have \nbeen identified under PRASHAD Scheme. \n \n(c) & (d): Development of any destination is an ongoing process, \nwhich is done in consultation with the respective State \nGovernment/Union Territory administration from time to time on the \nbasis of need-based assessment. \n \n(e) & (f): identification of destination and approval of projects under \nPRASHAD Scheme is a continuous process based on receipt of \nproposals from the State Government s/Union T erritories \nadministrations inline with the scheme guidelines. \n  \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |600", "t": "Ban on Single Use Plastic", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 600 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nBan on Single Use Plastic \n \n600.  DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:  \nSHRI B.B. PATIL:  \nDR. AMAR SINGH:  \nSHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI:  \nSHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking measures to enforce ban on manufacturing, import, \nstocking, distribution, sale and use of single use plastic in the country, if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the steps taken to monitor plastic reduction in the country in \na time bound manner; \n(b) whether the Government has found that many manufacturers continue to produce \nplastic items like straws, straw covers, cigarette packets etc. and such items are also \navailable in small shops, if so, the details of the action taken against them; \n(c) whether the Government has penalised any persons/companies for deviation from the \nsaid Rules so far and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether the Government has issued any notices or deadlines to the handful of \nCigarette manufacturers who are making or selling products in the country for not to \nuse any plastic material in packaging in any form, if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the action taken by the Government on manufacturers of tabacoo products who are \nviolating the guidelines issued on ban on single use plastic? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n \n(a)The Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016, as amended, provide the statutory framework \nand the prescribed authorities for enforcement of the rules, including ban on identified single \nuse plastic items, which became effective from 1st July 2022. Further, States and Union \nterritories have been asked to undertake regular enforcement drives to implement ban on \nidentified single use plastic items and on plastic carry bags having thickness less than one \nhundred twenty microns, covering fruit and vegetable markets, wholesale markets, local \nmarkets, flower vendors, units manufacturing plastic carry bags etc. States and UTs have also \nbeen asked to undertake random checking at border checkpoints to stop inter-state movement \nof banned single use plastic items. A one-month long pan India enforcement campaign was \n\nundertaken for implementation of ban on identified single use plastic items from 1st to 31st \nJuly 2022. Special pan India enforcement drives has undertaken in months of October, \nNovember and December, 2022 by Central Pollution Control Board, State Pollution Control \nBoards and Pollution Control Committees. \n \nFor effective monitoring of ban on identified single use plastic items and plastic waste \nmanagement in the country the following online platforms are in operation (a) National \nDashboard on for monitoring of comprehensive action plan implementation, (b) CPCB \nMonitoring Module for Compliance on Elimination of Single Use Plastic, and (c) CPCB \nGrievance Redressal App.  \n \n(b) & (c): During the enforcement campaign, non-compliance of the ban on identified \nsingle use plastic has been found in commercial establishments and manufacturing units \nincluding in small shops in local markets. Actions have been taken on the deviations, which \ninclude seizure of banned single use plastic items and levy of penalty. As per available \ninformation, the penalty levied during the enforcement campaigns is approximately Rs. \n6,01,67,701/- and 8,04,501 kg. material was seized. \n \n(d) to (e):Directions were issued to Gutkha / Pan Masala Manufacturers & Cigarette \nmanufacturers who were using plastic in packaging of their products. Action, as appropriate, \nincluding closure of units and levying of environmental compliance has been taken against \nnon-compliant units. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |601", "t": "Removal of Anti-Dumping Duties", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n***** \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.601 \n  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/MAGHA 17,1944 (SAKA) \n \nREMOVAL OF ANTI-DUMPING DUTIES \n \n601.         DR. T. SUMATHY \n (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n (a) whether the Ministry has received a letter from the Tamil Nadu Federation of Powerlooms  \nAssociations urging the Government to reject the recommendations of the Directorate General of Trade \nRemedies (DGTR) for levying of anti -dumping duty on Viscose Staple Fibre (VSF) and if so, the details \nthereof and the response of the Government thereto;  \n(b) whether the Government is aware that removal of the duty had enabled the exports of VSF to grow \nfrom 25.49 million sq. mt. in 2019-20 to 61.3939 million sq. mt. in 2021-22;  \n(c) if  so, whether the Ministry has taken this fact into consideration while taking a decision on anti -\ndumping duty and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government is aware that if the aforesaid recommendation is notified, the entire \npowerloom sector in the country, specially in Tamil Nadu would reportedly face closure and if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto; and  \n(e) the steps taken by the Government to tackle the said issue? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n(a to e) No representation from Tamil Nadu Federation of Powerlooms Associations has been received. \nHowever, several other representations have been received from across the country requesting the \nGovernment to reject t he recommendations of the Directorate General of Trade Remedies (DGTR) for \nlevying of anti-dumping duty on Viscose Staple Fibre (VSF) originating in or exported from Republic of \nIndonesia.  \nIn terms of Rule 18 of Customs Tariff (Identification, Assessment and Collection of Anti-Dumping Duty \non Dumped Articles and for Determination of Injury) Rules, 1995, Central Government may, within three \nmonths of the date of publication of final findings by the designated authority under Rule 17 of Anti -\nDumping Rules, d ecide on the imposition of Anti -Dumping Duty upon importation into India of the \narticle covered by the final finding. The findings of DGTR in this regard were published on 19th \nDecember, 2022, and Central Government shall take appropriate decision regardin g imposition of Anti -\nDumping Duty on Viscose Staple Fibre originating in or exported from Republic of Indonesia.  \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |602", "t": "Tribal Sub Plan (TSP)", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 602 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nTRIBAL SUB PLAN (TSP)  \n  \n602. SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Tribal Sub-Plan (TSP) have achieved the desired results in the country; \n  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n  \n(c) the details of financial and other assistance given under ‘Special Central Assistance to Tribal \nSub-scheme’ to the State of Andhra Pradesh during the last three years, district-wise; and \n  \n(d) the details of targets set and achieved during the last three years, year-wise? \n  \nANSWER \n  \n MINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n (SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a) to (b): Tribal Sub-Plan (TSP) now known as Development Action Plan for Scheduled Tribes \n(DAPST) came into existence in 1974-75 as a strategy for the development of Scheduled Tribes \nand areas having tribal concentration. Besides Ministry of Tribal Affairs, 41 \nMinistries/Departments are allocating certain percentage of their total scheme budget every year \nfor tribal development under DAPST for various tribal development projects relating to \neducation, health, agriculture, irrigation, roads, housing, electrification, employment generation, \nskill development, etc.State Governments are also supposed to earmark TSP funds in proportion \nto ST population (Census 2011) in the State with respect to total State Plan. \nThough there have been challenges in achieving the desired pace of development for tribal \npeople and their habitations on account of remoteness of habitations, dispersed population, \ndifficult geographical conditions and lack of employment opportunities in their areas. \nConsiderable improvements have been registered in socio-economic indicatorswith respect to \nthe Scheduled Tribes (STs) in the country, for example, literacy rate for STs has improved from \n59% (Census) in 2011 to 71.6% (as per Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) report (July 2020 \n- June 2021). Gross Enrolment Ratio (GER) at upper Primary level has improved from 91.3 \n(2013-14) to 98.0 (2021-22); GER for ST students at secondary level (IX-X) has increased from \n70.2 (2013-14) to 78.1 (2021-22); GER for ST students at senior secondary level (XI-XII) has \nincreased from 35.4 (2013-14) to 52.0 (2021-22) and GER for ST students at higher education \nlevel has increased from 13.7 (2014-15) to 18.9 (2020-21). As per National Family Health \nSurvey (NFHS) conducted by Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, in respect of Scheduled \nTribes, Infant Mortality Rate has declined from 44.4 (2015-16) to 41.6 (2019-21); Under Five \nMortality Rate has declined from 57.2 (2015-16) to 50.3 (2019-21), and Institutional Delivery \n\nhas increased from 68% (2015-16) to 82.3% (2019-21). Further, full immunization of children \naged 12-23 months has increased from 55.8 % (2015-16) to 76.8 % (2019-21). As per the \nestimates of the earlier Planning Commission, percentage of ST people living below the poverty \nline in rural areas has declined from 62.3% in 2004-05 to 45.3% in 2011-12. \n(c) to (d): The scheme of Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) was \nbeing implemented till the year 2020-21 wherein 100% grants were provided to the State \nGovernments with notified STs including Andhra Pradesh inter-alia for activities related to \neducation, health, sanitation, water supply, livelihood, skill development, minor infrastructure \netc. for the development of Scheduled Tribes (ST). Ministry of Tribal Affairs has revamped the \nexisting Scheme of ‘Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Scheme (SCA to TSS) as ‘Pradhan \nMantri Adi Adarsh Gram Yojna (PMAAGY),’ for implementation during 2021-22 to 2025-26, \nwhich aims at transforming villages with significant tribal population into model village (Adarsh \nGram).  The details of the funds released to the State of Andhra Pradesh during last three years \nunder the scheme of ‘SCA to TSS’ / ‘PMAAGY’ are as under: \n \n \nScheme Year Fund Released \n(Rs. in lakh) # \n \nUtilization reported \n(Rs. in lakh) \nSCA to TSS 2019-20 12470.50 3742.59 \n2020-21 4954.96 0.00 \nPMAAGY 2021-22* 0.00 - \n \n# Funds are not provided district -wise by the Ministry. \n * Funds could not be released due to pending Utilization Certificates (UCs) for previous years’ \nreleases. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |603", "t": "Forest Cover", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 603 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nForest Cover \n \n603. SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of forest cover in the country as on date, State-wise; \n(b) whether the rapid depletion of forests is a serious threat to the environment; \n(c) whether any steps are being taken to increase the forest land during the current five year \nplan; \n(d) the quantum of amount allocated for this purpose along with the details of the \nachievement made during the said period; and \n(e)  whether the Government proposes to enact a new law for the conservation of forest land, \nif so,the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) The Forest Survey of India (FSI), Dehradun, an organization under the Ministry carries \nout the assessment of forest cover of the country biennially since 1987 and the findings are \npublished in India State of Forest Report (ISFR). As per latest ISFR 2021, the total forest \ncover of the country is 7,13,789 square kilometre which is 21.72% of the geographical area \nof the country. The State/UTs wise details of forest cover as per ISFR 2021 is given in \nAnnexure I. \n \n(b) Depletion of forests is a serious threat to the environment. However, in India there has \nbeen no decrease in the forest cover. In fact, as per ISFR 2021, the forest cover of the country \nhas increased by 1540square kilometreas compared to the last assessment i.e., ISFR 2019.As \nper the latest ISFR 2021, the total forest cover of the country has increased by 12,294 square \nkilometres in the last seven years (ISFR 2015 to ISFR 2021). \n \n(c) & (d) For restoring and enhancing India’s forest cover, afforestation and tree plantation \nactivities are undertaken by States and Union Territories Governments under various State \nschemes. The Ministry provides financial assistance to States and Union Territories \nGovernments under various Centrally Sponsored Schemes namely Green India Mission to \n\nsupport and supplement the efforts of States and Union Territories. National Mission for a \nGreen India (GIM) is one of the eight Missions outlined under the National Action Plan on \nClimate Change.  \n \nIt aims at protecting, restoring, and enhancing India's forest cover and responding to Climate \nChange by undertaking plantation activities in the forest and non-forest areas. GIM activities \nstarted in the FY 2015-16. During the last five FY (2017-18 to 2021-22) a sum of Rs. 500.85 \nCrores have been released to sixteen States namely Andhra Pradesh, Arunachal Pradesh, \nChhattisgarh, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Karnataka, Kerala, Manipur, Mizoram, Odisha, \nPunjab, Uttarakhand, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Sikkim, West Bengal, and one union \nterritory, Jammu and Kashmir for creation of plantation over an area of 76587 hectares. \n \n \nThe Ministry is implementing Nagar Van Yojana (NVY) since the year 2020 which \nenvisages creation of 400 Nagar Vans and 200 Nagar Vatika in the country during the period \n2020-21 to 2024-25 with an objective to enhance the forest and green cover in the urban and \nperi-urban areas including biological diversity and provide ecological benefits and improving \nthe quality of life of city dwellers from funds available under National Compensatory \nAfforestation Fund (CAMPA). So far, the Ministry has approved 270 projects under Nagar \nVan Yojana with the total cost of Rs.238.64 Crore, which includes an amount of Rs. 57.14 \nCrore for creation of 97 Nagar Van/Vatika during 2022-23. \n \nThe Compensatory Afforestation Fund (CAMPA fund) are being utilized by States/UTs for \ntaking up various afforestation activities through planting of native species, and forest & \nwildlife protection activities as per approved Annual Plan of Operations for compensating the \nloss of forest & tree cover due to diversion of forest land for developmental projects as per \nprovisions of Compensatory Afforestation Fund Act, 2016 (CAF Act) and CAF Rules, 2018. \nThe details of CAMPA funds utilized by the States/UTs during 2017-18 to 2021-22 are \nenclosed at Annexure-II, and Afforestation taken up utilizing CAMPA fund during 2017-18 \nto 2021-22 are enclosed at Annexure-III. \n \nAfforestation activities are also taken up under various programs/schemes of line Ministry \nsuch as Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme, National Bamboo \nMission, Sub-Mission on Agroforestry etc. and under various schemes of State Government \n/UT Administration, and by various departments, Non-Government Organizations, Civil \nSociety, Corporate bodies etc. The multi departmental efforts have yielded good results in \nconserving forest and environment by addressing the problem of deforestation, besides \nkeeping the pace of development, which is evident from the fact that the fo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |604", "t": "Infrastructure Facilities/Amenities in Kendriya Vidyalayas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.604 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nINFRASTRUCTURE FACILITIES/AMENITIES  \nIN KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS \n \n604.  SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether complaints have been received from parents/public about lack of \namenities, poor maintenance of buildings of Central Schools in the country, \nespecially in Tamil Nadu region;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government thereto;  \n(c)  whether there are separate washroom facilities for boys and girls in all KV \nSchools and if not, the details of schools having common washroom facilities are \nbeing used by both boys and girls, State-wise; and  \n(d)  the details of action taken to improve the infrastructure facilities and amenities in KV \nSchools, State-wise? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) & (b) Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) has informed that no complaints have \nbeen received from parents  / public about lack of amenities, poor maintenance of \nbuildings of Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) in the country, especially in Tamil Nadu region.  \n(c) There are separate washroom facilities for both girls and boys in all KVs across \nthe country.  \n(d) KVS has taken up structural audit of KV buildings , which are more than 10 years \nold, through IITs  / NITs / Government Engineering Colleges for security and safety of \nstudents and KV staff . Regular maintenance has been done in all K Vs. Cleanliness in \nKV campus is maintained on a regular basis. There is a Monitoring Committee in every \nKV to ensure proper facilities provided to the students for  healthy and conducive \nenvironment for effective teaching -learning process. Besides t his, special repair works \nare also carried out in KVs wherever required.  \n***** \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |605", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendras", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO - 605 \nANSWERED ON – 06/02/2023 \n  \nPRADHAN MANTRI KAUSHAL KENDRAS \n \n605. SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased \nto state:- \n \n(a) the details of the number of Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendras in the country, \nState wise including Odisha; \n \n(b)  the details of the total number of students trained over the last five years, \nincluding Odisha; \n \n(c) the data of total number of students who got employed after the training \nduring the last five years, including the State of Odisha; and \n \n(d) the PMKVY targets allocated to PMKK in the Nabarangpur, Malkangiri and \nKoraput districts of Odisha and the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a)  Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) promotes \nestablishment of model training centres  in every district of the country known as \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendra (PMKK). The State wise details of  number of \nPMKKs including State of Odisha are placed at Annexure-I. \n \n(b)  Under Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY), 1.13 crore candidates \nhave been trained across the country during last five  years.  State-wise details of \ntrained candidates including State of Odisha under PMKVY are placed  at Annexure-\nII. \n \n(c) PMKVY has two training components, namely, Short Term Training (STT) and \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL). Under PMKVY, placement opportunities are \nbeing provided to STT certified candidates, while RPL is not linked with placements \nas it recognizes the existing skills of  candidate. Under the Scheme PMKVY , 21.45 \nLakh candidates are repo rted placed across the cou ntry including States of Odisha  \nduring last five years. State wise details of reported placed candidates under PMKVY \nare placed at Annexure-III. \n \n(d) Targets allocated to PMKK under PMKVY in the Nabarangpur, Malkangiri and \nKoraput districts of Odisha are 1482, 1793 and 2297 respectively. \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I  \n \nANNEXURE REFER TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 605 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nState/UT-wise Details of PMKKs as on 31.12.2022. \n \nSN. State /UT No. of PMKK \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Island 0 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 23 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 7 \n4 Assam 28 \n5 Bihar 47 \n6 Chandigarh 1 \n7 Chhattisgarh 22 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu 0 \n9 Delhi 8 \n10 Goa 1 \n11 Gujarat 28 \n12 Haryana 24 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 11 \n14 Jammu And Kashmir 18 \n15 Jharkhand 20 \n16 Karnataka 32 \n17 Kerala 20 \n18 Ladakh 2 \n19 Lakshadweep 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 52 \n21 Maharashtra 41 \n22 Manipur 13 \n23 Meghalaya 6 \n24 Mizoram 3 \n25 Nagaland 3 \n26 Odisha 26 \n27 Puducherry 4 \n28 Punjab 22 \n29 Rajasthan 32 \n30 Sikkim 3 \n31 Tamil Nadu 35 \n32 Telangana 28 \n33 Tripura 4 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 81 \n35 Uttarakhand 13 \n36 West Bengal 41 \nGrand Total 699 \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-II  \n \nANNEXURE REFER TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 605 TO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023  \n \nState/UT-wise Details of candidates trained under PMKVY during the last Five \nyears. \n \nSN\n. \nStates FY-17-18 FY-18-\n19 \nFY-19-\n20 \nFY-20-\n21 \nFY-21-\n22 \nTotal \n1.  Andaman And \nNicobar Islands \n- 74 1,259 1,464 613 3,410 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 57,484 62,475 1,05,66\n7 \n66,404 13,199 3,05,229 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 1,022 4,545 15,700 51,991 8,884 82,142 \n4.  Assam 37,582 49,854 1,98,89\n6 \n3,62,50\n6 \n24,517 6,73,355 \n5.  Bihar 90,988 84,574 1,91,90\n2 \n96,288 47,643 5,11,395 \n6.  Chandigarh 1,923 5,198 9,507 3,834 893 21,355 \n7.  Chhattisgarh 22,990 42,360 46,548 16,151 9,495 1,37,544 \n8.  Delhi 86,321 81,488 1,46,20\n5 \n55,121 19,965 3,89,100 \n9.  Goa 846 1,751 4,300 1,709 604 9,210 \n10.  Gujarat 31,661 83,956 1,55,19\n5 \n48,489 35,001 3,54,302 \n11.  Haryana 1,83,565 1,15,36\n4 \n1,75,38\n6 \n54,719 18,191 5,47,225 \n12.  Himachal Pradesh 16,627 32,048 48,870 15,612 8,724 1,21,881 \n13.  Jammu And Kashmir 44,752 35,455 1,22,65\n9 \n58,927 21,339 2,83,132 \n14.  Jharkhand 30,939 36,487 1,20,10\n3 \n15,452 34,233 2,37,214 \n15.  Karnataka 70,487 1,10,11\n1 \n1,65,24\n7 \n53,066 23,153 4,22,064 \n16.  Kerala 63,068 35,973 78,523 31,077 12,968 2,21,609 \n17.  Ladakh - 149 1,937 181 731 2,998 \n18.  Lakshadweep - - 60 90 120 270 \n19.  Madhya Pradesh 1,87,711 1,54,47\n8 \n2,23,48\n3 \n95,403 46,659 7,07,734 \n20.  Maharashtra 95,390 1,65,35\n2 \n6,37,00\n2 \n1,48,35\n2 \n39,864 10,85,960 \n21.  Manipur 4,876 4,430 28,962 34,540 6,424 79,232 \n22.  Meghalaya 4,440 7,117 11,999 17,769 3,406 44,731 \n23.  Mizoram - 3,062 10,837 11,433 4,742 30,074 \n24.  Nagaland 1,718 1,422 17,364 14,399 4,184 39,087 \n25.  Odisha 65,799 89,011 2,39,05\n0 \n68,828 12,645 4,75,333 \n26.  Puducherry 3,385 6,317 7,172 3,241 1,622 21,737 \n27.  Punjab 1,02,251 63,732 97,681 57,054 18,539 3,39,257 \n28.  Rajasthan 2,11,916 1,38,92\n3 \n4,46,90\n0 \n97,822 38,511 9,34,072 \n29.  Sikkim 525 1,932 5,123 3,634 1,322 12,536 \n\n30.  Tamil N"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |606", "t": "Upgradation of Food Craft Institutes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†606 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nUPGRADATION OF FOOD CRAFT INSTITUTES \n \n†606. SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes  to release administrative and \nfinancial approval for some institutions in Rajasthan; \n(b) whether Food Craft Institutes in Udaipur and Dholpur are proposed \nto be upgraded as State Hotel Management Institutes; \n(c) whether Heritage Circuit, Eco Adventure Cir cuit, Tribal  Circuit and \nDesert Circuit are proposed to be upgraded under Swadesh Darshan \nScheme; and \n(d) whether College of Excellence Tourism Training, Udaipur is \nproposed to be upgraded as State Tourism Training Institute? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM            (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a): Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Tourism has approved the following \nInstitutes with funding support in the State of Rajasthan: \n \nOperational: \n \ni. Central Institutes of Hotel Management (CIHM) : Jaipur \nii. State Institutes of Hotel Management (SIHM)    : Jodhpur \niii. State Institutes of Hotel Management (SIHM)    : Udaipur \niv. Food Craft Institute(FCI)           : Pali \nv. Food Craft Institute (FCI )                                    : Ajmer \n \nOngoing:  \n \ni. State Institutes of Hotel Management (SIHM)  : Dholpur \nii. State Institutes of Hotel Management (SIHM)  : Jhalawar \niii. State Institutes of Hotel Management (SIHM)  : Sawai Madhopur \niv. Food Craft Institute (FCI)                                  : Baran \n \n\n(b): Yes, Sir. Food Craft Institutes in Udaipur and Dholp ur have already \nbeen upgraded as State Hotel Management Institutes. \n \n(c): The Ministry of Tourism has now revamped its Swadesh Darshan  (SD) \nscheme as SD2.0 with the objective to develop sustainable and \nresponsible de stinations following  a tourist and destina tion centric \napproach. \n \n(d):  No proposal has been received in Ministry of Tourism to upgrade \nCollege of Excellence Tourism Training, Udaipur to State Tourism Training \nInstitute. \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |607", "t": "Education to Child Labourers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 607 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nEDUCATION TO CHILD LABOURERS \n \n†607. SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:  \n SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI: \n  \n Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)the steps taken by the Government to impart education to child \nlabours;  \n(b)the State/UT -wise number of special schools (child labour \nrehabilitation-cum-welfare centres) working in the co untry at \npresent; \n(c)the State/UT -wise details of the financial assistance extended to \nthese special schools and funds utilised particularly in Maharashtra \nduring the last five years and the current year; \n(d)whether the Government is aware of the data of i ncreased number \nof child labour after Covid pandemic; \n(e)whether the Government has examined the increasing number of \nchildren in the work force; \n(f)if so, State/UT-wise details thereof; \n(g)number of people, companies and institutions found guilty in the \nmatter and the action taken by the Government thereupon; and  \n(h)the steps taken/being taken by the Government to prevent Child \nLabour? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Labour &  Employment has been implementing \nNational Child Labour Project (NCLP) Scheme for rehabilitation of child \nlabourers through District Project Societies under the chairmanship of \nthe District Magistrate.  Under the NCLP scheme, the children in the \nage group of 9-14 years are rescued/withdrawn from work and enrolled \nin the NCLP Special Training Centres (STCs), where they are \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n provided with bridge education, vocational training, mid -day meal, \nstipend, health care, etc. before being mainstream ed into formal \neducation system.  NCLP scheme has now been subsumed under \nSamagara Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) Scheme with effect from 01.04.2021. \nHenceforth, the recused child labourers will be mainstreamed into \nformal education system through STC operational under SSA.  \n   The details of operational Special Training Centres (STC) \nunder NCLP scheme which were sanctioned before 31.03.2021 and did \nnot complete two year as on 31.03.2022 is at Annexure – I.   \n(c):  The details of release of grant under NCLP scheme, State-\nwise, including Maharashtra during 2018 -19   to 2022 -23(upto \n31.01.2023) is at Annexure II. \n \n(d) to (g): COVID-19 pandemic has impacted all section of the society \nincluding children.  \n \n         As per “Crime in India” a publication of National Crime \nRecords Bureau, 772, 476 and 613 number of cases were registered \nduring calendar years 2019 to 2021 respectively under the Child and \nAdolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986 in the \ncountry. The State-wise details are at Annexure III. \n(h):  The Government has enacted the Child Labour (Prohibition \n& Regulation) Act, 1986 which was amended in 2016.  The amended \nAct is called the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation)   Ac t, 1986.  The Act provides for complete prohibition of \nwork or employment of children below 14 years in any occupation and \nprocess and adolescents in the age group of 14 to 18 years in \nhazardous occupations and processes. The amendment also provides \nfor stricter punishment of employers for violation of the Act and made \nthe offence as cognizable.   \n \n***                                                                ******** \n  \n\nAnnexure I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 607 FOR 06.02.2023 BY SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI \nTUMANE AND SHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI REGARDING \nEDUCATION TO CHILD LABOURERS \n \nDetails of Special Training Centres (STC) which were sanctioned before 31.03.2021 and were \noperational on 31.03.2022. \n \nSl. No. State District No. of STCs given \npermission \n1 Madhya Pradesh Shajapur 15 \n2 Odisha Jharsuguda 16 \n3 Odisha Sundargarh 28 \n4 Assam Kamrup (Metro) 40 \n5 Assam Nagaon 93 \n6 West Bengal Dakshin Dinajpur 30 \n7 West Bengal Alipurduar 13 \n8 West Bengal Cooch Behar 19 \n9 Tamil Nadu Vellore 10 \n10 Tamil Nadu Dharmapuri 14 \n11 Tamil Nadu Krishnagiri 9 \n \n \n\nAnnexure II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 607 FOR 06.02.2023 BY SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE AND \nSHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI REGARDING EDUCATION TO \nCHILD LABOURERS \nGrant Released under National Child Labour Project Scheme, State-wise, during 2018-19   to \n2022-23: \n( Rs. In Lakhs) \n     \n       * Upto 31.01.2023 \n  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of  State 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23* \n1 Andhra Pradesh 309.46 202.68 306.29 32.01 116.37 \n2 Assam 1109.45 198.28 49.64 81.10 140.68 \n3 Gujarat 99.41 154.31 61.36 12.23 0 \n4 Haryana 234.66 191.77 116.83 34.79 0 \n5 Jammu & Kashmir 56.14 0 32.48 0 0 \n6 Jharkhand 0 274.54 177.42 0 60.72 \n7 Karnataka 184.23 127.38 82.74 7.53 12.27 \n8 Madhya Pradesh 514.34 491.67 363.41 143.29 236.50 \n9 Maharashtr"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |608", "t": "Linking of Pilgrim Places of Bihar", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†608 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nLINKING OF PILGRIM PLACES OF BIHAR  \n \n†608. SHRI DULAL CHANDRA GOSWAMI:  \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of pilgrim places of Bihar which have been linked under \nPilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual Augmentation Drive (PRASAD \nScheme) by the Government for tourism during the year 2015; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether the said pilgrim places of North -Eastern Bihar have also \nbeen linked and if not, whether the Government proposes to link \npilgrim places of these regions under PRASAD Scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): Project proposals under PRAS HAD Schem e of the Ministry of \nTourism are received from the respective State Government s/Union \nTerritory Administrations. On the basis of the proposals received from the \nState Government of Bihar, Patna for Patna Sahib, Gaya  for Vishnupad \nTemple and Sitamarhi  for Punaura Dham  have been included in the \nscheme. Status of the projects approved under the scheme for the State \nof Bihar is given in the below Table: \nSr. \nNo. \nName of the project Year of \nSanction \nApproved cost \n(Rs. in crore) \nPhysical \nProgress \n1 Development of basic \nfacilities at Vishnupad \ntemple, Gaya, Bihar \n2014-15 4.27 100% \n2 Development of Patna Sahib, \nPatna, Bihar  \n2015-16 41.54 100% \n \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |609", "t": "Filing of Income Tax", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 609 \n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 06, 2023 / MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nFILING OF INCOME TAX  \n \n609. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of persons between the age of 18 to 35 years that have filed income tax in the \ncurrent financial year; \n \n(b) the amount of income tax collected from the above age category under the head s of \nincome from salary, income from house property, income from profits and gains from business \nor profession, income from capital gains and income from other sources in the same period;  \n \n(c) the number of persons below the age of 18 years that filed inco me tax in the same period; \nand  \n \n(d) the amount of income tax collected from below 18 age category under the heads of \nincome from salary, income from house property, income from profits and gains from business \nor profession, income from capital gains and income from other sources during the same period?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a). Total number of Income tax Returns (ITRs)  filed by individuals (between age of 18 to 35 \nyears) during FY 2022-23 till 31st January 2023: 2,09,06,829 \n \n(b). As per Income -tax Act,1961, tax is calculated and collected based on total income returned \nin ITR. Total Income includes income from different heads of income such as income from \nsalary, income from house property, income from profit and gains from business or profession, \nincome from capital gains and income from other sources. Tax is not collected separately under \ndifferent heads. Gross taxes paid as per ITRs filed, before claim of refund: Rs 93,318 crore  \n \n  \n\n \n(c). Total number of ITR filed by individual (below age of 18 years) during FY 2022-23 till 31st \nJanuary 2023: 4,861 \n \n(d). As per Income -tax Act,1961, tax is calculated and collected based on total income returned \nin ITR. Total Income includes income from different heads of income such as income from \nsalary, income from house property, income from profit and gains from business or profession, \nincome from capital gains and income from other sources. Tax is not collected separately under \ndifferent heads. Gross taxes paid as per ITRs filed, before claim of refund: Rs 6.31 crore.  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |61", "t": "Smart City Advisory Forum", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n61\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n7\nTH\n \n \nFEBRUARY\n, 202\n3\n \n \n \nPRODUCTION OF BIO\n-\nFERTILIZERS\n \n \n*\n61\n.\n \nSHRI \nMARGANI BHARAT\n:\n \n \nSHRI \nPOCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY\n:\n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \n    \nthe \ntotal production of fertilizers including bio\n-\nfertilizers, type\n-\nwise\n;\n \n \n(b)\n \n   \nwhether the Government has taken \nany steps to increase the production of bio\n-\nfertilizers\n;\n \n \n(c)\n \n   \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n \n(d)\n \n   \nwhether the Government has experienced any challenges in promoting bio\n-\nfertilizers \namong farmers; and\n \n \n(e)\n \n   \nif so, the details there\nof and the steps ta\nken to address these challenges\n?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \n                          \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n       \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (e\n):\n \n    \nA \ns\ntatement is laid on the Table of \nthe House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEME\nNT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (e\n) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. \n61 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 07\n.0\n2\n.202\n3\n \nREGARDING \n“\nPRODUCTION OF \nBIO\n-\nFERTILIZERS\n”\n.\n \n \n(a)\n:  \nThe total production of various types of fertilizers during \nlast 3 \nyear\n \n(2019\n-\n20 to \n2021\n-\n22\n)\n.\n \n(in LMT)\n \nYear\n \nUrea\n \n    \nDAP\n \n   \nNPK\n \n   \nSSP\n \nTotal \nfertilizers \n \n2019\n-\n \n20\n \n244.58\n \n45.50\n \n93.34\n \n42.53\n \n425.95\n \n2020 \n-\n \n21\n \n246.05\n \n37.74\n \n100.54\n \n49.35\n \n433.68\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n250.72\n \n42.22\n \n89.67\n \n53.34\n \n435.95\n \nSource: dbtfert.nic.in\n \n \nThe total p\nroduction of Bio\n-\nfertilizers during last 3 years (2019\n-\n20 to 2021\n-\n22)\n \n \nYear\n \nCarrier based (in metric \ntonnes)\n \nLiquid Based   (in KL)\n \n2019\n-\n \n20\n \n79436.70\n \n30105.90\n \n2020 \n-\n \n21\n \n192329.19\n \n42239.76\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n169379.04\n \n232934.29\n \nSource: NCONF\n \n \n   \nThe total production of various types of bio\n-\nfertilizers (Liquid and Carrier based) for the \nyear 2021\n-\n2022 is given below. \n \n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nType \nof \nbio\n-\nf\nertilizers\n \nLiquid \nBased\n \n(in \nMT)\n \nCarrier \nBased\n \n(in \nKL)\n \n1\n \nAzotobacter\n \n6276.43\n \n10720.75\n \n2\n \nAzospirillum\n \n3559.3\n \n4963.18\n \n3\n \nRhizobium\n \n3419.01\n \n5996.98\n \n4\n \nPhosphate Solubilising Bacteria\n \n28832.2\n \n11235.6\n \n5\n \nPotassium Mobilizing Biofertilizers\n \n122261\n \n69976.02\n \n6\n \nZinc solubilizing Bacteria\n \n15362.4\n \n5229.93\n \n7\n \nMycorrhizal bio\n-\nfertilisers\n \n992.09\n \n17528.4\n \n8\n \nAcetobacter\n \n17095\n \n724.65\n \n9\n \nNPK Consortium \n \n30011.8\n \n10315.34\n \n10\n \nPhosphate Solubilising Fungal Bio\n-\nfertilizers\n \n0.6\n \n377.16\n \n11\n \nOther\n \n5123.76\n \n31881.66\n \n \nTotal\n \n232934\n \n169379.04\n \nSource: NCONF\n \n \n \n\n \n(b) & (c):\n \n     \nUnder the \nSoil Health Card \n(SHC)/ Soil Health Management (SHM) schemes of \nNational Project on Management of Soil Health & Fertility\n, bio\n-\nfertilizers are promoted through \nprovisions of financial assistance for strengthening of infrastructure for production and quality \ncontrol. \nUnder \nthe scheme, there is provision for providing assistance for setting up of bio\n-\nfertilizer production unit @ Rs. 160 lakh/ unit of 200 MT capacities. Since 2014\n-\n15 to 2021\n-\n22 \na total 25 bio\n-\nfertilizers units have been established in different States across t\nhe country to \nincrease the production of bio\n-\nfertilizers.\n \n \n(d) & (e):\n \n   \nThe major challenges of bio fertilizers are maintaining viability/performance of Bio \nFertilizers and its quality control. There is less demand for the bio fertilizer in \ncomparison to \nchemical fertilizers amongst farmers primarily on account of perception that chemical fertilizers \nhave high nutrient content vis\n-\nà\n-\nvis bio fertilizer which is not direct source of nutrients secondly \nbio fertilizers being live products are tem\nperature sensitive and have short shelf life posing \ndifficulty in storage and transportation to farmer’s doorstep. Further, after chemical fertilizer \nusage, the impact of bio fertilizer on crop productivity is limited.\n \n \n    \nTo encourage farmers to use the \nbio\n-\nfertilizers, Government has taken various initiatives \nsuch as:\n \n \ni.\n \nUnder SHC/SHM schemes Government is promoting Integrated Nutrient Management \n \n(INM) through judicious use of chemical fertilizers in conjunction with bio\n-\nfertilizers and \norganic \nmanures for improving soil health and fertility\n.\n \nii.\n \nMinistry of Fertilizer directed all fertilizer marketing companies for mandatory off\n-\ntake of \nFermented Organic Manure and other organic and bio\n-\nfertilizers with chemical fertilizers \nas a “Basket Approach” to\n \nensure balanced and Integrated Nutrient Management for the \ncrops.\n \niii.\n \nThe Integrated use of bio\n-\nfertilizer, organic fertilizer and organic manure has been made \nintegral part of package of practices developed by ICAR and State Agriculture Universities.\n \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |610", "t": "Inspection of Dying Units", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.610 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nInspectionof Dying Units  \n \n610. SHRI A.K.P. CHINRAJ:  \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the name of Units which Tamil Nadu Pollution Control Board has taken against for \nnon-compliance based on the joint inspections carried from June to August 2022 by \nthe Committee comprising members from Integrated Regional Office, Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change, Chennai, Central Pollution Control Board, \nTamil Nadu Pollution Control Board and representatives of Hon’ble Member of \nParliament to carry out inspection of dying units in Namakkal, Karur, Erode and \nTiruppur districts of Tamil Nadu; \n(b) the amount of fine levied against the 25 units which were found non-compliant in \nrespect of discharge of effluent, non-operable condition of Reverse Osmosis (RO), \nSEP, Unit-wise; \n(c) whether any criminal action is taken as per provisions of IPC and other act against the \n25 units which were found non-compliant in respect of discharge of effluent, non-\noperable condition of RO, SEP; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (d) Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC) had constituted a \ncommittee comprising of members from Integrated Regional Office (IRO)Chennai, \nMoEF&CC, Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), Tamil Nadu Pollution Control Board \n(TNPCB) and representatives of Hon’ble Member of Parliament to carry out inspection of \ndying units in Namakkal, Karur, Erode and Tirrupur districts of Tamil Nadu.   \n  \nJoint inspections were carried from June to August 2022 in 44 industrial units, \nselected through computerized randomization. Out of 44 units, 10 units were from Namakkal \nDistrict, 06 units were from Karur District, 23 units werefrom Erode District and 05units \nwere from Tirrupur. All the textile units inspected had ZLD system, 20 units had Solar \nEvaporation Pond (SEP) and remaininghad Agitated Thin Film Dryer (ATFD). During \ninspection, 25 units were found non-compliant in respect of discharge of effluent, non-\noperable condition of reverse osmosis (RO), SEP etc. The TNPCB has taken action for non-\ncompliance against 25 units. The details are enclosed at Annexure I. \n \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I  \n \nName of Units for which Tamil Nadu Pollution Control Board (TNPCB) has taken action \nagainst for non-complying based on the joint inspections which were carried from June to \nAugust 2022. \n \n(a).  Closure direction and disconnection of power supply issued to the Units \n1. M/s. Kandhan Palani Textiles Dyeing Factory, S.F.No.18/1, Kumarapalayam \nAgraharam Village, Tiruchengode Taluk, Namakkal District \n2. M/s. Akhil Fab, S.F.No.407/1, Samayasangili Agraharam Village, Kumarapalayam \nTaluk, Namakkal District \n3. M/s. Subbulakshmi Textiles, S.F.No.56/1,18A/4, South Colony, Komarapalayam \nAmani Village, Kumarapalayam Taluk, Namakkal District [Vacated the premises] \n4. M/s. RithanyaColours, SF.No. 331/5, Erode Village, Erode Taluk, Erode District \n(b). Show Cause Notice issued to the Units \n5. M/s. Sri Kuppannasamy Labour Dyeing Works, R.S.No.598/2, Thenmugam \nVellode village, Perundurai Taluk, Erode District. \n6. M/s. Abirami Dyeing, S.F No. 66/2, Attavanipidariyur Village, Perundurai Taluk, \nErode District \n7. M/s. Indian Dyeing, SF.No.254/20, Murungathozhuvu Village, Perundurai Taluk, \nErode District \n8. M/s. Hemalatha Dyeing Factory, R.S.No. 33/5, 33/6, Senkattu§ Thottam, Kadayampatti \n(Post), Andikulam Village, Bhavani —Taluk, Erode District \n9. M/s. Sri Jeyalakshmi Dyeing, S.F. No. 456/14, Kadaiyampatty, Bhavani Taluk, \nErode District \n10. M/s. Crystal Knitters Pvt Ltd, S F.No.353, Punnam Village, Bhavani (Tk), Erode \nDistrict \n11. M/s. CheranTextiles,SF.No. 44/A, 44/B,B.P.Agraharam Village, Erode Taluk, \nErode District \n12. M/s. Sri Velmurugan Textiles, SF.No. 234/2, Erode Village, Erode Taluk, Erode \nDistrict \n13. M/s. ASMS Printers, SF.No, 33 part,43 part, 30 part,32 part, 32.45.46, Erode \nVillage, Erode Taluk, Erode District \n14. M/s. Jai Textile Unit Private Limited, SF.No. 90/ 5, 94/1, Suriyampalayam Village, \nErode Taluk, Erode District \n15. M/s. Visalatchi International (Formerly Sri Kandhan Rugs Exports Private Limited), \nS.No.6 569 Part, Komarapalayam, Komarapalayam Taluk, Namakkal \n16. M/s. A.V.R. Dyeing, SF.No. 108/1C, Kaliyanoor village, Kumarapalayam Taluk, \nNamakkal District \n(C). Directions/ Instructions issued to the Unit \n17. M/s. Sri Jai Maruthi Textiles Processors, S.F. No. 13/2, Urachikottai Village, \nBhavani Taluk, Erode District \n18. M/s. Sri Om Sakthi Dyeing, S.F. No. 751/2, Bhavani Village, No. 69f, Sengadu, \nKadayamapatty, Bhavani Taluk, Erode District \n19. M/s. Armstrong Process, SF.No.14/1,14/2, Nanja"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |611", "t": "Local Government Institutions", "head": "                                      GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 611 \n \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, 06 FEBURARY 2023 \n \nLOCAL GOVERNMENT INSTITUTIONS \n \nQUESTION \n \n611. SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS             कारपोर े ट कार्य मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government concurs with the view that corporations \ncan work as an evidence -based voice for decentralised community \naction under the umbrella of local government institutions;  \n(b) if so, the details of the steps that are proposed to be taken by the \nGovernment in this regard; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n                                                       ANSWER \n \nMinister of State (Independent Charge) of the Ministry of Statistics and \nProgramme Implementation; Minister of State (Independent Charge) of \nthe Ministry of Planning and Minister of State in th e Ministry of \nCorporate Affairs. \n \nसांख्यिकी और कार्यक्रम कार्ायन्वर्न मंत्रालर् के राज्य मंत्री (स्वतंत्र प्रभार); र्ोजना मंत्रालर् के राज्य मंत्री (स्वतंत्र प्रभार) और \nकॉपोर े ट मामलों के मंत्रालर् में राज्य मंत्री। \n                                                                          (Rao Inderjit Singh) \n                                                                                  राव इंद्रजीत ससंह  \n \n(a) to (c): No view has been formed by the Government on this \nmatter. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |612", "t": "Seva Bhoj Yojana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.612 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6thFEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nSEVA BHOJ YOJANA \n \n612. SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT:   \nWill the MINISTER OF CULTURE be pleased to state:   \n(a) whether the Government is implementing the SevaBhojYojana in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the funds set aside, allocated and disbursed under the scheme so far, State/UT-wise; and \n \n(d) the number of States applied and selected/ rejected under the scheme so far, State/UT-wise \nincluding various districts of Uttar Pradesh? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)Yes Sir, the Ministry of Culture is implementing a Central Sector Scheme by the name of \n‘SevaBhojYojna’ throughout the country. \n \n(b) Under the Scheme of SevaBhojYojna, financial assistance in the form of reimbursement of \nCGST and Central Government’s share of IGST paid by charitable/religious institutions like \nGurudwaras, Temples, Dharmik Ashram, Mosques, Dargah, Church, Math, Monasteries \netc.on purchase of specific raw food items viz. (i) Ghee (ii) Edible Oil (iii) \nSugar/Burra/Jaggery (iv) Rice (v) Atta/Maida/Rava/Flour and (vi) Pulses for serving free \nfood to public / devotees is re-imbursed.  \n \nThese Charitable/Religious Institutions are required to distribute free food in the form of \n‘prasad’, ‘langar’/bhandara (community kitchen) to at least 5000 persons in a calendar \nmonth for at least past 3 years. \n \n(c)The details of funds allocated and disbursed under SevaBhojYojna is at Annexed. \n \n(d) SevaBhojYojna is a Central Sector Scheme under which financial assistance, in the form of \nreimbursement of CGST and Central Government’s share of IGST, is directly being given to \neligible Charitable / Religious institution for distributing free food to public throughout the \ncountry including the various districts of Uttar Pradesh. As State Governments are not \neligible to apply under the Scheme, no application has been received from any State \nGovernment under SevaBhojYojna. \n \n**** \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Part (c) of the LokSabhaUnstarred Q. No. 612 due for \nanswer on 06-02-2023 \nDetails of funds allocated and disbursed under the SevaBhojYojna: \n \n   (Rs. in lakhs) \nSl. \nNo.  \n  \nYear Fund \nAllocated  \nName of Organizations Fund \nDisbursed  \n 1.  2019-2020 400.00 Sikh GurudwaraParbandhak Committee (SGPC) \n195.90 TirumalaTirupatiDevasthanams, Tirupati,  \nSri VenkateswaraAnnaprasadam Trust, Tirupati \n2.  2020-2021 203.00 Sikh GurudwaraParbandhak Committee (SGPC) \n169.00  Dreams & Beauty Charitable Trust, Ludhiana \n Durgiana Temple, Amritsar \n3.  2021-2022 205.00 Sikh GurudwaraParbandhak Committee (SGPC) \n154.00  Dreams & Beauty Charitable Trust, Ludhiana \n Durgiana Temple, Amritsar \n4.  2022-2023 \n(as on \n31.01.2023) \n305.00 Sikh GurudwaraParbandhak Committee (SGPC) \n143.00  Dreams & Beauty Charitable Trust, Ludhiana \n Durgiana Temple, Amritsar \n TOTAL 661.90 \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |613", "t": "Funds for Heritage Sites in West Bengal", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.613 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nFUNDS FOR HERITAGE SITES IN WEST BENGAL \n \n613.  MS. MIMI CHAKRABORTY: \n \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the heritage sites in the State of West Bengal; \n(b) whether State of West Bengal has received any fund for these heritage sites from the \n“World Heritage Fund” during the last three years and current    year; \n(c) if so, year-wise details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d) the details of plans to include State-wise heritage sites in the world heritage list during this \ncurrent year; and \n(e) so, the details of funding thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n \n(a)  Details of 135 monuments and sites which are declared to be of national importance in \nWest Bengal, are given at Annexure.  \n(b) Archaeological Survey of India which conserves, preserves and maintains centrally \nprotected monument has not received any such funds. \n (c) Question does not arise. \n(d) \n \nIndia has already submitted nomination dossiers for ‘Shantiniketan’, West Bengal for \n2022 and ‘Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysala’, Karnataka for the year 2023. \n(e) Expenditure incurred on conservation and preservation of these monuments during the \ncurrent year is as under:  \n                     (Amount in crore rupees) \nShantiniketan, West \nBengal. \n1.19 \nSacred Ensembles  of \nthe Hoysala, \nKarnataka  \n 2.47 \n \n--- \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED    \nQUESTION NO. 613 FOR 06.02.2023. \n \nDETAILS OF MONUMENTS AND SITES OF NATIONAL  IMPORTANCE IN THE STATE \nOF WEST BENGAL \n \nSl.No. Name of Monument / Site Locality District \n1.  Ancient Temple Bahulara Bankura \n2.  Brindaban Chandra Temple,  Birsingha Bankura \n3.  Radha Damodar Temple Birsingha Bankura \n4.  Dalmadal Gun and the platform on which it is \nmounted \nBishnupur Bankura \n5.  Gate of Old Fort Bishnupur Bankura \n6.  Jora Mandir Bishnupur Bankura \n7.  Jore-Bungla Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n8.  Kala Chand Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n9.  Lalji Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n10.  Madan Gopal Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n11.  Madanmohan Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n12.  Maleswar Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n13.  Muralimohan Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n14.  Nanda Lal Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n15.  Patpur Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n16.  Radhabinode Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n17.  Radhagovinda Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n18.  Radhamadhab Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n19.  Radhashyam Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n20.  Rashmancha Bishnupur Bankura \n21.  Shyam Rai Temple Bishnupur Bankura \n22.  Small Gateway of Fort Bishnupur Bankura \n23.  Stone Chariot Bishnupur Bankura \n24.  Saileswar Temple Dihar Bankura \n25.  Sareswar Temple Dihar Bankura \n26.  Temple of Radha Damodar Jew Ghutgeria Bankura \n27.  Gokulchand Temple Gokulnagar Bankura \n28.  Temple of Ratneswar Jagannathpur Bankura \n29.  Temple of Shyam Sundar Madanpur Bankura \n30.  Temple site now represented only by a Mound and \na Statue of Surya. \nPareshnath Bankura \n31.  Temple site of an old Jain Temple now \nrepresented only by a Mound with a Jain statue. \nPareshnath Bankura \n32.  Image of Durga slaying Mahisasura under a tree Sarengarh Bankura \n33.  Temple site now represented only by a Mound  Sarengarh Bankura \n34.  Temple site now represented only by a Mound \nwith statues of Ganesh and Nandi on it. \nSarengarh Bankura \n35.  Temple site now represented only by a Mound Sarengarh Bankura \n\nSl.No. Name of Monument / Site Locality District \nwith an Image of Nandi on it. \n36.  Rock Inscription of Chandra Varman Susunia Bankura \n37.  Two Mounds Bhadeswar Birbhum \n38.  Temple of Radha Binode commonly known as \nJoydeb. \nJoydeb-Kenduli Birbhum \n39.  Old temple of Dharmaraj Kubilashpur Birbhum \n40.  The temple of Basuli and the mound together with \nfourteen other temples near them containing the \n“Linga” image of Siva \nNanoor Birbhum \n41.  Temple and Rasmancha Suri Birbhum \n42.  Two Ancient Temples (Joined together) Baidyapur Burdwan \n43.  Rudreswar Temple Bamunara Burdwan \n44.  Group of four Ancient Temples Begunia Burdwan \n45.  Ancient Mound Bharatpur Burdwan \n46.  The tombs of Baharam Sakka, Sher Afgan and \nNawab Kutabuddin \nBurdwan Burdwan \n47.  Jain Brick Temple known as Sat-Deul Deulia Burdwan \n48.  Stone Temple Garui Burdwan \n49.  Temple of Ichai Ghose Gourangpur Burdwan \n50.  Group of Temples (12 nos. Temples) \n(i) Bijoy Vaidyanath Temple, \n(ii) Giri Gobardhan Temple, \n(iii) Gopalji Temple, (iv) Jaleswar Temple, (v) \nKrishna Chandraji Temple, (vi) Lalji Temple, (vii) \nNaba-Kailasha Temple, (viii) Pancharatna \nTemple, \n(ix) Pratapeswar Siva Temple in Rajbari      \nComplex, (x)Rameswar Temple, (xi) Ratneswar \nTemple, (xii) Rupeswar Temple \nKalna Burdwan \n51.  The area situated between the road to Amlajore \nand the river Damodar \nNadhia Burdwan \n52.  Ancient Site and Remains of Pandurajar "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |614", "t": "Vacancies in the National Commission on ST", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 614 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nVACANCIES IN THE NATIONAL COMMISSION ON ST \n  \n      614.  SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n  \n      Will the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of vacancies in the National Commission on Scheduled Tribes; \n \n(b) the reasons for said vacancies and the steps taken to fill them; \n \n \n(c) whether it is also true that the Annual Reports of the Commission are pending since \n2018; and \n \n(d) if so, the reasons for the delay and the expected time by which it is likely to be presented? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n  \n(a) to (b):  As on 31.1.2023, against the total sanctioned posts of 124,  54  posts are filled and the \nvacant posts are 70. For Group A posts in NCST, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs is the cadre \ncontrolling authority and for the Group B and Group C, the NCST is the cadre controlling \nauthority. The Ministry of Tribal Affairs and National Commission for Scheduled Tribes are in \nthe process of filling of vacant posts. Since, promotion and filling up of vacant positions in \nNCST is a continuous process, the Ministry of Tribal Affairs and the NCST are continuously \nmaking efforts for filling up of vacant positions on priority basis.    \n(c) to (d): As per Clause 6 of Article 338 A of the Constitution of India, the President shall cause \nall such reports to be laid before each House of the Parliament along with a memorandum, \nexplaining action taken or proposed to be taken on recommendations related to the Union and \nreasons for non-acceptance, if any, of any of such recommendations. \nFor preparation of the Explanatory Memorandum, the concerned stakeholders i.e. \nCentral  Ministries / Departments of the Government of India, State Governments, Statutory \nBodies, Autonomous Institutions, Public Sector Banks and undertakings are requested to furnish \nAction taken Reports/comments/inputs. As and when Action Taken Report/comments/inputs are \nreceived from various stakeholders, an Action Taken Memorandum on Recommendations and \nPolicy issues are compiled and prepared. Thereafter, after getting the approval of the Hon'ble \nPresident of India, the Reports of the NCST alongwith Action Taken Memorandum are laid in \nboth the Houses of the Parliament. \nThree Annual Reports (9th, 10th & 11th) of National Commission for Scheduled Tribes have \nbeen laid by the Ministry before the Parliament since 2018. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |615", "t": "Meetings held by CAQM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 615 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON06.02.2023 \n \nMeetings held by CAQM \n \n615. SHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government/Commission for Air Quality Management (CAQM) in NCR \nand Adjoining Areas held number of meetings in the recent years and recently also in \norder to address the dangerous and deteriorating level of air quality and pollution in \nDelhi and NCR region; \n(b) if so, the details of the meetings held during the last three years, year-wise; \n(c) whether the CAQM propose to rope in experts and also emergency and long term \nmeasures are being initiated to address the issues; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) \nWith a view to abate high levels of air pollution arising from various contributing factors / \nactivities in the NCR, the Commission for Air Quality Management (CAQM)has held regular \nmeetings with the stakeholder agencies concerned, including officials in the State Govt. of \nPunjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan and GNCT of Delhi. The details of the meetings \nheld during the last three years, year-wise are as given as Annexure-I. \n \n(c)& (d) \nThe CAQMfrom time to time seeks expert advice from academic / technical / research \ninstitutes of experts from Indian Metrological Department (IITM), CPCB, IITs, CSIR-\nNEERI, ISRO, IARI etc. in order to deal with various issues related to air quality of Delhi \nand measures for management of air pollution in Delhi and NCR. \n \nFurther, to monitor, prevent, control and abate air pollution in Delhi–NCR arising from \nvarious contributing sources / sectors, the Commission has approved 07 (seven) research and \ndevelopment pilot projects for field demonstration and evaluation of various technology \ninterventions. \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \nDetails of meetings held by CAQM \n \nMeeting of the Commission \n/ Sub Committees \nNumber of meeting held \n2021-2022 2022-2023 \nCommission for Air Quality \nManagement in NCR and \nAdjoining Areas (Full \nCommission) \n08 03 \nStatutory Sub-Committee 10 08 \nOther Sub-Committee \nMeetings, Expert \nCommittees meetings & \nMisc. subject specific \nmeetings etc. \n43 105 \nTotal 61 116 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |616", "t": "Regional Variations in CSR Spending", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 616 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/ \nMAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nREGIONAL VARIATIONS IN CSR SPENDING \n \nQUESTION \n \n616. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA: \n \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken cognisance of the fact that there are wide-spread \nregional and sectoral variations in Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) spending; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons for  such variation in CSR spending across \nsectors and regions; and  \n \n(c) the measures being taken by the Government  to reduce such variation in CSR \nspending? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS                                                                 \n [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n(a) to ( c): The broad framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has been \nprovided under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the Act \nand Companies (CSR Policy) Rule s, 2014. The CSR framework is disclosure based and \nCSR mandated companies are required to file details of CSR activities annually in the \nMCA21 registry. All data related to CSR filed by companies in MCA21 registry is available \nin public domain and can be accessed at www.csr.gov.in.   \n \nAn analysis of CSR data during the period FY 2014-15 to FY 2020-21, reveals that around \n35% of the total CSR spent by the companies is in the states of Maharashtra, Karnataka, \nGujarat, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu. Similarly, around 60% of the total CSR spent by \nthe companies is in the areas of education, healthcare and rural development related \nactivities.  \nSection 135 (5) o f the Act provides that the company shall give preference to the local \narea and areas around it where it operates. In order to ensure an all India spread of CSR \nexpenditure, the Ministry vide General Circular no. 14/2021 dated 25.08.2021 had clarified \nthat the emphasis on local area is only directory and not mandatory in nature and \ncompanies need to balance local area preference with national priorities. It was further \nclarified that the activities mentioned in Schedule VII are broad based and  can be \ninterpreted liberally. Under the Act, CSR is a Board driven process and the Board of the \ncompany is empowered to plan, decide, execute and monitor CSR activities of the \ncompany based on the recommendation of its CSR Committee. The Government does not \nissue any specific direction to the companies to spend in any particular geographical \narea or activity. \n  ****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |617", "t": "Proposal from Kerala", "head": "MINISTRY OF CULTURE \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.617  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPROPOSAL FROM KERALA \n \n617.      DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state \n \n(a) whether the Government is currently evaluating any recommendations for inclusion \nin UNESCO’s List of Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether any of the aforesaid recommendations pertains to the State of Kerala; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Government has received any proposals from the State Government or \nother bodies/persons in Kerala for inclusion in the UNESCO’s List of Intangible \nCultural Heritage of Humanity; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN \nREGION  \n \n(SHRI G.KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (d) As per the latest Operational Directives of UNESCO, only one nomination per \nsubmitting country is to be considered every two years. “Garba of Gujarat” has been nominated \nby the Government in 2022 which will be evaluated for possible inscription in 2023. The next \nnomination would be submitted in 2024 for a possible inscription in 2025 and would be finalized \nin due course. \n \n(e) & (f) Yes Sir, following three nominations related to Kerala have been received: \n \n1) Shadow puppet theatre traditions (which includes Tolpava Kuthu of Kerala along with \nshadow puppet forms from other states) \n \n2) Chettikulangara Kumbha Bharani Kettukazhcha (practiced in various villages \nof Alleppey district of Kerala State) \n \n3) Aranmula Metal Mirror, Aranmula Vallasadhya (proposal submitted by Aranmula \nHeritage Trust, Kerala) \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |618", "t": "Investment in Stock Exchange", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 618 \nANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2023/ MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nINVESTMENT IN STOCK EXCHANGE \n \n618: SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of Finance be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any step to increase investment in stock exchange;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has taken any special efforts to protect interest of investors; \nand  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nSHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n \n(a) and (b): The Securities Contracts (Regulation) (Stock Exchanges and Clearing \nCorporations) Regulations, 2018, provide for an investment limit of 5% of the paid up equity \nshare capital in a recognized stock exchange, per investor or group of investors . For certain \nclass of investors, this limit has been fixed at 15%.  \n \n(c) and (d): SEBI undertakes various investor education and awareness activities to create \nawareness on relevant issues and to protect the interests of investors in securities market. \nInvestor awareness programs/workshops and mass media campaigns are conducted covering \nbasic aspects of securities market, precautions to be taken while investing in securities \nmarket, rights and obligations of investors, Do ’s and Don’ts, grievance redressal mechanism \nin the securities market, etc.  \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |619", "t": "Regulation of Web3 Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.619 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nRegulation of Web3 Sector \n619. SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has prepared any roadmap for the future to regulate the Web3 \nsector in the country, and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has undertaken any consultati on process in the past with the \nrelevant stakeholders, including ind ustry, on the regulation of Web3 and related policy \nformulations, and if so, details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is undertaking any initiative to create awareness about the Web3 \necosystem and have plan to engage with the industry in this regard; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) & (b):  Web3 refers to the next generation of the internet, characterized by \ndecentralized technology and increased user control over data and privacy. The technologies \nrelated to Web3 and Blockchain are still evolving. Currently, there is no specific regulation \nfor Web3 other than the provisions of the IT Act 2008 and the Information Technology \n(Intermediary Guidelines and the Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules 2022. Further, Ministry \nof Electronics & Information Technology ( MeitY) has released the National Strategy on  \nBlockchain. This document provides strategies and recommendations for the Indian \nBlockchain ecosystem to harness the benefits of this emerging technology by focusing on \ntechnological and administrative aspects. The strategy document is available at \nhttps://www.meity.gov.in/content/national-strategy-on-blockchain. \n \n(c) & (d): The Government is cognizant of the emergence of these technologies and has \nbeen taking steps to create awareness and the ecosystem building around Web3. During \nDigital India Week in July 2022, MeitY had organized a panel discussion on “Metaverse and \nWeb3”. Further, Government of India is supporting industry in organizing conferences on \nWeb3 and related technologies for understanding the ecosystem and their challenges. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |62", "t": "Schemes for MSME Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 62\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n02.02.2023\n \n \nSCHEMES FOR MSME SECTOR\n \n \n62.\n     \n \n   \nSHRI RAJU BISTA:\n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe steps taken by the Government to boost MSME sector after the COVID\n-\n19 pandemic;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe details of ongoing schemes for MSME sector under the Ministry;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe details of beneficiarie\ns under the said schemes including the districts of Darjeeling, \nKalimpong and North Dinajpur of West Bengal; and\n \n(d)\n   \n \nthe schemes and programmes run by the Government under the Atmanirbhar Bharat \nAbhiyan?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) & (d)\n:\n \nThe Government has taken various initiatives to boost the MSME Sector. Some of \nthese include:\n \ni. \n \nRs. 5 lakh crore\n \nEmergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS) for businesses, \nincluding MSMEs.\n \nii.  \n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through Self Reliant India (SRI) Fund.\n \niii. \n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \niv. \n \nNo global tenders for procurement up\n \nto Rs. 200 crores.\n \nv. \n \n“Udyam Registration” for MSMEs, for Ease of Doing Business.\n \nvi. \n \nLaunching of an online Portal “Champions” in June, 2020 to cover many aspects of e\n-\n \ngovernance including redressing grievances and handholding of MSMEs.\n \nvii. \n \nInclusio\nn of Retail and Wholesale traders as MSMEs.\n \nviii. \n \nNon\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of MSMEs.\n \nix. \n \nLaunch of Udyam Assist Platform (UAP) on 11.01.2023 to bring Informal Micro \nEnterprises (IMEs) under the formal am\nbit for availing the benefit under Priority Sector \nLending (PSL). \n \n \n(b)& (c)\n:\n \nThe Ministry of MSME is implementing various schemes.  The details of some \nmajor schemes are as under:\n \n1\n.\n \nCredit Guarantee Scheme (CGS):\n \nMinistry of MSME has been operating Credit \nGuarantee Scheme for Micro & Small Enterprises through Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro \n& Small Enterprises (CGTMSE) since July, 2000. Under this scheme upto 85% guarantee is \nextended upto credit facility of\n \nRs. 200 lakh, for both term loan and working capital. As reported by \nCGTMSE, under the CGS, the number of guarantees approved in the country during current \nfinancial year 2022\n-\n23 (upto 31.12.2022) is \n8,43,601\n \nand in the districts of Darjeeling, Kalimpong \nand North Dinajpur of West Bengal  during the same period is as follows:\n \n \nSl.\n \nNo.\n \nName of District\n \nNo. of Guarantees  Approved\n \ni\n \nDarjeeling\n \n1,338\n \nii\n \nKalimpong\n \n45\n \niii\n \nNorth Dinajpur\n \n392\n \n \n \n\n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \n2\n.\n \nEmergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS):\n \nECLGS was announced as part of \nthe Aatma Nirbhar Bharat package in May, 2020 to provide collateral free automatic Loans for \nbusinesses, including MSMEs. The ECLGS is extended up to March 2023 with guarantee coverage \nof Rs. 5 lakh crore. As reported by NCG\nTC under the ECLGS Scheme, 1.19 crore number of \nguarantees have been issued to businesses, including MSMEs, amounting to Rs.\n \n3.60 lakh Crore. \n1.13 crore number of guarantees have been issued to MSMEs, amounting to Rs.\n \n2\n.38 lakh Crore. \nThe number of guarant\nees issued to beneficiaries including MSMEs in the State of West Bengal \nincluding districts of Darjeeling, Kalimpong and North Dinajpur since 2020\n-\n21 to January 2023 is \n20,35,873\n \namounting to Rs.\n \n20,104.66 crore.\n \n     \n \n3\n.\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generatio\nn Programme (PMEGP): \nThe Ministry of \nMSME implements Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), which is a \nmajor credit\n-\nlinked subsidy programme aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment opportunities through \nestablishment of micro\n-\nenterprises in \nthe non\n-\nfarm sector by helping traditional artisans and \nunemployed youth. Under the PMEGP Scheme the number of beneficiaries in the country are \n55,499 in the current financial year 2022\n-\n23 (upto 30.01.2023). The number of beneficiaries in the \ndistricts of \nDarjeeling, Kalimpong and North Dinajpur of West Bengal  during the same period is as  \nfollows:\n \n \n \nS\nl. \nN\n.\n \nName of District\n \nNo. of Project Assisted to set up\n \n1\n \nDarjeeling\n \n34\n \n2\n \nKalimpong\n \n16\n \n3\n \nNorth Dinajpur\n \n19\n \n \n \n \n \n***\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |620", "t": "Consumer Confidence Survey", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 620  \n To be answered on the February 6, 2023/Magha 17, 1944 (Saka)   \nConsumer Confidence Survey \n620. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.:   \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government and/or RBI issues bi-monthly consumer confidence survey on the \ncurrent consumers perceptions and future expectations and if so, the details thereof;   \n(b) the parameters which are considered for the indexes in the said survey; and  \n(c) the details of the decision taken by the Government on the recovery of consumer sentiments \nindex?   \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) to (c): The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has informed that, it conducts bi -monthly Consumer \nConfidence Survey (CCS). The survey obtains current perception (vis -à-vis a year ago) and one \nyear ahead expectations on parameters including general economic situation, emp loyment \nscenario, overall price situation, own income and spending across 19 major cities. The two \nconsumer confidence indices, viz., Current Situation Index (CSI) and the Future Expectation \nIndex (FEI) are compiled on the basis of net responses on these parameters for the current period \n(as compared to the one year ago) and a year ahead, respectively. \nRBI has further informed that it does not compile the “consumer sentiments index”.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |621", "t": "Tax on Crude Oil", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n***** \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.621 \n  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/MAGHA 17,1944 (SAKA) \n \nTAX ON CRUDE OIL \n \n621.   SHRI VIJAYAKUMAR (ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n (a) whether the Government proposes to reduce taxes on crude oil and if so, the details thereof and if not, \nthe reasons therefor;  \n(b) whether the proposed reduction in tax on crude oil will soften the international oil prices and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n(c) whether the Government proposes to reduce the tax on the export of diesel for overseas shipments and \nif so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n(a) (b) and (c):  Review of Special Additional Excise Duty (SAED) on Crude Oil and Diesel meant for \nexports is done on a regular basis and changes thereon are made on the basis of  the prevailing \nInternational Prices. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |622", "t": "Establishment of KVs/NVs in Andaman & Nicobar Islands", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.622 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nESTABLISHMENT OF KVs/NVs  \nIN ANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS \n \n622.  SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of Kendriya Vidyalayas / Navodaya Vidyalayas established in \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands during the last three years, district-wise; and  \n(b)  whether the Government has implemented/ propos es to implement any \nschemes to establish new KVs/JNVs in Andaman & Nicobar Islands and if so, \nthe details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) & (b) At present, 02 Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) namely KV Port Blair No. 1 & 2 in \nSouth Andaman  district and 03 Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas  (JNVs) in all 03 \ndistricts viz.  Car Nicobar, North & Middle Andaman and South Andaman  are \nfunctioning in the Union Territory of Andaman & Nicobar Islands. No KV and JNV are \nestablished in Andaman & Nicobar Islands during the last three years.  \n \nOpening of new KVs is a continuous process . KVs are opened primarily to \ncater to the educational needs of the wards of transferable Central Government \nEmployees including Defence & Para -military personnel, Central Autonomous \nBodies, Central Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) and Central Institute o f Higher \nLearning (IHL) by providing a common programme of education throughout the \ncountry. Proposals for opening of new KVs may be sponsored by Ministries or \nDepartments of the Government of India / State Governments / Union Territories \n(UTs) Administrations, with commitment to provide requisite resources for setting up \na new KV as per norms.  The proposals are subject to approval of the competent \nauthority.  \n\n \nThe Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme envisages opening of one Jawahar \nNavodaya Vidyalaya (JNV) in each district of the country.  Opening of new JNVs is a \ncontinuous process which depends on the willingness of the concerned State \nGovernment / UT administration to make available requisite suitable land, free of \ncost, for construction of the permanent building and making available required \ntemporary building, free of rent, to run the Vidyalaya till the permanent building is \nconstructed.  Sanction and opening of new JNV depends on approval by the \ncompetent authority.  All the districts ( as on 31 st May, 2014) of the States / UTs, \nwhich have accepted the NVS scheme, including all the 03 districts of Andaman & \nNicobar Islands, have been covered under this Scheme. \n***** \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |623", "t": "SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED NO- 623 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 06/02/2023 \n  \nFAKE CASTE CERTIFICATES \n  \n623. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE:  \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the large number of fake caste certificates being issued \nfor the Scheduled Tribes especially in Maharashtra, Telangana and Gujarat; \n(b) if so, the steps taken by the Government to eradicate the rampant/increasing cases of issuing/ \nprocuring Fake Caste Certificates of the Scheduled Tribes in the above mentioned States; \n(c) whether the Government is contemplating to frame a separate law at Union level in this \nregard; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof?   \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n  \n(a) to (d): Ministry of Tribal Affairs is nodal Ministry for Notification of a community as \nScheduled Tribe under Article 342 of the Constitution. However, the responsibility of issuance \nand verification of Scheduled Tribe certificate / social status rests with the concerned State \nGovernment / UT Administration. All complaints/grievances/representations received in this \nregard are forwarded to State Government/UT Administration concerned. \n \n        The Ministry has been issuing advisories from time to time regarding issuance of ST \ncertificates with proper verification and ensure that deserving ST persons are issued certificates \nwithout undue delays and difficulties. The last such advisory was issued on 17/02/2020.   \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |624", "t": "Inclusion of Kolar and Chikballapur in Tourist Circuit", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.624 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nINCLUSION OF KOLAR AND CHIKBALLAPUR IN TOURIST CIRCUIT \n \n624.  SHRI S. MUNISWAMY:  \n     \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there is a scope for including the places of historical \nimportance from the Districts of Kolar and Chikballapur as a tourist \ncircuit; \n(b)  whether the Government recognize places like Kolaramma temple, \nAntargange, Avni village, Kotlingeshwara templ e, Bangaru Tirupati, \nLord Someshwara Temple and other places as places of historical and \ntourist destined places; \n(c)  whether there is any proposal to start a tourist circuit by clubbing the \nimportant destinations of both the districts of Kolar and Chikba llapur \nin Karnataka; and \n(d)  whether the Government has sanctioned any funds in these two \ndistricts and if so, the details thereof?  \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                    (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b): Development of Tourism is primarily the responsibility of the \nState Governments/UT Administrations. However, the Ministry of Tourism \nunder its schemes of ‘Swadesh Darshan’ and ‘National Mission on Pilgrimage \nRejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD)’  \nprovides financial assistance to State Governments/UT Administrations etc. \nfor development of tourism infrastructure in the country and the projects \nare sanctioned in consultation with the State Government/UT \nAdministrations s ubject to availability of funds, submission of suitable \ndetailed project reports, adherence to scheme guidelines and utilization of \nfunds released earlier etc. The Ministry of Tourism under its scheme  of \n‘Domestic Promotion and Publicity including Hospital ity (DPPH)’ promotes \ntourism destinations of the country holistically including those located in \nKarnataka.    \n \n(c) & (d):  The Ministry of Tourism has now revamped its Swadesh Darshan \nscheme as Swadesh Darshan 2.0 (SD 2.0) in order to develop sustainable \nand responsible tourism destinations in the country with tourist & \ndestination centric approach. The Ministry has identified Hampi and Mysuru \nfor development under SD2.0. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |625", "t": "CSR Projects", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 625 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023 MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nCSR PROJECTS   \nQUESTION \n   625. SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA:  \n       SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA:  \n       SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the works/projects completed under the Corporate Social Responsibility \n(CSR) in the country, State-wise and sector-wise; \n(b) the details of the works/projects completed under the CSR in Andhra Pradesh, district-\nwise and sector-wise; \n(c) the number of beneficiaries of such CSR projects/works in Andhra Pradesh, district -\nwise; and \n(d)  the details and the names of the projects which have not been completed under CSR \nin Andhra Pradesh along with the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CORPORATE \nAFFAIRS                                                                 \n   [RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n \n(a) to (d): The broad framework for Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) has been \nprovided under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 (‘Act’), Schedule VII of the Act \nand Companies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014. All data related to CSR filed by companies in \nMCA21 registry is available in public domain at www.csr.gov.in.  However, the present \nCSR filing does not capture the beneficiary -wise details of the CSR projects of the \ncompanies. Further, completion status of CSR projects or activities are not maintained \nseparately by the Government. \n \nOn the basis of the filings made by the companies in the MCA21 registry, State/UT-wise \nand Development sector-wise details of CSR spent by all the companies during last three \nFinancial Years (FY) 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21 are at Annexure - I & II respectively. \nFurther, the District-wise and Development sector-wise CSR spent by all the companies \nin the state of Andhra Pradesh during last three FY 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21 are at \nAnnexure - III & IV respectively.  \n \n******* \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure - I \n ANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 625 FOR 06.02.2023   \nState/UT-wise CSR exp