m 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n24.  Nagaland 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n25.  Odisha 246 5 1 2 5 0 0 11 \n26.  Puducherry 8 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 \n27.  Punjab 14 20 0 0 1 0 0 0 \n28.  Rajasthan 71 138 9 12 3 0 0 2 \n29.  Sikkim 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 \n30.  Tamil Nadu 35 6 1 0 12 1 0 8 \n31.  Telangana 6 2 2 2 2 0 1 1 \n32.  The Dadra and \nNagar Haveli and \nDaman and Diu \n0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n33.  Tripura 1 1 4 0 2 0 1 0 \n34.  Uttar Pradesh 407 752 22 52 89 3 3 47 \n35.  Uttarakhand 61 15 1 1 2 0 0 0 \n36.  West Bengal 38 88 2 0 6 5 1 22 \nTotal 2027 1699 127 156 261 21 15 209 \nGrand total of ITIs established in last 8 years 4515 \n \n  \n\nAnnexure 3 \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 643 to be answer"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |644", "t": "Shortcomings in Education Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 644 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSHORTCOMINGS IN EDUCATION SECTOR \n \n644.  SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has made any assessment in regard to the shortcomings in the education \nsector; \n(b) if so, any announcement has been made to provide special funds for doing research and quality \nimprovement in education; \n(c) the names of the States which have provided figures relating to research and quality improvement \nin the education sector during the last three years; \n(d) whether funds are being allocated to the States which are lagging behind in research; and \n(e) if so, the details of funds allocated for the above purpose, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(Dr. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a): As per the information received from the University Grants Commission (UGC), an expert \ncommittee constituted in 2019 to promote and improve the quality of research in Indian \nuniversities/colleges observed that the overall quality of university and col lege level research in India \nneeds improvement.  \n(b) to (e): Education being in the Concurrent List, enhancing the quality of education and research is \nthe responsibility of both the Central and State Governments. UGC has approved Quality Mandate on \n24.05.2018. It comprises  5 objectives and 10 verticals, to improve the quality of higher education in \nthe country. The 10 verticals are: (i)  Induction programme for students; (ii) Learning outcome -based \ncurriculum framework revision of curriculum in regular inte rvals; (iii) Use ICT based learning tools \nfor effective teaching-learning process; (iv) Soft skills for students; (v) Social and Industry connect for \nevery institution: Every institution shall adopt at least 5 villages for exchange of knowledge and for the \noverall social/economic betterment of the village communities; (vi) Examination Reforms - test the \nconcept, and application; exit examinations; (vii) Tracking of the student progress after completion of \ncourse; (viii) Induction training for all new teachers, and annual refresher training for all teachers - role \nof the National Resource Centres (NRCs); and mandatory leadership/management training for all \neducational administrators (ix) Promoting quality research by faculty and creation of new knowledge; \n(x) Mentoring of non-accredited institutions. Detail of the grants released by the UGC to Universities \n& Colleges under various schemes relating to Research Projects and Fellowships / Scholarships for the \nlast three years is as under: \nYear Amount Released (Rs. in Crore)  \n2019-20 1214.78 \n2020-21 1229.63 \n2021-22  1186.65 \n \n\nFurthermore, recognizing the need for central assistance, the Central Government has implemented \nvarious schemes for supporting State Governments as given hereunder: -  \n \nResearch Parks: Complementing the renewed focus of the Government on developing indigenous \nR&D capabilities, boosting manufacturing and creating a successful start -up culture in the country, \nResearch Parks are being established at various Indian Institutes of Technology ( IITs)/Indian Institute \nof Science (IISc). A full-fledged Research Park is already functioning in IIT Madras. Research Park at \nIIT-Kharagpur was inaugurated in Aug 2019. A Research Park at IIT Gandhinagar is funded by \nDepartment of Science & Technology (DST ). Research Park at IIT Delhi was inaugurated on \n30.09.2022. The amount released during the last three years is as under:- \nYear Amount Released (Rs. in Crore)  \n2019-20 43.87 \n2020-21 167.50 \n2021-22  30.00 \n \nIMPacting Research INnovation  and Technology (IMPRINT) India Initiative : IMPRINT-II was \nformulated in 2018 by merging the schemes IMPRINT -I (implemented up to Mar’22) and Uchhatar \nAvishkar Yojana (UAY).  The projects under IMPRINT -II are funded jointly by MoE and DST in the \nratio of 50:50 by creating a joint corpus.  Other participating Ministries/ Industries may volunteer to \nfund the projects relevant to them. Details of the projects approved and cost thereof are as under:- \nPhases Project Cost (Rs. in Crore) \nIMPRINT-II(A+B) 125 104.58  \nIMPRINT-II-C 51 41.18  \nIMPRINT-II-C-II 8 17.66 \nTOTAL  184 163.42 \n \nPrime Minister’s Research Fellows: In order to attract best talent for undertaking research in the \nfrontier areas of science & technology, preferably those addressing the national requirements,  talented \nstudents from all recognized Universities/Institutes are selected as per PMRF guidelines to enroll in the \nPh.D program in IITs/IISc/ Indian Institute of Science Education and Research ( IISERs) & selected \nCentral Universities (CUs)/National Institute of Technology ( NITs) (in NIRF top 25) and incentivized \nby providing attractive rates of fellowship @ Rs.70,000/- per month for the first two years, Rs.75,000/- \nper month for the 3 r"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |645", "t": "Battery Waste Management Rules, 2022", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 645 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nBattery Waste Management Rules, 2022 \n \n \n645.    SHRI BRIJENDRA SINGH:  \nSHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \nSHRIMATI SUNITA DUGGAL:  \nSHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has data regarding newly developed \ncollection/recycling/refurbishing centres for waste batteries after notification of \nBattery Waste Management Rules, 2022;  \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor, State-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Government is aware about the manner of utilization of environmental \ncompensation prescribed in the Battery Waste Management Rules, 2022; and  \n(d)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) Battery Waste Management Rules, 2022 have been notified under Environment \n(Protection) Act, 1986 by Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change on 22.08.2022 \nin supersession of Batteries (Management & Handling) Rules, 2001 to ensure \nenvironmentally sound management of waste batteries. The new rules broadened the scope of \nbattery which encompasses all types of batteries viz. Electric Vehicle batteries, portable \nbatteries, automotive batteries and industrial batteries whereas Batteries (Management & \nHandling) Rules, 2001 covered only lead acid batteries. As per the new rules, every recycler/ \nrefurbisher of used / waste battery is required to register with State Pollution Control Board / \nPollution Control Committee through Centralised Online Portal of CPCB.  \n \nState-wise details of recycling capacities of lead acid batteries are provided in Annexure I.  \n \n(c) & (d) The environmental compensation collected under Battery Waste Management \nRules, 2022 shall be utilised in collection and refurbishing or recycling of uncollected and \nnon-recycled or non-refurbished waste batteries. The modalities for utilisation of the funds \nfor waste batteries management would be recommended by the Committee for \nImplementation, constituted under the rules, for the approval of Central Government. \n \n*** \n\nAnnexure I \nDetails of Recyclers of Used Lead Acid Batteries & Lead Wastes /Scraps \n \nS. No States No. of Units Authorized  Capacity (MTA) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 17 251728 \n2 A & N  0 0 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 \n4 Assam 6 26100 \n5 Bihar 4 6870 \n6 Chandigarh 0 0 \n7 Chhattisgarh 5 3308 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 0 0 \n9 Daman & Diu   0 0 \n10 Delhi   0 0 \n11 Goa   0 0 \n12 Gujarat 41 381210 \n13 Haryana 58 274864 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 13 82335 \n15 Jammu  & Kashmir  7 72660 \n16 Jharkhand 2 3730 \n17 Karnataka 32 419056 \n18 Kerala 4 6875 \n19 Lakshadweep 0 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 56 126921 \n21 Maharashtra 94 350871 \n22 Manipur   0 0 \n23 Meghalaya   0 0 \n24 Mizoram   0 0 \n25 Nagaland   0 0 \n26 Orissa   0 0 \n27 Pondicherry   0 0 \n28 Punjab 55 79446,1 \n29 Rajasthan 121 440.722 \n30 Sikkim   0 0 \n31 Tamil Nadu   27 452023 \n32 Telangana 32 314000,96 \n33 Tripura   0 0 \n34 Uttar Pradesh   34 68309,9 \n35 Uttarakhand 3 34200 \n36 West Bengal 61 135612 \nTotal   672 35,30,842 \n \n* The available information collected from SPCBs/PCCs annual reports and website "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |646", "t": "Infrastructure of Government Schools in India", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.646 \nANSWERED ON 06/02/2023 \n \nInfrastructure of Government Schools in India \n \n646. SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has data regarding  the number of Government schools in the \ncountry, which have access to internet connections and smart-classes, State-wise; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, UT-wise and district-wise especially at Saran in Bihar; \n(c) the number of students per computer and the  number of science lab facilities in \nGovernment schools, State-wise, UT-wise and district-wise especially at Saran in Bihar; \n(d) the steps taken by the Government and to  ensure universal internet connectivity in public \nschools in India; and \n(e) the estimated time period by which this goal is likely to be achieved? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SHRIMATI ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) & (b):  The details of Government Schools having internet connections and smart classes \nState / UT wise are at Annexure – 1 (may be accessed at link : \nhttps://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques). The district wise details of Government Schools \nwhich have internet connections and smart classes in the State of Bihar including Saran are at \nAnnexure–2 (may be accessed at link: https://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques).  \n  \n(c):  Education is in the Concurrent list of the Constitution and majority of the school are \nunder the administrative control of respective State Government and UT Administration. The \nDepartment of School Education and Literacy with effect from 2018 -19 has laun ched an \nIntegrated Centrally Sponsored Scheme for School Education - Samagra Shiksha. Under the \nscheme, financial assistance is provided to States and UTs for undertaking various activities \nfor universalisation of school education including setting up of IC T Labs and Smart Class \nrooms.  \n\nThe ICT and Digital Initiatives component of Samagra Shiksha covers Government Schools \nhaving classes VI to XII. The provisions regarding the same are as under:  \nA. Budgetary provisions \nThe non- recurring/recurring grant under ‘ICT and Digital Initiatives’ for schools is available \nto the States and UTs for following two options: \nOption I: Under this option schools which have not availed the ICT facility earlier can either \nopt for ICT or smart classrooms as per their requirement a nd need. In case of more than 700 \nenrolments, an additional ICT lab can also be considered.  \nOption II: Under this option schools which have already availed the ICT facility earlier can \navail smart classrooms/tablets as per the norms of the scheme.  \nB. Financial provisions \nICT Lab: A non-recurring grant of up to Rs. 6.40 lakh per school and recurring grant of upto  \nRs. 2.40 lakh per school per annum for a period of 5 years.  \nFrom 2023 -24, the scheme also offers step -wise funding based on school enrolment. \n(Strength < 100   Rs. 2.5 Lakhs, Strength between 100 – 250 Rs. 4.5 Lakhs, Strength between \n250 – 700 Rs. 6.4 Lakhs)  \nSmart Classrooms: The non-recurring grant for Smart Class rooms is of Rs. 2.40 lakh and \nthe recurring grant is Rs. 0.38 lakh \nThe details of Sci ence Lab facilities in Government Schools State / UT wise are shown at \nAnnexure – 3 (may be accessed at link : https://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques). The district \nwise details of Science Labs in Govt. Schools in Bihar State including Saran are shown at \nAnnexure – 4 (may be accessed at link: https://www.education.gov.in/en/parl_ques).  \n \n(d) & (e): An advisory has been issued by the Dept. of School Education and Literary, \nGovernment of India to all the States and UTs to  enter into an MoU / Agreement with BSNL \nand provide FTTH Internet connection to all the Government Schools which have com puting \ndevices.  States / UTs have been advised that the Internet Charges can be met from the \nfollowing: \n• For ICT Labs / Smart Class rooms sanctioned under Samagra Shiksha, internet \ncharges can be met from the recurring charges being released under Samagra Shiksha. \n \n• For Schools in which ICT/ Smart Class room are not sanctioned under Samagra \nShiksha and which have computing devices, internet charges can be met from the \nManagement Monitoring Evaluation and Research (MMER) funds being released under \nSamagra Shiksha or can be met from any other State/UT Govt. funds. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |647", "t": "Allocation of Funds to the State of Telangana", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF EXPENDITURE \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 647 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, FEBRUARY 6, 2023/MAGHA 17, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nAllocation of Funds to the State of Telangana \n \n647. SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU \n SHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA \n DR. G. RANJITH REDDY \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of request received from the Government of Telangana for providing \nallocation in the Budget 2023-24 for the development of Hyderabad-Vijayawada \nIndustrial Corridor and Huzurabad, Jadcharia, Gadwal and Kothakota nodes \ndevelopment; \n \n(b) the response of the Government thereto; \n \n(c) whether Government of Telangana has sought Rs. 1,500 crores in the Budget 2023-24 \nproposed by the Government; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the amount likely to be allocated to the State Government in \nthe said Budget? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d): Yes Sir. As per the information provided by the State Government of Telangana, \nthe Government of Telangana has submitted a proposal vide letter No. 1099/D/M (IT, \nMA&UD, I&C)/2023 dated 14.01.2023 for consideration of a number of projects including \nthe Hyderabad-Vijayawada Industrial Corridor in the Union Budget 2023-24. The Union \nGovernment provides substantial resources to States through Ministries/Departments under \nthe heads Tax Devolution, Finance Commission Transfers, Centrally Sponsored Schemes \n(CSS), Central Sector Schemes (CS), ACA for EAPs, Special Assistance and Special \nAssistance to States for Capital Investment etc., which may be deployed by the State for \ndesired projects as per State’s priorities.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |648", "t": "Maternity Benefits", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 648 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nMATERNITY BENEFITS \n \n648.  SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)the number of construction workers, mine workers and factory \nworkers have received maternity benefits through the Maternity \nBenefit (Amendment) Act, 2017 through the labour department in \nthe year 2019-20, 2020-21 and 2021-22, category-wise data; \n(b)number of creches run under Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, \n2017, State-wise and sector-wise data; and \n(c)whether the Government has any plans of providing the maternity \nbenefits and child care services to the above -mentioned group of \nworkers, if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Maternity Benefit Act, 1961, as amended vide the \nMaternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017, provides inter -alia for paid \nmaternity leave to women workers and crèche facility by \nestablishments. The Act is implemented by the Central Government as \nwell as by State Government as appropriate Government in their \nrespective spheres which includes establishments engaged as mines, \nfactories and construction. \n \nThe maternity benefits are also provided to women workers \nwho are covered under the provisions of Employees’ State Insurance \n(ESI) Act, 1948. Every woman, entitled to the payment of maternity \nbenefit under the Maternity Benefit Act, continues to be covered under  \nthis Act until she becomes qualified to claim maternity benefit under \nthe ESI Act, 1948. \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n \nThe number of women workers who received maternity \nbenefit under ESI Act, 1948 during years 2019-20, 2020-21 and 2021-22 \nis as under:- \n \nYears Number \n2019-2020 49200 \n2020-2021 46327 \n2021-2022 45610 \n \nData regarding availability of crèches, under the Maternity \nBenefit Act, 1961, as amended in the year 2017, are not maintained \ncentrally in this Ministry.  \n \nAs per the scheme run by the Ministry of Women and Child \nDevelopment, the state -wise details of the crèches are placed at \nAnnexure. \n*                                                     ****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.648 ASKED BY SHRI RAVIKUMAR  D. FOR ANSWER ON \n06.02.2023 REGARDING MATERNITY BENEFITS.   \n \nDetails of number of crèches under the scheme run by the Ministry of Women and Child \nDevelopment \nSl. No. Name of State/ Union Territory  Number of Creches  \n \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 66 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 67 \n3.  Assam  611 \n4.  Bihar 0 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 0 \n6.  Goa 3 \n7.  Gujarat 54 \n8.  Haryana 167 \n9.  Himachal Pradesh 125 \n10.  Jharkhand 0 \n11.  Karnataka 430 \n12.  Kerala 333 \n13.  Madhya Pradesh 16 \n14.  Maharashtra 0 \n15.  Manipur 427 \n16.  Meghalaya 28 \n17.  Mizoram 175 \n18.  Nagaland 163 \n19.  Odisha 0 \n20.  Punjab 158 \n21.  Rajasthan 79 \n22.  Sikkim 25 \n23.  Tamil Nadu 314 \n24.  Telangana 0 \n25.  Tripura 137 \n26.  Uttar Pradesh 0 \n27.  Uttarakhand 0 \n28.  West Bengal 0 \n29.  A &N Islands (UTs) 0 \n30.  Chandigarh 54 \n31.  Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman Diu 0 \n32.  Delhi 52 \n33.  Jammu & Kashmir 0 \n34.  Ladakh 0 \n35.  Lakshadweep 0 \n36.  Puducherry 94 \n Total 3578 \nNote:  States/Union Territories running Creches: 22 \n            States/ Union Territories not running Creches: 14 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |649", "t": "ESI Hospitals/Dispensaries in West Bengal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 649 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nESI HOSPITALS/DISPENSARIES IN WEST BENGAL \n \n649. MS. NUSSRAT JAHAN: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)the number of districts in West Bengal that do not have any ESI \ndispensaries or ESIC hospitals; \n(b)the total number of workers engaged in unorganized sector in West \nBengal who are provided health facilities by ESI dispensaries and ESIC \nhospitals since last three years; \n(c)whether Government proposes to set up more ESIC hospitals in West \nBengal; and \n(d)if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d): At present, one district, namely J hargram in the state of West \nBengal does not have any Employees’ State Insurance (ESI) hospital or \ndispensary. \n \nThe Employees’ State Insurance (ESI) Act, 1948 is applicable \nto the factories and establishments having ten or more employees, \ndrawing wages up to Rs.21000/ - (Rs. 25,000/- for persons with \ndisabilities).  As such, the Act  does not apply to the unorganised sector.   \nAs on 31.03.2022, there were a total of 71,21,003 ESI beneficiaries \nincluding 18,35,310 insured persons in the state of West Bengal. \n \nThe ESI Corporation has given in -principle approval for setting \nup of 100 bedded ESI hospitals at the following locations in the state of \nWest Bengal: \n1. Siliguri \n2. Haldia \n3. Garshyamnagar \n4. Kharagpur \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |65", "t": "Health Hazards due to Water Contaminants", "head": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 65 \n  ANSWERED ON 07/02/2023 \n \nWAGE RATES UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*65.  SHRI N. REDDEPPA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it has come to the notice of the Government that the wage rates for Mahatma \nGandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) workers are highly \nfluctuating in the States and UTs; \n(b)  if so, whether the Government is considering any measures to adopt a uniform wage rate \nin the country; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d)  whether the Government is aware that nominal wages under MGNREGS discourage \nbeneficiaries to register and instead encourage them to migrate to urban areas in search of \nwork; \n(e)  if so, whether the Government has taken any steps for making the wages more fair by \nindexing MGNREGS wages to Consumer Price Index (CPI) -Rural as opposed to CPI -\nAgricultural Labour;  and \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n   \n \n(a) to (f):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n \n \nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (f) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 65 for 07.02.2023 \n  \n(a) to (c)    As per Section 6(1) of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act \n(Mahatma Gandhi NREGA), the Central Government may by notification specify the wage rate \nfor its beneficiaries. Accor dingly, the Ministry of Rural Development notifies Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGA wage rate every financial year for States/UTs. To compensate the Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGA workers against inflation, the Ministry of Rural Development revises the wage rate \nevery financial  year based on change in Consumer Price Index for Agricultural Labour (CPI -\nAL). The wage rate is made applicable from 1st  April of each financial year. However each \nState/UT can provide wage over and above the wage rate notified by the Central Government.  \n  \n(d)    No, Sir. \n  \n(e) & (f)   Does not arise. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |650", "t": "Special Package to States for Development of Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†650 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nSPECIAL PACKAGE TO STATES FOR DEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM \n \n†650. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA:  \n SHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY:  \n \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the implemented schemes and main projects to \ndevelop tourism in the country including Rajasthan, NCT of \nDelhi and Jharkhand and the funds provided under the same \nduring the last three years, State/UT-wise;  \n(b)  the details of the utilization of allocated funds out of the funds \napproved under these projects to develop tourism during each \nof the last three years along with the present status thereof, \nState/UT-wise;  \n(c) whether the Governm ent provides any special package to \nvarious States/Union Territories including Rajasthan, NCT of \nDelhi and Jharkhand to develop and promote tourism and if so, \nthe details of package provided during the said period, \nState/UT-wise; and  \n(d)  whether the Gove rnment has set up any mon itoring \nmechanism to review the progress of the said projects and if \nso, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM           (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Tourism has provided financial \nassistance under the ‘Swadesh Darshan’,  ‘National Mission on \nPilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive \n(PRASHAD)’ and ‘Assistance to Central Agencies for Tourism \nInfrastructure Develop ment’ Schemes for the development of \ntourism related infrastructure in different State s/UTs including \nRajasthan, NCT of Delhi and Jharkhand.  \n\n \n The details of sanctioned projects, amount sanctioned, \namount utilized along with their status under the above m entioned \nschemes in different States/UTs including Rajasthan, NCT of Delhi \nand Jharkhand is given at Annexure. \n \n(c): The Ministry of Tourism does not provide special package to \nthe States/UTs in order to develop and promote tourism. \n \n(d): The Ministry of T ourism monitors the physical progress of the \nsanctioned projects through field inspect ions and review meetings \nheld periodically with the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations/Central Agencies. In addition, the Ministry of \nTourism has developed  a dashboard for monitoring the \nimplementation of the sanctioned projects under the Sw adesh \nDarshan and PRASHAD Schemes. \n******\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †650 ANSWERED ON \n06.02.2023 REGARDING SPECIAL PACKAGE TO STATES FOR DEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM.  \n \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme                      (Rs. in Crore)  \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT Circuit/ \nSanction \nYear \nName of the Project Amt. \nSanctioned \nAmt. \nReleased \nAmt. \nUtilised* \nPhysical \nStatus \n(%) \n1.  Andaman & \nNicobar \nIslands \nCoastal \nCircuit  \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Long Island -\nRoss Smith Island - Neil Island- \nHavelock Island - Baratang \nIsland-Port Blair \n27.57 20.89 14.51 Complete \n2.  Andhra \nPradesh \n \nCoastal \nCircuit \n2014-15 \nDevelopment of Kakinada - \nHope Island - Coringa Wildlife \nSanctuary - Passarlapudi - \nAduru - S Yanam – Kotipally \n67.84 67.84 67.84 Complete \n3.  Andhra \nPradesh \n \nCoastal \nCircuit \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Nellore - \nPulikat Lake - Ubblamadugu \nWater Falls – Nelapattu- \nKothakoduru- Mypadu - \nRamateertham –  Iskapalli \n49.55 47.76 50.96 Complete \n4.  Andhra \nPradesh \n \nBuddhist \nCircuit \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of: Sha lihundam- \nBavikonda- Amravati- Anupu \n24.14 24.14 24.14 Complete \n5.  Arunachal \nPradesh \nNorth-East   \nCircuit \nDevelopment of Bhalukpong - \nBomdila and Tawang \n49.77 47.28 39.81 Complete \n\n 2014-15 \n6.  Arunachal \nPradesh \n \nNorth East   \nCircuit \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Nafr a- Seppa- \nPappu, Pasa, Pakke Valleys - \nSangdupota- New Sagalee - \nZiro- Yomcha \n96.72 91.88 84.24 Complete \n7.  Assam \n \nWildlife \nCircuit \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Manas – \nProbitora– Nameri– Kaziranga– \nDibru– Saikhowa \n94.68 89.94 89.53 Complete \n8.  Assam \n \nHeritage \nCircuit \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Tezpur – Majuli \n– Sibsagar \n90.98 86.42 86.42 Complete \n9.  Bihar \n \nTirthankar \nCircuit \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Vaishali - \nArrah- Masad- Patna- Rajgir- \nPawapuri- Champapuri \n33.97 30.04 \n \n26.19 Complete \n10.  Bihar \n \nSpiritual \nCircuit \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Kanwaria \nRoute: Sultanganj – \nDharmshala- Deoghar \n44.76 \n \n42.52 \n \n40.51 Complete \n11.  Bihar \n \nBuddhist \nCircuit \n2016-17 \nDevelopment of Buddhist \ncircuit- Construction of \nConvention Centre at \nBodhgaya \n95.18 93.22 93.22 Complete \n12.  Bihar \n \nRural \nCircuit \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of Bhitiharwa - \nChandrahia- Turkaulia \n44.27 39.96 35.72 Complete \n13.  Bihar \n \nSpiritual \nCircuit \n2017-18 \nDevelopment of Mandar Hill & \nAng Pradesh \n44.55 42.32 39.34 Complete  \n\n14.  Chhattisgarh Tribal \nCircuit \n2015-16 \nDevelopment of Jashpur - \nKunkuri- Mainpat- Kamleshpur \n- Maheshpur -Kurdar - \nSarodhadadar- Gangrel- \nKondagaon– \nNathiyanawagaon- Jagdalpur- \nChitrakoot- "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |651", "t": "Promotion of Border Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †651 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF BORDER TOURISM \n \n†651. SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the scheme being formulated by the Gove rnment to promote border \ntourism; \n(b) the number of borders which have been develope d as tourist places \nin the country during the last three years; and \n(c) the schemes to connect the villages in the border areas with \ntourism?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Tourism promotes tourism in the country, \nincluding in the border areas, through its various Schemes. It provides \nfinancial assistance for development of tourism infrast ructure in the \ncountry, including in the border States under its major Schemes of \nSwadesh Darshan and National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and \nSpiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD) to State Governments / \nUnion Territory Administrations/Central Agencies. The financial assistance \nis given on the basis of submission of Detailed Project Reports, availability \nof funds and adherence to Scheme Guidelines.  The Swadesh Darshan \nscheme has now been revamped as  Swadesh Darshan 2.0(SD2.0) for \ndevelopment of sustainable and responsible tourist destinations. The \ndetails of the projects sanctioned by Ministry of Tourism, under the above \nSchemes, in the border States, are given in the Annexure. \n \nFurther, Ministry also promotes India in a holistic manner through va rious \ninitiatives under its Schemes of “Domestic Promotion & Publicity including \nHospitality” (DPPH) and “Overseas Promotion & Publicity (OPP)”, including \nin the border States.  As part of its on-going activities; it regularly releases \nprint, electronic, o nline and outdoor media campaigns in the international \n\nmarkets, under the “Incredible India” brand -line, to promote various \ntourism destinations and products of India with the objective o f increasing \nforeign tourist arrivals to the Country.  The Ministry o f Tourism also \nregularly promotes various tourism destinations and products through its \nwebsite and social media promotions. \n \nSome of the major initiatives and efforts of Ministry of Tour ism for \npromotion of tourism in the border States are given below:-  \n \ni. An amount of Rs . 1312 lakh was sanctioned to Border Security \nForce (BSF) for the tourism related Infrastructural Development \nat Joint Check Post (JCP), Attari, Punjab in 2017-18. \n \nii.  Road connectivity and way side amenities: –  \n \nMinistry of Tourism  has taken up the matter with the Ministry of Road \nTransport & Highways for improving Road connectivity and wayside \namenities to the tourist destinations including in the border areas for  \npromotion of Tourism. For promotion of Border Tourism, Ministry of \nTourism has taken up the matter with the Border Road Organization (BRO) \nfor improving road connectivity and wayside amenities to the following \nthree (03) border areas: \n \ni. Pangong Lake, Ladakh \nii. Kargil, Ladakh \niii. Rishikesh, Uttarakhand \n \n \nFurther, Ministry of Home Aff airs, Government of India , has identified 17 \nvillages in 4 States, namely Arunachal Pradesh, Sikkim, Uttarakhand and \nHimachal Pradesh and Union Territory of Ladakh, on the northern border, \nto transform these villages as “Vibrant Villages”, wherein developm ent of \ntourism infrastruct ure and activities, have been identified as one of the \nkey interventions.  \n********* \n \n\nANNEXURE  \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART S (a) to (c)  OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 651 ANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 FOR PROMOTION OF BORDER \nTOURISM  \n \nDetails of the Projects sanctioned by the Ministry of Tourism in the Border \nStates under the Swadesh Darshan Scheme:           \n        (Rs. in crore) \nSl \nNo \nState/UT Circuit Sanction \nYear \nName of the Project Amount \nSanctio\nned \n1.  Arunachal \nPradesh \n \nNorth-East   \nCircuit \n2014-15 Development of Mega Circuit \nat Bhalukpong - Bomdila and \nTawang \n49.77 \n2.  Arunachal \nPradesh \n \nNorth East   \nCircuit \n2015-16 Development of Nafra- Seppa- \nPappu, Pasa, Pakke Valleys - \nSangdupota- New Sagalee - \nZiro- Yomcha \n96.72 \n3.  Assam \n \nWildlife \nCircuit \n2015-16 Development of Manas – \nProbitora– Nameri– \nKaziranga– Dibru– Saikhowa \n94.68 \n4.  Assam \n \nHeritage \nCircuit \n2016-17 Development of Tezpur – \nMajuli – Sibsagar \n90.98 \n5.  Bihar \n \nTirthankar \ncircuit \n \n2016-17 Development of Vaishali - \nArrah- Masad- Patna- Rajgir- \nPawapuri- Champapuri \n37.19 \n6.  Bihar \n \nSpiritual \ncircuit \n \n2016-17 Integrated Development of \nKanwaria Route: Sultanganj – \nDharmshala- Deoghar \n44.76 \n7.  Bihar \n \nBuddhist \nCircuit \n2016-17 Development of Buddhist \ncircuit- Construction of \nConvention Centre a t \nBodhgaya \n98.73 \n8.  Bihar \n \nRural \nCircuit \n2017-18 Development of Gandhi \nCircuit: Bhitiharwa - \nChandrahia- Turkaulia \n44.65 \n9.  Bihar \n \nSpiritual \nCircuit \n2017-18   Development "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |652", "t": "Guidelines to Skill Certificate", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 652 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGUIDELINES TO SKILL CERTIFICATE \n \n652. SHRI MOHAN MANDAVI: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP be  pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)       whether the Ministries or the concerned Departments/Undertakings have issued \nany orders or guidelines to include the relevant Skill certificate as an essential/desirable \nqualification along with the necessary Educational qualifications in the Recruitment rules \nmeant for the class III and IV employees in the Departments or Undertakings under the \nGovernment, and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND \nENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) to (b)  Directorate General of Training (DGT) under Ministry of Skill Development &  \nEntrepreneurship (MSDE) imparts vocational training through a network of Industrial \nTraining Institutes under Craftsmen Training Scheme (CTS) across the country and \nawards National Trade Certificate (NTC) on the successful completion of training.  DGT, \nMSDE has requested all Ministries of Government of India and their various \nDepartments and State Government & UT Administrations to recognise skill certificate \n(NTC) for the purpose of recruitment of subordinate posts and services. Further, in \nterms of guidelines of DoPT on framing/amendment/relaxation of Recruitment Rules, \nthe Administrative Ministries/ Departments are empowered to frame / amend \nRecruitment Rules in respect of Group 'C' posts keeping in view the guidelines issued \nby DoPT on various aspects. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |653", "t": "Bank Loans", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †653 \n To be answered on the February 6, 2023/Magha 17, 1944 (Saka)   \nBank Loans  \n†653. SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUDHARY:  \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the banks solicit certain formalities from institutions and general public at the time of \nsanctioning of various loans in the country, if so, the details thereof;   \n(b) whether it is true that while sa nctioning loans to the general public by some private banks, an arbitrary \namount i.e. 5 to 10 thousand rupees is being taken as file charge; \n(c) if so, whether the Government proposes to give relief to the common man by ensuring a fixed fees as \nfile charge keeping in view the nature of the loan, if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether sometimes even after giving photocopies and copies of necessary documents for taking loan by \nthe common people, private banks are still charging Rs. 1500 to Rs. 2000 as document charge, if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(e) whether while taking loan and getting back the deposit after repaying the gold loan, the debtor \nhimself/herself has to pay the fee for checking and testing of gold and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) whether it is fair for the banks to charge the said amount arbitrarily and if not, the corrective steps being \ntaken by the Government to stop this practice?   \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a): Banks take credit decisions based on their Board approved policies and extant regulatory guidelines of \nReserve Bank of India (RBI). For sanctioning of loans, banks ask for submission of documents  from borrowers \nand guarantors  based on loan product, activity of borrower and purpose of loan including, inter alia, KYC \ndocuments, asset and liability statement, proof of income like IT returns etc, financial statements, documents \nrelated to securities offered like sale deed, encumbrance certificates etc, project report, techno economic viability \nreport, quotations and estimates of supply, external rating, details of borrowings and statutory approvals. \n  \n(b) to (f): As per RBI master circular on customer service in banks, the decision to prescribe service charges rests \nwith banks as per their Board approved policy and banks should ensure that the charges are reasonable and are \nnot out of line with the average cost of providing these services. Also, RBI vide its guidelines on fair practice \ncode for lenders, advised banks to inform ‘all in cost’ to the customer to enable him to compare the rates charged \nwith other sources of finance. Further, with a view to bringing in fairness and transparency, banks are required \nto transparently disclose to the borrower all in formation about fees / charges including, inter alia,  file charge,  \ndocument charge,  fee for checking and testing of gold  payable for processing the loan application, the amount \nof fees refundable if loan amount is not sanctioned/ disbursed, pre -payment options and charges, penalty for \ndelayed repayments, conversion charges for switching loan from fixed to floating rates or vice versa, existence \nof any interest reset clause and any other matter which affects the interest of the borrower. Such information \nshould also be displayed and updated on the website of the banks for all categories of loan products and it should \nbe ensured that such charges /fees are non-discriminatory.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |654", "t": "Global Initiative for Academic Network", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 654 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nGlobal Initiative for Academic Network \n \n654.   SHRI ANUMULA REVANTH REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has invited foreign faculties under Global Initiative for Academic \nNetwork (GIAN) programme, if so, details thereof along with the number of faculties that \narrived under the programme; \n(b) if not, the time by which the Government will invite the foreign faculties in India to encourage \ntheir engagement with the institutes of Higher Education in India; \n(c) whether the GIAN has improved the quality of education in the country, if so, the details thereof; \nand \n(d) the steps taken/bei ng taken by the Government to adopt the new educational technologies \navailable around the world? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes  Sir. Under Global Initiative for Academic Network  (GIAN) Internationally reputed \nacademicians from all over the world are invited to teach in the Higher Educational Institutes and to \nshare their experiences and expertise on wide spectrum of subjects. Till date, 1685 foreign experts \nhave visited India to teach 1725 courses in 204 Higher Education Institutes. \n \n(c) 'National Institute of Educational Planning and Administration ’ (NIEPA) has evaluated the GIAN \nscheme in 2019. The Evaluation Report observed that GIAN is an important initiative to promote the \nquality of teaching, research and further the adva ncement of knowledge in I ndian Higher Education \nInstitutions. GIAN has opened the gates for international collabo ration and global exposure for I ndian \nstudents and faculty. There has been much exposure to the lates t advancements in science and \ntechnology. \n \n\n \n-2- \n \nGoals achieved in earlier phases of GIAN are: \n \n• Knowledge creation and Capacity building by bringing foreign faculty into lndian universities \nthereby facilitating Indian faculty to have an insight into the modern pedagogy and topics. \n  \n(d)  PM e-VIDYA - NDEAR (National Digital Education Architecture) is a key enabler of N ational \nEducation Policy (N EP) 2020 and act as a connector to cross leverage ecosystem capabilities and \ncatalyse innovation in education. P M e-VIDYA - NDEAR Ecosystem Policy and Open Standards & \nSpecifications was released on 11 th November 2022 and the same shall be accessed at \nhttps://www.ndear.gov.in/    \nDepartment of Higher Education, Ministry of Education has also extended the flagship scheme of \n‘National Mission on Education through Information and Communication Technology’ (NMEICT)  \nfrom 2021-2022 to 2025 -2026 for adoption of education technologies from  across the world in cross -\ncutting areas. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |655", "t": "Revival of Tourism Sector", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†655 \nANSWERED ON 06.02.2023 \n \nREVIVAL OF TOURISM SECTOR \n \n†655. SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:  \n \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking steps to review the tourism \nsector in the country;  \n(b) if so, the State-wise details thereof; and  \n(c) the number of tourist spots identified for integrated \ndevelopment of tourism and the details thereof, State -wise \nincluding Maharashtra? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM           (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Tourism organizes National & Regional State \nTourism Ministers Conferences from time to time to review the \ntourism sector and  discuss with the States/UTs and stakeholders \nabout schemes, pol icies and steps being taken for overall tourism \ndevelopment and growth in India. Details of the meetings which \nhave been held recently are: \n \na. National Conference of State Tourism Ministers held from \n18th to 20th September 2022 in Dharamshala, Himachal \nPradesh. \nb. Tourism & Culture Ministers Conference of the North \nEastern States held on 13 th & 14 th September 2021 at \nGuwahati, Assam.  \nc. Tourism & Culture Ministers Conference of the South ern \nRegion held on 28 th & 29 th October 2021 in Bengaluru, \nKarnataka. \n \n\n \n \n(c): Identification and development of tourist spots is primarily the \nresponsibility of the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations. However, t he Ministry of Tourism has prov ided \nfinancial assistance under the ‘Swadesh Darshan’ and ‘National \nMission on  Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage \nAugmentation Drive (PRASHAD)’ Schemes for the development of \ntourism related infrastructure in different States/UTs including \nMaharashtra. \n \n The details of projects sanctioned in the States/UTs including \nMaharashtra under the above mentioned schemes is given at \nAnnexure. \n******\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (c ) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.†655 ANSWERED ON 0 6.02.2023 REGARDING REVIVAL OF \nTOURISM SECTOR.  \n \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme                (Rs. in Crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT Name of \nthe Circuit \nName of the Project Amt. \nSanctioned \n* \nAmount \nReleased \nYear 2014-15 \n 1 Arunachal \nPradesh \nNorth-East \nCircuit \nDevelopment of \nBhalukpong- \nBomdila and \nTawang \n49.77 47.28 \n 2 Andhra \nPradesh \nCoastal \nCircuit \nDevelopment of \nKakinada - Hope \nIsland - Coringa \nWildlife Sanctuary - \nPassarlapudi - \nAduru - Syanam – \nKotipally \n67.84 67.84 \nTotal of 2014-15 117.61 115.12 \nYear 2015-16 \n 3 Manipur North-East \nCircuit \nDevelopment of \nTourist Circui t in \nManipur: Imphal - \nKhongjom \n72.23 61.32 \n 4 Sikkim North-East \nCircuit \nDevelopment of \nTourist Circuit \nlinking Rangpo \n(entry) – Rorathang- \nAritar- \nPhadamchen- \nNathang-\nSherathang- \nTsongmo- Gangtok-\nPhodong- Mangan- \nLachung-Yumthang- \nLachen- Thangu-\n98.05 97.41 \n\nGurudongmer- \nMangan- Gangtok-\nTuminLingee- \nSingtam (exit) \n 5 Uttarakhand Eco \nCircuit \nDevelopm