 ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING, मत्स्यपालन \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) Whether the Government has received any proposal from the State of Maharashtra regarding \nsetting up of fisheries, animal husbandry and dairy projects under the centrally sponsored schemes \nand if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government thereto;  \n(b) the details of various schemes being implemented by the Government for the development of \nFisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairy sector in the State of Maharashtra during the last three \nyears;  \n(c) the total number of farmers who have been benefited by these schemes so far;  \n(d) whether the Government has failed to achieve the growth rate targets in the said sector and if \nso, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n(e) the details of corrective steps taken by the Government to promote the ani mal husbandry and \nfisheries sectors in the country? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairyi ng, Government of India is  \nimplementing the following schemes and programme in the country including the State of \nMaharashtra for all round development of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairy Sectors:  \n1. Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY),  \n2. Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund (FIDF),  \n3. Rashtriya Gokul Mission (RGM),  \n4. National Livestock Mission (NLM),  \n5. National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD),  \n6. Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund (DIDF),  \n7. Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organizations (SDCFPO)  \n8. Entrepreneurship Development and Employment Generation (EDEG), and  \n9. Livestock Health and Disease Control Programme (LHDCP). \n  \n\nIn respect of Fisheries Sector, under the Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana, the Department \nof Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying during the financial years, \n2020-21, 2021-22 and 2022-23 has approved the proposals of Government of Maharashtra to the \ntune of Rs. 792.32 crore with total central share of Rs. 355.32 crore for development of fisheries \nand aquaculture sector in the state  benefitting an estimated 13603 beneficiaries including fish \nfarmers. Further, under Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund, t he \nDepartment of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries , Animal Husbandry and Dairying  so far has \naccorded approval to the project proposals with a total outlay of Rs. 943.69 crore to  the State of \nMaharashtra including the proposals of private beneficiaries for providing concessional finance in \nthe form of interest subvention of up to 3%.  \nIn respect of Dairy Sector, u nder the National Programme for Dairy Development ( NPDD) \nscheme, three projects have been approved for Maharashtra with a total outlay of Rs. 49 .47 crore \nand central share being Rs. 45.07 crore. An amount of Rs. 31.92 crore has been released till date . \nUnder Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund  (DIDF), 2 projects have been \nsanctioned for the state of Maharashtra  at a total cost of Rs. 488.33 crore and loan amount of Rs \n290.66 crore for providing interest subvention. Under DIDF, 1.9 lakh farmers have been indirectly \nbenefitted in Maharashtra as on 31-12-2022. Further, under the central sector scheme Supporting \nDairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Orga nizations engaged in dairy activities  (SDCFPO), \nthe National Dairy Development Board  (NDDB) has released an interest subvention of Rs . 4.40 \ncrore for the year 2020 -21 and Rs . 4.71 crore for the year 2021 -22 for two milk unions  in \nMaharashtra State.   \nIn respect of Animal Husbandry Sector, under Animal Husbandry Infrastructure Development \nFund (AHIDF), 36 projects have been sanctioned for the state of Maharashtra at a total cost of Rs \n1325.61 crore and loan amount of Rs. 947.72 crore for providing interest subvention. Further, the \nState Government has also submitted a proposal for Livestock Insurance and the same has been \napproved. Under Rashtriya Gokul Mission (RGM), an amount of Rs 68.96 crore has been released \nto Maharashtra.  Under Livestock Health and D isease Control Programme (LHDCP) , i n \nMaharashtra 1,47,11,259 animals have been vaccinated for Foot and Mouth Disease (FMD) during \nRound-I, 1,68,78,945 animals have been vaccinated for FMD during Round-II, 3,74,523 animals \nhave been vaccinated for Brucella and 43,263 animals have been vaccinated for Peste des Petits \nRuminants (PPR). Support has been extended to Maharashtra state for establishing and \nstrengthening mobile veterinary units (MVUs) and 80 such "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |819", "t": "National Policy on Cooperation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 819 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07/02/2023 \n \nNational Policy on Cooperation \n \n819.    SHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA: \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (सहकाįरता मंũी) be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has proposed to introduce National Policy on Cooperation and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government for strengthening India’s cooperative architecture syncing it \nwith country’s economic and social needs? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकाįरता मंũी (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n \n(a): Yes, Sir. A National level committee has been constituted on 2nd September 2022 under the \nchairmanship of Shri Suresh Prabhakar Prabhu, consisting of experts of the cooperative sector, \nrepresentatives from National/ State/ District/ Primary level cooperative societies, Secretaries \n(Cooperation) and RCSs from States/UTs, officers from Central Ministries/ Departments to formulate \nthe New National Cooperation Policy. The formulation of New National Cooperation Policy will help \nin realising the vision of 'Sahakar se Samriddhi', promoting the cooperative based economic \ndevelopment model, strengthening the cooperative movement in the country and deepening its reach \nup to the grassroots. In this regard, consultations were earlier held with stakeholders and suggestions \nfor formulating the new Policy were invited from the Central Ministries/ Departments, States/UTs, \nNational Cooperative Federations, Institutions and also from the general public. The National level \ncommittee will analyse the collated feedback, policy suggestions & recommendations to formulate the \ndraft of the new Policy. \n \n(b): Number of steps have been taken by the Government, after the formation of the Ministry of \nCooperation, to strengthen India’s cooperative architecture syncing it with country’s economic and \nsocial needs, inter-alia, including the following:  \n1. Computerization of PACS: Process to onboard 63,000 functional PACS on an ERP based common \nnational software with an outlay of ₹2,516 Crore started.  \n \n\n2. Model byelaws for PACS: Model byelaws prepared and circulated for their adoption as per the \nrespective State Cooperatives Act to enable PACS to undertake more than 25 business activities \nlike dairy, fishery, setting up of godowns, LPG/ Petrol/ Green energy distribution agency, banking \ncorrespondents, CSC, etc. \n \n3. PACS as Common Service Centres (CSC): MoU signed between Ministry of Cooperation, Ministry \nof Electronics and Information Technology, NABARD and CSC -SPV to facilitate functioning of \nPACS as CSCs to improve their viability, provide e-services at village level and generate \nemployment. \n \n4. National Cooperative Database: Preparation of an authentic and updated data repository of \ncooperatives in the country started to facilitate stakeholders in policy making and implementation.  \n \n5. National Cooperative Policy: A National level committee comprising of experts and stakeholders \ndrawn from all over the Country constituted to formulate the New Cooperation Policy to create an \nenabling ecosystem to realize the vision of ‘Sahakar-se-Samriddhi’. \n \n6. Amendment of MSCS Act, 2002: Bill introduced in the Parliament to amend the centrally \nadministered MSCS Act, 2002 to incorporate provisions of 97th Constitutional Amendment, \nstrengthen governance, enhance transparency, increase accountability and reform electoral process \nin the Multi State Cooperative Societies. \n \n7. National Cooperative Development Corporation: New schemes for cooperatives launched by \nNCDC in various sectors such as ‘Swayamshakti Sahkar’ for SHG; ‘Deerghavadhi Krishak Sahkar’ \nfor long term agricultural credit; ‘Dairy Sahkar’ for dairy and ‘Neel Sahkar’ for fisheries. Total \nfinancial assistance of Rs. 34,221 Crores disbursed in FY 2021-22. \n \n8. Member Lending Institutions in Credit Guarantee Fund Trust: Non-scheduled UCBs, StCBs and \nDCCBs notified as MLIs in CGTMSE Scheme to increase share of cooperatives in lending. \n \n9. Cooperatives as ‘buyers’ on GeM portal:  Cooperatives permitted to register as ‘buyer’ on GeM, \nenabling them to procure goods and services from nearly 40 lakh vendors  to facilitate economical \npurchases and greater transparency. \n \n10. Reduction in surcharge on cooperative societies: Surcharge reduced from 12 % to 7% for co-\noperative societies having income between Rs. 1 to 10 Cr.  \n \n11. Reduction in Minimum Alternate Tax: MAT reduced for Co-operatives from 18.5% to 15%. \n \n12. Relief under Section 269ST of IT Act: A clarification has been issued to remove difficulties in each \ntransaction by cooperatives under Section 269ST of IT Act. \n \n\n13. Lowering tax rate for new cooperatives: Announcement made in the Union Budget 2023-24 to \ncharge flat lower tax rate of 15%, compared with current rate of upto 30% plus surcharge, for new \ncooperatives commencing manufacturing activities till March 31, 2024.  \n \n14. Increase"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |82", "t": "Elevated Roads on NH-48", "head": "�\n������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��\n��������� �\n����������������������� �� �\n������������ � �� ��������� ����� � �\n��\n�������������������� ��� �\n��\n�� �� �������������������� ��\n��\n������������������������������������������������ �\n�������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� �\n���� ������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ����������������� ����� ��� ��������� ������ ��� ���������\n������������������������ ���������������������������� �\n���� ������������������������������������������������������������ �\n�\n������ �\n������������������������������������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n��\n������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������� �\n�\n����� ��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |820", "t": "Group Accident Insurance for Fishermen", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.820 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \nGroup Accident Insurance for Fishermen \n820. SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether there is any insurance coverage scheme for persons involved in fishing and fisheries \nrelated activities and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the number of families who have been assisted and the number of claims settled under the \nscheme during the last three years, State/UT-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to expand the group accident insurance for fishermen; \nand \n(d)  the details of the steps taken by the Government to ensure the safety and security of fishermen? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (c):  The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying, \nGovernment of India under the Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) being \nimplemented from 2020 -21 provides insurance coverage to fishers across the Country.  The \ninsurance component implemented under PMMSY comprehensively catering to the needs of the \ninsurance of fishers, fish workers, fish farmers and any other categories of persons directly involved \nin fishing and fisheries related allied activities. The insurance coverage provided under the PMMSY \nincludes (i) Rs.5,00,000/- against accidental death or permanent total disability, (ii) Rs.2,50,000/ - \nfor permanent partial disability and (iii) hospitalization expenses in the event of accident for a sum \nof Rs. 25,000 .  The State/UT-wise details of number of fishers’ families assisted and number of \nclaims settled during last three years 2019-20 to 2021-22 (i.e. 2019-20 under CSS-Blue Revolution: \nIntegrated Development and Management of Fisheries and 2020-21 to 2021-22 under PMMSY) is \nat Annexure. \n (d): Further, the Department has taken several steps to ensure the safety and security of \nfishermen which inter -alia includes support for providing safety kits for fishermen of traditional \nand motorized fishing vessels, providing communication and/or tracking \ndevices(VHF/DAT/NAVIC/Transponders)  for traditional and motorized vessels. \n  \n\nAnnexure \nStatement referred to in reply of parts (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha unstarred Question No \n820 put in by Smt Poonamben Maadam , Honourable Member of Parliament for \nanswer on 7th February, 2023 regarding ‘Group Accident Insurance for Fishermen’: \nState-wise details of Insurance settled during last 3 years (i.e. 2019-20 under CSS-Blue \nRevolution: Integrated Development and Management of Fisheries and 2020 -21 to 2021-\n22 under the PMMSY)  \nSl. No Name of the State/UTs \nNumber of \nFishers’s families \nassisted  \nNumber of \nclaims settled \ni ii (iii) (iv) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 1 1 \n2 Assam 2 2 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 \n4 Bihar 8 8 \n5 Chhattisgarh 8 8 \n6 Daman & Diu and DNH 0 0 \n7 NCT of Delhi 0 0 \n8 Gujarat 1 1 \n9 Haryana 0 0 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 7 7 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 1 1 \n12 Jharkhand 3 3 \n13 Karnataka 8 8 \n14 Ladakh 0 0 \n15 Lakshadweep 0 0 \n16 Manipur 0 0 \n17 Maharashtra 5 5 \n18 Odisha 24 24 \n19 Puducherry 5 5 \n20 Punjab 0 0 \n21 Rajasthan 0 0 \n22 Tamil Nadu 420 420 \n23 Telangana 187 187 \n24 Sikkim 0 0 \n25 Tripura 0 0 \n26 Uttar Pradesh  2 2 \n27 Uttarkhand 0 0 \n Total 682 682 \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |821", "t": "Natural Farming in Rainfed Areas", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 821 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7Th FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nNATURAL FARMING IN RAINFED AREAS \n \n \n821. SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is focusing on the promotion of natural farming in rainfed \nareas beyond Ganga as well as Yamuna river and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Union Government has any p roposal to bring any new plans/economic \nplans and development projects in coordination with the State Government in those districts \nof Haryana (Karnal, Panipat, Kurukshetra etc.) which produce for agriculture market and \nexport at international level; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ षि एवं ककसान  कल्याण मंत्री     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a): Government is promoting natural farming through a sub scheme namely Bhartiya \nPrakritik Krishi Padhati (BPKP) under Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY) since \n2020-21. So far, 4.09 lakh ha area has been brought under BPKP in 8 States including rain-\nfed areas. \n \n(b) & (c): Under PKVY, assistance is provided @ Rs. 15.0 lakh / 1000 ha cluster for 3 \nyears to facilitate farmers for direct marketing of their organic products to the buyers  in the \ncountry including all districts of Haryana. In addition, assistance is provided @ Rs. 53.0 lakh \n/1000 ha clusters for 3 years for branding, publicity, exhibition, trade fairs and other \nmarketing initiatives to support direct buying of organic products from farmers.  \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |822", "t": "Coverage of Crops under MSP", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  822 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nCOVERAGE OF CROPS UNDER MSP \n \n  822. SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received any complaints/suggestion on the coverage of \ncrops under Minimum Support Price (MSP) scheme and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the percentage of MSP coverage for all crops during the last five years;  \n(c) whether it is true that there are crops covered under MSP but not procured from farmers \nat that price and if so, the reasons therefor, crop-wise;  \n(d) whether the Government is taking steps to increase the coverage  of MSP on all crops \nand if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री      (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (e): Government fixes Minimum Support Prices (MSP) for 22 mandated agricultural \ncrops and Fair & Remunerative Price (FRP) for sugarcane on the basis of the \nrecommendations of the Commiss ion for Agricultural Costs & Prices (CACP), after \nconsidering the views of State Governments and Central Ministries/Departments concerned \nand other relevant factors.CACP also holdsconsultations withall the stakeholders and seeks \nsuggestions for both price and non -price recommendations. Suggestions/Representations \n\n \n \n \nfrom State Governments and various farmer organizations are also received from time to \ntime for the coverage of crops under MSP . The inclusion of crops under MSP framework is  \nhowever, dependent on several factors which include essential for food security , relatively \nlarge shelf life, widely grown, item of mass consumption, among others.  \nTo realize the objective MSP Policy, Government extends price support for paddy and \nwheat through the Food Corpor ation of India (FCI) and State Agencies. Under this policy, \nwhatever food grains are offered by farmers within the stipulated period & conforming to the \nspecifications prescribed by Government are purchased at MSP by the State Government \nagencies including  FCI for Central Pool. Additionally, Oilseeds, pulses and copra of Fair \nAverage Quality (FAQ) are procured from registered farmers under Price Support Scheme \nunder Umbrella Scheme of PM-AASHA, as per its guidelines at MSP in consultation with the \nconcerned State Government as and when market price of these produce fall below the \nMSP. Details of crop-wise procurement at MSP for last five years is given at annexure. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (e)  of Lok Sabha unstarred Question No. \n822 due for reply on 07.02.2023 \n \nCrops procurement at Minimum Support Price (MSP) \n \n \n                                                                                                                                                   (Figures in LMT) \n \nSl. No. Commodity 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n \n 1 PADDY 660.11 770.93 895.66 857.30 651.70  \n2 WHEAT 357.95 341.33 389.93 433.44 187.92  \n3 OIL SEEDS  18.29 15.43 2.95 1.52 0.48  \n4 PULSES 19.45 28.54 8.17 30.31 1.2 \n  \n5 \nSHREE \nANNA*and \nMAIZE \n2.18 4.32 12.08 6.30 1.28  \n6 COTTON# 10.78 124.61 99.33 -  -  \n7 COPRA  - 0.0031 0.0509 0.0003 0.4084  \n8 JUTE  0.1308 0.1481 0.0072 0.0025  0.2654   \n                   *Shree Anna include Jowar, bajra, ragi \n# Procurementof cotton is in Lakh Bales  \nDuring 2021 -22& 2022 -23, FAQ grade seed cotton prices were ruling much above MSP level and \nfarmers were getting higher prices above MSP. Hence procurement under MSP was NIL. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |823", "t": "Rashtriya Dairy Vikas Yojana", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.823 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07TH FEBRUARY 2023 \nRASHTRIYA DAIRY VIKAS YOJANA \n823. SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA:  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालनऔरडेयरीमंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the measures being taken by the Government to upgrade Swachh Dugdh Utpadan Karyakram \nYojana and Rashtriya Dairy Vikas Yojana;  \n(b) the reasons for giving clearance to the said schemes under National Livestock Mission, Sub-\nMission on Feed and Fodder Development;  \n(c) the facilities provided by the Government to farmers to promote dairy project; and \n(d) the details of work done so far for welfare and upliftment of farmers and residents of tribal areas \nunder Rashtriya Dairy VikasYojana and NABARD in Udaipur Lok Sabha Constituency? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a)& (b) National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD) was launched in February 2014 by \nmerging three existing schemes i.e. Intensive Dairy Development Programme, Strengthening \nInfrastructure for Quality & Clean Milk Production  and Assistance to Cooperatives. In July 2021, \nNPDD has been restructured with the  aim of enhancing  quality of mil k and milk products and \nincreasing the  share of organised procurement, processing, value addition and marketing ; for \nimplementation from 2021 -22 to 2025 -26. National Livestock Mission, Sub-Mission on Feed and \nFodder Development is a separate scheme aiming to increase availability of fodder and feed. \n(c) Supplementing the efforts of the State Government, Department of Animal Husbandry and \nDairying has been implementing the following dairy development schemes:- \ni.  National Programme for Dairy Development \nii.  Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund \niii. Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organisations engaged in dairy \nactivities \nThe above schemes benefit farmer -members, including financially weaker farmers in the dairy \nsector. \n\n(d) Under NPDD scheme, 2 projects has been approved in Udaipur (Udaipur Dudh  Utpadak \nSahakari Sangh Limited) with the total project cost of Rs. 1278.67 lakh with a central sha re of \nRs.957.32 lakh and out of this an amount of Rs. 957.32 lakh has been released . The project has \nassisted in creation of additional 50 thousand litres milk processing capacity, installation of 115 \nAutomatic Milk Collection Units and 114 Electronic Milk Adulteration Testing Machines. \n \nBesides the above, subsidy was channelized through NABARD under Dairy Entrepreneurship \nDevelopment Scheme (DEDS)  and National Livestock Mission (NLM) schemes. The details of \nsubsidy released by NABARD under these schemes in Udaipur district are as under:- \nName of the scheme No. of beneficiaries \nbenefitted \nAmount of subsidy released \n(₹ lakh) \nDairy Entrepreneurship \nDevelopment Scheme (DEDS) 202 96.80* \nNational Livestock Mission \n(NLM) 3 22.89# \n  *Post September 2017; #Post December 2018 \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |824", "t": "Small Food Processing Cold Chain", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 824 \nANSWERED ON 07TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nSMALL FOOD PROCESSING COLD CHAIN  \n  \n824. SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \n  \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  the number of small food processing cold chain set up/functioning in the country; \n(b)  whether the Government has any proposal to provide grants for establishing small food \nprocessing cold chain units in near future; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof, especially in Andaman & Nicobar Islands and if not, the reasons \ntherefor; and \n(d)  whether there is any other scheme to benefit small and medium farmers through low investment \nprocessing units and if so, the details thereof?  \n \n  \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a) to (c):    The Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) is implementing the scheme of \n\"Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure” which is a demand driven scheme. Project \nproposals are invited from interested individuals/ entities through Expression of Interest (EoI) floated \nfrom time to time and projects are sanctioned based on procedure enumerated in the scheme guidelines. \nA total of 376 cold chain projects have been accorded approval under the said scheme till date, of which \n271 projects are completed.  Two cold chain projects have been approved in Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands, out of which one is completed.  \n(d):  Interested individuals can avai l benefits available under Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro \nFood Processing Enterprises (PMFME) scheme being implemented by MoFPI to support low \ninvestment processing facilities.   \n \n**** \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |825", "t": "National Fellowship for PwD", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF EMPOWERMENT OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 825 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2023 \n  \nNATIONAL FELLOWSHIP FOR PwD \n  \n825.   SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI \n  \n            Will the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government maintains a record of the number of beneficiary students selected \nfor ‘National Fellowship for Persons with Disabilities (PwD)’; \n  \n(b) if so, the details for the State of Uttar Pradesh in this regard and if not, the reasons therefor; \n  \n(c) whether the budget allocated for the benefit of persons with disabilities has been \nunderutilized; and \n  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE & EMPOWERMENT \n(Shri A.Narayanaswamy) \n  \n(a) to (d) “National Fellowship for Persons with Disabilities (NFPwD)” is one of six components \nof “Umbrella Scheme of Scholarships for Students with Disabilities” being implemented by \nDepartment of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities (Divyangjan).  \n  \nThe Department maintains a record of the number of selected students under “National \nFellowship for Persons with Disabilities (NFPwD)”. Since 2014-15, a total 1865 students are \nselected under NFPwD out of which 376 students belong to Uttar Pradesh.  \n  \nThe details of Budget Allocation (BE), Revised Allocation (RE) and Actual Expenditure (AE) \nunder Umbrella Scheme titled ‘Scholarships for Students with Disabilities (Divyangjan)’  during \nlast three year is as under:- \n(₹ in Crore) \nYear BE RE AE \n2019-20 125.00 108.67 95.75 \n2020-21 125.00 100.00 97.37 \n2021-22 125.00 124.88 120.25 \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |826", "t": "Domino effect in MGNREGS", "head": "   \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 826 \n  ANSWERED ON 07/02/2023 \n \nDOMINO EFFECT IN MGNREGS \n \n826.  SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the Government owes as on December 14, 2022, Rs. 4,700 \ncrore in wages to 18 States and Rs. 5,450 crore in material cost to 19 States and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(b)  whether it is also a fact that the delay in material costs has a domino effect on work \nsince a delay in the payment breaks the supply chain and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether it is also true that the vendors are reluctant to supply new material because of \nprolonged delays in payments and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  whether it is also true that the Government is considering meeting the shortfall and if \nso, the details and the manner thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a),(b)&(d): Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS) is a demand driven wage employment Scheme. Funds are released to the \nStates/UTs on the basis of \"agreed to\" Labour Budget (LB) and performance of the \nStates/UTs during the financial year. Fund release to States/UTs is a continuous process and \nCentral Government is committed to making funds available keeping in view the demand for \nwork on the ground. No State/UT -wise financial allocation is made under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS.  \n  \nDuring the current financial year 2022 -23 (as on 03.02.2023) an amount of Rs. 72813.73 \ncrore was released for wage, material & administrative components under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS. \n  \n(c):     Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, no fund is directly released to the vendors. \n \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |827", "t": "Obscene Advertisements", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.827                              \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2023) \n \nOBSCENE ADVERTISEMENTS \n \n827. SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \n  \n        Will the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether some advertisements of cigarettes, tobacco, liquor \nhave been/are being broadcast for the last three years and \nduring the current year by doordarshan kendras/centres; \n \n(b) whether obscene pictures are also being broadcasted on a \nlarge scale by doordarshan kendras;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) whether the Government has received any suggestions or \nmemorandum during the last three years and as on date to ban \nor impose legal  restrictions on the broadcast of these \nadvertisements/obscene pictures; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(f) the steps taken by the Government in this regard?   \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING ; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS (SHRI ANURAG SINGH \nTHAKUR) \n \n(a) to (c): No, Sir.  The p rogramme and advertisement on \nDoordarshan are telecast in accordance with the Programme and \nAdvertising Codes issued from time to time.  \n(d) to (f): Does not arise in view of the reply to parts (a) to (c) above.  \nHowever, in case of Private Satellite TV Channels, all \nadvertisements are regulated in terms of Cable Television \nNetworks (Regulations) Act, 1995 and the rules framed \nthereunder.  Appropriate action is taken if any violation of the \nProgramme and Advertisement Code is established.  The Ministry \nalso issues advisories from time to time to private satellite TV \nchannels for ensuring compliance to the Programme and \nAdvertising Codes. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |828", "t": "Smart Farming Methods", "head": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n828\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \nTHE 7\nTH \nFEBRUARY 2023\n \n \nSMART FARMING METHODS\n \n \n828\n.\n \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government is promoting smartfarming methods through the use of \ntechnology andinnovation in the agriculture sector of the countryand if \nso, the details \nthereof;\n \n(b) the details of funds sanctioned, allocated andutilized so far across the country \nincluding Rajasthan;\n \n(c) the details of the achievements made so faracross the country as a result thereof;\n \n(d) whether the Government has set \nany targetin this regard in the country; and\n \n(e) if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (\ne\n): \n \nThe adoption of modern \nand smart farming technologies \nby the farmers \ndepends on various factors such as socioeconomic conditions, geographical conditions, \ncrop grown, irrigation facilities etc. However, the Government of India supports and \nfacilitates the State Governments to promote agriculture throughout th\ne country and \ninfusing modern \nand smart farming \ntechnologies into the agriculture sector. \nThe use of \nmodern machines including kisan drones is promoted under \nSub\n-\nMission on Agricultural \nMechanization\n. Under NeGPA program, funding is given to State Governme\nnts for Digital \nAgriculture Projects using emerging technologies like Artificial Intelligence, and Machine \nLearning (AI/ML), Internet of Things (IOT)\n, \nBlock chain\n \netc. \nA component called \n“Innovation and Agri\n-\nEntrepreneurship Development” has been \nlaunched under Rashtriya\n \nKrishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY\n-\nRAFTAAR) in 2018\n-\n19 with the objective of promoting \ninnovation and agri\n-\nentrepreneurship by providing financial support and nurturing the \nincubation ecosystem. Under this programme, start\n-\nups are encourage\nd to use innovative \ntechnologies to resolve challenges faced in agriculture and allied sectors.\nThe \nfunds are \nreleased to the States based on their proposals and \nno specific targets have been\n \nfixed \ntowards\n \nsmart farming methods.  \nThe details of \nState\n-\nwise \nf\nunds \nreleased\n \nunder SMAM \n\nand achievements so far are given in Annexure I and \ndetails of projects \nsanctioned under \nNeGP\nA during 2020\n-\n21 and 2021\n-\n22 are given in Annexure\n-\nI\nI.\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nState\n-\nwise funds released to the States and achievements since 2014\n-\n15 \nunder \nSub\n-\nMission on Agricultural Mechanization \n \n \nState\n \nFunds \nReleased \n(Rs. in \nCrores)\n \nNumber of \nAgricultural \nmachinery \ndistributed on \nsubsidy\n \nTotal Number of \nCustom Hiring \nCentres/Hi\n-\nTech \nHubs and Farm \nMachinery Banks \nestablished \n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n808.72\n \n251514\n \n10598\n \nArunachal \n \n46.54\n \n35984\n \n13\n \nAssam\n \n53.33\n \n733\n \n561\n \nBihar\n \n94.93\n \n28559\n \n1035\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n198.94\n \n94485\n \n2219\n \nGujarat\n \n66.17\n \n27240\n \n62\n \nHaryana\n \n258.14\n \n24870\n \n2313\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n152.53\n \n43290\n \n53\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n51.64\n \n14719\n \n333\n \nJharkhand\n \n24.69\n \n0\n \n527\n \nKarnataka\n \n722.95\n \n175827\n \n786\n \nKerala\n \n208.06\n \n61879\n \n1030\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n336.12\n \n199528\n \n1046\n \nMaharashtra\n \n473.51\n \n85292\n \n1364\n \nManipur\n \n104.71\n \n18445\n \n738\n \nMeghalaya\n \n8.84\n \n2457\n \n8\n \nMizoram\n \n32.21\n \n3947\n \n255\n \nNagaland\n \n152.94\n \n16162\n \n400\n \nOrissa\n \n289.24\n \n49397\n \n1738\n \nPunjab\n \n102.68\n \n12666\n \n1256\n \nRajasthan\n \n96.2\n \n23501\n \n1243\n \nSikkim\n \n29.18\n \n5454\n \n52\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n468.89\n \n37182\n \n3178\n \nTelangana \n \n40.67\n \n28954\n \n195\n \nTripura \n \n154.90\n \n41177\n \n562\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n403.37\n \n139937\n \n7078\n \nUttarakhand\n \n260.60\n \n30617\n \n1814\n \nWest Bengal\n \n59.71\n \n6497\n \n445\n \nD&D Nagar\n \n1.10\n \n89\n \n0\n \nPuducherry\n \n7.51\n \n489\n \n13\n \nLadakh\n \n1.03\n \n1314\n \n0\n \nTOTAL\n \n5710.05\n \n1462205\n \n40915\n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nII\n \n \nP\nilot projects sanctioned in differ\nent States/UTs under NeGPA for Financial Year \n2020\n-\n2021 and 2021\n-\n22\n \n \n \nS. No.\n \nState\n \nTechnology\n \nProject Cost\n \n(Rs in Lakhs) \n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n1\n \nArunachal \nPradesh\n \nResilient Supply Chain for identified \nAgricultural \nCrops Leveraging Digitization\n \n81\n \n2\n \nBihar\n \nDigital Package of Practices for Major Crops and  \nAutomation Technique in Irrigation in State Seed \nMultiplication Farms\n \n354\n \n3\n \nMeghalaya\n \nDevelopment of web and mobile based \"Crop Pest              \nSurveillance and Advisory Project\n \n63\n \n4\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n(i) Unmanned Aerial Vehicle for Agriculture \n \nApplications, (ii) Hand Held Tool based Soil Health            \nMonitoring Integrated system and (iii) Automated \nFire Alert System for Crop Residue Burning in U.P. \n \n121\n \n5\n \nTelangana\n \n(i\n) Crop Monitoring (ii) Automated irrigation \nSystems \n \n(iii) Fertilizer Calculation \n \n(iv) Automated Fa"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |829", "t": "Promoting innovation in Seed Research", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n829\n \nTO BE\n \nANSWER\nED\n \nON THE\n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 202\n3\n \n \nPROMOTING INNOVATION IN SEED RESEARCH\n \n \n829\n. \n \nSHRI JUAL ORAM:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n      \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) the details of roadmap proposed by the Government to bring revolution in agriculture \nand enhance food systems from the source;\n \n \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to promote innovations, research and development \nin the agriculture sector; and \n \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plans to focus on promoting innovations at the source \nlevel in seed research to tackle the impact of \nclimate change on agriculture and food \nsecurity and if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) to (c\n)\n:\n \n \nGovernment of India is \ncontinuously engaged in innovation and research to \ntake care\n \nof the adverse impacts of climate change, environmental pollution, soil \ndegradation, groundwater depletion and loss of biodiversity for increasing yield. The \nobjective is \nalso \nto produce more cro\np from less water, less land, less pesticide, less \nfertilizer. \nIn this regard, \nIndian Council of Agriculture Research (\nICAR\n)\nhas one deemed to be \nuniversity, 21 National Institutes, 3 Bureaus; 2 Project Directorates, one National Research \nCenter, 32 All\n-\nInd\nia Coordinated Research Projects (AICRP) and All\n-\n \nIndia Network Projects \nand four Consortia Research Platforms.\n \nThese institutions are \nmandated to produce breeder \nseed of crops, which in turn is converted to other classes of seeds through multiplication by\n \ndifferent agencies to ensure the \navailability\n \nof\n \nquality\n \nseed\n \nto\n \nthe\n \nfarmers.\n \nThe major emphasis \nof these efforts is \non the development of high yielding climate \nresilient new varieties/ hybrids which are tolerant to various biotic and abiotic stresses wit\nh\n \nenhanced quality. During 2014 \nto 2022, a total of \n1752climate resilient \nvarieties have been \nreleased through Central Sub\n-\nCommittee on Crop Standards\n.\n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |83", "t": "Toll Collection", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��������� �\n����������������������� �� �\n������������ � ��� �������� ����� � �\n��������������� �\n� �