7 Uttar Pradesh 601701.90 1201410.42 850956.51 770692.84 \n28 Uttarakhand 45580.30 88626.63 64203.24 59587.19 \n29 West Bengal 850761.08 1145405.21 750780.15 0 \n30 Andaman and Nicobar \nIslands 583.78 485.89 763.16 461.41 \n31 Lakshadweep 23.82 0.00 30.05 0 \n32 Puducherry 1691.87 2683.69 1307.28 1982 \n33 Ladakh 0.00 0.00 5904.39 4970.6 \n34 Dadra & Nagar Haveli \n& Daman & Diu 0.00 0.00 0.00 80.93 \n   Total 71,04,628.56 1,10,35,182.74 97,79,432.94 65,74,913.86 \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |882", "t": "PMGSY-III in Kerala", "head": "   \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 882 \n  ANSWERED ON 07/02/2023 \n \nPMGSY-III IN KERALA \n \n882.  SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the steps taken by the Union Government for the completion of the entire length of \n1425 kms of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY)-III roads allocated to the \nState of Kerala; \n(b)  whether some portion of the work is yet to begin as it is proposed with a surface \ndressing; \n(c)  whether it has come to the notice of the Union Government that the road construction \ncontractors in Kerala are not equipped with necessary machinery and are not familiar \nwith such a technology; and \n(d)  if so, the course of action from the Union Government to carry on the work and \nensure its timely completion? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)    The State of Kerala has been allocated target of 1,425 Km road  length under Pradhan \nMantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY)-III, against which the State has so far been sanctioned \n686.232 Km . Out of total sanctioned length, 71.74 Km has already been completed.   The \nimplementation period of PMGSY-III is upto March, 2025. The Ministry has been constantly \nfollowing-up with the State Government for submission of proposals, which conform to the \nguidelines, for the balance length. \n(b) to (d) Rural roads under PMGSY are constructed and maintained  as per the technical \nspecifications and geometric design standards given in the Ministry of Rural Development \nSpecifications for Rural Roads, Rural Roads Manual of the Indian Roads Congress (IRC) \n(IRC-SP:20) and also, where required, the Hill Road Manual (IRC:SP:48) and other relevant \nIRC Codes & Manuals. As per IRC: SP:72 -2015, which is meant for design of low volume \nrural roads, Surface Dressing (SD) is recommended as surface course for rural roads designed \nfor cumulative Equivalent Single -Axle Load (ESAL) repetition up to 3,00,000 (T5) \ncategories. Surface dressing can also be adopted for traffic category up to T9 as per Para \n7.3.3 of IRC: SP:72 -2015. Surface dressing is suitable for all climatic/ rainfall conditions as \nper IRC: SP:72-2015. \n  \nThe State of Kerala has been sanctioned 58 road works of 251.209 Km using surface dressing \ntechnology. The choice of surface dressing for rural roads in Kerala has been made as per the \ntraffic category of the roads, as specified in IRC code. As reported by the State Government, \nof the sanctioned project s under surface dressing, 53 road works of 227.116 Km stand \nawarded. Agreement for the awarded works was executed from April, 2022 to August, 2022 \nand the works are reaching the base course layer.  \n\n \nExecution of works under PMGSY is discussed with State Go vernments during various \nreview meetings. Also training programmes/ webinars have been conducted for States, \nProgramme Implementation Units (PIUs) and contractors from time to time. Recently, during \nInternational Conference on New Technologies and Innovati ons in Rural Roads held from \n24-26 May, 2022 in New Delhi, a booklet has been published and circulated to States on \nsurface dressing covering the execution process/ methodology in detail. PMGSY had adopted \nnew technologies in construction of rural roads, and for this purpose, suitable hand-holding is \nprovided to the states. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |883", "t": "Fungal Disease in Apple Crops", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 883\n \nANSWERED ON 7\nTH \nFEBRUARY, 2023\n \n“FUNGAL DISEASE\n \nIN APPLE CROPS\n”\n \n \n883\n.\n \nDR. AMAR SINGH:\n \n \n \n \nWill\n \nthe\n \nMinister\n \nof\n \nAgriculture\n \nand\n \nFarmers\n \nWelfare \n \nकृ िष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी \nbe\n \npleased\n \nto\n \nstate:\n \n \n(a) whether it has come to the notice of the\n \nGovernment that fungal diseases like scab, premature\n \nleaf\n-\nfall, blight, powdery mildew, etc. are more\n \ndang\nerous for the app\nle crop than bacterial and viral \ndiseases; and\n \n(b) \nif so, the details of the steps taken/proposed\n \nto be taken by t\nhe Government to encourage apple \ngrowers across the country to integrate bio\n-\ncontrol\n \nmethods with chemical sprays to control \ndiseases?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ िष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी     \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (b\n) \nThe o\nccurrence of f\nungal diseases like scab, premature leaf\n-\nfall, blight, powdery mildew \nin apple\n \ndepend on favourable environment\nal\n \nconditions and presence of pathogen of respective \ndiseases\n. F\nungal diseases are of \neconomic concern as\n \nthey reduce\n \nyield \nand \nquality of fruits\n. \nIn \norder to increase the production & productivity of apple crop grown in th\ne country and to manage \nvarious pest/diseases in apple\n; \nDirectorate of Plant Protection Quarantine and Storage (DPPQS) \nthrough \nC\nentral \nI\nntegrated \nP\nest \nM\nanagement \nC\nentre\ns\n \n(CIPMCs)\n \nin\n \napple growing \nStates conduct \nregular surveys and training programs (\nFarmers Field Schools, 2 Day & 5 Day Human Resource \nDevelopment\n \nprogrammes\n) and\n \neducate \nfarmers about importance of bio control agents as \nan \nalternative to\n \nchemical control of pests & diseases of apple.\n \nDPPQS in coordination with ICAR\n-\nCentral Institute of \nTemperate Horticulture, Srinagar has developed I\nntegrated \nP\nest \nM\nanagement \n(IPM)\n \nPackage\n \nof Practices for Apple (for P\nroducing Quality Fruits for Export)\n. \nVarious \nfungicides have been registered \nfor the management of fungal diseases in apple. \nAlso, \nadvisori\nes \nare issued to the States for \nthe management of the diseases in apple.\n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |884", "t": "Conservation and Promotion of Indigenous Breed of Cows", "head": " \n \nOIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH AND EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 884 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nCONSERVATION AND PROMOTION OF INDIGENOUS BREED OF COWS \n \n884.  DR. RAMESH POKHRIYAL “NISHANK”: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE               \nक ृ षि और षकसान कल्याण मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) whether any schemes for the conservation and promotion of the indigenous breed \nof cows is under implementation by the Government/Agricultural Universities/Research \nInstitutions;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether any special scheme has been  implemented for the conse rvation and \npromotion of cows of Himalayan region and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ षि और षकसान कल्याण मंत्री             (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c): Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying implements Rashtriya Gokul \nMission for development and conservation of indigenous bovine breeds and genetic \nupgradation of bovine population since December 2014. All indigenous breeds of cattle \nincluding breeds of Himalayan region are covered under the scheme. \nFurther, in order to conserve and propagate the indigenous cattle breeds of the \ncountry, Indian Council of Agricultural Research - Central Institute for Research on \nCattle, Meerut implements the Indigenous Breeds Project (IBP) under the All India Co-\nOrdinated Research Project (AICRP) on Cattle. Under this project, the genetic \nimprovement of three important indigenous cattle breeds of the country viz., Gir, \nKankrej and Sahiwal is undertaken in collaboration with different State Agricultural / \nVeterinary Universities, other ICAR institutes, NGOs and Gaushalas. \nICAR-National Bureau of Animal Genetic Resources conserves indigenous breeds \nof cows through cryopreserving the germplasm in its National Gene Bank. Presently, \nthe germplasm of 29 indigenous cattle breeds in form of semen and six breeds in form \nof somatic cells have been cryopreserved. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |885", "t": "Setting up of Sugarcane based Processing Industries", "head": " \nGOVERNMENTOF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 885 \nANSWERED ON 07TH FEBRUARY,2023 \n  \nSETTING UP OF SUGARCANE BASED PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n  \n†885. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY: \n  \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state; \n  \n(a)  the schemes for setting up of sugarcane based processing industries in each of the sugarcane \nproducing/processing or districts of the country; \n(b)  whether there is any scheme for setting up of food processing industr ies in view of providing \nemployment; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof, State and district- wise; and \n(d)  if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a):  Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) has been incentivizing setting up/ upgradation of \nFood Processing Industries, including sugarcane based Processing Industries, through its Central Sector \nUmbrella Scheme Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (P MKSY), Production Linked Incentive \nScheme for Food Processing Industry (PLISFPI) and Centrally sponsored PM Formalization of Micro \nFood Processing Enterprises (PMFME) scheme across all the districts of the country. So far, a total of \n499 sugarcane & jagger y based micro food processing enterprises have been approved for assistance \nunder PMFME scheme and  a total of 5  solid & powder jaggery based Food Processing Units have been \napproved under Creation/Expansion of Food Processing and Preservation Capacities (CEFPPC) scheme \nof PMKSY. \n \n(b) to (d):  Schemes administered by MoFPI  have resulted in creation of substantial off -farm \nemployment opportunities in Food Processing Sector.   Creation of more than 9.4 lakh direct/ indirect \nemployment opportunities are estimated through projects completed under PMKSY. In the evaluation \nstudy of Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure Scheme conducted by M/s NABARD \nConsultancy Limited (NABCONS) in Year 2020, it has been estimated that each captive project under \nthe scheme has resulted in 600 direct/indirect employment opportuniti es on an average. State -wise \nprojects approved under PMKSY and PMFME schemes are provided in Annexure-I & Annexure-II \nrespectively. \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.885 FOR ANSWER ON 07TH FEBRUARY, 2023 REGARDING “SETTING UP \nOF SUGARCANE BASED PROCESSING INDUSTRIES” \nState/UT wise sanctioned projects under component schemes  \nof Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY) PMKSY as on 31.12.2023 \n  \nSTATES /  UTs Mega F\nood Par\nk Schem\ne  \nIntegrated \nCold Chain \nAnd Value \nAddition In\nfrastructur\ne Scheme \nInfrastruct\nure For Agr\no-Processin\ng Clusters S\ncheme \nCreation / Expa\nnsion Of Food P\nrocessing & Pre\nservation Capac\nities Scheme \nScheme For Cr\neation Of Back\nward And For\nward Linkages \nOperatio\nn Greens \nAndhra \nPradesh \n3 33 4 6 0 20 \nArunachal  \nPradesh \n1 1 0 9 0 0 \nAssam 1 2 8 30 1 1 \nBihar 2 6 1 5 0 0 \nChhattisgarh 1 2 1 4 1 0 \nGoa 0 0 0 1 0 0 \nGujarat 2 29 4 36 8 9 \nHaryana 2 19 3 17 0 0 \nHimachal 1 17 2 22 2 2 \nJharkhand 0 0 0 0 0 0 \nKarnataka 2 17 5 20 3 0 \nKerala 2 6 2 19 1 0 \nMadhya  \nPradesh \n2 13 5 17 1 2 \nMaharashtra 3 68 14 75 11 9 \nManipur 1 1 0 5 0 0 \nMeghalaya 1 0 1 2 0 0 \nMizoram 1 2 0 1 0 0 \nNagaland 1 3 0 4 0 0 \nOdisha 2 7 1 4 0 0 \nPunjab 3 24 3 16 4 1 \nRajasthan 2 13 4 16 6 1 \nSikkim 0 0 0 1 0 0 \nTamil   Nadu 1 16 11 29 9 2 \nTelangana 2 14 5 6 1 0 \nTripura 1 0 0 5 0 0 \nUttar  Pradesh 0 28 3 30 5 5 \nUttarakhand 2 30 1 14 4 0 \nWest  Bengal 1 15 0 14 1 0 \nAndaman &  \nNicobar \n0 2 0 0 0 0 \nChandigarh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \nD & N   Haveli an\nd D&D \n0 0 0 1 0 0 \nDelhi 0 0 0 0 0 0 \nJammu &  \nKashmir \n1 7 1 18 3 0 \nPudduchery 0 1 0 0 0 0 \n\n Annexure-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.885 FOR ANSWER ON 07TH FEBRUARY, 2023 REGARDING “SETTING UP \nOF SUGARCANE BASED PROCESSING INDUSTRIES” \nState/UT wise number of Micro Food Processing Enterprises to whom assistance approved \nunder PMFME as on 03.02.2023 \n  \n  \nStates / UTs \nNumber of Micro Food \nEnterprises to which \nLoans  sanctioned  \nNumber of Micro Food \nEnterprises to which Loans \nDisbursed \nUttar Pradesh 1534 933 \nMaharashtra 3781 2059 \nTamil Nadu 2551 1751 \nKarnataka 1557 1233 \nMadhya Pradesh 1046 440 \nBihar 793 118 \nAndhra Pradesh 1212 741 \nGujarat 77 51 \nPunjab 838 732 \nRajasthan 244 189 \nJharkhand 40 10 \nOdisha 653 305 \nTelangana 1332 342 \nHaryana 329 242 \nKerala 306 196 \nChhattisgarh 160 78 \nUttarakhand 162 93 \nHimachal Pradesh 726 666 \nGoa 39 25 \nTotal States 17380 10204 \nAssam 407 248 \nTripura 38 20 \nManipur 242 222 \nNagaland 20 12 \nMeghalaya 25 18 \nMizoram 2 1 \nArunachal Pradesh 17 14 \nSikkim 22 23 \nTotal NER 773 558 \nJammu And Kashmir 170 91 \nDelhi 83 35 \nPuducherry 31 16 \nAndaman & Nicobar 9 5 \nChandigarh 5 5 \nD & N   Haveli and D&D 2 2 \nLadakh 19 16 \nLakshadweep 0 0 \nTotal UTs 319 170 \nGrand Total 18472 109"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |886", "t": "Pension to Farmers in Bihar", "head": "     O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \n                               LOK SABHA     \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 886 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2023 \n \nPENSION TO FARMERS IN BIHAR \n \n886. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there is any plan of the Government regarding pension for the farmers in Bihar; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  the details of the Government’s plan to improve the economic condition of farmers in \nBihar particularly in Gaya; and \n \n(d)  the details of Government’s plane for imparting training to the farmers in Bihar particular \nin Gaya? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nक ृषि एवं कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री  (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) and (b) The Government of India is  running Pradhan Mantri Kisan MaanDhan Yojana in \norder to provide social security net for the Small and Marginal Farmers (SMF) by way of \npension. Under this scheme, a minimum assured pension of Rs. 3,000/- will be provided to the \neligible small and marginal farmers, subject to certain exclusion clauses, on attaining the age \nof 60 years. The Scheme is a voluntary and contributory pension scheme, with entry age \nbetween 18 to 40 years.  \n \n(c) and (d) The Government of India is running various Schemes for improvement of economic \ncondition of farmers viz. Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi Yojana (PM -KISAN), Pradhan \nMantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), Soil Health Card, Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana, \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yoj ana (PKVY), Sub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization \n(SMAM) etc. These Schemes provide income assistance to the eligible farmers of the country \nand aid in upliftment of farmers economically including in Gaya, Bihar. Further training is \nimparted to farmers under different schemes for capacity building and skill improvement. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |888", "t": "Crop Diversification", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 888\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n7\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n \n2023\n \n \nCROP \nDIVERSIFICATION \n \n \n888.      \nSHRI \nT.R. BAALU\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \nिकसान\n \nक\u0000ाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether there is need to consolidate and\n \ncreate a separate fund for the \npromotion of diversification from paddy and wheat to crops such as oilseeds, pul\nses, \nfruits and vegetables; and  \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the steps taken/being taken by the Government keeping in mind that one \nof the ways to increase crop diversification is publicizing a bonus on PM\n-\nKISAN for the \nfarmers who shifted from paddy\n-\nwheat crop cycle to the G\novernment\n-\n \nsuggested crops \nbased on agroecology and market research?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \nिकसान\n \nक\u0000ाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nThe Department of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare (DA&FW) \nis\n \nimplementing Crop D\niversification Programme (CDP) \nin Original Green Revolution \nStates viz; Haryana, Punjab and Western Uttar Pradesh to divert the area of water \nintensive paddy crop to alternative \ncrops, like pulses, oilseeds, coarse cereals, nutri \ncereals, cotton etc. \n \nFurther, \nGovernment of India supplements the efforts of state governments to \nencourage diversified production of crops such as pulses, coarse cereals, nutri cereals \n\n& cotton under Na\ntional Food Security Mission (NFSM) and high value horticultural \ncrops under Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH). \n \nGovernment of India also provide\ns\n \nflexibility to the states for state specif\nic \nneeds/priorities under RKVY. \nThe states can promote crop diversification under RKVY \nwith the approval of State Level Sanctioning Committee (SLSC) headed by Chief \nSecretary of the respective states. \n \nIndian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR), throug\nh its crop based institutes \nand All India Coordinated Res\nearch Projects (AICRPs), and \nin active collaboration \nwith State Universities (SAUs) is engaged in basic and strategic research to develop \nhigh yielding climate resilient \nvarieties\n/hybrids of oilseed\ns\n \nand pulses to diversify the \nwheat \na\nnd rice \ncropping\n \nsystem for cultivation in different agro\n-\necologies of country.\n \n \n \n \nT\nhe Pradhan Mantri \nKisan Samman Nidhi (PM\n-\nKISAN) \naims at providing \nfinancial assistance to cultivable landholding farmer families across \nthe country, \nsubject to certain exclusion criteria, to enable them to take care of expenses related \nto agriculture and allied activities as well as domestic needs. \n \n \n \n \n************\n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |889", "t": "Viewership Rating", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.889 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2023) \nVIEWERSHIP RATING \n889. DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR:  \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is true that the viewership rating of the Government owned \nDoordarshan channels has fell down very drastically when compared  \nwith other private channels;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to improve/ increase Door darshan \nchannels so as to compete with other private channels particularly \nthe regional channels;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof;  \n(e) the details and the total number of Doordarshan regional \nKendras/channels which are functioning as on date along with th e \ntotal number of staff working on full time and part-time basis; and  \n(f) the details of the budget allotted to each Doordarshan Kendra during \nthe last three years and the current year? \n \n\nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER O F YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS (SHRI ANURAG SINGH \nTHAKUR) \n(a) to (d) : Prasar Bharati has informed that as per the Broadcast \nAudience Research Council ( BARC) Viewership data of subscribed \nDoordarshan (DD) channels for the last 5 years, viewership data across \nthe Network is as under: \nYear Gross Unique Viewership (in Millions) for Doordarshan \nChannels \n2018 743.1 \n2019 760.4 \n2020 747.0 \n2021 684.9 \n2022 724.3 \n \nDoordarshan has been revamping the content of the channels both at \nNational and Regional levels according  to the need and taste of \naudience, including by way of opening of portal inviting proposals for \nfresh programmes from reputed production houses. A number of mega \nshows have been launched along  with new serials to attract viewers \nacross different socio -economic segments. Doordarshan is also \nstrengthening its marketing vertical for publicity of programmes \nwhich will enhance viewership.  \nModernization of Doordarshan is a continuous process and schemes in \nthis regard are formulated and implemented on periodica l basis \ndepending upon availability of resources and funds. \n \n\n(e) Presently, there are 66 Doordarshan Studio Centres functioning in \nthe country  consisting of 23 major Studio Centre s where 24x7 \ntransmission originates, 7 Studio Centres where Non 24x7 \ntransmission originates and 36 other contributory Studio Centres. \nDoordarshan has 36 Satellite Channels  consisting of 7 All India \nChannels, 21 Regional channels (24x7), 7 Regional Channels (Non \n24x7) and one International Channel . Details of channels are at \nAnnexure – I. \nThere are 7319 regular staff working in Doordarshan . Number of \ncontractual employees in Doordarshan and DD News are 192 and 404 \nrespectively. \n(f) The details of budget allocated to D oordarshan Kendras (D DKs) \nduring the last 3 years and current year is at Annexure- II. \n******* \n\n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e)  OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.889 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2023 \nAnnexure – I \nSatellite Channels : 36 \n   \n     International Channel – 1        DD India \n    \nAll India Channels – 7    \nDD National   DD Sports   DD Urdu DD Retro \nDD News  DD Bharati   DD Kisan  \n \n   Regional Channels - 21 (24X7) \n  \nDD Malayalam   DD Saptagiri  DD Kashir  DD Madhya \nPradesh \nDD Podhigai  DD Chandana  DD Assam  DD Uttar Pradesh   \nDD Odia   DD Sahyadri  DD Punjabi   DD Bihar  \nDD Bangla  DD Girnar  DD Rajasthan   DD Yadagiri \nDD Arunprabha  DD \nUttarakhand \n DD \nJharkhand  \n DD Chhattisgarh \nDD Tripura     \n \n     Regional Channels - 7 (Non 24X7) \nDD Himachal \nPradesh \n DD Haryana   DD Mizoram  DD Nagaland \nDD Goa  DD \nMeghalaya \n DD Manipur    \n         \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (f) OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.889 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2023 \nAnnexure – II \n \nDetails of the Budget alloca tion to each Doordarshan Kendra  during \nthe last three years and the current Financial Year \n (Rs. In Thousand) \nS.No. DDKs Budget \nAllotted \n2019-20 \nBudget \nAllotted \n2020-21 \nBudget \nAllotted \n2021-22 \nBudget \nAllotted \n2022-23 \n1.  DDK AGARTALA 123360 121541 119618 116539 \n2.  DDK AHMEDABAD 326226 306266 324624 356764 \n3.  DDK AIZWAL 138488 123570 150358 132087 \n4.  DDK ALLAHABAD 50639 54321 57473 48502 \n5.  DDK BANGALORE 454750 396538 427528 410599 \n6.  DDK BAREILLY 56031 49088 80231 63485 \n7.  DDK BHAWANIPATNA 29838 26370 39574 35495 \n8.  DDK BHOPAL 267258 258319 294895 320519 \n9.  DDK BHUBANESWAR 292326 247017 244281 276391 \n10.  DDK KOZHIKODE 22564 18483 16753 16660 \n11.  DDK CHANDIGARH 63774 64780 51553 57320 \n12.  DDK CHENNAI 538791 476995 445701 455315 \n13.  DDK COIMBATORE 46184 45695 41372 39642 \n14.  DDK DALTONGANJ 47249 42900 44980 39774 \n15.  DDK DEHRADUN 81220 89337 90057 103552 \n16.  DDK DELHI 1550573 1549633 1765098 1422178 \n17.  DDK CPC DELHI 367553 337170 358873 462826 \n18.  DDK DIBRUGARH 103807 97295 109510 119711 \n19.  DDK GANGTOK 30165 54541 44929 43480 \n20.  DD"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|11|UNSTARRED |89", "t": "MSME Udyam", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 89\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n02.02.2023\n \n \nMSME UDYAM\n \n \n89\n.\n     \n \n   \nSHRI DIBYENDU ADHIKARI:\n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe detail\n \nof fu\nnds allotted and sanctioned to U\ndyam/new registered firms during the last t\nhree \nyears and the current year, State\n-\nwise break\n-\nup;\n \n(b) \n \nthe\n \npresent minimum and maximum \ncollateral free bank credit guarantee amount for MSME \nUdyam  registered organisations \nalong with the terms and conditions thereof;\n \n(c) \n \nthe details of procedures and co\nmpliance in granting fund from Credit Guarantee Fund Trust \nto Micro and Small E\nnterprises (\nCGTMSE\n) therefor; and\n \n(d) \n \nthe condition and eligibility to avail bank credit without hassles of collaterals/third party \nguarantees to startup organisations thereto?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM EN\nTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) to (d)\n:\n \nThe Ministry of MSME provides funds to implementing agencies under various \nschemes. Udyam\n \nRegistration Certificate is used by enterprises to be classified as MSMEs and for \navailing benefits of Ministry of MSME’s schemes and programmes. The major schemes that provide \ncollateral free bank credit are Emergency Credit Lined Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS\n) and Credit \nGuarantee Scheme (CGS) for Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs). The details of the State wise \nguarantees issued during the last three years and the current year for the scheme ECLGS and Credit \nGuarantee Scheme (CGS) for MSEs are at \nAnnexure I\n. \nCredit Guarantee Trust Fund for Micro and \nSmall Enterprises (CGTMSE) provides guarantee coverage for MSE loans including startups for \nloans upto Rs 2.00 crore extended by its Member Lending Institutions (MLIs) as per their own \nprocedures. \n \n \n               \n                                                                                                                      \n \n****\n \n \n         \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n  \nAnnexure I\n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply\n \nto part (a) to (d)\n \nof Lok Sabha Unstarred\n \nQuestion No. 89 for answer on \n02.02.2023\n \n(i) Number and Amount of Guarantees issued under Emergency Credit Lined Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS)\n \nStates/UTs data for ECLGS\n \n \nState/UT\n \nFY 2020\n-\n21\n \nFY 2021\n-\n2022\n \nFY (2022\n-\n2023) Till 31 Dec \n2022\n \nNo.  of \nGuarantees \nIssued\n \nLoan \nAmount \nGuaranteed \n(Rs. In Cr)\n \nNo.  of \nGuarantees \nIssued\n \nLoan Amount \nGuaranteed \n(Rs. In Cr)\n \nNo.  of \nGuarantees \nIssued\n \nLoan Amount \nGuaranteed \n(Rs. In Cr)\n \nAndaman & Nicobar\n \n1,978\n \n96.01\n \n123\n \n44.55\n \n24\n \n13.11\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n2,53,111\n \n8,659.26\n \n37,711\n \n3,560.14\n \n3,033\n \n833.77\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n2,228\n \n7,1.32\n \n159\n \n57.94\n \n44\n \n5.77\n \nAssam\n \n5,29,128\n \n2,715.09\n \n23,527\n \n807.08\n \n1,690\n \n269.54\n \nBihar\n \n6,